var e=[];e.push({id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin`,title:`Pyrexia of unknown origin: classic, nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Pyrexia of unknown origin (PUO)** is fever of **38.3 C or more on several occasions for at least 3 weeks** that stays undiagnosed after a **defined set of obligatory investigations** (Durack-Street: 3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital); it is split into **classic, nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated** PUO, and in India it is most often **tuberculosis, enteric fever, lymphoma or adult-onset Still disease** - an uncommon disease presenting typically far more often than a common disease presenting atypically.`,keywords:[`PUO`,`FUO`,`fever of unknown origin`,`Petersdorf-Beeson`,`Durack-Street`,`nosocomial PUO`,`neutropenic fever`,`HIV-associated PUO`,`inflammation of unknown origin`,`potentially diagnostic clues`,`FDG PET-CT`,`adult-onset Still disease`,`Yamaguchi criteria`,`naproxen test`,`drug fever`,`factitious fever`,`disseminated tuberculosis`,`Kikuchi disease`,`giant cell arteritis`,`bone marrow biopsy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Petersdorf and Beeson (1961)** first defined PUO as temperature **over 38.3 C (101 F) on several occasions**, illness lasting **more than 3 weeks**, and no diagnosis after **1 week of inpatient study**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Durack and Street (1991)** replaced the 1 week in hospital with **3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital** (or 1 week of intelligent and invasive outpatient investigation) and added **nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated** categories. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Harrison 22e** uses a **qualitative definition**: fever 38.3 C or more on at least two occasions, illness of 3 weeks or more, **no known immunocompromised state**, and no diagnosis after a **specified list of obligatory investigations** - the quality of the work-up matters, not the days spent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammation of unknown origin (IUO)** is the same problem with raised CRP/ESR but temperature under 38.3 C - it is worked up identically and has a similar spectrum, with more vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`In Western series, the share of PUO caused by **infection has fallen to about 20-25%**, non-infectious inflammatory disease (NIID) is 20-30%, neoplasm 10-15%, and **up to half stay undiagnosed** - better imaging diagnoses many causes before they become PUO. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **Indian series** (AIIMS, PGIMER, CMC Vellore cohorts), **infection still causes 40-60%**, and **tuberculosis is the commonest single cause** (most extrapulmonary or disseminated), followed by enteric fever, infective endocarditis, kala-azar, brucellosis and melioidosis; undiagnosed is usually 10-20%. [API 2019]`,`**Neoplasms** cause 10-20% of Indian PUO - **lymphoma** (Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin) leads, then leukaemia, renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma and metastatic disease. [API 2019]`,`**NIID** cause 10-20% in India - **adult-onset Still disease (AOSD)** in young adults, SLE, vasculitis (Takayasu, polyarteritis), and **giant cell arteritis or polymyalgia rheumatica** in those over 50. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Age changes the spectrum**: under 50, infection and AOSD dominate; over 65, **GCA/PMR, malignancy and TB** - so ESR, temporal arteries and a cancer search matter more in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infections (India)**: disseminated, miliary and extrapulmonary **TB** (lymph node, abdominal, spinal, genitourinary), enteric fever (often partially treated), **culture-negative infective endocarditis**, liver or splenic abscess, **visceral leishmaniasis** (Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal), brucellosis, melioidosis, chronic malaria, HIV and its opportunistic infections. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Hidden pyogenic foci**: subphrenic, pelvic, psoas, perinephric and dental abscess, prostatitis, cholangitis, sinusitis, vertebral osteomyelitis and **infected vascular grafts or devices**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neoplastic**: lymphoma (B symptoms, LDH rise), acute leukaemia and myelodysplasia, **renal cell carcinoma** (classic paraneoplastic fever), hepatocellular carcinoma, colon cancer, atrial myxoma and **Castleman disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NIID**: AOSD, SLE, **giant cell arteritis/PMR**, Takayasu arteritis, polyarteritis nodosa, ANCA vasculitis, sarcoidosis, inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn), reactive arthritis and **Kikuchi-Fujimoto disease** (necrotising lymphadenitis in young women). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Miscellaneous**: **drug fever**, thyroiditis (subacute), **recurrent pulmonary emboli or DVT**, haematoma, alcoholic hepatitis, adrenal insufficiency, pheochromocytoma, periodic fever syndromes (FMF), **factitious fever** and habitual hyperthermia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest drugs causing fever**: beta-lactams, sulphonamides (cotrimoxazole), anticonvulsants (phenytoin, carbamazepine), **antitubercular drugs**, allopurinol, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa, quinidine and heparin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Risk factors that reorder the list**: diabetes (melioidosis, TB, abscess), HIV risk behaviour, prosthetic valve or pacemaker (endocarditis), raw milk and animal contact (brucellosis), residence in a kala-azar block, **rice-field work in Odisha or coastal Karnataka** (melioidosis), and recent travel. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Partial antibiotic treatment** is a peculiarly Indian cause - over-the-counter quinolones and cephalosporins turn enteric fever, endocarditis and abscess into **culture-negative PUO**. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why fever persists`,points:[`**Fever** is a raised hypothalamic set-point: **IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha and interferons** induce endothelial **COX-2** in the OVLT, and **PGE2 acting on EP3 receptors** raises the set-point - so any process that keeps cytokines flowing keeps the fever going. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intracellular pathogens** (Mycobacterium, Salmonella, Brucella, Leishmania, Coxiella) survive inside macrophages, evade blood culture and drive **granuloma formation** - the reason liver and marrow biopsy find them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tumour fever** comes from cytokines released by tumour cells or by tumour necrosis (lymphoma, RCC, hepatoma, liver metastases) - classically responds to **naproxen**, unlike most infective fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AOSD** is an **autoinflammatory** disease of innate immunity with an **IL-1 and IL-18 storm** and macrophage activation - which explains quotidian spiking fever, neutrophilia, **very high ferritin** and the dramatic response to IL-1 blockade. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Hyperferritinaemia** reflects activated macrophages; ferritin over 3000-5000 ng/mL in PUO points to **AOSD, HLH or disseminated infection**, and a **glycosylated ferritin under 20%** is specific for AOSD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug fever** is mostly a **type III or IV hypersensitivity** (some are pyrogenic contamination or altered thermoregulation), so it starts after sensitisation - typically **7-10 days** after the drug - and settles **48-72 hours** after stopping it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative bradycardia** (pulse rising under 10 beats per degree C) in PUO points to drug fever, enteric fever, brucellosis, leptospirosis, CNS lesions or **factitious fever**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why PUO is mostly common diseases**: most cases are **atypical presentations of common diseases** (TB, abscess, lymphoma) rather than rare ones - so the work-up starts with the common and treatable. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - Durack-Street categories`,points:[`**Classic PUO**: fever over 38.3 C on several occasions, **over 3 weeks**, undiagnosed after **3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital** - causes are infection, neoplasm, NIID and miscellaneous. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Nosocomial (healthcare-associated) PUO**: fever over 38.3 C in a hospitalised patient, **not present or incubating at admission**, undiagnosed after **3 days including at least 2 days of culture incubation** - think line infection, C. difficile, drug fever, DVT/PE, sinusitis, acalculous cholecystitis, pancreatitis. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Neutropenic PUO**: fever over 38.3 C with **ANC under 500/microlitre** (or expected to fall below it in 1-2 days), undiagnosed after **3 days including 2 days of cultures** - think invasive fungal infection (Aspergillus, Candida), perianal infection, typhlitis; managed under febrile neutropenia protocols (see the fever in the immunocompromised topic). [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**HIV-associated PUO**: fever over 38.3 C in confirmed HIV, lasting **over 4 weeks as an outpatient or over 3 days in hospital**, undiagnosed after 3 days with 2 days of culture - in India **TB is the leading cause**, then MAC, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis, **Talaromyces marneffei (Manipur)**, CMV, leishmaniasis, lymphoma and drug fever. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Category by cause (classic PUO)**: infection, neoplasm, NIID, miscellaneous, and **undiagnosed** - the five-way split examiners expect in a long answer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Category by pattern**: continuous (enteric, lobar pneumonia), remittent, intermittent (malaria, abscess, lymphoma), **quotidian double spike** (AOSD, gonococcal endocarditis, kala-azar), **Pel-Ebstein** cyclical fever (Hodgkin) - patterns are suggestive only, and are blunted by antipyretics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrent (episodic) PUO**: febrile episodes separated by fever-free intervals of at least 2 weeks - think periodic fever syndromes, Hodgkin, Crohn, recurrent cholangitis, malaria relapse and factitious fever; it carries a good prognosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Document the fever** first: ask for a **temperature chart** with 4-6 hourly readings off antipyretics, and witness a recording - 10-15% of referred PUO have no true fever or have habitual hyperthermia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constitutional symptoms**: drenching night sweats, **weight loss over 10% in 6 months** and pruritus suggest lymphoma or TB; rigors suggest abscess, cholangitis, endocarditis or pyelonephritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Joint pain, sore throat and an evanescent salmon rash** appearing with the fever spike in a young adult - **AOSD**; arthralgia, photosensitivity, oral ulcers and alopecia - SLE. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Headache, jaw claudication, visual loss and shoulder-girdle stiffness** in a person over 50 - **GCA/PMR**; it is rarer in Indians but is missed because nobody asks. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Exposure history**: TB contact, raw milk or goat/cattle contact (**brucellosis**), soil and water exposure in diabetes (**melioidosis**), residence in a **kala-azar** block, sexual and injection history (HIV), travel and pets. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Drug history**: every drug started in the last 6 weeks including **ATT, anticonvulsants and ayurvedic preparations**, and all antibiotics already taken - partial treatment explains negative cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Past history**: valve disease or prosthetic valve, dental procedures, recent surgery, **previous TB treatment**, splenectomy, malignancy and immunosuppressants including steroids and biologics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clues to factitious fever**: a health-care worker, rapid defervescence without sweating, **temperature over 41 C with normal pulse**, absence of diurnal variation and discordance between oral and urine temperature. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Repeat the full examination every few days** - new murmurs, nodes, rashes and organomegaly appear over time; Harrison calls these **potentially diagnostic clues (PDCs)** that steer the next test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulse-temperature relation**: relative bradycardia (drug fever, enteric, brucella, factitious); disproportionate tachycardia (thyrotoxicosis, PE, abscess). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: salmon-pink evanescent rash (AOSD), splinter haemorrhages, Janeway lesions and Osler nodes (endocarditis), erythema nodosum (TB, sarcoid, IBD), livedo reticularis (PAN), **hyperpigmentation of kala-azar**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eyes**: fundal **Roth spots** (endocarditis), choroid tubercles (miliary TB - examine every PUO fundus), uveitis (sarcoid, Behcet), conjunctival suffusion (leptospirosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymph nodes**: matted cervical nodes (TB), rubbery painless nodes (lymphoma), tender posterior cervical nodes in young women (**Kikuchi**), generalised nodes (HIV, SLE, AOSD) - a palpable node is the easiest biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdomen**: **massive splenomegaly** (kala-azar, chronic malaria, lymphoma, CML), tender liver (abscess, hepatoma), ballotable kidney (RCC), and **per rectal and pelvic examination** for perirectal or prostatic abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: a new or changing regurgitant murmur (endocarditis), **unequal pulses or bruits** (Takayasu), tender thickened temporal artery with reduced pulsation (GCA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spine and joints**: percussion tenderness over vertebrae (TB spine, brucellosis, pyogenic spondylodiscitis), sacroiliac tenderness (brucellosis), synovitis (AOSD, SLE, reactive arthritis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - the discriminating clue`,points:[`**Disseminated TB versus lymphoma**: both give night sweats, weight loss and nodes; necrotising granuloma on biopsy, **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** or culture confirms TB, while LDH rise and Reed-Sternberg or monoclonal cells confirm lymphoma - **always biopsy before ATT** when a node is available. [WHO TB 2024]`,`**AOSD versus sepsis**: both have high spiking fever and neutrophilia; AOSD has **sore throat, salmon rash, arthritis, ferritin over 3000** with low glycosylated fraction and sterile cultures - and is a **diagnosis of exclusion**. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Infective endocarditis (culture-negative)**: prior antibiotics, Bartonella, Coxiella, Brucella or HACEK - send **3 sets of blood cultures before any antibiotic**, serology and **echocardiography (TOE)**; modified Duke-ISCVID 2023 criteria apply. [ESC 2023]`,`**Visceral leishmaniasis versus lymphoma or CML**: fever with massive spleen and pancytopenia in Bihar - **rK39 rapid test** and splenic or marrow aspirate for LD bodies separate them. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Drug fever versus infection**: patient looks well relative to the fever, relative bradycardia, eosinophilia (about 20%) and rash (about 20%), and **fever falls within 72 hours of stopping the drug**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GCA versus PMR alone**: PMR has proximal stiffness with raised ESR; headache, jaw claudication, visual symptoms or **halo sign on temporal artery ultrasound** mean GCA and need urgent high-dose steroid. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**HLH**: PUO with cytopenias, splenomegaly, ferritin over 500 (often over 10000), triglycerides 265 mg/dL or more and fibrinogen 150 mg/dL or less - **HLH-2004 criteria** (5 of 8) and HScore; see the HLH topic. [HLH-2004]`,`**Factitious and habitual hyperthermia**: no inflammatory markers rise - a **normal CRP and ESR with 'fever'** should make you witness the reading before ordering a PET-CT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line (obligatory stage)`,points:[`**Harrison obligatory tests**: ESR and **CRP**, CBC with differential and smear, electrolytes, creatinine, total protein and **protein electrophoresis**, ALP, AST, ALT, **LDH**, CK, **ferritin**, ANA, RF, urinalysis, **3 sets of blood cultures**, urine culture, chest X-ray, abdominal ultrasound and an **IGRA or tuberculin test**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India adds**: malaria smear and RDT, **HIV serology**, dengue/scrub IgM where fever began acutely, **Widal is not used**, rK39 in kala-azar areas, and **Brucella serology (SAT titre 1:160 or more)** with exposure. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Blood cultures**: 3 sets from separate sites, **20 mL per set** in adults, taken **before any antibiotic** and incubated for at least 5 days; ask for prolonged incubation and Brucella or HACEK alert - yield falls by half after a single antibiotic dose. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CRP and ESR**: a **normal CRP and ESR** make an organic cause unlikely and lower the yield of PET-CT; ESR over 100 mm/h suggests TB, endocarditis, GCA, myeloma, lymphoma or abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smear clues**: pancytopenia (kala-azar, TB marrow, HLH, lymphoma, SLE), atypical lymphocytes (EBV, CMV), monocytosis (TB, endocarditis, brucellosis), eosinophilia (drug fever, parasites, lymphoma, vasculitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine**: microscopic haematuria (endocarditis, RCC, PAN), **sterile pyuria** (genitourinary TB - send 3 early-morning urine samples for Xpert and culture). [NTEP 2025]`,`**Imaging first line**: chest X-ray may show miliary mottling or nodes; **ultrasound abdomen** finds abscess, nodes, splenic lesions and renal mass; **echocardiography** if a murmur or blood culture positive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Do not order** a panel of every serology at once - false positives multiply; let **PDCs direct** the second-stage tests. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tissue diagnosis`,points:[`**CECT chest-abdomen-pelvis** is the usual second-stage test in India - it finds abdominal nodes, abscesses, renal and pancreatic masses and splenic lesions. [API 2019]`,`**18F-FDG PET-CT** is the most useful imaging when obligatory tests fail and **CRP or ESR is raised** - it contributes to the final diagnosis in about **40-60%**, localises a biopsy target, and a **normal PET-CT predicts a good prognosis** and spontaneous remission. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PET-CT pitfalls**: FDG is taken up by infection, tumour and inflammation alike (it does not separate TB from lymphoma); recent **steroids reduce uptake**, high glucose degrades images, and the brain, heart and urinary tract are poorly seen. [EANM/SNMMI 2013]`,`**Biopsy the most accessible abnormal tissue**: **excision biopsy of a whole node** (not FNAC alone) with histology, AFB stain, **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** and mycobacterial culture - FNAC misses lymphoma architecture. [WHO TB 2024]`,`**Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy** (with culture) helps when there is **cytopenia or a raised LDH**: it finds lymphoma, leukaemia, TB granulomas, leishmania, brucella, histoplasma and haemophagocytosis; yield without cytopenia is low. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver biopsy** finds granulomas (TB, sarcoid, brucella) in a minority and is reserved for **raised ALP or hepatomegaly**; **temporal artery ultrasound then biopsy** for those over 50 with high ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mycobacterial tools**: **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra or Truenat** on any tissue or fluid, **MGIT liquid culture**, ADA in pleural/peritoneal fluid (over 40 U/L), urine **LF-LAM in advanced HIV** (CD4 under 200 or seriously ill). [NTEP 2025]`,`**Advanced microbiology**: 16S rRNA PCR on valve or tissue, **plasma metagenomic NGS** where available at tertiary centres, Coxiella and Bartonella serology for culture-negative endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AOSD labs**: ferritin often over 3000 ng/mL, **glycosylated ferritin under 20%**, neutrophil leucocytosis, raised transaminases, negative ANA and RF; **Yamaguchi criteria** need 5 features including 2 major, after exclusions. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Rule one - stop and think, do not treat blindly**: in a stable patient, **withhold empirical antibiotics and steroids** until tissue and cultures are taken - they cure nothing and erase the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stop all non-essential drugs** (or switch to unrelated classes) for 72 hours when drug fever is possible - fever that resolves confirms it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Symptomatic relief**: **paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly (max 4 g/day)** only when fever is distressing, and record the temperature before each dose so the chart stays useful. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Naproxen test** (naproxen **375 mg twice daily for 3 days**): rapid sustained defervescence favours tumour fever over infection - it is suggestive only, not a diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empirical ATT** is justified only in a deteriorating patient with strong TB clues (miliary pattern, granuloma on biopsy, high ADA) after samples are sent - give **HRZE by NTEP weight band for 2 months then HRE for 4 months**; response is usually seen in **2-3 weeks** and lack of response mandates re-evaluation. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Suspected GCA with visual symptoms**: start **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day** immediately (IV methylprednisolone if visual loss) and arrange biopsy or ultrasound within 1-2 weeks - steroids must not wait. [EULAR 2023]`,`**Suspected culture-negative endocarditis**: after 3 blood culture sets, empirical **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus gentamicin or vancomycin** per the endocarditis protocol (see the infective endocarditis topic). [ESC 2023]`,`**Counsel and follow up**: explain that PUO is a process, keep a temperature diary, review every **1-2 weeks**, and refer to a tertiary centre after 3 weeks if stage 1 is negative. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** when the patient is unstable, has **weight loss with cytopenias**, needs invasive biopsy, has HIV with low CD4, or is neutropenic, elderly or pregnant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neutropenic fever** (ANC under 500 and temperature 38.3 C once or 38.0 C for an hour) is an emergency: cultures and **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly (or cefepime or meropenem)** within 1 hour - this is not a PUO to watch. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Nosocomial PUO**: remove or change lines older than 72 hours with signs, culture catheter tip and peripheral blood, test **stool for C. difficile toxin**, Doppler the legs, check sinuses in intubated patients, and stop suspect drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV-associated PUO**: CD4 count, viral load, **urine LF-LAM, Xpert on sputum and other samples**, serum cryptococcal antigen, mycobacterial blood culture, and marrow biopsy for MAC, histoplasma, talaromyces and leishmania. [NACO 2021]`,`**Clinically deteriorating, still undiagnosed**: after all samples, a time-limited empirical trial - **ATT**, or **broad-spectrum antibiotics** in sepsis, or **steroids** only after lymphoma and TB are excluded - each trial with predefined stop rules. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AOSD confirmed**: **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day**; refractory or steroid-dependent disease - **anakinra 100 mg SC daily**, canakinumab or **tocilizumab 8 mg/kg IV 4-weekly**; screen daily for **macrophage activation syndrome** (falling ESR with rising ferritin). [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Kala-azar confirmed**: **liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg single IV infusion** (Indian programme regimen) - see the visceral leishmaniasis topic. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Monitor** a daily temperature chart, weight, CBC, LFT and ferritin, and repeat imaging or biopsy if a new PDC appears. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: GCA/PMR, TB, malignancy, endocarditis and drug fever dominate; fever may be blunted, so **CRP/ESR and delirium** carry more weight than the thermometer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: avoid ionising imaging where possible - **MRI and ultrasound** first; listeriosis, pyelonephritis, TB and SLE; ATT first line (HRZE) is safe; avoid streptomycin. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: melioidosis (multiple liver and splenic abscesses, prostatic abscess), TB, emphysematous pyelonephritis and mucormycosis - **CT abdomen** early, culture on Ashdown medium for Burkholderia. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: line infection, endocarditis, TB (peritoneal and pericardial) and drug fever; dose all drugs for GFR (see the antimicrobial pharmacology topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: infections (enteric, TB, UTI, malaria) cause most PUO, then **systemic JIA (Still disease)**, Kawasaki and leukaemia - a marrow examination precedes any steroid. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Steroid or biologic users**: TB reactivation (anti-TNF), nocardia, fungal and CMV infection; **screen for latent TB before biologics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a careful repeat examination, smear, CBC, ESR, chest X-ray, ultrasound, Xpert/Truenat on any sample and **node biopsy** solve most Indian PUO before PET-CT is needed. [API 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Prognosis depends on the cause**: malignancy-related PUO carries the highest mortality; infection and NIID do well when treated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Undiagnosed PUO has a good prognosis** - most resolve spontaneously within weeks to months, and mortality in long-term follow-up is low when the initial work-up was complete. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Harm from blind therapy**: empirical antibiotics mask endocarditis and abscess; empirical steroids accelerate TB, mask lymphoma (and make the biopsy uninterpretable) and precipitate **strongyloides hyperinfection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complications of the causes**: valve destruction and emboli (endocarditis), visual loss (GCA), **MAS/HLH** (AOSD, lymphoma, TB, EBV), and dissemination (TB, fungal) - each with high mortality if missed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complications of the work-up**: radiation dose from repeated CT, bleeding after liver biopsy, and the cost of a long admission - hence the stepwise approach. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a tertiary centre** when stage 1 tests are negative at 3 weeks, a PET-CT or invasive biopsy is needed, or the patient is HIV-positive, neutropenic or deteriorating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Follow up stable undiagnosed patients** every 2-4 weeks with history, examination, CBC, CRP/ESR - repeat the obligatory tests if a new clue appears. [Harrison 22e]`,`**On empirical ATT**: review at 2 weeks for fever and weight, LFT at baseline and if symptomatic, and **register on Ni-kshay**; persisting fever at 4-6 weeks means re-evaluate rather than add drugs. [NTEP 2025]`,`**On steroids for AOSD or GCA**: monitor glucose, BP, bone protection (calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate), and taper by the guideline schedule. [EULAR 2023]`,`**Write a fever summary** for each referral: chart, every test with date, antibiotics already given and the PDCs found - it prevents a costly repeat of the same work-up. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Harrison 22e (2025)** keeps the quality-based definition (obligatory investigations rather than days in hospital) and places **FDG PET-CT** as the next step when CRP/ESR is raised and stage 1 is negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EULAR/PReS 2024 Still disease recommendations** treat **systemic JIA and AOSD as one disease (Still disease)**, advise early **IL-1 or IL-6 blockade** to spare steroids, aim for steroid-free remission by 6 months, and screen for MAS. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 GCA classification criteria** (age 50 or more as entry, then weighted clinical, imaging and biopsy items) and **EULAR 2023 large-vessel vasculitis recommendations** favour **ultrasound of temporal and axillary arteries** as the first test. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline** adopts updated Duke criteria with **FDG PET-CT and cardiac CT as major imaging criteria** - relevant to culture-negative endocarditis presenting as PUO. [ESC 2023]`,`**WHO TB diagnosis guidelines (2024 consolidated update)** endorse molecular WHO-recommended rapid diagnostics (Xpert Ultra, Truenat) as initial tests on extrapulmonary samples, and targeted NGS for resistance - adopted by **NTEP**. [WHO TB 2024]`,`**India's kala-azar programme** has kept every endemic block below **1 case per 10,000** since 2023, so a PUO with splenomegaly outside the old belt is now less likely to be VL - but rK39 is still sent in Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and UP. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: define PUO and give the **four Durack-Street categories** with their time criteria - write 3 weeks, 3 visits/3 days, 2 days of culture, and 4 weeks for HIV outpatients. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Commonest cause of PUO in India**: **tuberculosis** (extrapulmonary/disseminated); commonest neoplastic cause: **lymphoma**; commonest NIID in young adults: **AOSD**; in the elderly: **GCA**. [API 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: starting ceftriaxone plus steroids on day 1 - write that empirical therapy is reserved for unstable patients, culture-negative endocarditis, suspected miliary TB and suspected GCA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Factitious fever clues** and the **naproxen test** are favourite short-answer items. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PET-CT question**: say it localises but does not characterise, has 40-60% diagnostic contribution, needs raised inflammatory markers to be useful, and a negative scan is reassuring. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Draw** the staged work-up flow chart (obligatory tests, PDC-guided tests, PET-CT, biopsy, watchful waiting) - it earns the most marks in a 10-mark answer. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Durack-Street categories of PUO`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Duration of undiagnosed work-up`,`Leading causes in India`],rows:[[`Classic`,`Over 38.3 C on several occasions for over 3 weeks`,`3 OPD visits or 3 days in hospital (or 1 week of intelligent outpatient study)`,`TB, enteric fever, endocarditis, lymphoma, AOSD, kala-azar`],[`Nosocomial`,`Over 38.3 C in an inpatient, not present or incubating at admission`,`3 days including 2 days of culture incubation`,`Line infection, C. difficile, drug fever, DVT/PE, sinusitis, acalculous cholecystitis`],[`Neutropenic`,`Over 38.3 C with ANC under 500 or expected to fall below 500 in 1-2 days`,`3 days including 2 days of culture incubation`,`Invasive candidiasis and aspergillosis, perianal infection, typhlitis, resistant Gram-negatives`],[`HIV-associated`,`Over 38.3 C in confirmed HIV infection`,`Over 4 weeks as outpatient or over 3 days in hospital, with 2 days of culture`,`TB, MAC, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis, talaromycosis, CMV, lymphoma, drug fever`]]},{heading:`Causes of classic PUO and their clues`,columns:[`Group`,`Main causes`,`Clinical clue`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Infection`,`Extrapulmonary or miliary TB, enteric fever, endocarditis, abscess, brucellosis, kala-azar, melioidosis`,`Night sweats, nodes, murmur, massive spleen, raw milk, diabetes`,`Xpert/culture on tissue, 3 blood cultures, echo, rK39, Brucella SAT, CT`],[`Neoplasm`,`Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, leukaemia, RCC, hepatoma, metastases`,`Weight loss, pruritus, rubbery nodes, raised LDH, haematuria`,`Excision node biopsy, marrow biopsy, CT, PET-CT`],[`Non-infectious inflammatory`,`AOSD, SLE, GCA/PMR, Takayasu, PAN, sarcoidosis, Crohn, Kikuchi`,`Salmon rash and sore throat, jaw claudication, unequal pulses, oral ulcers`,`Ferritin and glycosylated ferritin, ANA, temporal artery ultrasound/biopsy, CT angiography`],[`Miscellaneous`,`Drug fever, thyroiditis, PE/DVT, haematoma, adrenal insufficiency, factitious fever`,`Well-looking patient, relative bradycardia, eosinophilia, normal CRP`,`Drug withdrawal for 72 h, TSH, Doppler, CTPA, witnessed temperature`],[`Undiagnosed`,`10-20% in Indian series, up to 50% in Western series`,`Normal CRP and ESR, normal PET-CT`,`Watchful waiting with review - good prognosis`]]},{heading:`Staged work-up of classic PUO`,columns:[`Stage`,`Tests`,`Interpretation / cut-off`,`Next step if negative`],rows:[[`1. Confirm fever`,`Witnessed 4-6 hourly chart off antipyretics`,`38.3 C on at least 2 occasions; CRP rise confirms organic fever`,`No fever - habitual hyperthermia or factitious; stop work-up`],[`2. Obligatory tests`,`CBC, smear, ESR, CRP, LFT, LDH, CK, ferritin, electrolytes, creatinine, electrophoresis, ANA, RF, urine, 3 blood cultures, urine culture, HIV, IGRA, chest X-ray, ultrasound abdomen`,`ESR over 100 - TB, endocarditis, GCA, myeloma; ferritin over 3000 - AOSD, HLH`,`Look for potentially diagnostic clues`],[`3. PDC-directed tests`,`Echo, CECT chest-abdomen-pelvis, rK39, Brucella SAT, Xpert on fluids, temporal artery ultrasound`,`Brucella SAT 1:160 or more; ADA over 40 U/L in effusion`,`FDG PET-CT if CRP/ESR raised`],[`4. FDG PET-CT`,`Whole-body PET-CT`,`Localises a biopsy target in 40-60%; normal scan - good prognosis`,`Biopsy the hottest accessible lesion`],[`5. Tissue`,`Excision node biopsy, marrow aspirate and biopsy with culture, liver biopsy, temporal artery biopsy`,`Granuloma, Reed-Sternberg cells, LD bodies, haemophagocytosis`,`Watchful waiting or a time-limited therapeutic trial`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in PUO (diagnostic tests and targeted therapy)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day (2-3 g in liver disease)`,`Only for distress; record temperature before each dose`,`Hepatotoxicity in overdose, alcohol use, malnutrition`],[`Naproxen (naproxen test)`,`375 mg oral twice daily`,`3 days`,`GI bleeding, AKI; suggestive of tumour fever only, not diagnostic`],[`HRZE fixed-dose combination (NTEP)`,`H75 R150 Z400 E275 tablets by weight band: 25-34 kg 2, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6`,`2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE (longer for CNS and spinal TB)`,`Hepatitis, rash, optic neuritis (E), gout (Z); rifampicin interactions`],[`Prednisolone (GCA, AOSD)`,`GCA 40-60 mg/day oral (IV methylprednisolone 0.25-1 g/day x 3 if visual loss); AOSD 0.5-1 mg/kg/day`,`Taper over 12-18 months in GCA; aim steroid-free by 6 months in Still disease`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, TB reactivation - exclude TB and lymphoma first`],[`Anakinra (Still disease)`,`100 mg SC once daily`,`Long-term, taper when in remission`,`Injection-site reactions, neutropenia, infection; screen latent TB`],[`Tocilizumab (Still disease, GCA)`,`8 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks or 162 mg SC weekly`,`As maintenance, steroid-sparing`,`Masks CRP and fever, raised lipids and LFT, GI perforation, TB reactivation`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B (kala-azar)`,`10 mg/kg single IV infusion`,`Single dose`,`Infusion reactions, hypokalaemia, nephrotoxicity (less than conventional)`],[`Doxycycline plus rifampicin or gentamicin (brucellosis)`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD oral plus rifampicin 600-900 mg OD, or gentamicin 5 mg/kg IV OD for 7-14 days`,`6 weeks (12 weeks or more for spondylitis)`,`Photosensitivity, oesophagitis; avoid doxycycline in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`PUO clues to the diagnosis (differential table)`,columns:[`Clue`,`Think of`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Relative bradycardia`,`Drug fever, enteric fever, brucellosis, factitious fever`,`Drug withdrawal, blood/marrow culture, SAT`],[`Quotidian spike with salmon rash and sore throat`,`Adult-onset Still disease`,`Ferritin over 3000, glycosylated ferritin under 20%`],[`Massive splenomegaly with pancytopenia`,`Kala-azar, lymphoma, chronic malaria, CML`,`rK39, marrow or splenic aspirate, smear`],[`New regurgitant murmur`,`Infective endocarditis`,`3 blood cultures, TOE, Duke-ISCVID criteria`],[`Jaw claudication, age over 50, ESR over 50`,`Giant cell arteritis`,`Temporal artery ultrasound (halo) or biopsy`],[`Raised CRP with no clue at all`,`Lymphoma, occult abscess, vasculitis`,`FDG PET-CT then biopsy`],[`Normal CRP and ESR`,`Factitious or habitual hyperthermia`,`Witnessed temperature, paired urine temperature`]]}],redFlags:[`Neutropenia (ANC under 500) with fever - treat as febrile neutropenia within 1 hour, not as PUO.`,`Haemodynamic instability, rising lactate or new organ dysfunction - sepsis pathway now.`,`New visual symptoms or jaw claudication in a patient over 50 - same-day steroids for GCA.`,`Pancytopenia with splenomegaly and ferritin over 10000 - suspected HLH; urgent haematology.`,`New or changing murmur with emboli - suspected endocarditis; admit for cultures and echo.`,`HIV with CD4 under 200 and fever - admit for TB, cryptococcal and fungal work-up.`,`Rapid weight loss with nodes and drenching sweats - urgent excision biopsy for lymphoma.`],pearls:[`PUO is usually an **uncommon presentation of a common disease**, not a rare disease.`,`In India, think **TB first**, then enteric fever, endocarditis, lymphoma and AOSD.`,`**Biopsy before ATT or steroids** - an empirical trial erases the diagnosis.`,`**Normal CRP and ESR** with fever: witness the thermometer before ordering PET-CT.`,`Durack-Street numbers: **3 weeks, 3 visits or 3 days; 2 days of culture; HIV 4 weeks OPD**.`,`**Ferritin over 3000** with low glycosylated ferritin (under 20%) - AOSD; ferritin over 10000 with cytopenias - HLH.`,`**PET-CT localises, it does not characterise** - always follow with biopsy.`,`Undiagnosed PUO after a complete work-up has a **good prognosis** - watchful waiting is a valid plan.`,`Examine the **fundus for choroid tubercles and Roth spots** in every PUO.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q1`,stem:`According to Durack and Street, which of the following best defines nosocomial PUO?`,options:[`Fever over 38.3 C for 3 weeks in an outpatient after 3 clinic visits`,`Fever over 38.3 C in a hospitalised patient, not present at admission, undiagnosed after 3 days including 2 days of culture incubation`,`Fever over 38.3 C with ANC under 500, undiagnosed after 3 days`,`Fever over 38.3 C in HIV lasting more than 4 weeks as an outpatient`],answer:1,explanation:`Nosocomial PUO is fever arising in hospital that was not present or incubating at admission and stays undiagnosed after 3 days of work-up including at least 2 days of culture incubation. The 3-week, 3-visit definition is classic PUO, the ANC under 500 definition is neutropenic PUO, and the 4-week outpatient rule belongs to HIV-associated PUO.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q2`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman has 5 weeks of daily evening fever spikes to 39.5 C, a salmon-pink rash that appears with the fever, sore throat and wrist arthritis. WBC is 18,000 with 88% neutrophils, ferritin 9,500 ng/mL, ANA and RF negative, and cultures sterile. Which test most supports the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Anti-dsDNA antibody`,`Antistreptolysin O titre`,`Glycosylated ferritin fraction under 20%`,`Rheumatoid factor IgA`],answer:2,explanation:`Quotidian fever, evanescent salmon rash, sore throat, arthritis, neutrophilia and very high ferritin with negative ANA and RF fit adult-onset Still disease, and a glycosylated ferritin fraction under 20% is the most specific supporting test. Anti-dsDNA points to SLE, which causes leucopenia rather than neutrophilia; ASO supports rheumatic fever, which lacks ferritin this high; RF is already negative and not part of Still diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q3`,stem:`In a stable patient with classic PUO, stage 1 obligatory tests are unremarkable but CRP is 85 mg/L. What is the most appropriate next investigation according to current practice?`,options:[`Empirical four-drug antitubercular therapy for 4 weeks`,`Whole-body 18F-FDG PET-CT to localise a biopsy target`,`Empirical ceftriaxone and doxycycline for 2 weeks`,`Blind liver biopsy`],answer:1,explanation:`When obligatory tests are negative and inflammatory markers are raised, FDG PET-CT is the most useful next test because it localises lesions for biopsy and contributes to the diagnosis in about half of cases. Empirical ATT and empirical antibiotics in a stable patient erase the diagnosis, and a blind liver biopsy has low yield unless ALP is raised or the liver is enlarged.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man started phenytoin 10 days ago and now has fever of 39 C with a pulse of 84/min, a mild maculopapular rash and eosinophils of 12%. He looks well. What is the best initial step?`,options:[`Start IV piperacillin-tazobactam after blood cultures`,`Start oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg`,`Order an FDG PET-CT`,`Stop or substitute phenytoin and watch for defervescence within 72 hours`],answer:3,explanation:`Fever 7-10 days after a new drug, relative bradycardia, eosinophilia and rash in a well-looking patient suggest drug fever, so the diagnostic and therapeutic step is to stop or substitute the drug, expecting fever to settle within 48-72 hours. Broad-spectrum antibiotics are not indicated in a stable patient without sepsis, steroids are not first-line and would mask other diagnoses, and PET-CT is premature.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q5`,stem:`In which of the following situations is an empirical therapeutic trial most clearly justified in PUO before a tissue diagnosis?`,options:[`Suspected giant cell arteritis with visual symptoms in a 70-year-old`,`A stable young man with 4 weeks of fever and normal CRP`,`Suspected lymphoma with a palpable cervical node`,`Undiagnosed fever after negative PET-CT in a well patient`],answer:0,explanation:`Suspected GCA with visual symptoms needs immediate high-dose steroids because blindness can follow within days, and a biopsy remains positive for up to 2 weeks after starting steroids. A palpable node in suspected lymphoma should be excised first, since steroids destroy the histology. A stable patient with normal CRP, or with a negative PET-CT, needs watchful waiting rather than a blind trial.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define pyrexia of unknown origin and classify it. Discuss the aetiology of classic PUO in India and outline a stepwise approach to its investigation and management.`,openingLines:[`Pyrexia of unknown origin is fever of 38.3 C or more on several occasions for more than 3 weeks that remains undiagnosed after 3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital (Durack-Street 1991), or after a defined set of obligatory investigations (Harrison 22e).`,`Durack and Street divide it into classic, nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated PUO; in India tuberculosis is the commonest single cause.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`Petersdorf-Beeson 1961, Durack-Street 1991, Harrison qualitative definition.`,`Four categories with their time criteria (draw the table).`,`Inflammation of unknown origin - same approach with raised CRP but temperature under 38.3 C.`]},{heading:`Aetiology in India`,points:[`Infection 40-60%: TB (extrapulmonary/disseminated), enteric fever, endocarditis, abscess, kala-azar, brucellosis, melioidosis, HIV.`,`Neoplasm 10-20%: lymphoma, leukaemia, RCC, hepatoma.`,`NIID 10-20%: AOSD, SLE, GCA/PMR, Takayasu, sarcoidosis, Kikuchi.`,`Miscellaneous: drug fever, thyroiditis, PE, factitious fever; undiagnosed 10-20%.`]},{heading:`History and examination - potentially diagnostic clues`,points:[`Confirm fever with a chart; night sweats, weight loss, exposures, drugs, past TB.`,`Repeat examination: fundus, nodes, murmurs, spleen, temporal arteries, spine, rectal examination.`]},{heading:`Stepwise investigation`,points:[`Stage 1 obligatory tests: CBC, ESR, CRP, LFT, LDH, ferritin, ANA, RF, urine, 3 blood cultures, HIV, IGRA, chest X-ray, ultrasound.`,`Stage 2 PDC-directed: echo, CECT, rK39, Brucella SAT, Xpert on fluids.`,`Stage 3: FDG PET-CT if CRP/ESR raised; Stage 4: excision node, marrow, liver, temporal artery biopsy.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Withhold empirical antibiotics and steroids in a stable patient; stop non-essential drugs.`,`Empirical trials only for unstable patients, culture-negative endocarditis, suspected miliary TB, suspected GCA.`,`Treat the cause: ATT by NTEP weight band, steroids/IL-1 blockade for AOSD, L-AmB for kala-azar.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`Undiagnosed PUO after a full work-up has a good prognosis; review every 2-4 weeks.`,`Malignancy carries the worst outcome; avoid harm from blind therapy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of the four Durack-Street categories`,`Flow chart: confirm fever - obligatory tests - PDC-directed tests - PET-CT - biopsy - watchful waiting`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Aetiology in India`,marks:2},{part:`Stepwise investigation with flow chart`,marks:4},{part:`Management and prognosis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Durack-Street`,`obligatory investigations`,`PDC`,`PET-CT`,`tuberculosis`,`AOSD`,`biopsy before treatment`]},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Short note: Role of FDG PET-CT in pyrexia of unknown origin.`,openingLines:[`18F-FDG PET-CT images glucose uptake by activated leucocytes and tumour cells, so it localises infection, inflammation and malignancy in one whole-body scan.`,`It is the preferred next test in classic PUO when obligatory investigations are negative and CRP or ESR is raised.`],answer:[{heading:`Principle`,points:[`FDG is taken up through GLUT transporters by neutrophils, macrophages and tumour cells and trapped after phosphorylation.`]},{heading:`Indications and yield`,points:[`Stage 1 negative with raised CRP/ESR; contributes to the diagnosis in about 40-60%.`,`Identifies a biopsy target (node, bone, vessel wall in large-vessel vasculitis).`,`A normal scan predicts spontaneous resolution and a good prognosis.`]},{heading:`Limitations`,points:[`Does not separate infection, tumour and inflammation - biopsy is mandatory.`,`Reduced sensitivity after steroids and with hyperglycaemia; physiological uptake in brain, heart and urinary tract.`,`Cost and availability in India; CECT is often done first.`]}],mustDraw:[`Position of PET-CT in the staged PUO work-up`],markSplit:[{part:`Principle`,marks:1},{part:`Indications and yield`,marks:2},{part:`Limitations and Indian context`,marks:2}],keywords:[`FDG`,`GLUT`,`biopsy target`,`negative predictive value`,`steroids reduce uptake`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Fever of Unknown Origin chapter`,`Durack DT, Street AC. Fever of unknown origin - re-examined and redefined. Curr Clin Top Infect Dis, 1991`,`Fautrel B et al. EULAR/PReS recommendations for the diagnosis and management of Still's disease, Ann Rheum Dis, 2024`,`ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria for giant cell arteritis; EULAR 2023 recommendations for large-vessel vasculitis`,`ESC 2023 Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 3: Diagnosis, 2024 update; NTEP Training Modules, MoHFW`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 11th edition, 2019`]});var t=[];t.push({id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis`,title:`Infective endocarditis`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Infective endocarditis (IE)** is microbial infection of the **endocardial surface - native or prosthetic valve, or an intracardiac device** - forming a **vegetation**; it is diagnosed by the **2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria** (blood cultures plus imaging), treated with **prolonged bactericidal IV antibiotics** counted from the first negative culture, and needs **early surgery** for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or embolic risk.`,keywords:[`IE`,`bacterial endocarditis`,`subacute bacterial endocarditis`,`SABE`,`prosthetic valve endocarditis`,`PVE`,`modified Duke criteria`,`Duke-ISCVID 2023`,`vegetation`,`Osler nodes`,`Janeway lesions`,`Roth spots`,`culture-negative endocarditis`,`HACEK`,`Streptococcus gallolyticus`,`Enterococcus faecalis`,`endocarditis team`,`POET trial`,`CIED infection`,`dental prophylaxis amoxicillin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Infective endocarditis** is infection of the endocardium, usually a **heart valve**, with a **vegetation** of platelets, fibrin, microcolonies and inflammatory cells as its hallmark lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Global incidence is about **3-10 per 100 000 person-years** and rising, driven by **older age, prosthetic valves, intracardiac devices and healthcare contact**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In high-income countries **Staphylococcus aureus** is now the commonest cause (about 30%), followed by oral streptococci and enterococci; **healthcare-associated IE** makes up about a quarter to a third of cases. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In **India** the patient is younger (mean age in the 30s-40s in most tertiary series) and **rheumatic heart disease** remains the commonest underlying lesion, though congenital, degenerative and prosthetic valve substrates are rising. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Indian series report **culture-negative IE in about 30-50%**, chiefly from **antibiotics given before blood cultures** - the single most avoidable failure in Indian practice. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE)** forms 10-30% of all IE; **early PVE** (under 1 year after surgery) is mostly staphylococcal and nosocomial, late PVE resembles native valve IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In-hospital mortality is **15-30%** and 1-year mortality about **30-40%**, highest with S. aureus, PVE, heart failure, stroke and perivalvular abscess. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Right-sided IE** (5-10%) is mainly tricuspid, in **people who inject drugs**, with indwelling lines or pacemaker leads, and is usually S. aureus. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Viridans (oral) streptococci** cause most community native-valve IE in India, with a **subacute** course; they enter through dental and gingival bacteraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Staphylococcus aureus** causes **acute, destructive IE** that can infect a **normal valve**, with rapid abscess formation and metastatic infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enterococcus faecalis** affects elderly men after genitourinary or bowel procedures; **Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis)** IE is linked to **colorectal neoplasia** and mandates colonoscopy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Coagulase-negative staphylococci** cause early PVE and device infection; **HACEK organisms** (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) cause slow-growing culture-positive IE with large vegetations. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Culture-negative causes**: prior antibiotics (commonest), **Coxiella burnetii, Bartonella, Brucella** (important in India), Tropheryma whipplei, fungi (Candida, Aspergillus) and Legionella. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**High-risk substrates**: previous IE, **prosthetic valve or valve repair material**, untreated cyanotic congenital heart disease and congenital repairs with prosthetic material. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Intermediate-risk substrates**: **rheumatic heart disease**, degenerative valve disease, **bicuspid aortic valve**, mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation, HCM and cardiac implantable electronic devices (CIED). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Host factors**: injection drug use, haemodialysis, indwelling central lines, diabetes, poor dental hygiene, immunosuppression and recent invasive procedures. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Endothelial injury** from turbulent jets (regurgitant or stenotic lesions, shunts) exposes subendothelial matrix, on which platelets and fibrin form a sterile **non-bacterial thrombotic vegetation (NBTE)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`During a **transient bacteraemia** (chewing, brushing, dental work), organisms with **adhesins** - dextran and FimA in streptococci, **MSCRAMMs** such as clumping factor and fibronectin-binding protein in S. aureus - stick to the NBTE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S. aureus can invade intact endothelium**, which is why it infects normal valves and why a structurally normal heart does not exclude IE in SAB. [Harrison 22e]`,`Vegetations sit on the **low-pressure side of the jet**: the **atrial surface of the mitral valve** and the **ventricular surface of the aortic valve** (Venturi effect). [Harrison 22e]`,`Organisms inside a vegetation are at very high density (**10^9-10^10 per gram**), in a **slow-growing, biofilm-like state** shielded from phagocytes - hence the need for **bactericidal drugs in high doses for weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Local destruction** causes leaflet perforation, chordal rupture and acute regurgitation (heart failure); extension into the annulus causes **abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula and heart block** (aortic root near the AV node). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolisation** of friable vegetation fragments causes stroke, splenic and renal infarcts, mycotic aneurysms and (right-sided) **septic pulmonary emboli**; risk is highest with vegetations **over 10 mm** and in the first 2 weeks of therapy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Immune-complex deposition** from sustained antigenaemia explains **Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis**, low complement and a positive rheumatoid factor in subacute IE. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By **course**: **acute IE** (days, toxic, destructive - usually S. aureus) versus **subacute IE** (weeks to months, low-grade fever, weight loss, immune signs - usually viridans streptococci). [Harrison 22e]`,`By **site and substrate**: **native valve IE (NVE)**, **prosthetic valve IE (PVE)** - early under 1 year, late after 1 year - **CIED-related IE**, and **right-sided IE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`By **acquisition**: community-acquired, **healthcare-associated** (nosocomial, or non-nosocomial with dialysis or lines) and **injection-drug-use** IE - this drives the empirical regimen. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`By **microbiology**: culture-positive, **culture-negative** (prior antibiotics or fastidious organisms) and **non-infective** (marantic NBTE in cancer, **Libman-Sacks** in SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`The diagnostic classification is the **2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria**: **definite** (pathology, or 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor), **possible** (1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor) and **rejected**. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**Rejected** IE: firm alternative diagnosis, resolution with **4 days or less** of antibiotics, no pathological evidence at surgery or autopsy after 4 days or less of antibiotics, or failure to meet possible criteria. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Fever** is present in about **90%**, often with chills, anorexia and weight loss; it may be absent in the elderly, in renal failure and after partial antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`,`The classic Indian OPD story is **prolonged low-grade fever for weeks in a young adult with known RHD**, already treated with several short antibiotic courses - think IE before calling it PUO. [API Textbook 12e]`,`Ask for **recent dental work, procedures, catheters, haemodialysis, injection drug use** and a **prosthetic valve or pacemaker**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic presentations** may be the first sign: **stroke, sudden visual loss, acute limb ischaemia, flank pain with haematuria (renal infarct) or left upper quadrant pain (splenic infarct)**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Breathlessness, orthopnoea and oedema** signal valve destruction with heart failure - the commonest complication and the commonest reason for surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Non-specific features - **back pain, arthralgia, myalgia** - are common and can mimic rheumatic fever, vertebral osteomyelitis or a connective tissue disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right-sided IE** presents with fever and **cough, pleuritic chest pain and haemoptysis** from septic pulmonary emboli, often misread as pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`A **new or changing regurgitant murmur** is found in about **85%**; a murmur may be absent in right-sided and mural IE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splinter haemorrhages** - linear dark-red streaks in the nail bed - are common but non-specific (also trauma, manual work). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osler nodes** are **tender**, pea-sized nodules in the **finger and toe pulps** (immune-complex vasculitis); **Janeway lesions** are **painless** macular haemorrhages on **palms and soles** (septic microemboli). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Roth spots** are retinal haemorrhages with **pale centres** on fundoscopy; **conjunctival petechiae** are seen on everting the lower lid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** (about 30% in subacute IE) and **clubbing** (long-standing subacute IE) favour a subacute course. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **signs of heart failure** (basal crackles, raised JVP) and **neurological deficits** - each changes the surgical decision. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Examine the **teeth and gums**, skin injection sites and line or pacemaker pocket for the portal of entry. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Peripheral stigmata are now seen in **under 10-15%** of cases because diagnosis is earlier - their absence never excludes IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute rheumatic fever** with carditis: young patient with RHD and fever, but **migratory polyarthritis, raised ASO, prolonged PR, negative cultures** and no vegetation on echo. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**Enteric fever, brucellosis and TB**: prolonged fever with splenomegaly; blood culture and echo separate them - brucella can itself cause IE. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**SLE with Libman-Sacks endocarditis** and **antiphospholipid syndrome**: sterile vegetations, positive ANA/anti-dsDNA or antiphospholipid antibodies, low C3 and C4. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Marantic (NBTE) endocarditis** in adenocarcinoma: embolic strokes with sterile vegetations and no fever response pattern. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atrial myxoma**: fever, emboli, raised ESR and a 'tumour plop'; echo shows a pedunculated mass attached to the **interatrial septum**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Vasculitis** (polyarteritis, ANCA vasculitis): fever, purpura, GN and mononeuritis, but negative cultures and no valve lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uncomplicated S. aureus bacteraemia** from a line or skin focus: separate by TOE and by whether bacteraemia clears within 72 hours - see the Bloodstream infection topic. [IDSA 2025 SAB]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line to advanced`,points:[`**Three sets of blood cultures** (aerobic and anaerobic bottles, about **10 mL per bottle** in adults) from **separate venepunctures at least 30 minutes apart**, all **before antibiotics**; bacteraemia is continuous, so timing to fever spikes is unnecessary. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In a stable patient who has had antibiotics, **stop them and repeat cultures** after a wash-out where safe; in a toxic patient, take cultures and start empirical treatment within an hour. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Transthoracic echo (TTE)** is the first imaging test; sensitivity is about **70% for native valves** and about **50% for prosthetic valves**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Transoesophageal echo (TOE)** (sensitivity over **90%**) is recommended when TTE is negative with persisting suspicion, in **prosthetic valve or device**, and even after a positive TTE to define complications (except isolated good-quality right-sided NVE). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`If the first echo is negative but suspicion remains, **repeat TTE/TOE in 5-7 days**; repeat at once with any new complication (murmur, heart block, embolism). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Supporting tests: **normocytic anaemia, raised ESR and CRP, microscopic haematuria**, positive rheumatoid factor and low complement; a **new PR prolongation or AV block** on serial ECG suggests an aortic root abscess. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Cardiac CT angiography** detects abscess, pseudoaneurysm and fistula as well as or better than TOE; **brain MRI and abdominal imaging** find silent emboli that add a minor criterion. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**18F-FDG PET/CT** is a **major imaging criterion** in PVE more than 3 months after implantation and helps diagnose device infection; within 3 months uptake is only a minor criterion. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`For culture-negative IE send **serology for Coxiella burnetii (phase I IgG 1:800 or more is a major criterion), Bartonella and Brucella**; send excised **valve tissue for culture, histology and 16S rRNA PCR / sequencing**. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`Do **ANA, anti-dsDNA and antiphospholipid antibodies** when cultures and serology are negative, to exclude non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Management - principles and empirical therapy`,points:[`IE is managed in a **reference centre by an Endocarditis Team** (cardiologist, cardiac surgeon, infectious diseases physician, microbiologist, imaging specialist) - a Class I recommendation. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Use **bactericidal** drugs at **high IV doses for prolonged periods**; duration is counted from the **first day of negative blood cultures** (or from surgery if the excised valve is culture-positive). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Empirical - community NVE or late PVE**: **ampicillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses plus (flu)cloxacillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day IV once daily**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Empirical - penicillin allergy**: **vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day IV in 2 doses plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Empirical - early PVE (under 12 months) or healthcare-associated IE**: **vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day IV plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day plus rifampicin 900-1200 mg/day** in 2-3 doses. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Narrow therapy as soon as the organism and **MIC** are known; **repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours** until negative. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Do not start aspirin or other antiplatelets** to prevent embolism; continue anticoagulation for a mechanical valve but **switch to heparin** in the acute phase and **stop it after an intracranial haemorrhage**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Remove the source**: dental extraction of infected teeth, drainage of abscesses and **complete removal of an infected CIED system** (generator and all leads). [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Targeted antibiotic regimens`,points:[`**Penicillin-susceptible oral streptococci / S. gallolyticus (MIC 0.125 mg/L or less), NVE**: **benzylpenicillin 12-18 million U/day IV** in 4-6 doses, or **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily**, for **4 weeks** (6 weeks in PVE). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Two-week regimen** for uncomplicated streptococcal NVE with normal renal function: penicillin or ceftriaxone **plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg once daily for 2 weeks**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Penicillin-resistant streptococci (MIC above 0.125)**: higher-dose penicillin (24 million U/day) or amoxicillin **plus gentamicin for the first 2 weeks**, total 4 weeks NVE, 6 weeks PVE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**MSSA NVE**: **(flu)cloxacillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses** or **cefazolin 6 g/day IV in 3 doses** for **4-6 weeks**; **gentamicin is not recommended** in staphylococcal NVE (nephrotoxic, no benefit). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**MRSA NVE**: **vancomycin** targeted to an **AUC of 400-600 mg.h/L** (or trough 15-20 mg/L where AUC is not available) or **daptomycin 10 mg/kg once daily**, for 4-6 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Staphylococcal PVE**: anti-staphylococcal drug **plus rifampicin 900 mg/day for 6 weeks or more plus gentamicin for 2 weeks**; add rifampicin only after **3-5 days** of effective therapy once bacteraemia clears. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Enterococcus faecalis**: **ampicillin/amoxicillin 200 mg/kg/day IV plus ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly for 6 weeks** (preferred, and the choice for high-level aminoglycoside resistance), or ampicillin plus gentamicin for 2 weeks within a 4-6-week course. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**HACEK**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily** for 4 weeks (NVE) or 6 weeks (PVE); **fungal IE** needs surgery plus amphotericin B-based therapy and long-term azole suppression. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Partial oral step-down** (POET trial) is endorsed for **stable left-sided IE** from streptococci, E. faecalis, S. aureus or CoNS after **at least 10 days IV** (7 days after surgery), with no abscess on TOE. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Surgery - indications and timing`,points:[`Surgery is needed in about **half of left-sided IE**; timing is **emergency (within 24 hours)**, **urgent (within 3-5 days)** or **non-urgent (same admission)**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Heart failure**: **emergency surgery** for refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock from severe regurgitation, obstruction or fistula; urgent surgery for severe regurgitation with heart failure symptoms. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Uncontrolled infection**: **abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula, enlarging vegetation, prosthetic dehiscence or new AV block** - urgent surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Difficult organisms**: fungal or multidrug-resistant IE, and **blood cultures still positive after 1 week** of appropriate therapy with metastatic foci controlled - urgent surgery; PVE due to **S. aureus or non-HACEK Gram-negatives** should be considered for surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic prevention**: urgent surgery for a **vegetation 10 mm or more with an embolic event** on therapy, or 10 mm or more with severe valve disease and low operative risk; **30 mm or more** alone may justify surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`After an **ischaemic stroke or TIA without haemorrhage or coma**, indicated surgery **should not be delayed**; after **intracranial haemorrhage** surgery is usually deferred for about **4 weeks**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Right-sided IE**: surgery for right heart failure with severe TR unresponsive to diuretics, persistent bacteraemia, large (over 20 mm) tricuspid vegetations with recurrent pulmonary emboli, or difficult organisms. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: IE is rare but carries high maternal and fetal mortality; penicillins, cephalosporins and vancomycin are usable, **aminoglycosides only when essential**; decide delivery timing with the Endocarditis Team. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Elderly**: atypical, afebrile or confusional presentation; enterococcal and S. gallolyticus IE are more common - **colonoscopy is recommended** for S. gallolyticus. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: dose vancomycin and gentamicin by **levels**; dialysis catheters are a major source of S. aureus IE; prefer **ampicillin-ceftriaxone** over gentamicin for E. faecalis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**People who inject drugs**: tricuspid S. aureus IE with septic emboli; a **shorter 2-week cloxacillin course** is acceptable in uncomplicated right-sided MSSA IE; offer **opioid agonist therapy** and harm reduction. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**CIED infection**: **complete hardware removal** (generator and all leads) plus 4-6 weeks of antibiotics; reimplant at a new site only after cultures clear. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: three good-volume cultures before antibiotics and a TTE are achievable at most district hospitals; refer for TOE, surgery and serology rather than stacking empirical antibiotics. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Children with congenital heart disease**: S. aureus and viridans streptococci dominate; dosing by weight, and post-repair residual shunts at a prosthetic patch remain high-risk lifelong. [AHA 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Heart failure** (about 40%) is the commonest complication and the strongest predictor of death; aortic regurgitation decompensates fastest. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Perivalvular extension** (abscess, fistula, heart block) is commoner in **aortic and prosthetic valve IE** and usually needs surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Systemic embolism** occurs in **20-50%**; the brain is involved in about two-thirds, and embolic risk falls sharply after the **first 2 weeks** of effective antibiotics. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Neurological complications** (15-30%): ischaemic stroke, haemorrhage, **mycotic (infectious) aneurysm**, abscess and meningitis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Renal**: immune-complex GN, infarcts, and **drug-induced AKI** (aminoglycosides, vancomycin) - a reason to keep aminoglycoside courses short. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Splenic infarct or abscess** - persistent fever with left upper quadrant pain after valve control suggests abscess needing drainage or splenectomy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Relapse** (same organism within 6 months) suggests inadequate duration or an uncontrolled focus; **recurrence risk** after one episode is several-fold, so prophylaxis is lifelong. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Any fever with a **murmur, prosthetic valve, device or S. aureus bacteraemia** needs blood cultures and **same-week echo**; refer to a centre with cardiac surgery if IE is likely. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`During treatment: **daily clinical review, blood cultures until clear, weekly CBC, creatinine and CRP**, drug levels (vancomycin, gentamicin) and **ECG** for conduction block. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`At the end of therapy: **baseline TTE** for future comparison, dental review, and teaching the patient to **report fever and get cultures before any antibiotic**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Follow-up at **1, 3, 6 and 12 months** for relapse, heart failure and valve dysfunction. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Dental hygiene and twice-yearly dental review** in all at-risk patients is the main preventive measure - daily bacteraemia from chewing exceeds that from a single extraction. [AHA 2021 prevention]`,`**Antibiotic prophylaxis** is given only to **high-risk patients** before **dental procedures involving gingival or periapical manipulation or perforation of oral mucosa**: **amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before** (children 50 mg/kg). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`For penicillin allergy use **azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg** or **doxycycline 100 mg**; **clindamycin is no longer recommended** because of C. difficile risk. [AHA 2021 prevention]`,`**RHD in India**: **benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis** prevents rheumatic recurrences but **does not cover IE** - these patients may carry penicillin-resistant oral streptococci, so use a non-penicillin agent for dental cover when prophylaxis is chosen. [AHA 2021 prevention]`,`Strict **asepsis for central lines, dialysis access and device implantation** prevents healthcare-associated IE, now a quarter or more of cases. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Duke-ISCVID 2023** (International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases) updated the 2000 modified Duke criteria: **cardiac CT and FDG PET/CT** became major imaging criteria and **surgical inspection** became a major criterion. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`Duke-ISCVID widened **'typical' organisms** to include **S. lugdunensis, E. faecalis (any setting), all streptococci except S. pneumoniae and S. pyogenes, Granulicatella, Abiotrophia and Gemella**, and added **PCR / sequencing** and **Bartonella serology**. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`Duke-ISCVID added new minor predispositions (**transcatheter valves, CIED, HOCM, prior valve repair**) and dropped the timing rules for culture positivity. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**ESC 2023 IE** made the **Endocarditis Team** Class I, defined **urgent surgery as within 3-5 days** (previously up to 7), and endorsed **partial oral step-down** after the POET trial. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`ESC 2023 gave **cefazolin** equal standing with (flu)cloxacillin for MSSA, removed **gentamicin from staphylococcal NVE**, and recommended **TOE even after a positive TTE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`ESC 2023 extended **high-risk prophylaxis to transcatheter valve recipients** and to patients with **ventricular assist devices**, and removed clindamycin for penicillin allergy (in line with **AHA 2021**). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Guideline difference**: ESC 2023 and AHA 2021 do not give routine prophylaxis to **RHD without a prosthesis**, while many Indian cardiologists still do; the high-risk list is the examinable answer. [AHA 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Commonest viva: **'Name the modified Duke criteria'** - give the 2023 Duke-ISCVID version, starting with the **two major criteria (micro and imaging)** and the new **surgical** major criterion. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**Osler is Ouch** (tender, pulps), **Janeway is painless** (palms and soles) - a favourite one-mark trap. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest mistake** in India: starting antibiotics in a febrile RHD patient **before three blood cultures**, which converts a curable streptococcal IE into culture-negative IE. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Vegetation site**: atrial side of the mitral, ventricular side of the aortic - the low-pressure side of the jet. [Harrison 22e]`,`**New PR prolongation** in aortic valve IE means a **root abscess** - an urgent surgical indication. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**S. gallolyticus means colonoscopy**; **S. aureus bacteraemia means echo**; **prosthetic valve means TOE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Duration is counted from the **first negative blood culture**, not from admission. [ESC 2023 IE]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antibiotic regimens in infective endocarditis (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Benzylpenicillin (streptococci, MIC 0.125 or less)`,`12-18 million U/day IV in 4-6 doses or continuous infusion`,`4 weeks NVE; 6 weeks PVE; 2 weeks if combined with gentamicin in uncomplicated NVE`,`Hypersensitivity; seizures at high dose in renal failure; sodium/potassium load`],[`Ceftriaxone (streptococci, HACEK; with ampicillin for E. faecalis)`,`2 g IV once daily (streptococci, HACEK); 2 g IV 12-hourly with ampicillin for E. faecalis`,`4 weeks NVE, 6 weeks PVE; 6 weeks for E. faecalis`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; avoid with calcium infusions in neonates`],[`(Flu)cloxacillin (MSSA)`,`12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses`,`4-6 weeks NVE; 6 weeks or more PVE (with rifampicin + gentamicin)`,`Phlebitis, cholestatic hepatitis, neutropenia with long courses`],[`Cefazolin (MSSA, alternative)`,`6 g/day IV in 3 doses`,`4-6 weeks`,`Usable in non-severe penicillin allergy; rash, C. difficile`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA, penicillin allergy, empirical PVE)`,`Loading 25-30 mg/kg, then 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly`,`AUC 400-600 mg.h/L (or trough 15-20 mg/L); 4-6 weeks`,`Nephrotoxicity (worse with gentamicin), infusion reaction - infuse over at least 1 hour, ototoxicity`],[`Daptomycin (MRSA, alternative)`,`10 mg/kg IV once daily`,`4-6 weeks; weekly CPK`,`Myopathy (CPK), eosinophilic pneumonia; useless in pneumonia (inactivated by surfactant)`],[`Gentamicin (synergy)`,`3 mg/kg IV once daily`,`2 weeks only (streptococci, enterococci, staphylococcal PVE); trough under 1 mg/L`,`Nephrotoxicity and irreversible vestibular/cochlear toxicity; not for staphylococcal NVE`],[`Rifampicin (prosthetic material)`,`900 mg/day IV or oral in 2-3 doses`,`Start after 3-5 days of effective therapy; 6 weeks or more`,`Hepatotoxicity, many drug interactions (warfarin, DOACs, ART), orange secretions; never alone`]]},{heading:`2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria for infective endocarditis`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Includes`,`Note`],rows:[[`Major - microbiological`,`Typical organism in 2 or more separate blood culture sets; non-typical organism in 3 or more sets (with intracardiac prosthesis for skin commensals); positive PCR/sequencing for Coxiella, Bartonella or T. whipplei; Coxiella phase I IgG 1:800 or more; Bartonella IgG 1:800 or more`,`Typical now includes S. aureus, S. lugdunensis, E. faecalis, most streptococci, HACEK, Granulicatella, Abiotrophia, Gemella`],[`Major - imaging`,`Echo or cardiac CT: vegetation, perforation, aneurysm, abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula; new significant regurgitation; new prosthetic dehiscence; FDG PET/CT uptake at valve, graft or leads`,`PET is major only if more than 3 months after implantation`],[`Major - surgical`,`IE seen on direct inspection at heart surgery`,`New in 2023; applies when imaging and microbiology fall short`],[`Minor - predisposition`,`Previous IE, prosthetic valve, prior valve repair, congenital heart disease, more than mild regurgitation or stenosis, CIED, HOCM, injection drug use`,`Transcatheter valves and devices included`],[`Minor - fever`,`Temperature above 38.0 C`,`-`],[`Minor - vascular`,`Arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, cerebral or splenic abscess, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial haemorrhage, conjunctival haemorrhage, Janeway lesions, purulent purpura`,`Includes silent emboli found on imaging`],[`Minor - immunological`,`Positive rheumatoid factor, Osler nodes, Roth spots, immune-complex glomerulonephritis`,`-`],[`Minor - microbiological / imaging / exam`,`Culture positive short of major; PET uptake within 3 months of implant; new regurgitant murmur on auscultation if echo unavailable`,`-`],[`Classification`,`Definite: pathology, or 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor; Possible: 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor`,`Rejected if alternative diagnosis or resolution with 4 days or less of antibiotics`]]},{heading:`Indications and timing of surgery (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Indication`,`Example`,`Timing`],rows:[[`Heart failure - shock or refractory pulmonary oedema`,`Acute severe aortic or mitral regurgitation, obstruction, fistula`,`Emergency (within 24 hours)`],[`Heart failure - symptomatic severe regurgitation`,`Severe AR/MR with heart failure symptoms, haemodynamically stable`,`Urgent (3-5 days)`],[`Locally uncontrolled infection`,`Abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula, enlarging vegetation, prosthetic dehiscence, new AV block`,`Urgent`],[`Difficult organism`,`Fungi, multidrug-resistant organisms, S. aureus or non-HACEK Gram-negative PVE`,`Urgent or non-urgent`],[`Persistent bacteraemia`,`Blood cultures positive after 1 week of appropriate therapy, metastatic foci controlled`,`Urgent`],[`Embolic risk`,`Vegetation 10 mm or more with embolic event on therapy; 10 mm or more with severe valve disease and low operative risk; 30 mm or more`,`Urgent`],[`After stroke`,`Ischaemic stroke or TIA without haemorrhage or coma`,`Do not delay`],[`After intracranial haemorrhage`,`Haemorrhagic stroke`,`Usually defer about 4 weeks`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever with a murmur`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Infective endocarditis`,`Persistent bacteraemia, vegetation, embolic and immune signs`,`3 blood cultures + TTE/TOE`],[`Acute rheumatic fever (carditis)`,`Migratory polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, prolonged PR`,`ASO/anti-DNase B, ESR/CRP, echo (no vegetation)`],[`Libman-Sacks endocarditis (SLE/APS)`,`Multisystem lupus features, sterile vegetations`,`ANA, anti-dsDNA, antiphospholipid antibodies, C3/C4`],[`Marantic endocarditis (NBTE)`,`Known or occult adenocarcinoma, embolic strokes, no fever pattern`,`Negative cultures; search for cancer`],[`Atrial myxoma`,`Positional symptoms, tumour plop, raised ESR`,`Echo: pedunculated mass on interatrial septum`],[`Enteric fever / brucellosis with flow murmur`,`Hepatosplenomegaly, relative bradycardia, raw milk exposure`,`Blood culture, brucella serology, normal echo`],[`Anaemia or thyrotoxicosis with flow murmur`,`Soft ejection systolic murmur, no regurgitation`,`Haemoglobin, TSH, normal echo`]]},{heading:`Echocardiography and imaging in IE`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`What it shows / cut-off`],rows:[[`Transthoracic echo (TTE)`,`First test in all suspected IE`,`Sensitivity about 70% NVE, 50% PVE; baseline LV function`],[`Transoesophageal echo (TOE)`,`Negative TTE with suspicion; prosthetic valve or CIED; positive TTE to define complications`,`Sensitivity over 90%; abscess, perforation, vegetation size (10 mm and 30 mm cut-offs)`],[`Repeat TTE/TOE`,`5-7 days after a negative study if suspicion persists; new complication`,`Evolving vegetation or abscess`],[`Cardiac CT angiography`,`Suspected perivalvular extension, pre-operative coronary assessment`,`Abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula`],[`18F-FDG PET/CT`,`Possible PVE or CIED infection`,`Major criterion if more than 3 months after implant; also finds distant foci`],[`Brain MRI / CT`,`Neurological symptoms; pre-operative screening`,`Emboli, haemorrhage, mycotic aneurysm`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with a murmur and **new breathlessness or pulmonary oedema** - acute valve destruction, needs emergency surgery assessment.`,`**New PR prolongation or heart block** on ECG in suspected aortic valve IE - perivalvular abscess.`,`**Focal neurological deficit, sudden visual loss or severe headache** in a patient with a murmur and fever - embolic stroke or mycotic aneurysm.`,`**Fever in a patient with a prosthetic valve or pacemaker** - blood cultures and TOE, never empirical oral antibiotics.`,`**S. aureus in any blood culture** - assume IE until TOE excludes it.`,`**Blood cultures still positive after 7 days** of appropriate therapy - uncontrolled infection, refer for surgery.`,`Acute limb ischaemia, flank pain with haematuria or left upper quadrant pain with fever - **systemic embolism**.`],pearls:[`**Three sets of blood cultures before the first antibiotic** - the single most important step in IE.`,`**Osler - Ouch** (tender pulps, immune); **Janeway - painless** (palms and soles, embolic).`,`**FROM JANE** for IE features: Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anaemia, Nail-bed (splinter) haemorrhages, Emboli.`,`Vegetations sit on the **low-pressure side of the jet** - atrial surface of mitral, ventricular surface of aortic.`,`**S. gallolyticus means colonoscopy** - linked to colorectal cancer.`,`Duration counts from the **first negative blood culture**, or from surgery if the valve culture is positive.`,`**Gentamicin is out for staphylococcal native-valve IE** (ESC 2023) - nephrotoxic without benefit.`,`**Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone** for E. faecalis - works by saturating complementary penicillin-binding proteins and avoids aminoglycoside toxicity.`,`Dental prophylaxis: **amoxicillin 2 g, 30-60 minutes before**, high-risk patients only; **clindamycin is out**.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve implanted 2 years ago has fever for 3 weeks. TOE is equivocal. Under the 2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria, which finding counts as a MAJOR criterion?`,options:[`Positive rheumatoid factor`,`Abnormal FDG uptake around the prosthetic valve on PET/CT`,`Temperature of 38.5 C`,`Splinter haemorrhages in three nail beds`],answer:1,explanation:`Duke-ISCVID 2023 made 18F-FDG PET/CT uptake at a prosthetic valve a major imaging criterion when the valve was implanted more than 3 months earlier, as here. A positive rheumatoid factor is an immunological minor criterion, fever above 38.0 C is a minor criterion, and splinter haemorrhages are non-specific and do not even appear as a named vascular criterion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q2`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve, allergic to penicillin (urticaria), needs a dental extraction. Which prophylaxis follows ESC 2023 and AHA 2021?`,options:[`Clindamycin 600 mg orally 1 hour before`,`No prophylaxis because she is on warfarin`,`Amoxicillin 2 g orally 1 hour before`,`Azithromycin 500 mg orally 30-60 minutes before`],answer:3,explanation:`A prosthetic valve is high risk and an extraction manipulates the gingiva, so prophylaxis is indicated; with penicillin allergy azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg (or doxycycline 100 mg) is used. Clindamycin was removed by AHA 2021 and ESC 2023 because of C. difficile risk, amoxicillin is unsafe in her allergy, and anticoagulation is not a reason to withhold prophylaxis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q3`,stem:`A 70-year-old man on haemodialysis has native mitral valve IE caused by Enterococcus faecalis with high-level gentamicin resistance. What is the preferred regimen?`,options:[`Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone IV for 6 weeks`,`Vancomycin plus gentamicin IV for 4 weeks`,`Ceftriaxone alone IV for 4 weeks`,`Cloxacillin plus rifampicin IV for 6 weeks`],answer:0,explanation:`Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone saturates complementary penicillin-binding proteins, is synergistic against E. faecalis and avoids aminoglycosides - ideal with high-level gentamicin resistance and renal failure. Gentamicin adds toxicity without synergy when resistance is high-level, ceftriaxone alone is inactive because enterococci are intrinsically cephalosporin-resistant, and cloxacillin-rifampicin is a staphylococcal PVE regimen.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q4`,stem:`A 68-year-old man is treated for aortic valve IE due to Streptococcus gallolyticus. Which additional investigation is specifically recommended?`,options:[`Bone marrow biopsy`,`Colonoscopy`,`Brucella serology`,`Upper GI endoscopy only`],answer:1,explanation:`S. gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis biotype I) bacteraemia and IE are strongly associated with colorectal adenoma and carcinoma, so ESC 2023 recommends colonoscopy. Upper GI endoscopy alone misses the colon, bone marrow biopsy has no role with a known organism, and brucella serology is for culture-negative IE.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q5`,stem:`On day 4 of appropriate antibiotics for MSSA aortic valve IE, a haemodynamically stable patient develops a new PR interval of 280 ms. What does this most likely indicate, and what is the next step?`,options:[`Drug-induced conduction delay; switch cloxacillin to cefazolin`,`Rheumatic carditis; add aspirin`,`Perivalvular aortic root abscess; urgent TOE and surgery within 3-5 days`,`Vagal tone from fever; repeat ECG in a week`],answer:2,explanation:`A new PR prolongation or AV block in aortic valve IE signals extension into the aortic root near the AV node - an abscess, which is locally uncontrolled infection and an urgent (3-5 day) surgical indication under ESC 2023. Beta-lactams do not cause heart block, aspirin is not recommended in IE, and waiting a week risks complete heart block and fistula.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`A 28-year-old man with known rheumatic mitral regurgitation presents with fever for 4 weeks. Discuss the diagnostic criteria, investigations and management of infective endocarditis, including indications for surgery.`,openingLines:[`Infective endocarditis is microbial infection of the endocardium, usually a valve, with a vegetation as its hallmark; in India rheumatic heart disease remains the commonest substrate and viridans streptococci the commonest organism.`,`It is diagnosed by the 2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria, combining blood cultures and imaging, and treated with prolonged bactericidal IV antibiotics with early surgery for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or embolic risk.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical assessment`,points:[`Fever, weight loss, previous antibiotic courses, dental work; embolic and heart failure symptoms.`,`Signs: changing murmur, splinter haemorrhages, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, Roth spots, splenomegaly, clubbing.`,`Look for portal of entry (teeth) and complications (heart failure, neurological deficit).`]},{heading:`Duke-ISCVID 2023 criteria`,points:[`Major: microbiology (typical organism in 2 sets, serology/PCR for Coxiella and Bartonella); imaging (echo, CT, PET/CT); surgical inspection.`,`Minor: predisposition, fever above 38 C, vascular phenomena, immunological phenomena, sub-major microbiology.`,`Definite: 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor; possible: 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Three blood culture sets from separate sites 30 minutes apart before antibiotics.`,`TTE first, TOE if negative with suspicion or to define complications; repeat in 5-7 days.`,`CBC, ESR/CRP, urine (haematuria), creatinine, RF, complement, ECG (PR interval).`,`Culture-negative: Coxiella, Bartonella, Brucella serology; valve PCR; cardiac CT, PET/CT, brain MRI as needed.`]},{heading:`Antibiotic therapy`,points:[`Empirical: ampicillin + (flu)cloxacillin + gentamicin; vancomycin + gentamicin if penicillin-allergic.`,`Viridans streptococci (MIC 0.125 or less): penicillin G 12-18 MU/day or ceftriaxone 2 g daily for 4 weeks; 2 weeks with gentamicin if uncomplicated.`,`MSSA: cloxacillin 12 g/day or cefazolin 6 g/day 4-6 weeks; MRSA: vancomycin (AUC 400-600) or daptomycin.`,`E. faecalis: ampicillin + ceftriaxone 6 weeks; HACEK: ceftriaxone 4 weeks.`,`Duration from first negative culture; partial oral step-down after 10 days in stable patients (POET).`]},{heading:`Surgery`,points:[`Emergency: refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock.`,`Urgent (3-5 days): abscess, fistula, AV block, enlarging vegetation, fungal/MDR organism, persistent bacteraemia, vegetation 10 mm or more with embolism.`,`Ischaemic stroke without haemorrhage does not delay surgery; haemorrhage defers it about 4 weeks.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Endocarditis Team; serial cultures, drug levels, ECG; end-of-treatment echo.`,`Dental hygiene; prophylaxis only for high-risk groups: amoxicillin 2 g before dental procedures.`,`Continue benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis for RHD; it does not prevent IE.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected IE - blood cultures - TTE - TOE - Duke-ISCVID classification - treatment`,`Table of Duke-ISCVID major and minor criteria`,`Diagram of vegetation sites on mitral and aortic valves`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Duke-ISCVID criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Antibiotic therapy`,marks:2},{part:`Surgery, follow-up and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Duke-ISCVID`,`blood cultures`,`TOE`,`vegetation`,`endocarditis team`,`surgery timing`,`RHD`]},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on antibiotic prophylaxis of infective endocarditis.`,openingLines:[`Antibiotic prophylaxis of IE is a single pre-procedure dose given only to patients at the highest risk of IE or of a poor outcome from it, before dental procedures that manipulate gingiva or periapical tissue.`,`Current guidance (ESC 2023, AHA 2021) restricts prophylaxis because daily bacteraemia from chewing and brushing far exceeds that from a single procedure; oral hygiene is the main prevention.`],answer:[{heading:`Who gets prophylaxis (high risk)`,points:[`Previous IE.`,`Surgically or transcatheter implanted prosthetic valve, and any material used for valve repair.`,`Untreated cyanotic congenital heart disease; repaired CHD with prosthetic material for 6 months, or lifelong with residual shunt or regurgitation.`,`Ventricular assist device (ESC 2023).`]},{heading:`Which procedures`,points:[`Dental extraction, oral surgery, scaling and procedures manipulating gingiva or periapical region or perforating oral mucosa.`,`Not routinely for respiratory, GI or genitourinary procedures, or for local anaesthetic injection in non-infected tissue.`]},{heading:`Regimens`,points:[`Amoxicillin 2 g orally (children 50 mg/kg) 30-60 minutes before; ampicillin or cefazolin/ceftriaxone IV if oral not possible.`,`Penicillin allergy: azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg, or doxycycline 100 mg orally.`,`Clindamycin is no longer recommended.`]},{heading:`Indian context`,points:[`RHD without a prosthesis is intermediate risk; prophylaxis is not routine in ESC/AHA, though often given in India.`,`Benzathine penicillin for rheumatic fever does not prevent IE; use a non-penicillin agent if prophylaxis is chosen.`,`Twice-yearly dental review and oral hygiene for all with valve disease.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: high-risk group, procedure, drug and dose`],markSplit:[{part:`Rationale and high-risk groups`,marks:2},{part:`Procedures and regimens`,marks:2},{part:`Indian context and oral hygiene`,marks:1}],keywords:[`amoxicillin 2 g`,`high-risk cardiac conditions`,`dental procedures`,`clindamycin removed`,`RHD`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infective endocarditis`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Infective endocarditis`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis (Delgado V et al, Eur Heart J 2023)`,`The 2023 Duke-International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases Criteria for Infective Endocarditis (Fowler VG et al, Clin Infect Dis 2023)`,`Prevention of Viridans Group Streptococcal Infective Endocarditis: AHA Scientific Statement (Wilson WR et al, Circulation 2021)`,`Iversen K et al. Partial Oral versus Intravenous Antibiotic Treatment of Endocarditis (POET), N Engl J Med 2019`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Infective endocarditis`]});var n=[];n.push({id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic`,title:`Systemic fungal infections: candidiasis, aspergillosis, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Invasive (systemic) fungal infections** are deep-tissue or bloodstream infections by yeasts (**Candida, Cryptococcus**), moulds (**Aspergillus**) or dimorphic fungi (**Histoplasma, Talaromyces**) in a host with broken barriers or depressed immunity; treatment is organism-led - an **echinocandin for candidaemia**, **voriconazole or isavuconazole for invasive aspergillosis**, and the **WHO 2022 single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine and fluconazole** regimen for HIV cryptococcal meningitis.`,keywords:[`invasive fungal infection`,`IFI`,`candidaemia`,`invasive candidiasis`,`Candida auris`,`invasive aspergillosis`,`chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`galactomannan`,`beta-D-glucan`,`cryptococcal meningitis`,`CrAg`,`histoplasmosis`,`talaromycosis`,`echinocandin`,`caspofungin`,`micafungin`,`voriconazole`,`isavuconazole`,`liposomal amphotericin B`,`flucytosine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Invasive fungal infection (IFI)** means fungus in sterile tissue or blood with tissue damage; it is graded **proven, probable or possible** by the **EORTC/MSGERC 2020** definitions, which need host factors, clinical signs and mycological evidence. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`Globally fungal disease kills an estimated **3.8 million people a year**, about 2.5 million directly attributable - more than malaria; the **WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List 2022** puts **Cryptococcus neoformans, Candida auris, Aspergillus fumigatus and Candida albicans** in the critical group. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`India carries one of the largest fungal burdens: an estimated **over 57 million people** have a serious fungal disease, with **chronic pulmonary aspergillosis (CPA) around 1.7 million** (mostly after treated TB) and the world's highest rate of **mucormycosis**. [Ray et al., Open Forum Infect Dis 2022]`,`The Indian ICU candidaemia study (27 ICUs) found an incidence of about **6.5 per 1000 ICU admissions**, with **C. tropicalis (about 42%)** the commonest species and over 30 species overall, and **C. auris** in about 5%. [Chakrabarti, Intensive Care Med 2015]`,`**C. auris** was first characterised as a nosocomial pathogen in **Indian ICUs** (South Asian clade I); it spreads on hands and surfaces, colonises skin, and is often **fluconazole-resistant (over 90%)**. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`Cryptococcal meningitis causes about **15-19% of AIDS-related deaths** worldwide and is the leading cause of adult meningitis where HIV is common; Indian HIV cohorts report **CrAg positivity of 3-8%** at CD4 under 100. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis** is endemic in the **Gangetic plains (West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar)** and Assam; **talaromycosis (Talaromyces marneffei)** is endemic in **Manipur** and the North-East. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Candidaemia risk**: central venous catheter, broad-spectrum antibiotics, ICU stay over 7 days, TPN, abdominal surgery or anastomotic leak, necrotising pancreatitis, haemodialysis, neutropenia and **colonisation at several sites**. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis risk**: **neutrophils under 500/mm3 for over 10 days**, allogeneic stem-cell transplant and GVHD, high-dose steroids (prednisolone 0.3 mg/kg/day or more for over 3 weeks), ibrutinib and other BTK inhibitors, and severe influenza or COVID in ICU (IAPA, CAPA). [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis** affects the **immunocompetent with a lung cavity** - post-tuberculosis cavities (commonest in India), sarcoidosis, COPD and bullae. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Cryptococcus neoformans** (pigeon droppings, decaying wood) causes disease mainly at **CD4 under 100 cells/mm3**; **C. gattii** (eucalyptus) can infect immunocompetent hosts and forms brain cryptococcomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other cryptococcal hosts: solid-organ transplant, long-term steroids, **anti-TNF and fingolimod**, cirrhosis, sarcoidosis and idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Histoplasma capsulatum** is inhaled from bat and bird guano (caves, poultry, old buildings); dissemination follows **HIV at CD4 under 150**, steroids or TNF blockers, but adrenal disease occurs in apparently healthy Indian men. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uncontrolled diabetes** raises risk of candidiasis, mucormycosis and cryptococcosis - see the Mucormycosis topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`**Neutrophils** kill hyphae and **T cells (Th1, Th17) with macrophages** control yeasts and dimorphs - so **neutropenia predisposes to moulds and candidaemia**, while **CD4 lymphopenia predisposes to Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Talaromyces and Pneumocystis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Candida** reaches blood from the **gut (translocation)** or a **colonised catheter biofilm**; biofilms resist azoles, which is why the **catheter must be removed**. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Aspergillus** conidia (2-3 micrometres) reach alveoli, germinate into hyphae and **invade blood vessels**, causing infarction - the **halo sign** (haemorrhage around a nodule) early and the **air-crescent sign** when neutrophils recover. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcus** is inhaled, disseminates and enters the CNS; its **polysaccharide capsule (glucuronoxylomannan)** blocks phagocytosis and **clogs arachnoid villi**, raising intracranial pressure - the cause of headache, papilloedema, cranial nerve palsies and early death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug targets**: **azoles** inhibit lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51) and ergosterol synthesis; **echinocandins** inhibit **beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase** in the cell wall; **polyenes (amphotericin)** bind ergosterol and punch membrane pores; **flucytosine** is converted to 5-fluorouracil inside the fungus. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Why some drugs fail**: Cryptococcus and Mucorales have little beta-glucan, so **echinocandins do not work** against them; fluconazole has no mould activity; **C. krusei is intrinsically fluconazole-resistant** and C. glabrata often dose-dependent. [Harrison 22e]`,`Echinocandins and fluconazole cross poorly into the eye and CSF - **endophthalmitis or meningitis needs fluconazole or amphotericin**, not an echinocandin alone. [IDSA 2016]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020**: **proven** = organism on histology or culture from sterile tissue; **probable** = host factor + clinical/radiological feature + mycological evidence (galactomannan, beta-D-glucan, culture, PCR); **possible** = host and clinical criteria without mycology. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Candidiasis spectrum**: mucocutaneous (oral, oesophageal, vulvovaginal) versus **invasive** - candidaemia, deep-seated (intra-abdominal abscess, endocarditis, osteomyelitis), **chronic disseminated (hepatosplenic)** after neutropenia, and endophthalmitis. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Aspergillus spectrum by immunity**: **invasive aspergillosis** (immunosuppressed), **chronic pulmonary aspergillosis** (simple aspergilloma, chronic cavitary, chronic fibrosing - over 3 months), and **ABPA** (hypersensitivity in asthma - see the Asthma topic). [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Cryptococcosis**: asymptomatic **cryptococcal antigenaemia** (CrAg positive, CSF negative), pulmonary, **meningitis/meningoencephalitis**, and disseminated (skin papules mimicking molluscum). [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis**: acute pulmonary, chronic cavitary pulmonary (TB mimic), **progressive disseminated (PDH)** and **adrenal** histoplasmosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Candidaemia**: an ICU patient on broad antibiotics with a central line who develops **new fever not responding to antibiotics** after day 5-7, often with no localising sign. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: neutropenic fever persisting **over 96 hours despite antibiotics**, pleuritic chest pain, cough and **haemoptysis**; sinus pain and epistaxis in sinus disease. [IDSA 2016]`,`**CPA**: cured TB patient with months of **cough, weight loss, fatigue and haemoptysis** and a 'sputum-negative TB relapse' label - the classic Indian misdiagnosis. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis**: **subacute headache over 1-3 weeks**, fever, vomiting, blurred or double vision and confusion; neck stiffness is **absent in about 70%** because inflammation is minimal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Disseminated histoplasmosis**: prolonged fever, weight loss, diarrhoea, **painful oral or tongue ulcers**, and features of **adrenal insufficiency** (fatigue, postural giddiness, pigmentation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Talaromycosis**: fever, anaemia and **umbilicated skin papules on face and trunk** in advanced HIV from Manipur or South-East Asia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Fundoscopy** in every candidaemia - fluffy white **chorioretinal exudates** (Candida endophthalmitis) change duration to at least 4-6 weeks. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Skin**: tender erythematous nodules in candidaemia (often C. tropicalis); **necrotic eschar** in mould infection; **molluscum-like umbilicated papules** in Cryptococcus, Histoplasma and Talaromyces. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CNS signs in cryptococcosis**: papilloedema, **sixth nerve palsy** (raised ICP), reduced vision, low GCS - a GCS under 15 predicts higher mortality. [WHO 2022]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy** with pancytopenia suggest disseminated histoplasmosis, talaromycosis or leishmaniasis in the right context. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral adrenal enlargement** with **postural hypotension and hyperpigmentation** in a middle-aged man suggests adrenal histoplasmosis (or TB). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing and coarse crackles** over an old TB cavity with haemoptysis suggest an **aspergilloma** within CPA. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Persistent ICU fever**: candidaemia versus resistant Gram-negative sepsis, Clostridioides difficile, drug fever and line infection by bacteria - **beta-D-glucan and blood culture** separate them. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Nodules with halo in a neutropenic patient**: invasive aspergillosis versus **mucormycosis** (reverse halo, over 10 nodules, sinusitis, voriconazole breakthrough) - galactomannan is negative in mucormycosis. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**CPA versus TB relapse**: both cavitate and bleed; CPA has **negative sputum NAAT, a positive Aspergillus IgG** (over 20 mg/L or kit cut-off) and a fungal ball or pericavitary thickening. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Chronic meningitis in HIV**: cryptococcal versus **TB meningitis** (high protein, low glucose, lymphocytes, basal exudates) - **CSF CrAg** is near 100% sensitive for cryptococcus. [WHO 2022]`,`**Disseminated histoplasmosis** mimics **disseminated TB, kala-azar and lymphoma** - bone marrow aspirate with fungal stains, urine antigen and culture separate them. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Blood culture** (2-3 sets, 20 mL each, automated system) is positive in only **about 50% of invasive candidiasis**; identify to species with **MALDI-TOF** - routine biochemical kits misidentify C. auris as C. haemulonii. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Serum (1,3)-beta-D-glucan**: positive at **80 pg/mL or more** (Fungitell), negative predictive value about 95% for invasive candidiasis; false positives with albumin, IVIG, haemodialysis, gauze and piperacillin batches; negative in Cryptococcus and Mucorales. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Galactomannan (Aspergillus)**: positive at an optical density index of **1.0 or more in serum or BAL**, or serum 0.7 plus BAL 0.8 or more (EORTC/MSGERC 2020); sensitivity falls on mould-active prophylaxis and in non-neutropenic patients. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**HRCT chest** in neutropenic fever over 3-5 days: nodules, **halo sign**, cavitation, **air-crescent sign**; CPA shows a cavity with fungal ball, pleural thickening and progression over 3 months. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Cryptococcal antigen (CrAg) lateral flow assay** on serum, plasma or CSF: sensitivity and specificity **over 95%**; India ink is cheap but misses about 20-30%; CSF opening pressure **25 cm H2O or more** is raised. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasma**: **urine and serum antigen** (sensitivity about 90% in disseminated disease), bone marrow or ulcer biopsy with **GMS/PAS stain** showing small intracellular yeasts, and culture (slow, 2-4 weeks). [WHO/PAHO 2020]`,`Always do **antifungal susceptibility testing** for invasive Candida, and **therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)** for voriconazole, posaconazole, itraconazole and flucytosine. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`]},{heading:`Management - candidiasis`,points:[`**Mucosal**: oral thrush - **clotrimazole troche 10 mg five times daily** or fluconazole 100-200 mg/day for 7-14 days; **oesophageal - fluconazole 200-400 mg/day for 14-21 days**. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Candidaemia first line is an echinocandin**: **caspofungin 70 mg IV loading then 50 mg daily**, **micafungin 100 mg IV daily** or **anidulafungin 200 mg loading then 100 mg daily**. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Remove or change all central venous catheters** as soon as possible; take **repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours** until negative. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Step down to oral fluconazole 400 mg (6 mg/kg) daily** after **5-7 days** if the isolate is susceptible, the patient is stable and repeat cultures are negative; voriconazole step-down for C. krusei. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Duration: 14 days after the first negative blood culture** and resolution of symptoms, provided there is no metastatic focus; endophthalmitis, endocarditis and osteomyelitis need weeks to months. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Fluconazole** (800 mg loading, then 400 mg daily; halve after loading if CrCl under 50 mL/min) is acceptable in a stable, non-critically ill patient with low risk of resistance; **liposomal amphotericin 3-5 mg/kg/day** if echinocandins are unavailable or not tolerated. [IDSA 2016]`,`**C. auris**: echinocandin first line, **contact isolation, chlorhexidine or chlorine-based cleaning** (quaternary ammonium compounds fail), and screening of contacts (axilla-groin swab). [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`]},{heading:`Management - aspergillosis, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis`,points:[`**Invasive aspergillosis**: **voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x 2 doses then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly** (oral 200-300 mg 12-hourly), trough **1-5.5 mg/L**; or **isavuconazole 200 mg 8-hourly for 6 doses then 200 mg daily** - equal efficacy, fewer side effects (SECURE trial). [IDSA 2016]`,`Aspergillosis alternatives: **liposomal amphotericin 3 mg/kg/day**; posaconazole; treat for at least **6-12 weeks** and until immunosuppression is reversed. [IDSA 2016]`,`**CPA**: **itraconazole 200 mg twice daily** or voriconazole 150-200 mg twice daily for **at least 6 months**; **bronchial artery embolisation** for haemoptysis; surgery for a single aspergilloma. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**HIV cryptococcal meningitis (WHO 2022)**: **single dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** + **flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses for 14 days** + **fluconazole 1200 mg/day for 14 days** (AMBITION-cm). [WHO 2022]`,`Then **consolidation fluconazole 800 mg/day for 8 weeks** and **maintenance 200 mg/day** until CD4 is 200 or more (or 100-200 with suppressed viral load) after at least 1 year. [WHO 2022]`,`If liposomal amphotericin is unavailable: **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day + flucytosine for 7 days**, then fluconazole 1200 mg/day for days 8-14; without amphotericin, **fluconazole 1200 mg + flucytosine for 14 days**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Raised ICP**: **therapeutic lumbar puncture** to drain CSF until pressure is under 20 cm H2O or halved, repeated daily; **no steroids, acetazolamide or mannitol** (CryptoDex harm). [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis (WHO/PAHO 2020)**: severe disease - **liposomal amphotericin 3 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks**, then **itraconazole 200 mg thrice daily for 3 days then twice daily for at least 12 months**; mild-moderate - itraconazole alone. [WHO/PAHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and ICU care`,points:[`**Admit** all invasive fungal infections: candidaemia, febrile neutropenia with lung nodules, suspected cryptococcal meningitis and disseminated histoplasmosis. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Amphotericin safety bundle**: **1 L normal saline before each dose**, give over 2 hours (deoxycholate over 4-6 hours), **daily potassium and magnesium supplements** (K 1-2 tablets of 8 mEq twice daily, Mg 1-2 tablets), creatinine and electrolytes **twice weekly**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Empirical antifungal** in persistent neutropenic fever after 4-7 days of antibiotics: caspofungin or liposomal amphotericin; prefer a **pre-emptive (biomarker and CT-driven)** strategy where tests are available. [IDSA 2016]`,`Echinocandin **empirical** therapy may be used in septic ICU patients with risk factors and multisite colonisation; **stop after 4-5 days** if beta-D-glucan and cultures are negative. [IDSA 2016]`,`Monitor **flucytosine** for marrow suppression (CBC twice weekly) and adjust dose to creatinine clearance; check **voriconazole trough on day 5**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Source control**: drain intra-abdominal abscesses, remove infected prostheses, and early valve surgery for Candida endocarditis. [IDSA 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **amphotericin B (liposomal) is the drug of choice**; avoid high-dose fluconazole (craniofacial defects in first trimester) and voriconazole; flucytosine is teratogenic in animals. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Neonates**: candidaemia in preterm infants - **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day** or fluconazole 12 mg/kg/day; always do LP and fundoscopy (high CNS spread). [IDSA 2016]`,`**CKD**: echinocandins, voriconazole oral and isavuconazole need **no renal dose change**; avoid IV voriconazole at CrCl under 50 (cyclodextrin vehicle accumulates); reduce fluconazole and flucytosine. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Liver disease**: reduce **caspofungin to 35 mg daily** in Child-Pugh B; voriconazole halves the maintenance dose in mild-moderate cirrhosis. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Drug interactions**: azoles inhibit **CYP3A4** - raise tacrolimus, cyclosporine, statins, warfarin and vincristine levels; **rifampicin cuts voriconazole levels by about 95%** (contraindicated). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where liposomal amphotericin or flucytosine is unaffordable, use **amphotericin deoxycholate with saline loading** and the oral fluconazole-flucytosine regimen; CrAg lateral-flow tests are cheap and need no laboratory. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Candidaemia mortality is 30-60%**; each day of delay in antifungal therapy raises mortality - early source control and echinocandin cut it. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`Candidaemia metastasises to **eye (up to 16%)**, heart valves, bone and kidney; hepatosplenic candidiasis presents as fever when neutrophils recover. [IDSA 2016]`,`Invasive aspergillosis carries **30-60% mortality** and up to 90% in cerebral disease; CPA has 5-year mortality around **40-50%** and causes life-threatening haemoptysis. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`Untreated cryptococcal meningitis is **almost always fatal**; with the WHO 2022 regimen **10-week mortality is about 25%**; blindness, deafness and hydrocephalus follow uncontrolled ICP. [WHO 2022]`,`**Cryptococcal IRIS** occurs in 10-30% after ART - hence the **4-6 week delay** before starting ART. [WHO 2022]`,`Drug toxicity: amphotericin - nephrotoxicity, **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia**, infusion reactions, anaemia; voriconazole - **visual disturbance (30%)**, hallucinations, photosensitivity, skin cancer, periostitis and hepatitis; flucytosine - neutropenia. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and the programme link`,points:[`**Refer** to a tertiary centre with mycology, ICU and TDM for C. auris, aspergillosis, cryptococcal meningitis needing repeated LPs, and Candida endocarditis. [ICMR AMRSN 2024]`,`**Follow-up**: CT chest every 2-4 weeks in aspergillosis; LFT and voriconazole levels; **visual acuity and fundus** in cryptococcal and Candida eye disease; CPA reviewed at 3 and 6 months with CT and Aspergillus IgG. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Antifungal prophylaxis**: **posaconazole delayed-release 300 mg daily** in AML/MDS induction and GVHD; fluconazole in allogeneic transplant; avoid blanket fluconazole in ICUs (selects C. auris and C. glabrata). [IDSA 2016]`,`**CrAg screening** of all PLHIV with **CD4 under 100 (consider under 200)** before ART is part of the WHO advanced HIV disease package and NACO care - see the Opportunistic infections in HIV topic. [WHO 2022]`,`**Infection prevention**: line-care bundles, chlorhexidine bathing, hand hygiene and **HEPA-filtered rooms** during construction near haematology wards. [IDSA 2016]`,`India's **ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance Network (AMRSN)** includes fungal surveillance; report C. auris clusters to the hospital infection control committee. [ICMR AMRSN 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ECMM/ISHAM/ASM global guideline on candidiasis (2025)** confirms **echinocandin first line** for candidaemia, adds **rezafungin 400 mg then 200 mg once weekly** as an option, and stresses early catheter removal and step-down at 5 days. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Rezafungin**, a once-weekly echinocandin, was approved by the FDA and EMA in **2023** (ReSTORE trial non-inferior to caspofungin). [FDA 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 cryptococcal guideline** replaced 7-day amphotericin regimens with **single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** plus 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List (2022)** - the first such list - names 19 fungi; critical priority: C. neoformans, C. auris, A. fumigatus, C. albicans. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020** revised galactomannan thresholds (serum or BAL 1.0) and added PCR as mycological evidence for probable aspergillosis. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**ISHAM 2024 revised ABPA criteria** use total IgE over 500 IU/mL and Aspergillus-specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more as obligatory criteria; managed under the Asthma topic. [ISHAM 2024]`,`Agents in late trials: **olorofim** (dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibitor, mould-active) and **fosmanogepix** (Gwt1 inhibitor) - not yet licensed in India. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Why not fluconazole for aspergillosis?'** - no mould activity; **'Why not an echinocandin for cryptococcus?'** - the capsule-bearing yeast lacks a beta-glucan-rich wall target. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: treating candidaemia without **removing the line, repeating cultures and doing fundoscopy** - examiners call these the three bundle steps. [IDSA 2016]`,`Common mistake: calling Candida in sputum or urine of a catheterised ICU patient an infection - it is usually **colonisation**; treat candiduria only if symptomatic, neutropenic, infant or before urological surgery. [IDSA 2016]`,`Know the **WHO 2022 crypto regimen** by heart: **10 mg/kg L-AmB once + flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day + fluconazole 1200 mg/day for 14 days, then 800 mg for 8 weeks, then 200 mg**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Galactomannan for Aspergillus, beta-D-glucan for Candida (and Pneumocystis), CrAg for Cryptococcus, urine antigen for Histoplasma** - and none of these for Mucorales. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Halo sign = early angioinvasive aspergillosis; air-crescent = recovery phase; reverse halo = think mucormycosis**. [IDSA 2016]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antifungal drugs: dose, duration and toxicity`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Fluconazole`,`Candidaemia 800 mg loading then 400 mg (6 mg/kg) PO/IV daily; crypto 1200 mg induction, 800 mg consolidation, 200 mg maintenance`,`Halve after loading if CrCl under 50; candidaemia 14 days after negative culture`,`QT prolongation, hepatitis, CYP3A4 interactions; no mould activity; C. krusei resistant; avoid high dose in pregnancy`],[`Caspofungin`,`70 mg IV loading then 50 mg IV daily`,`Step down to fluconazole after 5-7 days if stable and susceptible; 35 mg in Child-Pugh B`,`Infusion reaction, raised transaminases; poor CSF, eye and urine penetration; no activity against Cryptococcus or Mucorales`],[`Micafungin / anidulafungin`,`Micafungin 100 mg IV daily; anidulafungin 200 mg then 100 mg IV daily`,`As caspofungin; no renal adjustment`,`Hepatotoxicity (micafungin); same spectrum gaps as caspofungin`],[`Voriconazole`,`6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x 2 then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly; oral 200-300 mg 12-hourly`,`Trough 1-5.5 mg/L on day 5; at least 6-12 weeks in IA`,`Visual disturbance, hallucinations, photosensitivity, skin cancer, periostitis (fluoride), hepatitis, QT; avoid with rifampicin; IV vehicle accumulates if CrCl under 50`],[`Isavuconazole`,`200 mg IV/PO 8-hourly x 6 doses then 200 mg daily`,`No renal adjustment; TDM optional`,`Shortens QT (contraindicated in familial short QT), fewer visual and liver effects than voriconazole`],[`Itraconazole`,`200 mg PO twice daily (CPA, histoplasmosis maintenance); 200 mg thrice daily x 3 days loading in histoplasmosis`,`6 months in CPA; 12 months in disseminated histoplasmosis; trough over 0.5-1 mg/L`,`Negative inotrope - avoid in heart failure; hepatitis; erratic absorption (capsules with food and acid)`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`3-5 mg/kg/day IV (single 10 mg/kg for HIV crypto)`,`Histoplasmosis 2 weeks; IA salvage; neutropenic empirical`,`Less nephrotoxic than deoxycholate; hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions; safe in pregnancy`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`0.7-1 mg/kg/day IV over 4-6 h after 1 L saline`,`7 days in WHO alternative crypto regimen`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, rigors, anaemia, phlebitis - monitor creatinine and K twice weekly`],[`Flucytosine`,`100 mg/kg/day PO in 4 divided doses`,`14 days in cryptococcal induction; adjust to CrCl`,`Marrow suppression, hepatitis, diarrhoea; never use alone (rapid resistance); limited availability in India`]]},{heading:`Classification and organism-host matching`,columns:[`Organism`,`Typical host`,`Key syndrome`,`Diagnostic test of choice`],rows:[[`Candida spp.`,`ICU, central line, TPN, abdominal surgery, neutropenia`,`Candidaemia, intra-abdominal candidiasis, endophthalmitis`,`Blood culture with MALDI-TOF ID; beta-D-glucan 80 pg/mL or more`],[`Candida auris`,`ICU, long stay, ventilated, prior antifungals`,`Outbreak candidaemia, often fluconazole-resistant`,`MALDI-TOF (not VITEK-2 older databases); susceptibility testing`],[`Aspergillus fumigatus / flavus`,`Neutropenia over 10 days, HSCT, steroids; post-TB cavity (CPA)`,`Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis; CPA; aspergilloma`,`CT halo sign, galactomannan ODI 1.0 or more; Aspergillus IgG for CPA`],[`Cryptococcus neoformans`,`HIV with CD4 under 100, transplant, steroids`,`Subacute meningitis with raised ICP`,`CrAg lateral flow (serum, CSF); India ink; CSF culture`],[`Histoplasma capsulatum`,`HIV CD4 under 150; healthy adults in Gangetic plains`,`Disseminated disease, oral ulcers, adrenal masses`,`Urine antigen; marrow or adrenal biopsy (GMS)`],[`Talaromyces marneffei`,`Advanced HIV, Manipur / North-East`,`Fever, anaemia, umbilicated papules`,`Skin scraping or marrow - septate yeasts; culture (red pigment)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fungal look-alikes`,columns:[`Presentation`,`Fungal cause`,`Main mimic`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Persistent ICU fever on antibiotics`,`Candidaemia`,`MDR Gram-negative sepsis`,`Beta-D-glucan positive, Candida in blood culture; procalcitonin often lower`],[`Neutropenic fever with lung nodules`,`Invasive aspergillosis`,`Mucormycosis`,`Galactomannan positive; mucor - reverse halo, sinus disease, voriconazole breakthrough`],[`Cavity with haemoptysis after TB`,`Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`TB relapse`,`NAAT negative, Aspergillus IgG positive, fungal ball, pericavitary thickening`],[`Subacute meningitis in HIV`,`Cryptococcal meningitis`,`TB meningitis`,`CSF CrAg positive, opening pressure high, low cell count; TBM - high protein, basal exudate`],[`Fever, pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly`,`Disseminated histoplasmosis`,`Kala-azar, disseminated TB, lymphoma`,`Urine Histoplasma antigen, intracellular yeasts on marrow (GMS)`],[`Bilateral adrenal masses with Addisonian features`,`Adrenal histoplasmosis`,`Adrenal TB, metastases`,`Biopsy with fungal stain; low-attenuation enlarged glands`]]},{heading:`Fungal biomarkers and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Positive cut-off`,`Use`,`Pitfalls`],rows:[[`(1,3)-beta-D-glucan (Fungitell)`,`80 pg/mL or more (60-79 indeterminate)`,`Invasive candidiasis, aspergillosis, Pneumocystis; high NPV`,`False positive with albumin, IVIG, haemodialysis, gauze, Gram-negative sepsis; negative in Cryptococcus and Mucorales`],[`Galactomannan (serum)`,`ODI 1.0 or more single, or 0.7 with BAL 0.8 (EORTC/MSGERC 2020)`,`Invasive aspergillosis in neutropenia; twice-weekly screening`,`Low sensitivity on mould-active prophylaxis and in non-neutropenic hosts`],[`Galactomannan (BAL)`,`ODI 1.0 or more`,`Probable IA, including ICU and CAPA`,`Contamination from airway colonisation`],[`Cryptococcal antigen LFA`,`Any positive (titre guides prognosis)`,`Screening at CD4 under 100-200; diagnosis on CSF`,`Prozone at very high titres (rare); does not guide cure - stays positive`],[`CSF opening pressure`,`25 cm H2O or more raised`,`Decides therapeutic LP in crypto meningitis`,`Must be measured lying lateral, relaxed`],[`Histoplasma urine antigen`,`Kit-specific positive`,`Disseminated histoplasmosis (sensitivity about 90%)`,`Cross-reacts with Talaromyces, Blastomyces`],[`Aspergillus-specific IgG`,`Kit-specific (about 20-40 mg/L)`,`Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`Low in immunosuppressed; not for invasive disease`]]},{heading:`WHO 2022 cryptococcal meningitis regimens (HIV)`,columns:[`Phase`,`Preferred`,`Alternative`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Induction (2 weeks)`,`L-AmB 10 mg/kg single dose + flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day + fluconazole 1200 mg/day x 14 days`,`AmB deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day + flucytosine x 7 days, then fluconazole 1200 mg days 8-14; or fluconazole 1200 mg + flucytosine x 14 days`,`Therapeutic LP if opening pressure 25 cm H2O or more; saline loading and K/Mg`],[`Consolidation (8 weeks)`,`Fluconazole 800 mg/day`,`-`,`Children: 6-12 mg/kg/day (max 800 mg)`],[`Maintenance`,`Fluconazole 200 mg/day`,`-`,`Stop at CD4 200 or more, or 100-200 with suppressed VL, after at least 1 year`],[`ART timing`,`Start 4-6 weeks after antifungal start`,`-`,`Earlier ART raises mortality (COAT trial)`],[`Asymptomatic CrAg positive (CSF negative)`,`Fluconazole 800 mg x 2 weeks, 400 mg x 8 weeks, then 200 mg`,`Single L-AmB + 2 weeks fluconazole-flucytosine if available (ACACIA)`,`LP before treatment to exclude meningitis`]]}],redFlags:[`Candidaemia with persisting positive blood cultures beyond 72 hours, new murmur or eye findings (endocarditis, endophthalmitis)`,`Neutropenic fever over 96 hours with haemoptysis or lung nodules`,`HIV patient with headache, vomiting, visual blurring or confusion - suspect cryptococcal meningitis and do LP with opening pressure`,`CSF opening pressure 25 cm H2O or more, papilloedema or sixth nerve palsy`,`Massive haemoptysis (over 200-600 mL/24 h) from an aspergilloma`,`Postural hypotension, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia with adrenal masses (adrenal crisis from histoplasmosis)`,`Candida auris isolated anywhere - immediate contact isolation and infection-control alert`],pearls:[`**Candida bundle**: echinocandin early, pull the line, repeat cultures till negative, fundoscopy, 14 days after first negative culture.`,`**C. tropicalis** is India's commonest ICU candidaemia species; **C. auris** was first recognised in Indian hospitals.`,`**Echinocandins miss Cryptococcus and Mucorales**; fluconazole misses all moulds and C. krusei.`,`**WHO 2022 crypto: 10 mg/kg L-AmB once + 14 days 5FC + fluconazole 1200 mg**, then 800 mg 8 weeks, then 200 mg; ART at 4-6 weeks.`,`**No steroids, no acetazolamide for crypto ICP** - drain CSF by daily therapeutic LP.`,`Post-TB patient with haemoptysis and negative sputum NAAT - think **chronic pulmonary aspergillosis**, check Aspergillus IgG.`,`**Voriconazole** - TDM 1-5.5 mg/L, visual flashes, photosensitivity, never with rifampicin.`,`Biomarker match: **GM - Aspergillus, BDG - Candida/PCP, CrAg - Cryptococcus, urine Ag - Histoplasma**.`,`Bilateral adrenal masses in a healthy Bengali man: **adrenal histoplasmosis** until proven otherwise.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old man on day 9 of ICU stay after bowel surgery has a central line, TPN and is on meropenem. He develops new fever and hypotension. Blood culture grows yeast. What is the most appropriate initial antifungal?`,options:[`Oral fluconazole 200 mg daily`,`Intravenous caspofungin 70 mg loading then 50 mg daily`,`Intravenous voriconazole 6 mg/kg twice on day 1`,`Oral flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day`],answer:1,explanation:`Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin) are first line for candidaemia, especially in shock and in India where C. auris and C. glabrata resistance to azoles is common. Low-dose fluconazole is under-dosed and unsafe in a septic patient; voriconazole is a mould drug used for step-down only for C. krusei; flucytosine must never be used alone because resistance develops rapidly.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q2`,stem:`In a neutropenic patient with acute myeloid leukaemia, a CT chest shows a nodule surrounded by ground-glass attenuation. Which serum test best supports probable invasive aspergillosis by EORTC/MSGERC 2020 criteria?`,options:[`Cryptococcal antigen lateral flow assay`,`Histoplasma urine antigen`,`Galactomannan optical density index of 1.0 or more`,`Anti-Candida mannan antibody`],answer:2,explanation:`The halo sign in a neutropenic host plus a serum galactomannan ODI of 1.0 or more (or BAL 1.0) fulfils probable invasive aspergillosis under EORTC/MSGERC 2020. CrAg detects the cryptococcal capsule, urine antigen detects Histoplasma, and Candida mannan serology plays no part in mould diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q3`,stem:`A 32-year-old man with HIV (CD4 42 cells/mm3, not on ART) has 2 weeks of headache. CSF CrAg is positive and opening pressure is 34 cm H2O. Per WHO 2022, which induction regimen is preferred?`,options:[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate 0.7 mg/kg/day alone for 14 days with dexamethasone`,`Fluconazole 400 mg daily for 8 weeks and start ART the same week`,`Caspofungin 70 mg loading then 50 mg daily with acetazolamide`,`Single liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg with 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole 1200 mg/day`],answer:3,explanation:`WHO 2022, based on the AMBITION-cm trial, prefers single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg plus 14 days of flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day and fluconazole 1200 mg/day, with daily therapeutic LP for the raised pressure. Dexamethasone and acetazolamide cause harm, fluconazole 400 mg alone is inadequate induction, ART must be delayed 4-6 weeks, and echinocandins have no activity against Cryptococcus.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man treated for pulmonary TB 4 years ago presents with 5 months of cough, weight loss and recurrent haemoptysis. Sputum CBNAAT is negative. CT shows an upper-lobe cavity with an intracavitary mass and pleural thickening. The most useful next test is:`,options:[`Aspergillus-specific IgG`,`Serum galactomannan`,`Repeat Widal test`,`Serum (1,3)-beta-D-glucan`],answer:0,explanation:`Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis in a post-TB cavity is diagnosed by more than 3 months of symptoms, a cavity with fungal ball or pericavitary thickening, and a raised Aspergillus-specific IgG. Serum galactomannan and beta-D-glucan are insensitive in the immunocompetent host with chronic disease, and Widal has no role.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q5`,stem:`A renal transplant recipient on tacrolimus is started on voriconazole for invasive aspergillosis. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Voriconazole should be combined with rifampicin to prevent TB reactivation`,`Voriconazole will lower tacrolimus levels, so the tacrolimus dose should be increased`,`Voriconazole will raise tacrolimus levels; reduce the tacrolimus dose and monitor troughs`,`Isavuconazole has no CYP3A4 interaction, so it can be swapped with no monitoring`],answer:2,explanation:`Voriconazole inhibits CYP3A4 and raises tacrolimus levels, so the tacrolimus dose is cut (often to about one-third) with close trough monitoring. Rifampicin induces CYP enzymes and lowers voriconazole levels by about 95%, so the pair is contraindicated. Isavuconazole is a milder CYP3A4 inhibitor but still raises tacrolimus and needs monitoring.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, clinical features, diagnosis and management of invasive candidiasis in the intensive care unit. Add a note on Candida auris.`,openingLines:[`Invasive candidiasis is infection of the bloodstream (candidaemia) or deep tissues by Candida species, most often in ICU patients with central lines, broad-spectrum antibiotics, TPN and abdominal surgery.`,`It carries 30-60% mortality; the ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025 guideline recommends an echinocandin first, early catheter removal, repeat cultures and fundoscopy, with step-down to fluconazole after 5-7 days.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and species`,points:[`Indian ICU incidence about 6.5 per 1000 admissions; **C. tropicalis** commonest; C. auris about 5% (Chakrabarti 2015).`,`Fourth commonest cause of hospital bloodstream infection worldwide.`]},{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Central venous catheter, broad-spectrum antibiotics, TPN, ICU stay over 7 days.`,`Abdominal surgery, anastomotic leak, necrotising pancreatitis, haemodialysis, neutropenia, steroids.`,`Multisite colonisation (Candida colonisation index over 0.5; Candida score 3 or more).`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fever not responding to antibiotics; septic shock; skin nodules.`,`Endophthalmitis (fluffy chorioretinal exudates), endocarditis, osteomyelitis; hepatosplenic candidiasis after neutrophil recovery.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Blood cultures (positive in about 50%); species by MALDI-TOF; antifungal susceptibility.`,`Serum beta-D-glucan 80 pg/mL or more; high NPV to stop empirical therapy.`,`Echocardiography if persistent candidaemia; fundoscopy within the first week.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`**Caspofungin 70 mg then 50 mg daily** (or micafungin 100 mg, anidulafungin 200/100 mg).`,`Remove all central lines; repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours.`,`Step down to **fluconazole 400 mg daily** at 5-7 days if stable, susceptible, cultures negative.`,`Duration **14 days after first negative culture**; longer for metastatic foci.`,`Liposomal amphotericin 3-5 mg/kg/day if echinocandins fail or unavailable; source control of abscesses.`]},{heading:`Candida auris`,points:[`Multidrug-resistant, persists on surfaces, colonises skin; first recognised in Indian ICUs.`,`Echinocandin first line; contact isolation, chlorine-based disinfection, contact screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected candidaemia - cultures, echinocandin, line removal, fundoscopy, step-down, duration`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors and epidemiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Management with doses`,marks:4},{part:`Candida auris`,marks:1}],keywords:[`candidaemia`,`echinocandin`,`catheter removal`,`beta-D-glucan`,`Candida auris`,`fluconazole step-down`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on cryptococcal meningitis in HIV, including CrAg screening and the WHO 2022 treatment regimen.`,openingLines:[`Cryptococcal meningitis is a subacute meningoencephalitis caused by Cryptococcus neoformans in people with advanced HIV (CD4 under 100), and causes about 15-19% of AIDS deaths.`,`WHO 2022 recommends CrAg screening before ART at CD4 under 100 and a single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B induction regimen.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`Headache over 1-3 weeks, fever, vomiting, visual loss; neck stiffness often absent.`,`LP with opening pressure; CSF CrAg (over 95% sensitive), India ink, culture.`]},{heading:`Treatment (WHO 2022)`,points:[`Induction: L-AmB 10 mg/kg once + flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day + fluconazole 1200 mg/day for 14 days.`,`Consolidation fluconazole 800 mg/day x 8 weeks; maintenance 200 mg/day until CD4 200 or more.`,`Therapeutic LP for opening pressure 25 cm H2O or more; no steroids or acetazolamide.`,`Start ART 4-6 weeks after antifungal therapy begins (COAT trial).`]},{heading:`CrAg screening`,points:[`Serum CrAg for all with CD4 under 100 (consider under 200) before ART.`,`Positive: LP; if CSF negative, pre-emptive fluconazole 800 mg x 2 weeks, 400 mg x 8 weeks, then 200 mg.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: induction, consolidation and maintenance phases with doses`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment regimen`,marks:2.5},{part:`CrAg screening`,marks:1}],keywords:[`cryptococcal meningitis`,`CrAg`,`AMBITION`,`liposomal amphotericin`,`flucytosine`,`therapeutic LP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on candidiasis, aspergillosis, cryptococcosis and histoplasmosis`,`Cornely OA et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of candidiasis: an initiative of ECMM, ISHAM and ASM, Lancet Infectious Diseases, 2025`,`Pappas PG et al. Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Candidiasis: IDSA, 2016`,`Patterson TF et al. Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Aspergillosis: IDSA, 2016; and Denning DW et al. ERS/ESCMID Chronic Pulmonary Aspergillosis guideline, 2016`,`Donnelly JP et al. Revision and update of the consensus definitions of invasive fungal disease (EORTC/MSGERC), Clinical Infectious Diseases, 2020`,`WHO Guidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV, 2022`,`WHO/PAHO Guidelines for diagnosing and managing disseminated histoplasmosis among people living with HIV, 2020; WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List, 2022`,`Chakrabarti A et al. Incidence, characteristics and outcome of ICU-acquired candidemia in India, Intensive Care Medicine, 2015`]});var r=[];r.push({id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail`,title:`Leptospirosis in depth: Weil disease, pulmonary haemorrhage and prophylaxis`,oneLiner:`**Leptospirosis** is a **zoonotic spirochaetal vasculitis** caught from **rat-urine-contaminated water or soil** through abraded skin or mucosa; most cases are a self-limiting biphasic fever, but 5-10% progress to **Weil disease (jaundice, AKI, bleeding)** or **severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome** - diagnosed by **IgM ELISA or MAT**, treated with **doxycycline (mild) or penicillin/ceftriaxone (severe)**, and prevented in the flood season with **doxycycline 200 mg once weekly**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`leptospirosis`,`Weil disease`,`Weil's syndrome`,`Leptospira interrogans`,`rat fever`,`field fever`,`Andaman haemorrhagic fever`,`severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome`,`SPHS`,`MAT`,`microscopic agglutination test`,`IgM ELISA`,`modified Faine's criteria`,`conjunctival suffusion`,`doxycycline prophylaxis`,`benzyl penicillin`,`ceftriaxone`,`NPPCL`,`flood fever`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Leptospirosis** is a systemic zoonosis caused by pathogenic spirochaetes of the genus **Leptospira** (mainly L. interrogans), with more than **250 pathogenic serovars** grouped into about 24 serogroups. [Harrison 22e]`,`The global burden is estimated at about **1 million cases and 59,000 deaths a year**, concentrated in tropical, low-income, flood-prone settings - more deaths than many better-known tropical diseases. [Costa et al, PLoS NTD 2015]`,`In India it is **endemic along the western and southern coast** - **Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Gujarat** - and in the **Andaman and Nicobar Islands**, where a pulmonary-haemorrhagic outbreak in 1988 was first called 'Andaman haemorrhagic fever'. [NCDC Leptospirosis Guidelines 2015]`,`Kerala consistently reports the **largest number of notified cases and deaths** in India, with surges after the **2018 and 2019 floods**. [NCDC 2024]`,`Cases rise sharply **2-4 weeks after heavy rainfall or flooding** (incubation plus exposure lag), peaking **July to November** in the south-west monsoon belt and later in Tamil Nadu with the north-east monsoon. [NCDC 2015]`,`It is a **notifiable disease** in India, reported through **IDSP on the IHIP portal**, and has a dedicated **Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis** under NCDC in endemic states. [NCDC 2024]`,`Leptospirosis accounts for an estimated **5-15% of acute undifferentiated fever** in endemic coastal districts during the monsoon - the reason it sits in every AUF panel there (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`Most infections are **mild or subclinical** (90% anicteric), but **5-10% develop severe disease** with a case fatality of **5-15%** for Weil disease and **over 50%** for severe pulmonary haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`The **maintenance host is the rat** (Rattus norvegicus, R. rattus), which carries leptospires lifelong in its **renal tubules** and sheds them in urine without illness; serogroup **Icterohaemorrhagiae** from rats causes most severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other reservoirs are **cattle (Hardjo, Pomona), pigs (Pomona), dogs (Canicola) and bandicoots** - livestock exposure explains rural cases away from rats. [Harrison 22e]`,`Humans are **accidental, dead-end hosts**; there is effectively **no person-to-person spread**, so isolation is unnecessary and standard precautions suffice. [Harrison 22e]`,`Leptospires survive for **weeks in warm, moist, neutral-pH soil and fresh water**, which is why paddy fields and stagnant floodwater stay infective long after the rain. [WHO 2003]`,`Entry is through **cuts and abrasions, softened (water-logged) skin, conjunctiva and oral or nasal mucosa**; ingestion of contaminated water is a minor route. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Occupational risk groups**: paddy and sugarcane farmers, **MGNREGS workers** clearing canals, sewage and sanitation workers, fishermen, veterinarians, abattoir and dairy workers, and miners. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Recreational and disaster risk**: wading or swimming in floodwater, rescue and relief work after floods, adventure water sports, and living in slums with **rodent infestation and open drains**. [NCDC 2015]`,`Walking **barefoot**, working with **open wounds**, and not using boots or gloves multiply the risk; this is where counselling and chemoprophylaxis are targeted. [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Leptospires are **highly motile corkscrew spirochaetes** that penetrate skin and mucosa, enter the blood within hours and seed **every organ during the leptospiraemic phase** (first 7-10 days). [Harrison 22e]`,`The core lesion is a **systemic vasculitis with endothelial damage and capillary leak**, explaining haemorrhages, conjunctival suffusion, pulmonary bleeding and hypotension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Leptospiral **LPS is weakly endotoxic** and signals through **TLR2 rather than TLR4** in humans; outer-membrane lipoproteins such as **LipL32** drive the cytokine storm and tubular injury. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kidney**: acute **tubulo-interstitial nephritis** with proximal tubular dysfunction - impaired sodium reabsorption increases distal delivery and **potassium wasting**, giving the characteristic **non-oliguric, hypokalaemic AKI**; oliguria is a late, severe sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver**: jaundice comes from **disruption of hepatocyte intercellular junctions and cholestasis** plus haemolysis, not necrosis - so **bilirubin is high (often above 20 mg/dL) while transaminases rise only modestly** (usually under 5 times normal) and liver failure is rare. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung**: endothelial injury of alveolar capillaries with **linear deposition of IgG and complement on the alveolar basement membrane** causes the **severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (SPHS)**, often without thrombocytopenia severe enough to explain it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Muscle**: direct invasion causes focal necrosis - hence **calf and lumbar myalgia and a raised CPK**, which helps separate leptospirosis from viral hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **immune phase** (after day 7) is driven by antibody and immune complexes: leptospires vanish from blood but persist in **kidney (urine), CSF and aqueous humour**, explaining aseptic meningitis and late uveitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why the drugs**: leptospires are **extracellular** and highly susceptible to **penicillins, third-generation cephalosporins, doxycycline and macrolides**; killing them can release antigen and provoke a **Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** in the first hours of treatment. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and clinical phases`,points:[`Incubation is **2-30 days, usually 5-14 days** - ask about exposure in the last month, not the last week. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospiraemic (acute) phase**, about 3-10 days: abrupt fever, rigors, severe headache, myalgia and conjunctival suffusion; organisms are in **blood and CSF** - the window for **PCR and blood culture**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immune phase**, day 7 to 30: antibodies appear, organisms are shed in **urine**; the patient may defervesce for 1-3 days and then relapse with **aseptic meningitis**, uveitis or rash - the classic **biphasic course** is often blurred in severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anicteric leptospirosis (about 90%)**: influenza-like, self-limiting fever, often with aseptic meningitis; mortality is very low. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Icteric leptospirosis - Weil disease (5-10%)**: the triad of **jaundice, acute kidney injury and haemorrhage**, often with myocarditis and altered sensorium; the phases merge into one continuous severe illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (SPHS)**: haemoptysis, breathlessness and bilateral alveolar infiltrates progressing to ARDS, sometimes **without jaundice**; it is now the leading cause of leptospirosis death in many Indian series. [Harrison 22e]`,`A practical bedside grading used in Indian protocols: **mild** (ambulant, no organ failure), **moderate** (needs admission - vomiting, jaundice, rising creatinine) and **severe** (shock, oliguria, pulmonary haemorrhage, altered sensorium, bleeding). [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Abrupt high fever with rigors**, intense **frontal or retro-orbital headache** and prostration are the commonest presenting complaints (over 90%). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe myalgia, especially of the calves and lumbar muscles**, is a key discriminating symptom - patients often cannot walk. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gastrointestinal symptoms - **nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhoea** - are common and can mimic acute abdomen, cholecystitis or pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cough, haemoptysis or breathlessness** in a febrile patient from a flood area is pulmonary leptospirosis until proved otherwise - haemoptysis may be the first sign of SPHS. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **reduced or dark urine, yellow eyes and bleeding** (epistaxis, gum bleeding, melaena) - the Weil disease symptoms. [NCDC 2015]`,`Take a **4-week exposure history**: floodwater, paddy, drains, sewage, animals, rodents at home, barefoot work, cuts on feet - this earns the epidemiological score in Faine's criteria. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**Atypical presentations**: aseptic meningitis, isolated myocarditis with arrhythmia, acalculous cholecystitis, acute pancreatitis, and **late anterior uveitis** weeks to months after the fever. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Conjunctival suffusion** - redness of the bulbar conjunctiva **without purulent discharge**, most marked on the palpebral fissure, appearing on day 3-4 - is the most specific bedside sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subconjunctival haemorrhage** with suffusion strongly suggests leptospirosis in the right setting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calf muscle tenderness** on squeezing, out of proportion to the fever, supports the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice** - often deep, with an orange tint (jaundice plus suffusion) - and a mildly **tender hepatomegaly**; splenomegaly is less common. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative bradycardia (Faget sign)** may be seen early; **tachycardia, hypotension, irregular pulse (AF, AV block)** indicate myocarditis or shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **petechiae, purpura and bleeding**; in the chest, **crackles and tachypnoea** may be the only clue before haemoptysis. [NCDC 2015]`,`Check **urine output and neck stiffness**; a transient maculopapular or petechial rash and lymphadenopathy occur in a minority. [Harrison 22e]`,`The absence of an **eschar** and the presence of **calf tenderness plus suffusion** help separate it from scrub typhus at the bedside. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Scrub typhus**: same season and AKI/ARDS, but look for an **eschar**, lymphadenopathy and bilirubin rarely above 5 mg/dL; both respond to doxycycline (see the Scrub typhus topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Falciparum malaria**: jaundice, AKI and ARDS also occur - **smear or RDT** is mandatory in every case; co-infection exists. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Dengue**: leucopenia and haemoconcentration rather than **neutrophilic leucocytosis**, and AKI or deep jaundice is unusual. [WHO 2025]`,`**Acute viral hepatitis**: transaminases in the **thousands**, little fever by the time of jaundice, no AKI or myalgia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hantavirus (HFRS)**: fever, AKI and haemorrhage after rodent exposure - rare in India but in the same differential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis with cholangitis, enteric fever and severe influenza or Goodpasture syndrome** for the pulmonary-renal picture - leptospirosis is the commonest infectious pulmonary-renal syndrome in the tropics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**CBC**: **neutrophilic leucocytosis** (often 10,000-30,000/uL) with **thrombocytopenia** in about half; a high ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine**: proteinuria, haematuria, granular casts and pyuria in the first week - an abnormal urine with fever is an early clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal**: raised creatinine with **low or normal potassium** (hypokalaemia in up to 40-45%), raised urea, hyponatraemia; **hyperkalaemia signals oliguric, late AKI**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LFT**: conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, ALP raised, **AST/ALT usually under 200-250 U/L**; **CPK raised** in about half; amylase may be raised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IgM ELISA** turns positive from **day 5-7** and is the practical test in district hospitals (NCDC sentinel labs); a negative test before day 5 does not exclude disease. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Microscopic agglutination test (MAT)** is the **reference serological test**: a **fourfold rise in paired sera**, or a single titre of **1:400 or more in endemic areas** (1:100-1:200 in non-endemic settings), is diagnostic; it needs live cultures and is done in reference labs (RMRC Port Blair, NCDC). [WHO 2003]`,`**PCR of blood (first 7-10 days) and urine (after the first week)** gives an early confirmed diagnosis before antibodies; **culture in EMJH medium** is slow (up to 13 weeks) and insensitive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dark-field microscopy is not recommended** - artefacts (fibrin threads) give false positives. [WHO 2003]`,`In severe disease: **chest X-ray** (patchy or diffuse alveolar shadows), ABG, ECG and troponin (myocarditis), coagulation profile, and a malaria smear in every case. [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Do not wait for serology** - start treatment on clinical and epidemiological suspicion; antibiotics are most effective in the **first 5 days**. [NCDC 2015]`,`Mild disease (adult): **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days** - also covers scrub typhus, the main look-alike. [NCDC 2015]`,`Alternatives: **amoxicillin 500 mg orally three times daily for 7 days** (pregnancy, children under 8 if preferred), or **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 3 days**. [NCDC 2015]`,`Children: **amoxicillin 25-50 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses** or **doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg twice daily** (short courses do not stain teeth), or azithromycin 10 mg/kg once daily. [IAP 2022]`,`Symptomatic care: **paracetamol only** (no NSAIDs - nephrotoxic and bleeding risk), oral fluids, and avoid IM injections. [ICMR 2022]`,`Review in **24-48 hours** with repeat creatinine, potassium, bilirubin and platelets; give written red flags (low urine, cough with blood, yellow eyes, drowsiness). [NCDC 2015]`,`Warn about a **Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** - fever, rigors and hypotension within hours of the first dose - which is usually mild and needs only fluids and antipyretics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for jaundice, creatinine above 1.5 mg/dL or oliguria, hypotension, breathlessness or haemoptysis, bleeding, altered sensorium, persistent vomiting, platelets under 50,000, pregnancy or myocarditis. [NCDC 2015]`,`Severe disease: **benzyl penicillin (crystalline penicillin G) 1.5 million units IV 6-hourly for 7 days**, or **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily for 7 days** (equivalent in trials), or cefotaxime 1 g IV 6-hourly. [Harrison 22e]`,`Where scrub typhus cannot be excluded, **add doxycycline 100 mg IV or orally twice daily** (or azithromycin) to the beta-lactam - beta-lactams do not cover Orientia. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fluids**: correct hypovolaemia cautiously with balanced crystalloid and replace **potassium** in polyuric AKI; avoid overload, which worsens pulmonary haemorrhage. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Dialysis early**: indications are oliguria not responding to fluids, hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload, uraemic encephalopathy or pulmonary haemorrhage; **early and daily dialysis** improved survival in a randomised trial. [Andrade et al, CJASN 2007]`,`**SPHS**: early ICU transfer, **lung-protective ventilation (tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight, plateau under 30 cmH2O, higher PEEP)**, prone position for severe ARDS, platelet and FFP support for bleeding. [SSC 2026]`,`**Corticosteroids (pulse methylprednisolone) and plasma exchange** have been used for pulmonary haemorrhage, but trials are small and conflicting - not routine; consider only in specialist ICUs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shock**: cultures, at least **30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid** guided by dynamic measures, and **noradrenaline for MAP 65 mmHg**; ECG monitoring for myocarditis-related arrhythmias. [SSC 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: leptospirosis can cause **miscarriage, fetal death and congenital infection**; treat with **amoxicillin or IV penicillin/ceftriaxone**; azithromycin is an alternative; avoid doxycycline for treatment courses. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Children**: usually milder, with less jaundice and AKI, but myocarditis, acalculous cholecystitis and pancreatitis are reported; doxycycline is acceptable for short courses at any age. [IAP 2022]`,`**Elderly and diabetics**: higher risk of AKI, myocarditis and death; lower threshold for admission and early nephrology input. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: no dose change for ceftriaxone or doxycycline; **penicillin G dose must be reduced** in severe renal impairment to avoid seizures. [BNF 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: start doxycycline or IV penicillin on clinical grounds, refer with a note of urine output and creatinine; do not delay for MAT or ELISA. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Post-flood clusters**: set up **fever camps**, line-list cases on IHIP, and offer chemoprophylaxis to relief workers and the exposed population. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Acute kidney injury** in up to 40-60% of severe cases; renal recovery is usually complete, though a few have persistent tubular defects. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary haemorrhage / ARDS** is the leading cause of death, with mortality **above 50%** once ventilation is needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myocarditis**: ST-T changes, AF, AV block and cardiogenic shock; ECG abnormalities are common in severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemorrhage and DIC**, thrombocytopenia, gastrointestinal bleeding and **haemolysis** add to the jaundice. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological**: aseptic meningitis (common, benign), encephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and **late uveitis** in up to 2-10% weeks to months later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognostic markers**: age over 40, **oliguria, hyperkalaemia, hypotension, pulmonary infiltrates or haemoptysis, altered sensorium, arrhythmia** and thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Overall case fatality in Indian hospital series ranges from **5% to 20%**, and is highest with combined pulmonary-renal involvement. [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`In hospital: **urine output hourly**, creatinine and potassium **daily** (twice daily in AKI), bilirubin and platelets every 24-48 hours, SpO2 and respiratory rate continuously in the first 72 hours. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Refer** to a centre with **dialysis and ventilation** at the first sign of oliguria, rising creatinine, haemoptysis, SpO2 under 94% or hypotension - SPHS can kill within 24-72 hours. [NCDC 2015]`,`Expect defervescence in **2-4 days**; jaundice can persist for weeks without meaning treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`After discharge, check **creatinine and urine at 2-4 weeks** and ask about **eye pain, redness or blurred vision** for 6 months (late uveitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Send **paired sera 10-14 days apart** for MAT when the first sample is negative or equivocal - a fourfold rise confirms the case for surveillance. [WHO 2003]`,`Notify every suspected and confirmed case on **IHIP**; a cluster triggers a rodent and environmental investigation. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, chemoprophylaxis and national programme`,points:[`**Pre-exposure chemoprophylaxis**: **doxycycline 200 mg orally once a week**, started at the beginning of exposure and continued throughout the period of exposure, for high-risk groups (farmers in paddy season, flood relief workers, sanitation workers). [NCDC 2015]`,`Doxycycline prophylaxis reduces **clinical disease and death** more than it reduces seroconversion; the evidence base is modest (small trials, Cochrane 2009), so it adds to, not replaces, protective measures. [Cochrane 2009]`,`In **pregnancy and young children**, where weekly doxycycline is avoided, **amoxicillin or azithromycin** is used under state protocols; follow the local state guideline for doses. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Personal protection**: gumboots, gloves, waterproof dressings over cuts, avoid wading or swimming in floodwater, and wash and dry feet after exposure. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Environmental**: rodent control, safe disposal of garbage, drainage, and **vaccination of cattle and dogs**; there is **no licensed human vaccine in India**. [NCDC 2015]`,`The NCDC **Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis** supports surveillance, lab strengthening (IgM ELISA at sentinel sites), case-management training and IEC in endemic states, with a One Health approach linking veterinary services. [NCDC 2024]`,`Kerala runs pre-monsoon **'doxycycline campaigns'** for MGNREGS workers, farmers and fishermen, distributing weekly doxycycline through primary health centres and **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs**. [Kerala DHS 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows (2022-23 updates)** place leptospirosis inside the **AUF syndromic algorithm** - empirical **doxycycline** for outpatients after day 3-5 of undiagnosed fever and **ceftriaxone plus doxycycline** for severe febrile illness. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**NCDC flood and monsoon advisories (2023-2025)** reiterate **doxycycline 200 mg weekly** for flood-exposed populations and mandatory reporting of AUF clusters on the **IHIP** digital platform. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** guidance applies to septic leptospirosis: screening with NEWS2/MEWS rather than qSOFA alone, **balanced crystalloids**, dynamic fluid assessment and MAP 65 mmHg. [SSC 2026]`,`Molecular diagnosis (**real-time PCR targeting lipL32**) is increasingly available in Indian VRDL network labs and is preferred over waiting for MAT in the first week. [ICMR VRDL 2024]`,`There is still **no change in first-line antibiotics** - penicillin, ceftriaxone and doxycycline remain equivalent; recent meta-analyses do not support routine steroids in pulmonary haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Weil disease - aetiology, clinical features, diagnosis and management'** - always draw the biphasic curve with the test windows (blood PCR/culture early; IgM, MAT and urine later). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Why is the AKI hypokalaemic and non-oliguric?' - **proximal tubular sodium wasting with increased distal delivery** causes potassium loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Bilirubin 25 mg/dL with ALT 120 - what fever?' - **leptospirosis** (cholestasis plus haemolysis, not necrosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **dismissing a negative IgM on day 3** - it is too early; repeat after day 5-7 or send PCR. [NCDC 2015]`,`Common mistake: giving **NSAIDs or aminoglycosides** in a febrile patient with rising creatinine. [ICMR 2022]`,`Know the **modified Faine's criteria** (WHO SEARO 2009/2012): clinical (A), epidemiological (B) and laboratory (C) scores; **A or A+B of 26 or more, or A+B+C of 25 or more**, is presumptive leptospirosis. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`Know the prophylaxis dose cold: **doxycycline 200 mg once weekly** - not 100 mg daily, which is the treatment dose. [NCDC 2015]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in leptospirosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (mild)`,`100 mg oral BD; child 2.2 mg/kg BD`,`7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis (take upright with water), photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Amoxicillin (mild)`,`500 mg oral TDS; child 25-50 mg/kg/day in 3 doses`,`7 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea; safe in pregnancy; J-H reaction`],[`Azithromycin (mild)`,`500 mg oral OD; child 10 mg/kg OD`,`3 days`,`GI upset, QT prolongation; pregnancy alternative`],[`Benzyl penicillin G (severe)`,`1.5 million units IV 6-hourly`,`7 days`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, anaphylaxis; reduce dose in severe renal failure (seizures)`],[`Ceftriaxone (severe)`,`1-2 g IV OD; child 50-75 mg/kg/day`,`7 days`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; add doxycycline if scrub possible`],[`Cefotaxime (severe)`,`1 g IV 6-hourly`,`7 days`,`As ceftriaxone; useful in neonates`],[`Doxycycline (prophylaxis)`,`200 mg oral once weekly`,`From start of exposure until it ends`,`Take with food and water; not for pregnancy or young children`]]},{heading:`Clinical forms and phases of leptospirosis`,columns:[`Form / phase`,`Timing`,`Features`,`Best test in this window`],rows:[[`Leptospiraemic phase`,`Days 1-7 (up to 10)`,`Fever, rigors, headache, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion`,`Blood PCR, blood culture`],[`Immune phase`,`Day 7 to 30`,`Relapse of fever, aseptic meningitis, uveitis, leptospiruria`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, urine PCR`],[`Anicteric (90%)`,`Biphasic`,`Influenza-like, self-limiting`,`IgM ELISA from day 5`],[`Weil disease (5-10%)`,`Continuous severe illness`,`Jaundice, AKI, bleeding, myocarditis`,`IgM ELISA/MAT; PCR if early`],[`SPHS`,`Often day 4-6`,`Haemoptysis, ARDS, alveolar infiltrates`,`PCR; MAT/ELISA`]]},{heading:`Modified Faine's criteria (WHO SEARO) - key items`,columns:[`Part`,`Item`,`Score`],rows:[[`A - clinical`,`Headache; fever; temperature above 39 C`,`2 each`],[`A - clinical`,`Conjunctival suffusion (bilateral); meningism; muscle pain (calf)`,`4 each`],[`A - clinical`,`Suffusion plus meningism plus myalgia together`,`10`],[`A - clinical`,`Jaundice 1; albuminuria or nitrogen retention 2`,`1-2`],[`B - epidemiological`,`Rainfall 5; contact with contaminated environment 4; animal contact 1`,`1-5`],[`C - laboratory`,`IgM ELISA, SAT or single high MAT titre 15; rising MAT titre or PCR 25; culture isolation`,`15-25; culture = certain`],[`Interpretation`,`A or A+B 26 or more, or A+B+C 25 or more`,`Presumptive leptospirosis`]]},{heading:`Leptospirosis versus look-alikes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Leptospirosis`,`Scrub typhus`,`Falciparum malaria`,`Dengue`,`Viral hepatitis`],rows:[[`Key bedside sign`,`Conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness`,`Eschar, nodes`,`Pallor, splenomegaly`,`Flush, rash, tourniquet test`,`Jaundice after fever settles`],[`WBC`,`Neutrophilic leucocytosis`,`Normal or high`,`Normal or low`,`Leucopenia`,`Normal or low`],[`Bilirubin vs ALT`,`Bilirubin very high, ALT modest`,`Both modest`,`High bilirubin (haemolysis)`,`ALT raised, little jaundice`,`ALT in thousands`],[`Kidney`,`Non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI`,`AKI possible`,`Oliguric AKI`,`Rare`,`Rare`],[`Lung`,`Pulmonary haemorrhage`,`ARDS, pneumonitis`,`ARDS`,`Effusion from leak`,`None`],[`Confirm`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, PCR`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`,`Smear, RDT`,`NS1, IgM`,`IgM HAV/HEV, HBsAg`]]},{heading:`Laboratory cut-offs and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding that supports leptospirosis`,`Caveat`],rows:[[`MAT`,`Fourfold rise in paired sera, or single titre 1:400 or more (endemic)`,`Reference lab only; cross-reacts between serogroups`],[`IgM ELISA`,`Positive from day 5-7`,`False negative early; may persist months`],[`PCR`,`Blood positive days 1-7/10; urine after week 1`,`Turns negative after antibiotics`],[`Serum potassium`,`Low or normal despite AKI`,`Hyperkalaemia means oliguric late AKI - dialysis`],[`Bilirubin and ALT`,`Bilirubin often above 20 mg/dL, ALT under 250 U/L`,`ALT in thousands suggests hepatitis`],[`CPK`,`Raised in about half`,`Also raised in rhabdomyolysis of any cause`]]}],redFlags:[`**Haemoptysis, breathlessness or SpO2 under 94%** - pulmonary haemorrhage; ICU now`,`**Oliguria (under 0.5 mL/kg/h)** or creatinine rising, or **hyperkalaemia** - dialysis centre`,`**Hypotension or shock** (SBP under 90 mmHg) despite fluids`,`**Deep jaundice with bleeding** (epistaxis, melaena, haematuria) or platelets under 50,000`,`**Altered sensorium or seizures**`,`**Irregular pulse, ECG changes or chest pain** - myocarditis`,`**Pregnancy** with suspected leptospirosis`],pearls:[`**Suffusion, calves, kidneys, jaundice** - think rat urine.`,`Leptospirosis **AKI is non-oliguric and hypokalaemic**; hyperkalaemia means late, severe disease.`,`**Bilirubin out of proportion to transaminases** - leptospirosis or falciparum, not hepatitis.`,`**Blood PCR in week 1, urine PCR and serology after week 1** - the test follows the phase.`,`**MAT is the reference test**: fourfold rise, or single titre 1:400 or more in endemic areas.`,`Treat on suspicion: **doxycycline for mild, penicillin G or ceftriaxone for severe** - equally effective.`,`**Prophylaxis is doxycycline 200 mg once weekly** throughout exposure.`,`Death comes from the **lung** (SPHS) more than the kidney now - watch respiratory rate and SpO2.`,`Leptospirosis is the commonest **infectious pulmonary-renal syndrome** in the tropics.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q1`,stem:`A 35-year-old sewage worker from Mumbai presents on day 6 of fever with calf pain, deep jaundice and reduced urine. Creatinine 3.2 mg/dL, potassium 3.1 mmol/L, bilirubin 18 mg/dL, ALT 110 U/L, WBC 18,000/uL. Which treatment is most appropriate?`,options:[`Oral doxycycline 200 mg once weekly`,`IV benzyl penicillin 1.5 million units 6-hourly for 7 days`,`IV gentamicin with metronidazole`,`Oral cefixime 200 mg twice daily for 14 days`],answer:1,explanation:`This is Weil disease (jaundice, AKI with hypokalaemia, leucocytosis, bilirubin far above ALT) and needs IV therapy: benzyl penicillin 1.5 MU 6-hourly or ceftriaxone for 7 days. Weekly doxycycline 200 mg is the prophylaxis dose, not treatment. Gentamicin is nephrotoxic and has no place in leptospiral AKI. Oral cefixime is an enteric-fever regimen and is inadequate for severe leptospirosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q2`,stem:`A farmer with suspected leptospirosis is seen on day 3 of illness. Which test is most likely to confirm the diagnosis at this point?`,options:[`Leptospira IgM ELISA`,`Microscopic agglutination test on a single sample`,`Real-time PCR on blood`,`Dark-field microscopy of urine`],answer:2,explanation:`In the leptospiraemic phase (first 7-10 days) organisms are in blood, so blood PCR is the test of choice on day 3. IgM ELISA turns positive only from day 5-7, and MAT needs antibodies (ideally paired sera), so both may be negative this early. Dark-field microscopy is unreliable because fibrin threads mimic spirochaetes, and urine shedding starts in the immune phase.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q3`,stem:`What is the recommended chemoprophylaxis for an adult flood-relief volunteer in Kerala?`,options:[`Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily throughout exposure`,`Azithromycin 1 g single dose after exposure`,`Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 7 days`,`Doxycycline 200 mg once weekly from the start of exposure until it ends`],answer:3,explanation:`Indian national guidelines advise doxycycline 200 mg once a week, starting at the beginning of exposure and continuing throughout it, for high-risk groups such as flood-relief workers. Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily is the treatment dose for mild disease, not prophylaxis. Amoxicillin for 7 days is a treatment course, and a single post-exposure azithromycin dose is not the standard regimen.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q4`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains the hypokalaemia seen in leptospiral acute kidney injury?`,options:[`Impaired proximal tubular sodium reabsorption increasing distal sodium delivery and potassium secretion`,`Hyperaldosteronism from adrenal invasion`,`Gastrointestinal potassium loss from diarrhoea alone`,`Intracellular shift from insulin release`],answer:0,explanation:`Leptospiral outer-membrane proteins injure the proximal tubule, so sodium reabsorption fails. More sodium reaches the distal nephron, where it is exchanged for potassium, giving non-oliguric, potassium-wasting AKI. Adrenal invasion is not a feature. Diarrhoea can add to the loss but does not explain the renal wasting, and an insulin-driven shift plays no part.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q5`,stem:`A patient with leptospirosis develops haemoptysis and SpO2 of 86% on room air on day 5. Chest X-ray shows bilateral alveolar shadows. What is the most important next step?`,options:[`Switch the antibiotic to meropenem`,`Transfer to ICU for lung-protective ventilation and organ support`,`Give oral prednisolone and continue ward care`,`Transfuse platelets to a target above 1 lakh and observe`],answer:1,explanation:`This is severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome, the leading cause of death in leptospirosis, and needs immediate ICU care with lung-protective ventilation (6 mL/kg), PEEP and dialysis if needed. Leptospires stay sensitive to penicillin and ceftriaxone, so switching to meropenem adds nothing. Steroids are unproven and not routine. Platelets help bleeding but do not treat the alveolar capillary injury, and observing on the ward is unsafe.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the epidemiology, pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of leptospirosis. Add a note on Weil disease and chemoprophylaxis.`,openingLines:[`Leptospirosis is a zoonotic spirochaetal infection by pathogenic Leptospira, acquired through abraded skin or mucosa from water or soil contaminated with the urine of rats and other animals.`,`It is usually a self-limiting biphasic fever, but 5-10% develop Weil disease (jaundice, AKI, haemorrhage) or pulmonary haemorrhage, with high mortality.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`About 1 million cases and 59,000 deaths a year worldwide.`,`India: Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Andaman; post-monsoon and post-flood peaks.`,`Reservoir rats (renal carriage); cattle, pigs, dogs; occupational - farmers, sewage workers, MGNREGS.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Entry through skin and mucosa, leptospiraemia, systemic vasculitis with capillary leak.`,`Kidney: tubulo-interstitial nephritis, proximal Na wasting, non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI.`,`Liver: cholestasis, junction disruption - high bilirubin, modest ALT; lung: alveolar haemorrhage.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 5-14 days; leptospiraemic phase (fever, headache, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion).`,`Immune phase: aseptic meningitis, uveitis.`,`Weil disease: jaundice, AKI, bleeding, myocarditis; SPHS: haemoptysis, ARDS.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`CBC neutrophilia, thrombocytopenia; urine casts; low K with raised creatinine; high CPK.`,`Blood PCR (week 1), IgM ELISA from day 5-7, MAT fourfold rise or 1:400 or more; urine PCR later.`,`Modified Faine's criteria for presumptive diagnosis.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Mild: doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days, or amoxicillin, or azithromycin.`,`Severe: penicillin G 1.5 MU IV 6-hourly or ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV OD x 7 days; add doxycycline if scrub possible.`,`Supportive: K replacement, early daily dialysis, lung-protective ventilation for SPHS; no NSAIDs.`]},{heading:`Prevention and chemoprophylaxis`,points:[`Doxycycline 200 mg once weekly throughout exposure for high-risk groups.`,`Boots, gloves, wound cover, rodent control, animal vaccination; no human vaccine in India.`,`Notify on IHIP; NCDC Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Biphasic course with fever curve, leptospiraemia, antibodies and the test windows`,`Table of mild versus severe treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and transmission`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and Weil disease`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Chemoprophylaxis and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Weil disease`,`MAT`,`IgM ELISA`,`doxycycline 200 mg weekly`,`penicillin G`,`SPHS`,`hypokalaemic AKI`]},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on pulmonary haemorrhage in leptospirosis.`,openingLines:[`Severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (SPHS) is alveolar haemorrhage from leptospiral injury of alveolar capillaries, often presenting with haemoptysis and ARDS around day 4-6.`,`It is now the leading cause of death in leptospirosis, with mortality above 50% once ventilation is required.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Endothelial damage with linear IgG and complement on the alveolar basement membrane.`,`Thrombocytopenia contributes but does not fully explain the bleeding.`]},{heading:`Recognition`,points:[`Cough, haemoptysis, tachypnoea, falling SpO2, bilateral alveolar infiltrates.`,`May occur without jaundice; often with AKI (pulmonary-renal syndrome).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`ICU, lung-protective ventilation 6 mL/kg, PEEP, prone position; avoid fluid overload.`,`IV penicillin G or ceftriaxone; early dialysis; platelet and FFP support.`,`Steroids and plasma exchange unproven - not routine.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Poor markers: oliguria, hyperkalaemia, hypotension, altered sensorium; mortality over 50% if ventilated.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: haemoptysis in febrile patient - SpO2, CXR - ICU - ventilation and dialysis`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical recognition`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Prognosis`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`SPHS`,`alveolar haemorrhage`,`ARDS`,`lung-protective ventilation`,`pulmonary-renal syndrome`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Leptospirosis chapter`,`National Guidelines: Diagnosis, Case Management, Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis, NCDC, MoHFW, 2015`,`WHO/ILS. Human leptospirosis: guidance for diagnosis, surveillance and control, 2003`,`WHO SEARO. Informal expert consultation on surveillance, diagnosis and risk reduction of leptospirosis (modified Faine's criteria), 2009`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India - Acute Undifferentiated Fever, 2022-2023`,`Costa F et al. Global morbidity and mortality of leptospirosis: a systematic review. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2015`,`Andrade L et al. Daily versus alternate-day haemodialysis in leptospirosis-associated AKI. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol 2007`,`Surviving Sepsis Campaign: International Guidelines for Management of Sepsis and Septic Shock, 2026`]}),r.push({id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral`,title:`Chikungunya, Zika and other arboviral fevers`,oneLiner:`**Arboviral fevers** are arthropod-borne viral infections; in India the ones beyond dengue and JE are **chikungunya** (Aedes, alphavirus - fever with **disabling polyarthritis** that becomes **chronic in a third**), **Zika** (Aedes flavivirus - mild rash illness that causes **congenital Zika syndrome** in pregnancy), tick-borne **Kyasanur Forest disease** and **CCHF**, Culex-borne **West Nile**, and the bat-borne zoonosis **Nipah** in Kerala - all managed supportively with **paracetamol, no NSAIDs until dengue is excluded**, and controlled by vector and infection-prevention measures.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`chikungunya`,`CHIKV`,`chronic chikungunya arthritis`,`Zika`,`congenital Zika syndrome`,`microcephaly`,`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`KFD`,`monkey fever`,`CCHF`,`Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever`,`West Nile virus`,`Nipah`,`Chandipura`,`Aedes albopictus`,`Haemaphysalis spinigera`,`Hyalomma`,`hydroxychloroquine`,`methotrexate`,`ribavirin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Arboviruses** are viruses maintained in nature by **blood-feeding arthropods** (mosquitoes, ticks, sandflies); the human syndromes are **fever-arthralgia-rash, haemorrhagic fever and encephalitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chikungunya** (Makonde for 'that which bends up') re-emerged in India in **2005-06** after 32 years, with about **1.4 million suspected cases in 2006**, driven by the **E1-A226V mutation** that adapted the virus to **Aedes albopictus**. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`India now reports chikungunya every year under **NCVBDC**, with the largest numbers from **Karnataka, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Delhi**, mostly July-November with dengue. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Zika** was first confirmed in India in **Ahmedabad (2016-17)**, then caused outbreaks in **Jaipur (2018)**, **Kerala (2021)** and **Maharashtra and Karnataka (2023-24)**, including clusters among pregnant women in Pune in 2024. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**Kyasanur Forest disease (KFD, 'monkey fever')** was discovered in **Shimoga, Karnataka in 1957** and has spread along the Western Ghats to **Kerala (Wayanad), Goa, Maharashtra (Sindhudurg) and Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris)**, with about 400-500 cases a year and a case fatality of 3-10%. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever (CCHF)** was first documented in India in **Gujarat in 2011** as a **nosocomial cluster**, with later cases in Gujarat and Rajasthan; case fatality is **10-40%**. [NCDC 2019]`,`**West Nile virus** circulates in south India; human encephalitis cases and deaths have been reported from **Kerala (Alappuzha, Malappuram, Kozhikode)** repeatedly since 2011, including 2024. [Kerala DHS 2024]`,`**Nipah virus** - a bat-borne paramyxovirus, not an arbovirus but in the same fever-encephalitis differential - caused outbreaks in **West Bengal (2001, 2007)** and **Kerala (2018, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2024, 2025)** with case fatality of **40-75%** (see the Emerging and epidemic infections topic). [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, vectors and risk factors`,points:[`**Chikungunya virus** is an **alphavirus (Togaviridae)**; vectors are **Aedes aegypti** (urban, indoor) and **Aedes albopictus** (peri-urban, outdoor), both **day-biting**, breeding in stored clean water. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zika virus** is a **flavivirus**; spread by Aedes, and also **sexually, vertically (transplacental), by transfusion and organ transplant** - the sexual route is unique among common arboviruses. [WHO 2025]`,`**KFD virus** is a tick-borne **flavivirus** spread by **Haemaphysalis spinigera** nymphs; **langurs and bonnet macaques** are amplifying hosts, and **monkey deaths in the forest herald human cases**. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`KFD risk groups are **forest workers, cashew and areca nut farmers, firewood and dry-leaf collectors and cattle grazers**; the season is **November to June**, peaking January-April. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF virus** is a **nairovirus (Bunyavirales)** spread by **Hyalomma ticks**, by **slaughtering viraemic livestock** and **person-to-person through blood and body fluids** - so health-care workers are at high risk. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile virus** is a flavivirus maintained in **birds and Culex mosquitoes**; humans and horses are dead-end hosts; risk is highest in the **elderly, diabetics and immunosuppressed**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah** spreads from **Pteropus fruit bats** through bat-contaminated fruit or raw **date-palm sap**, via pigs (Malaysia 1998), and **person to person** through respiratory secretions in hospitals and families. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chandipura virus** (a sandfly-borne vesiculovirus) caused a large outbreak of **acute encephalitis with high mortality in children in Gujarat in 2024** - remember it in paediatric AES. [WHO DON 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Chikungunya** replicates in **fibroblasts, synovial macrophages, muscle satellite cells and tendons**, causing very high viraemia (up to 10^9 copies/mL) and intense type I interferon and IL-6 responses - hence the abrupt fever and severe joint pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic chikungunya arthritis** results from **persistent viral RNA and antigen in synovial macrophages** driving a chronic inflammatory synovitis (IL-6, GM-CSF, Th1), which can become **erosive and RA-like**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zika** is **neurotropic** for **neural progenitor cells**: infection of the fetal brain causes apoptosis and failed cortical development - **microcephaly, subcortical calcifications and ventriculomegaly**; risk is highest with first-trimester infection. [WHO 2025]`,`**Zika-associated Guillain-Barre syndrome** is post-infectious and immune-mediated (often AMAN or AIDP pattern) and appears within days of the rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**KFD and CCHF** cause **endothelial and hepatocyte injury with platelet dysfunction and DIC**, producing a **viral haemorrhagic fever**; KFD then has an immune-mediated **second neurological phase** in some patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**West Nile virus** crosses the blood-brain barrier and infects **neurons of the brainstem, basal ganglia and anterior horn cells**, explaining encephalitis, tremor and **asymmetric acute flaccid paralysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah** causes **vasculitis with endothelial syncytia** in brain and lung, giving encephalitis and ARDS; relapsing and late-onset encephalitis can occur months later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why no NSAIDs early**: chikungunya and Zika co-circulate with **dengue**, where NSAIDs and aspirin increase bleeding; paracetamol is used until dengue is excluded. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**By syndrome**: fever-arthralgia-rash (**chikungunya, Zika, dengue**), viral haemorrhagic fever (**KFD, CCHF, dengue, yellow fever**), and encephalitis (**JE, West Nile, Chandipura, Nipah, KFD late phase**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By vector**: Aedes (dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever), Culex (JE, West Nile), ticks (KFD, CCHF), sandflies (Chandipura). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chikungunya phases**: **acute (up to 3 weeks)**, **post-acute (3 weeks to 3 months)** and **chronic (over 3 months)** - each with different treatment. [French SPILF 2014; NCVBDC 2016]`,`Chronic chikungunya is further split into **inflammatory arthritis** (RA-like, spondyloarthritis-like, undifferentiated polyarthritis) and **non-inflammatory musculoskeletal pain** (tendinitis, carpal tunnel, fibromyalgia-like). [French SPILF 2014]`,`**Atypical/severe chikungunya** (WHO): neurological (encephalitis, GBS), cardiac (myocarditis), renal, hepatic, bullous skin disease, and **neonatal chikungunya** - these patients need admission. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**Biosafety level**: CCHF and Nipah are **risk group 4 / BSL-3-4 pathogens** - samples go only to **NIV Pune** (or designated BSL-3 labs such as NIV Kerala unit) with triple packaging. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Chikungunya**: incubation **3-7 days**; abrupt high fever with **severe symmetrical polyarthralgia** of wrists, hands, ankles and feet - the patient is **bent and immobile**; over 90% of infected people are symptomatic. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chikungunya also gives **maculopapular rash (40-50%)**, headache, myalgia, and in some **aphthous ulcers and facial oedema**; fever lasts 3-5 days, but joint pain persists. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`**Zika**: about **80% are asymptomatic**; the rest have **low-grade fever, pruritic maculopapular rash, non-purulent conjunctivitis and arthralgia** for 2-7 days. [WHO 2025]`,`**KFD**: incubation 3-8 days, then **sudden fever, headache, severe myalgia and prostration**, with bleeding from gums, nose and gut; **10-20% get a second phase** after 1-2 weeks afebrile with meningoencephalitis. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF**: after a **tick bite (1-3 days) or blood exposure (5-6 days)**, abrupt fever, myalgia, dizziness, neck pain, photophobia and vomiting, followed on day 3-5 by **haemorrhage - large ecchymoses**, epistaxis, haematemesis. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile**: most are asymptomatic; about 20% get **West Nile fever**; under 1% get neuroinvasive disease - **encephalitis, meningitis or polio-like flaccid paralysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: fever, headache and drowsiness progressing over days to **encephalitis and coma**, often with **cough and breathlessness**; ask about **fruit bats, half-eaten fruit, date-palm sap and contact with a sick person**. [NCDC 2024]`,`In every arboviral history ask for **travel or residence** (Western Ghats forest - KFD; Gujarat/Rajasthan livestock - CCHF; Kerala - Nipah, West Nile), **pregnancy** and **sexual partners' travel** (Zika). [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Chikungunya: **swollen, tender small joints** with **tenosynovitis**; joint effusions are common; pain prevents a firm grip. [Harrison 22e]`,`**'Chik sign'**: **brownish hyperpigmentation of the nose and face** appearing in or after the acute illness is a useful bedside clue in India. [Indian J Dermatol 2016]`,`Zika: fine **pruritic maculopapular rash** spreading from face to limbs, with **conjunctival hyperaemia without discharge**; in a newborn look for **head circumference below the 3rd centile**. [WHO 2025]`,`KFD: **conjunctival suffusion, papulovesicular lesions on the soft palate, cervical lymphadenopathy**, bradycardia and hypotension; bleeding signs. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`CCHF: **petechiae progressing to large ecchymoses and haematomas** at injection sites, hepatomegaly; shock and bleeding indicate poor prognosis. [WHO 2023]`,`West Nile and Nipah: **altered sensorium, tremor, myoclonus, cranial nerve palsies**, flaccid weakness with **preserved sensation** (West Nile), or **segmental myoclonus, areflexia, hypertension and tachycardia** (Nipah brainstem involvement). [Harrison 22e]`,`Check every febrile patient for **pulse pressure, capillary refill and tourniquet test** - dengue shock must be excluded first. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Dengue**: retro-orbital pain, leucopenia, **rising haematocrit and thrombocytopenia**, critical phase at defervescence; joint pain is milder than chikungunya. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chikungunya**: arthritis dominates, **lymphopenia**, platelets usually normal or mildly low; bleeding and shock are rare. [WHO 2025]`,`**Zika**: mildest of the three, with **conjunctivitis and itch** - and the important consequence is in pregnancy. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chronic chikungunya arthritis versus rheumatoid arthritis**: history of an epidemic febrile onset, positive CHIKV IgG, and usually **RF and anti-CCP negative**. [French SPILF 2014]`,`**Other fever-with-rash**: measles, rubella, parvovirus B19, rickettsial spotted fever, drug rash and acute HIV (see the Approach to fever with rash topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemorrhagic fevers**: dengue, falciparum malaria, leptospirosis, meningococcaemia and severe scrub typhus are far more common than KFD or CCHF and must be tested for. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Encephalitis**: JE, HSV, bacterial meningitis, cerebral malaria, scrub typhus and Chandipura; **seasonal and geographic clustering** points to the virus. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`First line in all: **CBC (lymphopenia in chikungunya; thrombocytopenia and leucopenia in KFD/CCHF), dengue NS1/IgM, malaria RDT/smear**, LFT, creatinine. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Chikungunya RT-PCR** is positive in the **first 5-7 days** (high viraemia); **IgM ELISA (NIV kit)** from **day 5-7**, persisting 2-3 months; IgG confirms past infection in chronic arthritis. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`**Zika RT-PCR**: serum up to **7 days** and **urine up to 14 days or longer**; **IgM cross-reacts with dengue**, so PRNT is needed for serological confirmation. [WHO 2025]`,`Zika in pregnancy: **serial ultrasound every 3-4 weeks** for microcephaly, calcifications and ventriculomegaly; **amniotic fluid RT-PCR** can be considered after 15 weeks. [CDC 2024]`,`**KFD**: RT-PCR of blood in the first week, IgM ELISA after; samples to **NIV Pune or the Shimoga VRDL**. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF**: RT-PCR of blood in a **BSL-3/4 lab (NIV Pune)**; coagulation profile, platelets, AST/ALT; samples packed and transported under strict biosafety. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile**: **IgM in CSF** is diagnostic of neuroinvasive disease; CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis; MRI may show thalamic and basal ganglia lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: **RT-PCR of throat swab, urine, CSF and blood** at NIV Pune or a designated state BSL-3 lab (Kozhikode); CSF and MRI (multiple small hyperintensities) support. [NCDC 2024]`,`In chronic chikungunya, check **ESR/CRP, RF, anti-CCP, HLA-B27 where relevant, and X-rays** before DMARDs, and screen for **hepatitis B and C** before methotrexate. [French SPILF 2014]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`There is **no specific antiviral** for chikungunya, Zika, KFD or West Nile; care is supportive. [WHO 2025]`,`Acute chikungunya: rest, oral fluids, **paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly (max 4 g/day)**, cold compresses; **no NSAIDs or aspirin until dengue is excluded** (usually after day 5-7). [WHO 2025]`,`Once dengue is excluded, add an NSAID such as **naproxen 250-500 mg twice daily** or ibuprofen for 1-2 weeks, with a PPI in older adults; avoid in CKD and pregnancy after 20 weeks. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`**Avoid corticosteroids in the acute phase** - they give rebound arthritis and may worsen viraemia. [WHO 2025]`,`Post-acute (3 weeks to 3 months): NSAIDs; if inadequate, a short course of **prednisolone 10 mg/day for 5 days, tapered over about 10 days**; physiotherapy and joint exercises. [French SPILF 2014]`,`Chronic inflammatory arthritis (over 3 months): **methotrexate 7.5-25 mg once weekly with folic acid**, or **hydroxychloroquine 200-400 mg/day** and **sulfasalazine 1-3 g/day** in combination - manage as RA with a rheumatologist. [French SPILF 2014]`,`Zika: rest, fluids, paracetamol and antihistamine for itch; **counsel pregnant women and couples** and refer for antenatal ultrasound. [WHO 2025]`,`Give **mosquito-bite protection during the viraemic first week** (bed net, repellent, long sleeves) so the patient does not infect household Aedes. [NCVBDC 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit chikungunya** with **atypical or severe features** (encephalitis, myocarditis, bullous lesions, hepatitis, AKI), in **neonates, the elderly over 65 with comorbidity**, and with dehydration or unbearable pain. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**KFD**: supportive ICU care - fluids, **platelet and FFP replacement** for bleeding, management of shock and encephalitis; no antiviral is proven. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF**: isolate in a single room with **contact and droplet precautions plus airborne precautions for aerosol-generating procedures**; full PPE (gown, gloves, N95, eye shield); notify immediately. [WHO 2023]`,`CCHF supportive care: blood products, fluids, organ support; **ribavirin** (oral or IV, WHO regimen **30 mg/kg loading, then 15 mg/kg 6-hourly for 4 days, then 7.5 mg/kg 8-hourly for 6 days**) is used in India but evidence is weak and **favipiravir** is under study. [WHO 2023]`,`**Needlestick exposure to CCHF**: monitor temperature for 14 days; post-exposure oral ribavirin is used in some protocols. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile neuroinvasive disease**: supportive - airway, seizure control, raised ICP management, ventilation for flaccid respiratory paralysis; IVIG and interferon are not proven. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: isolate with **airborne, droplet and contact precautions**, supportive ICU care; the **m102.4 monoclonal antibody** and **remdesivir** are used only under ICMR compassionate or trial protocols. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy and Zika**: infection at any trimester can cause **congenital Zika syndrome** (microcephaly, calcifications, chorioretinal atrophy, arthrogryposis, hypertonia), highest risk in the **first trimester**; about **5-10% of infected pregnancies** have Zika-associated defects. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pregnancy and chikungunya**: no teratogenicity, but **intrapartum viraemia transmits to about half of neonates**, causing **neonatal chikungunya** (fever, poor feeding, rash, encephalopathy, myocarditis) in days 3-7 of life; **caesarean section does not prevent** transmission. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`Neonates of mothers febrile with chikungunya at delivery need **7 days of observation** in hospital. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**Breastfeeding** is advised to continue in chikungunya and Zika - benefits outweigh the theoretical risk. [WHO 2025]`,`**Elderly and comorbid**: higher risk of atypical chikungunya, decompensation of diabetes, heart failure and CKD, and of **chronic arthritis**; avoid NSAIDs in CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Methotrexate in women of child-bearing age** needs reliable contraception; stop 3 months before planned conception. [ACR 2020]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: syndromic diagnosis, paracetamol, early referral of bleeding or encephalopathy, and line-listing on **IHIP** - lab confirmation through the nearest **VRDL or sentinel surveillance hospital**. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Chronic chikungunya arthritis** affects about **30-40%** of patients at 3 months and a smaller proportion beyond a year; risk factors are **age over 45, severe acute arthritis, high viral load and pre-existing joint disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chikungunya mortality is low (about **0.1%**), mainly in the elderly and neonates; atypical forms include **encephalitis, GBS, myocarditis and bullous dermatosis**. [WHO 2025]`,`Zika complications: **congenital Zika syndrome** and **Guillain-Barre syndrome** (about 2-3 per 10,000 infections). [WHO 2025]`,`KFD case fatality is **3-10%**; survivors may have prolonged weakness; neurological phase adds morbidity. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`CCHF case fatality is **10-40%**, with death usually in the second week from **haemorrhage, DIC and shock**. [WHO 2023]`,`West Nile neuroinvasive disease has a **case fatality of about 10%** (higher in the elderly), and survivors of flaccid paralysis often have permanent weakness. [Harrison 22e]`,`Nipah has a case fatality of **40-75%**; about 20% of survivors have **residual neurological deficits** and relapse can occur. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Review chikungunya at **3-4 weeks** and at **3 months**: persistent inflammatory arthritis is referred to a **rheumatologist** for DMARDs. [French SPILF 2014]`,`On methotrexate: **CBC, LFT and creatinine every 4-8 weeks** at start, then every 3 months. [ACR 2020]`,`Zika in pregnancy: obstetric referral for **serial ultrasound**; newborns get **head circumference, eye examination, hearing screen and neurodevelopmental follow-up**. [WHO 2025]`,`KFD and CCHF contacts: **daily temperature check for 14 days** (CCHF contacts) and referral at first fever. [WHO 2023]`,`Nipah contacts are line-listed and **quarantined and monitored for 21 days** from last exposure (state protocols). [NCDC 2024]`,`**Notify** chikungunya, Zika, KFD, CCHF, West Nile, AES and Nipah immediately on **IHIP**; a single case of CCHF or Nipah triggers a rapid response team. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Aedes control** under **NCVBDC**: source reduction, **weekly 'dry day'**, covering water containers, larvivorous fish, temephos in containers and fogging only in outbreaks. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Personal protection**: repellents (DEET, picaridin), long sleeves, **bed nets for day sleepers (infants, the sick, pregnant women)**, screens. [WHO 2025]`,`**Zika sexual transmission**: use condoms or abstain for **3 months for men and 2 months for women** after symptoms or possible exposure; pregnant women should avoid travel to outbreak areas. [WHO 2022]`,`**KFD vaccine**: formalin-inactivated tissue-culture vaccine for people aged 7-65 in affected villages - **2 doses 1 month apart, booster at 6-9 months, then every 5 years**; efficacy has been suboptimal and a new vaccine is under development by ICMR. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`KFD protection: **DMP (dimethyl phthalate) oil repellent**, full clothing when entering forests, and reporting monkey deaths to health and forest officials. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF prevention**: acaricide treatment of livestock, gloves during slaughter, and strict **standard and contact precautions** in hospitals. [WHO 2023]`,`**Nipah prevention**: avoid **half-eaten fruit and raw date-palm sap**, wash fruit, and strict infection-control in hospitals; contacts traced through the One Health approach. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Chikungunya vaccines**: live-attenuated **VLA1553 (Ixchiq)** and virus-like-particle **Vimkunya** have been licensed abroad for travellers; neither is in the Indian programme. [FDA 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases (dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever), July 2025** - the first unified guideline: paracetamol for pain and fever, **no NSAIDs until dengue is excluded**, **no corticosteroids in the acute phase**, and crystalloid-based fluid care. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chikungunya vaccines**: FDA approved **Ixchiq (VLA1553) in November 2023** and **Vimkunya (VLP) in February 2025**; after serious adverse events in older adults, the **FDA suspended the Ixchiq licence in August 2025** and regulators restricted its use in people aged 65 and over. [FDA 2025]`,`**Zika in India 2023-24**: clusters in Maharashtra and Karnataka prompted MoHFW advisories (2024) for **screening pregnant women in affected areas for Zika and fetal anomaly surveillance**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Nipah 2023-2025 in Kerala**: rapid point-of-care RT-PCR at the **Kozhikode state lab** and early monoclonal antibody use; outbreaks were contained with contact tracing and quarantine. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Chandipura AES outbreak, Gujarat 2024** - WHO Disease Outbreak News; sandfly control and early referral of encephalitic children. [WHO DON 2024]`,`**KFD**: ICMR began development and trials of a **new-generation KFD vaccine (2024)** to replace the old formalin-inactivated vaccine. [ICMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Chronic chikungunya arthritis'** - define the three phases (acute up to 3 weeks, post-acute to 3 months, chronic beyond 3 months) and the step-up therapy. [French SPILF 2014]`,`Viva: **'Why not ibuprofen for this arthralgic fever on day 2?'** - dengue has not been excluded; NSAIDs and aspirin raise the bleeding risk. [WHO 2025]`,`Viva: **'Which arbovirus spreads sexually?'** - **Zika**; counsel condoms for 3 months (men) and 2 months (women). [WHO 2022]`,`Viva: **'Monkey deaths in a Shimoga forest followed by fever with bleeding'** - **Kyasanur Forest disease**, Haemaphysalis tick. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`Viva: **'Which viral haemorrhagic fever in India spreads in hospitals?'** - **CCHF** (Gujarat 2011, nosocomial); Nipah also spreads person to person. [WHO 2023]`,`Common mistake: **starting steroids for acute chikungunya** joint pain - it causes rebound and is discouraged; steroids are for the post-acute phase only. [WHO 2025]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing Zika on **IgM alone** in a dengue-endemic area - cross-reactivity requires RT-PCR or PRNT. [WHO 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and vaccines in chikungunya and other arboviral fevers`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day; child 15 mg/kg`,`Acute phase, as needed`,`Hepatotoxicity; first choice until dengue excluded`],[`Naproxen (or ibuprofen)`,`250-500 mg oral BD (ibuprofen 400 mg TDS)`,`1-2 weeks after dengue excluded; post-acute phase`,`GI bleed, AKI; avoid in CKD, pregnancy after 20 weeks, suspected dengue`],[`Prednisolone (post-acute chikungunya)`,`10 mg oral OD`,`5 days, then taper over about 10 days`,`Rebound arthritis if used acutely; hyperglycaemia`],[`Methotrexate (chronic chikungunya arthritis)`,`7.5-25 mg oral once weekly + folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`At least 3-6 months, titrate to response`,`Hepatotoxicity, cytopenia, teratogenic; check CBC/LFT 4-8 weekly`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`200-400 mg oral OD (max 5 mg/kg/day)`,`3-6 months or longer, usually in combination`,`Retinopathy - baseline and annual eye check after 5 years`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`500 mg OD titrated to 1 g BD`,`3-6 months or longer`,`GI upset, rash, cytopenia; avoid in sulfa allergy`],[`Ribavirin (CCHF)`,`30 mg/kg IV/oral loading, then 15 mg/kg 6-hourly x 4 days, then 7.5 mg/kg 8-hourly x 6 days`,`10 days`,`Haemolytic anaemia, teratogenic; evidence weak`],[`KFD vaccine (inactivated)`,`Subcutaneous, 2 doses 1 month apart`,`Booster at 6-9 months, then 5-yearly`,`Local reactions; modest efficacy; ages 7-65 in endemic villages`]]},{heading:`Indian arboviral and allied viral fevers at a glance`,columns:[`Virus`,`Vector / reservoir`,`Indian distribution`,`Hallmark`,`Case fatality`],rows:[[`Chikungunya (alphavirus)`,`Aedes aegypti, A. albopictus`,`Nationwide, urban and peri-urban`,`Disabling polyarthritis, chronic in a third`,`About 0.1%`],[`Zika (flavivirus)`,`Aedes; sexual, vertical`,`Gujarat, Rajasthan, Kerala, Maharashtra, Karnataka`,`Mild rash, conjunctivitis; congenital Zika syndrome`,`Very low`],[`KFD (flavivirus)`,`Haemaphysalis tick; monkeys`,`Western Ghats - Karnataka, Kerala, Goa, Maharashtra, TN`,`Haemorrhagic fever then neurological phase`,`3-10%`],[`CCHF (nairovirus)`,`Hyalomma tick; livestock; nosocomial`,`Gujarat, Rajasthan`,`Ecchymoses, DIC`,`10-40%`],[`West Nile (flavivirus)`,`Culex; birds`,`Kerala, south India`,`Encephalitis, flaccid paralysis in elderly`,`About 10% of neuroinvasive`],[`Nipah (paramyxovirus)`,`Pteropus bats; person to person`,`Kerala, West Bengal`,`Encephalitis with respiratory illness`,`40-75%`],[`Chandipura (vesiculovirus)`,`Sandfly`,`Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh`,`Rapid encephalitis in children`,`High in children`]]},{heading:`Dengue versus chikungunya versus Zika`,columns:[`Feature`,`Dengue`,`Chikungunya`,`Zika`],rows:[[`Fever`,`High, 2-7 days`,`High, 3-5 days`,`Low-grade or none`],[`Joints`,`Myalgia, mild arthralgia`,`Severe polyarthritis, tenosynovitis`,`Mild arthralgia`],[`Rash`,`Flush, then convalescent 'islands of white'`,`Maculopapular, nasal hyperpigmentation`,`Pruritic maculopapular`],[`Eyes`,`Retro-orbital pain`,`Occasionally conjunctivitis`,`Non-purulent conjunctivitis`],[`Blood counts`,`Leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, rising haematocrit`,`Lymphopenia, platelets near normal`,`Usually normal`],[`Danger`,`Plasma leak and shock`,`Chronic arthritis, neonatal disease`,`Congenital Zika syndrome, GBS`]]},{heading:`Chikungunya phases and treatment`,columns:[`Phase`,`Duration`,`Clinical picture`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute`,`Up to 3 weeks`,`Fever, polyarthritis, rash`,`Rest, fluids, paracetamol; NSAID only after dengue excluded; no steroids`],[`Post-acute`,`3 weeks to 3 months`,`Persistent arthritis, tenosynovitis`,`NSAIDs; short prednisolone 10 mg taper; physiotherapy`],[`Chronic inflammatory`,`Over 3 months`,`RA-like or SpA-like arthritis`,`Methotrexate weekly; HCQ, sulfasalazine; rheumatology`],[`Chronic non-inflammatory`,`Over 3 months`,`Tendinitis, carpal tunnel, diffuse pain`,`Analgesics, physiotherapy, local injections`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic test windows`,columns:[`Virus`,`Early test (days 1-7)`,`Late test`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Chikungunya`,`RT-PCR serum`,`IgM ELISA from day 5-7`,`IgG only shows past infection`],[`Zika`,`RT-PCR serum (7 days), urine (14 days+)`,`IgM, PRNT`,`IgM cross-reacts with dengue`],[`KFD`,`RT-PCR blood`,`IgM ELISA`,`Only NIV/VRDL labs`],[`CCHF`,`RT-PCR blood (BSL-3/4)`,`IgM/IgG ELISA`,`Biohazard - strict packaging`],[`West Nile`,`RT-PCR (low yield)`,`IgM in CSF`,`Cross-reacts with JE`],[`Nipah`,`RT-PCR throat swab, urine, CSF, blood`,`IgM/IgG ELISA`,`BSL-3/4 only`]]}],redFlags:[`**Bleeding, ecchymoses or shock** in a febrile patient from a forest (KFD) or livestock area (CCHF)`,`**Altered sensorium, seizures or flaccid weakness** - encephalitis (West Nile, Nipah, Chandipura, JE)`,`**Fever with cough and encephalopathy in Kerala with a contact history** - suspect Nipah; isolate and notify`,`**Neonate born to a mother febrile with chikungunya** at delivery - observe 7 days`,`**Pregnant woman with rash and conjunctivitis** in a Zika-affected area`,`**Chikungunya with atypical features** - myocarditis, bullous skin, hepatitis, AKI or age over 65 with comorbidity`,`**Needlestick or blood exposure** to a suspected CCHF patient`],pearls:[`Chikungunya - **'that which bends up'**: the patient is stooped and stiff with symmetrical small-joint arthritis.`,`**Chik sign**: brown nasal hyperpigmentation after chikungunya.`,`**Paracetamol only, no NSAIDs, no steroids** until dengue is excluded.`,`Chikungunya phases: **acute 3 weeks, post-acute 3 months, chronic beyond 3 months**.`,`**Zika is the arbovirus that spreads sexually and causes microcephaly**.`,`**Monkey deaths in the forest herald KFD** - Haemaphysalis tick, Western Ghats.`,`**CCHF and Nipah spread in hospitals** - PPE and isolation are the treatment that saves staff.`,`Intrapartum chikungunya infects **about half the neonates**; caesarean does not prevent it.`,`West Nile: **flaccid paralysis with intact sensation** in an elderly patient with encephalitis.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old woman had an acute febrile illness with polyarthritis during a chikungunya outbreak 4 months ago. She still has symmetrical swelling of the wrists and MCP joints with morning stiffness of 1 hour. RF and anti-CCP are negative; CHIKV IgG is positive. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Oral acyclovir`,`Methotrexate once weekly with folic acid`,`Repeated intra-articular steroid injections only`,`Reassurance - it will resolve within days`],answer:1,explanation:`Arthritis persisting beyond 3 months after chikungunya is chronic inflammatory chikungunya arthritis, managed like RA with methotrexate once weekly with folic acid, alone or with hydroxychloroquine and sulfasalazine. Acyclovir has no activity against alphaviruses. Intra-articular injections can help a single joint but do not control polyarthritis, and reassurance ignores an established inflammatory arthritis that can become erosive.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q2`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman, 10 weeks pregnant, develops a pruritic maculopapular rash, low-grade fever and non-purulent conjunctivitis during a Zika outbreak in Pune. Which test is best to confirm current infection?`,options:[`Dengue NS1 antigen`,`Zika IgG ELISA`,`Zika RT-PCR on serum and urine`,`Widal test`],answer:2,explanation:`In the first week Zika RNA is detectable in serum (about 7 days) and for longer in urine (about 14 days), so RT-PCR on both is the confirmatory test. Zika IgG only indicates past infection and cross-reacts with dengue. Dengue NS1 detects dengue, not Zika, though dengue should also be excluded. The Widal test is for enteric fever and is irrelevant here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q3`,stem:`A forest worker from Shimoga presents in February with high fever, severe myalgia, gum bleeding and papulovesicular lesions on the soft palate. Villagers report dead langurs nearby. Which vector transmits the likely infection?`,options:[`Haemaphysalis spinigera tick`,`Aedes albopictus mosquito`,`Culex tritaeniorhynchus mosquito`,`Leptotrombidium chigger mite`],answer:0,explanation:`Fever with bleeding in a Western Ghats forest worker in the dry season, with monkey deaths, is Kyasanur Forest disease, transmitted by nymphs of the Haemaphysalis spinigera tick. Aedes albopictus carries chikungunya and dengue, Culex tritaeniorhynchus carries Japanese encephalitis, and the Leptotrombidium chigger transmits scrub typhus, not KFD.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q4`,stem:`On day 2 of an epidemic fever with severe joint pain in Delhi, a patient asks for a strong painkiller. Dengue has not yet been tested. What is the best analgesic advice?`,options:[`Ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily`,`Prednisolone 20 mg daily for 5 days`,`Aspirin 650 mg four times daily`,`Paracetamol, with NSAIDs only once dengue is excluded`],answer:3,explanation:`Chikungunya and dengue co-circulate, and NSAIDs or aspirin increase bleeding in dengue, so paracetamol is the analgesic until dengue is excluded (WHO 2025). Ibuprofen and aspirin are therefore premature on day 2. Corticosteroids are discouraged in the acute phase of chikungunya because of rebound arthritis and are reserved for the post-acute phase.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q5`,stem:`A nurse in Ahmedabad develops fever, myalgia and ecchymoses 6 days after a needlestick while caring for a herdsman who died of a haemorrhagic fever. Which infection is most likely?`,options:[`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever`,`West Nile fever`,`Chikungunya`],answer:1,explanation:`CCHF, a Hyalomma tick-borne nairovirus endemic in Gujarat and Rajasthan livestock areas, spreads person to person through blood, causing nosocomial clusters such as Ahmedabad 2011; incubation after blood exposure is about 5-6 days. KFD has no person-to-person spread and occurs in the Western Ghats. West Nile and chikungunya are mosquito-borne and do not cause nosocomial haemorrhagic fever.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and management of chikungunya. Add a note on chronic chikungunya arthritis and on chikungunya in pregnancy.`,openingLines:[`Chikungunya is an Aedes-borne alphavirus infection causing abrupt fever with severe, often disabling, symmetrical polyarthralgia.`,`It is rarely fatal, but arthritis persists beyond 3 months in about a third, and intrapartum infection causes severe neonatal disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and virology`,points:[`Alphavirus (Togaviridae); Aedes aegypti and albopictus; E1-A226V mutation drove the 2005-06 Indian epidemic.`,`Reported nationally under NCVBDC; July-November with dengue.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 3-7 days; high fever, symmetrical polyarthralgia of small joints, tenosynovitis.`,`Maculopapular rash, nasal hyperpigmentation (chik sign), aphthous ulcers.`,`Atypical: encephalitis, GBS, myocarditis, bullous lesions, hepatitis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`CBC lymphopenia; exclude dengue (NS1/IgM) and malaria.`,`RT-PCR days 1-7; IgM ELISA from day 5-7.`]},{heading:`Management by phase`,points:[`Acute: rest, fluids, paracetamol; NSAIDs only after dengue excluded; no steroids.`,`Post-acute: NSAIDs, short prednisolone 10 mg taper, physiotherapy.`,`Chronic inflammatory: methotrexate weekly, HCQ, sulfasalazine; rheumatology.`]},{heading:`Chronic chikungunya arthritis`,points:[`Over 3 months; 30-40%; risk - age over 45, severe acute disease, prior joint disease.`,`Persistent viral antigen in synovial macrophages; RA-like, may be erosive; RF/anti-CCP usually negative.`]},{heading:`Pregnancy and neonate`,points:[`Not teratogenic; intrapartum viraemia transmits to about half of neonates.`,`Neonatal chikungunya day 3-7: fever, rash, encephalopathy; caesarean does not prevent; observe 7 days; continue breastfeeding.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Aedes source reduction, weekly dry day, repellents, bed nets; vaccines licensed abroad, not in Indian programme.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: acute, post-acute and chronic phases with treatment`,`Dengue versus chikungunya versus Zika comparison`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and virology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management by phase`,marks:2.5},{part:`Chronic arthritis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pregnancy and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`alphavirus`,`Aedes`,`polyarthralgia`,`chronic chikungunya arthritis`,`methotrexate`,`neonatal chikungunya`,`no NSAIDs before dengue excluded`]},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on Kyasanur Forest disease.`,openingLines:[`Kyasanur Forest disease is a tick-borne flaviviral haemorrhagic fever endemic in the Western Ghats, first recognised in Shimoga, Karnataka in 1957.`,`It is transmitted by Haemaphysalis spinigera ticks, with monkeys as amplifying hosts; case fatality is 3-10%.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`Karnataka, Kerala (Wayanad), Goa, Maharashtra (Sindhudurg), Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris); November-June.`,`Forest workers, nut farmers, leaf collectors; monkey deaths herald outbreaks.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 3-8 days; fever, myalgia, prostration, bleeding, palatal papulovesicles.`,`Second neurological phase in 10-20% after 1-2 weeks.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and treatment`,points:[`RT-PCR in week 1, IgM ELISA later (NIV Pune, Shimoga VRDL); thrombocytopenia, leucopenia.`,`Supportive: fluids, blood products, ICU; no antiviral.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Inactivated vaccine: 2 doses 1 month apart, booster 6-9 months, then 5-yearly; new vaccine in development.`,`DMP oil repellent, full clothing, report monkey deaths.`]}],mustDraw:[`Transmission cycle: tick - monkeys and small mammals - humans (dead end)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis and treatment`,marks:1},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`KFD`,`monkey fever`,`Haemaphysalis spinigera`,`Shimoga`,`biphasic`,`KFD vaccine`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Arthropod-borne and rodent-borne viral infections`,`WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever, 2025`,`National Guidelines for Clinical Management of Chikungunya, NCVBDC, MoHFW, 2016`,`WHO SEARO. Guidelines for prevention and control of chikungunya fever, 2009`,`French Society for Infectious Diseases (SPILF). Recommendations for chronic chikungunya, 2014 (Simon F et al, Med Mal Infect 2015)`,`ICMR-NIV Pune. Kyasanur Forest disease and CCHF guidance, 2024; NCDC Nipah guidelines, 2024`,`WHO Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever fact sheet and clinical management, 2023`,`US FDA communications on chikungunya vaccines (Ixchiq, Vimkunya), 2023-2025`]});var i=[];i.push({id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis`,title:`Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) and PKDL`,oneLiner:`**Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)** is a chronic systemic infection of the reticulo-endothelial system by **Leishmania donovani**, spread in India by the sandfly **Phlebotomus argentipes**, presenting as **fever over 2 weeks with massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia and weight loss** in a resident of the Bihar-Jharkhand-Bengal-UP belt; it is diagnosed by the **rK39 rapid test** and treated with **single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg**, and its sequel **post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL)** is the hidden reservoir the elimination programme must find.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`kala-azar`,`black fever`,`Dumdum fever`,`Leishmania donovani`,`Phlebotomus argentipes`,`sandfly`,`LD bodies`,`amastigote`,`rK39`,`liposomal amphotericin B`,`LAmB`,`miltefosine`,`paromomycin`,`sodium stibogluconate`,`PKDL`,`post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis`,`HIV-VL co-infection`,`KAMIS`,`indoor residual spraying`,`NCVBDC`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Visceral leishmaniasis (VL)** is a vector-borne protozoal infection of macrophages of the spleen, liver, bone marrow and lymph nodes; untreated, symptomatic VL is **fatal in over 95%** within about 2 years. [WHO 2023]`,`Globally VL occurs in about **80 countries**; WHO estimates **50,000-90,000 new cases a year**, and the burden has shifted from South Asia to **East Africa (Sudan, South Sudan, Ethiopia, Kenya)**, which now reports the majority. [WHO 2023]`,`In the **Indian subcontinent** VL is **anthroponotic** - humans are the only reservoir - so treating every case and every PKDL patient breaks transmission, unlike the zoonotic (dog) VL of the Mediterranean and Brazil. [Harrison 22e]`,`India's endemic focus is **Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and eastern Uttar Pradesh** - **54 districts, 633 blocks**; Bihar contributes the large majority of cases (Saran, Siwan, Vaishali, Muzaffarpur, Gopalganj belt). [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Indian cases fell from about **77,000 in 1992 and over 44,000 in 2007** to **under 1,000 a year since 2022** under the **Kala-azar Elimination Programme**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`The **elimination target** is **under 1 VL case per 10,000 population per year at block level**; India reached it in **all 633 endemic blocks in 2023** and must sustain it for **3 consecutive years** before WHO validation. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Bangladesh** became the **first country validated by WHO** for eliminating VL as a public-health problem in **October 2023**; Nepal and Bhutan are on the same pathway. [WHO 2023]`,`VL is a disease of the **rural poor**: mud-walled houses with cracks, damp floors, cattle sheds next to the house, poor nutrition and sleeping outdoors, mainly among **Musahar and other marginalised communities** in Bihar. [Park 27e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, vector and risk factors`,points:[`**Leishmania donovani** causes VL in India and East Africa; **L. infantum (L. chagasi)** causes zoonotic VL in the Mediterranean, Middle East, Central Asia and Brazil. [Harrison 22e]`,`The parasite has two forms: the flagellated **promastigote** in the sandfly gut and the non-flagellated intracellular **amastigote (Leishman-Donovan or LD body, 2-4 micrometres)** in human macrophages. [Harrison 22e]`,`The Indian vector is the female sandfly **Phlebotomus argentipes** - tiny (2-3 mm), hairy, a **weak hopping flier** with a range of under 50-100 m, active from dusk, resting low on walls (under 6 feet) and breeding in **moist organic soil and cracks in mud walls and cattle sheds**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Transmission is also possible by **blood transfusion, shared needles, congenital (transplacental)** and organ transplant routes. [Harrison 22e]`,`Incubation is usually **2-6 months** (range 10 days to several years); most infections are **asymptomatic** - only about 1 in 5-10 infected people develop disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors for disease: **malnutrition, HIV, immunosuppression (transplant, steroids, biologics), young age**, a household case in the last year and living within a cluster. [WHO 2023]`,`Seasonal peak of sandfly density is the **pre-monsoon and monsoon months (March-October)**, which is why indoor residual spraying is timed around **February-March and May-June**. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Inoculated promastigotes are taken up by **macrophages via complement receptors (CR3)**; the surface **lipophosphoglycan and gp63** inhibit the oxidative burst and phagolysosome fusion, letting amastigotes multiply inside. [Harrison 22e]`,`Control depends on a **Th1 response (IL-12, IFN-gamma activating macrophage nitric oxide)**; disease follows when **IL-10 and TGF-beta dominate** - so the skin-test (leishmanin) is **negative during active VL** and becomes positive after cure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Parasitised macrophages fill the spleen, liver and marrow, producing **massive splenomegaly** and **hypersplenism**, which with marrow infiltration explains **pancytopenia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Polyclonal B-cell activation** gives marked **hypergammaglobulinaemia (IgG)** with **reversed albumin-globulin ratio** - the basis of the obsolete **aldehyde (Napier) test** and of high ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemia** is multifactorial - hypersplenism, marrow suppression, haemolysis and blood loss; neutropenia and immune suppression make **secondary bacterial infection (pneumonia, TB, dysentery)** the commonest cause of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Grey-black skin hyperpigmentation**, giving the name kala-azar (black fever), is seen mainly in Indian patients; its mechanism is uncertain (proposed adrenal involvement or melanocyte stimulation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amphotericin B** binds **ergosterol-like sterols** in the Leishmania membrane, forming pores; the **liposomal form** is taken up by the same reticulo-endothelial macrophages that harbour the parasite, delivering drug to the target and sparing the kidney. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Miltefosine** (alkylphosphocholine) disrupts parasite membrane phospholipid and signalling and is the only **oral** drug; **paromomycin** is an aminoglycoside that blocks parasite ribosomes; **antimonials** inhibit trypanothione reductase and glycolysis - resistance in Bihar is linked to parasite thiol up-regulation. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Classification and case definitions`,points:[`**Clinical spectrum**: asymptomatic infection, **symptomatic VL**, **VL-HIV co-infection**, relapse VL, and **PKDL** as a dermal sequel. [WHO 2023]`,`**Suspected VL case (India)**: a person from an endemic area with **fever of more than 2 weeks and splenomegaly**, not responding to antimalarials and antibiotics. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Confirmed VL**: a suspected case with a **positive rK39 rapid test** or **LD bodies on splenic, marrow or lymph-node aspirate**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Primary unresponsiveness**: no initial cure at end of treatment; **relapse**: return of symptoms and parasites after initial cure, usually within **6-12 months**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Initial cure** is clinical improvement at end of treatment; **definitive (final) cure** is being **symptom-free at 6 months** after treatment. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**PKDL is classified clinically** as **macular (hypopigmented, the commonest in India), papular, nodular, or polymorphic** (mixed); WHO grades PKDL severity by extent - grade 1 face only, grade 2 face plus upper chest and arms, grade 3 most of the body. [WHO 2023]`,`**Splenic aspirate parasite density** is graded **0 to 6+** on a log scale (Chulay-Bryceson) - from fewer than 1 parasite per 1,000 fields to over 100 per field - and is used to judge response and relapse. [WHO 2010]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Prolonged fever** over 2 weeks, often irregular or **double-quotidian (two spikes in 24 hours)**, in a patient who looks surprisingly **well and ambulant** for the degree of fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`Progressive **abdominal fullness or left upper quadrant dragging pain** from splenomegaly, **weight loss and wasting** despite preserved appetite. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Darkening of the skin** of face, hands and abdomen, and thinning of hair, are noticed by the family. [Park 27e]`,`**Bleeding** - epistaxis, gum bleeding - and pallor with exertional breathlessness reflect thrombocytopenia and anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the **residence in an endemic block**, a **past VL episode** or treatment, **family or neighbour with VL or PKDL**, migration back from endemic Bihar, and **HIV risk**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Atypical presentations: **HIV-VL** with fever and cytopenia but small or absent spleen, gut, lung or skin involvement; **acute onset** mimicking malaria or typhoid; and VL first found during work-up of **pancytopenia or PUO** outside the endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PKDL history**: painless hypopigmented patches or nodules starting **around the mouth and chin**, appearing **months to years after treated VL** (in India median about 2-3 years; 15-20% give no VL history). [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Massive splenomegaly** - soft, non-tender, often crossing the umbilicus or reaching the right iliac fossa; splenomegaly of over 8 cm in a fever of weeks is kala-azar until proven otherwise in an endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Moderate hepatomegaly**, usually smaller than the spleen; jaundice and ascites are late signs suggesting advanced disease or co-infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pallor, wasting, oedema** (hypoalbuminaemia) and **dry, thin, brittle hair**; **grey-dark hyperpigmentation** of the skin. [Park 27e]`,`**Lymphadenopathy** is **uncommon in Indian VL** but common in **Sudanese/East African VL** - useful in a viva. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Petechiae, epistaxis, gum bleeding**; look for signs of secondary infection - crepitations, oral thrush, **cancrum oris** in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PKDL**: symmetric **hypopigmented macules** with preserved sensation (unlike leprosy), or papules and nodules on face, chin, lips, ears and trunk; **no nerve thickening**. [WHO 2023]`,`Record **spleen size in cm below the costal margin** at baseline - regression of the spleen and rise in Hb are the clinical markers of cure. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Chronic malaria and hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly** (tropical splenomegaly syndrome): very high IgM, high anti-malarial antibody, responds to prolonged antimalarials; rK39 negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haematological malignancy** - **CML** (leucocytosis, basophilia, BCR-ABL), **myelofibrosis** (tear-drop cells, dry tap), **lymphoma** (nodes, LDH): leucocytosis argues against VL, which causes leucopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enteric fever, tuberculosis (disseminated, splenic), brucellosis** and **infective endocarditis** - prolonged fever with splenomegaly but usually smaller spleen; cultures and imaging discriminate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cirrhosis with portal hypertension** - varices, ascites, stigmata of liver disease and no fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemolytic anaemias (thalassaemia)** - lifelong, family history, target cells and HbF/HbA2 on HPLC. [Harrison 22e]`,`For PKDL: **leprosy** (anaesthetic patches, thickened nerves, AFB on slit skin), **vitiligo** (depigmented, not hypopigmented), **pityriasis versicolor**, sarcoidosis and lupus vulgaris. [WHO 2023]`,`Outside the endemic belt, consider **cutaneous leishmaniasis (L. tropica) of Rajasthan and Himachal** - a skin ulcer, not a visceral disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**CBC**: pancytopenia - Hb often **under 8 g/dL**, WBC **under 3,000/microlitre** with neutropenia, platelets under 1 lakh; ESR very high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serum proteins**: albumin low, **globulin high with reversed A:G ratio**; LFT, creatinine and electrolytes before amphotericin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**rK39 immunochromatographic RDT** (recombinant kinesin antigen) is the **first-line diagnostic at PHC level**: sensitivity **about 95-99% and specificity about 90-98% in South Asia**; it uses a fingerprick sample and reads in **10-20 minutes**. [WHO 2023]`,`rK39 detects antibody, so it **stays positive for years after cure** - it cannot diagnose **relapse**, which needs parasitological confirmation; it is also **less sensitive in HIV-VL and in East Africa**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Splenic aspirate** has the highest sensitivity, **93-99%**, but carries a risk of fatal haemorrhage (about **1 in 1,000**); do it only with platelets over 40,000, INR normal and a palpable spleen over 3 cm. [WHO 2010]`,`**Bone marrow aspirate** is safer but less sensitive (**53-86%**); **lymph-node aspirate** 53-65%, more useful in East Africa; Giemsa stain shows **LD bodies (amastigotes with nucleus and rod-shaped kinetoplast)** in macrophages. [Harrison 22e]`,`Advanced: **PCR / qPCR** on blood or tissue (best in HIV-VL and PKDL; also for monitoring), **DAT (direct agglutination test)**, culture in **NNN medium**, and **rK28** RDT; **leishmanin skin test** is negative in active VL. [WHO 2023]`,`**Screen every VL patient for HIV and TB**; and in PKDL, rK39 positivity with typical lesions in an endemic area is enough for programme diagnosis, with **slit-skin smear or PCR** for confirmation. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Suspect, test with **rK39 at the PHC/CHC**, and **refer every confirmed case** to the designated kala-azar treatment centre - treatment is free under NCVBDC and is given **in a facility under supervision**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**First line in India: single-dose liposomal amphotericin B (LAmB) 10 mg/kg IV** in 5% dextrose over **2 hours** - cure rates **over 95%** with one hospital day. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Second-line combinations** (when LAmB is unsuitable or unavailable): **LAmB 5 mg/kg single dose + miltefosine for 7 days**; **LAmB 5 mg/kg + paromomycin for 10 days**; or **miltefosine + paromomycin for 10 days**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Miltefosine** dose is about **2.5 mg/kg/day orally**: adults over 25 kg **50 mg twice daily**, under 25 kg 50 mg once daily, children 2-11 years 2.5 mg/kg/day; take with food to reduce vomiting. [WHO 2010]`,`**Paromomycin** is **11 mg/kg base (15 mg/kg sulphate) IM once daily**; as monotherapy it is given for **21 days**. [WHO 2010]`,`**Amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg IV daily or on alternate days for 15 doses** (total 15 mg/kg) remains a backup where LAmB is unavailable - needs admission for renal and potassium monitoring. [WHO 2010]`,`**Miltefosine monotherapy (28 days) is no longer recommended** in India because relapse rose to **10-20%**, and **sodium stibogluconate is abandoned in Bihar** because over 60% are unresponsive. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Supportive care: **nutrition, iron and folate**, treat **intercurrent infections first**, transfuse if Hb under 5-6 g/dL or bleeding, and counsel on the **wage-loss compensation** paid to VL and PKDL patients under the programme. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Admit** for LAmB infusion; also admit for **severe anaemia (Hb under 5 g/dL)**, **bleeding, jaundice, oedema, severe malnutrition, age under 2 or over 45 years, HIV co-infection, suspected secondary infection** or relapse. [WHO 2023]`,`Before amphotericin: weight, **creatinine, potassium, magnesium**, CBC; **test dose** is not required for LAmB in programme practice but **watch the first 30 minutes** for infusion reactions. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Infusion reactions** (fever, rigors, back pain, flushing, hypoxia) are commoner with **deoxycholate**; slow the infusion, give paracetamol and antihistamine; hydrocortisone premedication for recurrent reactions. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`For **deoxycholate AmB**, prehydrate with **500 mL-1 L normal saline**, monitor creatinine and **potassium at least twice weekly**, and replace potassium and magnesium - nephrotoxicity and hypokalaemia are dose-limiting. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Treat bacterial infections empirically and aggressively** - pneumonia, sepsis and diarrhoea kill more VL patients than the parasite; screen for **TB** in prolonged illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relapse or unresponsive VL**: confirm parasitologically, then give **LAmB total 15-30 mg/kg** (for example 5 mg/kg on alternate days) or a combination; refer to a tertiary centre and test for HIV. [WHO 2023]`,`**Severe bleeding**: platelet transfusion and FFP; avoid splenic aspiration and IM injections while platelets are low. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HIV-VL co-infection** (about 2-6% of VL in Bihar): WHO recommends **LAmB 5 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11 (total 30 mg/kg) plus miltefosine 100 mg/day for 14 days** in South Asia (28 days in East Africa). [WHO 2022]`,`In HIV-VL start **ART** (dolutegravir-based) early, and give **secondary prophylaxis with LAmB 3-5 mg/kg every 3-4 weeks** while **CD4 is under 200 cells/microlitre**; relapse is common. [WHO 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: **LAmB is the drug of choice** (10 mg/kg single dose or 3 mg/kg daily to 21 mg/kg total); **miltefosine is teratogenic** and contraindicated, and antimonials cause abortion. [WHO 2023]`,`**Women of reproductive age on miltefosine** must have a pregnancy test first and use **effective contraception during treatment and for at least 3 months after** (current product labels advise up to 5 months). [WHO 2010]`,`**Children**: same single-dose LAmB 10 mg/kg; miltefosine 2.5 mg/kg/day (allometric dosing preferred); watch nutrition and secondary infection. [WHO 2023]`,`**CKD and elderly**: prefer **LAmB**, avoid deoxycholate and paromomycin (nephrotoxic, ototoxic); adjust supportive care. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Transplant and biologic users** develop VL with atypical, severe disease - think of it with unexplained cytopenia after travel or residence in an endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Secondary bacterial infections** - pneumonia, TB, sepsis, dysentery, **cancrum oris** - are the major cause of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe anaemia, bleeding** (epistaxis, GI), **hepatic dysfunction**, oedema and malnutrition; splenic rupture or infarction is rare. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug toxicity**: deoxycholate AmB - **nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, myocarditis**; antimonials - **QT prolongation, arrhythmia, pancreatitis**; paromomycin - **ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, raised transaminases, injection pain**; miltefosine - **vomiting, diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity, nephrotoxicity, teratogenicity, keratitis**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**PKDL** follows treated VL in **about 5-15% of Indian patients** (up to 50% in Sudan, where it appears within 6 months); in India it persists for years and **does not heal spontaneously**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Relapse** occurs in about **5% after single-dose LAmB** and much more often in HIV co-infection; most relapses appear within **6-12 months**. [WHO 2023]`,`With prompt treatment, mortality is **under 5%**; poor prognostic markers are **age under 2 or over 45, severe anaemia, jaundice, bleeding, oedema, HIV and malnutrition**. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`PKDL - the reservoir`,points:[`**Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis** is a dermal form with **parasites in the skin** after (or occasionally without) VL; nodular lesions contain abundant parasites and **infect sandflies**, so PKDL keeps transmission going after VL cases fall. [WHO 2023]`,`It is thought to reflect a **post-treatment Th1 response against persistent skin parasites** - IFN-gamma and IL-10 both high in the lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diagnosis**: typical lesions + endemic area + rK39 positive; **slit-skin smear** is often negative in macular PKDL, so **PCR of skin biopsy** confirms. [WHO 2023]`,`**Treatment (WHO 2022, South Asia)**: **miltefosine for 12 weeks with allometric dosing**, or **LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly for 3 weeks (total 30 mg/kg)** where miltefosine is unsuitable. [WHO 2022]`,`Stop miltefosine and refer to ophthalmology if **eye pain, redness or blurring** appear - **keratitis and ocular toxicity** were reported with 12-week courses in India. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Programme case search for PKDL is **house-to-house in villages with VL cases in the previous 3 years**, using ASHAs and photo cards. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer **all suspected cases** to the block/district kala-azar treatment centre; refer relapse, HIV-VL, pregnancy, severe disease and drug toxicity to a **tertiary centre (for example RMRIMS Patna)**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Follow up treated VL at **1, 3, 6 and 12 months** for fever, spleen size, Hb and weight; **definitive cure is declared at 6 months** if symptom-free. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Continue watching for **PKDL for at least 3 years** - teach the patient to report any new skin patch. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`During miltefosine: **monthly LFT and creatinine**, pregnancy test, contraception and eye symptoms; during paromomycin: creatinine, LFT and hearing. [WHO 2010]`,`Every case is entered into **KAMIS (Kala-azar Management Information System)** and the village is mapped for **active case detection and focal spraying**. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and the national elimination programme`,points:[`The programme runs under **NCVBDC** with four pillars: **early case detection and complete treatment, integrated vector management, community mobilisation, and surveillance with operational research**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Indoor residual spraying (IRS)** with the synthetic pyrethroid **alpha-cypermethrin** (replacing DDT, to which sandflies became resistant) in **two rounds a year**, walls and cattle sheds sprayed **up to 6 feet**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Active case detection** (camp approach and index-case-based search within **500 m**) plus incentives to **ASHAs** for referring a confirmed case and ensuring completion. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Pucca housing** under PMAY-G, **plastering of mud walls**, keeping cattle away from sleeping areas and **fine-mesh insecticide-treated nets** reduce sandfly contact. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`There is **no vaccine** for human VL; post-elimination surveillance must also screen **HIV patients from endemic districts** and migrants. [WHO 2023]`,`VL is a **notifiable disease** in endemic states; the **WHO NTD Road Map 2021-2030** targets elimination as a public-health problem in South-East Asia. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2022** guideline for **HIV-VL co-infection**: LAmB 30 mg/kg total plus **miltefosine** (14 days South Asia, 28 days East Africa) replaces LAmB monotherapy. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2022** guideline for **PKDL in South Asia**: miltefosine 12 weeks with allometric dosing, or LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly for 3 weeks. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2024** guideline for **East Africa**: **paromomycin + miltefosine for 14 days** replaces the 17-day SSG + paromomycin combination - an important contrast with India's single-dose LAmB. [WHO 2024]`,`**India reached the elimination target in all endemic blocks in 2023** and has been sustaining it through 2024-2025, entering the 3-year validation window; Bangladesh was validated in **October 2023**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**rK39 remains the programme diagnostic**, but PCR-based tools (qPCR, LAMP) are increasingly used for HIV-VL, PKDL and post-elimination surveillance. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Kala-azar - diagnosis and treatment'** or **'PKDL'**; always write the **case definition, rK39, single-dose LAmB 10 mg/kg** and the **elimination target under 1 per 10,000 at block level**.`,`Common mistake: **using rK39 to diagnose relapse** - it remains positive for years; relapse needs a tissue aspirate or PCR.`,`Common mistake: writing **SSG or miltefosine monotherapy** as first line in India - both are obsolete for Bihar.`,`Viva: **why is PKDL important?** - it is the **reservoir** in an anthroponotic disease, and nodular lesions infect sandflies.`,`Viva: **why is lymphadenopathy a clue to geography?** - common in Sudanese VL, uncommon in Indian VL.`,`Viva: **why is LAmB more effective and less toxic?** - liposomes are taken up by the **reticulo-endothelial macrophages** that harbour amastigotes and reach the kidney less.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for visceral leishmaniasis and PKDL`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Liposomal amphotericin B (first line, India)`,`10 mg/kg IV in 5% dextrose over 2 h`,`Single dose`,`Infusion reactions, back pain, mild hypokalaemia, rare nephrotoxicity; safe in pregnancy`],[`LAmB + miltefosine`,`LAmB 5 mg/kg IV once, then miltefosine about 2.5 mg/kg/day orally`,`Miltefosine 7 days`,`Miltefosine: vomiting, diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity, teratogenic`],[`LAmB + paromomycin`,`LAmB 5 mg/kg IV once, then paromomycin 11 mg/kg base IM OD`,`Paromomycin 10 days`,`Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, raised transaminases, injection pain`],[`Miltefosine + paromomycin`,`Miltefosine 2.5 mg/kg/day oral + paromomycin 11 mg/kg base IM OD`,`10 days (India); 14 days in East Africa (WHO 2024)`,`Contraception needed; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`1 mg/kg IV over 4-6 h, daily or alternate days`,`15 doses (total 15 mg/kg)`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, rigors, myocarditis, anaemia`],[`HIV-VL regimen`,`LAmB 5 mg/kg IV on days 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 + miltefosine 100 mg/day`,`Miltefosine 14 days (South Asia); then secondary prophylaxis LAmB every 3-4 weeks if CD4 under 200`,`As above; interactions minimal with dolutegravir ART`],[`PKDL - miltefosine`,`Allometric dose (adult about 50 mg BD)`,`12 weeks`,`Keratitis and eye toxicity - stop and refer; GI upset, hepatotoxicity`],[`PKDL - LAmB`,`5 mg/kg IV twice weekly`,`3 weeks (total 30 mg/kg)`,`Infusion reactions; hypokalaemia`],[`Sodium stibogluconate (obsolete in Bihar)`,`20 mg/kg/day IM or IV`,`30 days`,`QT prolongation, arrhythmia, pancreatitis, hepatitis; over 60% unresponsive in Bihar`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests for VL - what each is good for`,columns:[`Test`,`Sensitivity / specificity`,`Strength`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`rK39 RDT (fingerprick)`,`About 95-99% / 90-98% in South Asia`,`PHC-level, 10-20 min, programme test`,`Positive for years after cure - not for relapse; less sensitive in HIV and East Africa`],[`Splenic aspirate (Giemsa)`,`93-99%`,`Gold standard; grading 0-6+ for response`,`Fatal bleed about 1 in 1,000; needs platelets over 40,000 and normal INR`],[`Bone marrow aspirate`,`53-86%`,`Safer; also excludes leukaemia`,`Lower sensitivity`],[`Lymph-node aspirate`,`53-65%`,`Useful in East African VL`,`Nodes rarely enlarged in Indian VL`],[`PCR / qPCR (blood, tissue)`,`Over 95% in blood`,`HIV-VL, PKDL, relapse, monitoring`,`Tertiary labs; detects asymptomatic infection`],[`Direct agglutination test (DAT)`,`About 95% / 86-98%`,`Quantitative serology`,`Needs lab; remains positive after cure`],[`Leishmanin skin test`,`Negative in active VL`,`Marks past infection or cure`,`Not diagnostic of active disease`]]},{heading:`Fever with massive splenomegaly - differential`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Visceral leishmaniasis`,`Endemic Bihar belt; fever over 2 weeks; pancytopenia; dark skin`,`rK39 RDT; splenic aspirate LD bodies`],[`Hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly`,`Repeated malaria; high IgM`,`Malaria antibody, IgM; response to antimalarials`],[`Chronic myeloid leukaemia`,`Leucocytosis with left shift and basophilia; often afebrile`,`Peripheral smear, BCR-ABL`],[`Myelofibrosis`,`Older age, tear-drop cells, dry tap`,`Marrow biopsy, JAK2/CALR`],[`Lymphoma`,`Lymphadenopathy, B symptoms, high LDH`,`Node biopsy, CT`],[`Thalassaemia major/intermedia`,`Lifelong anaemia, facies, family history`,`HPLC`],[`Cirrhosis with portal hypertension`,`Varices, ascites, liver stigmata, no fever`,`USG Doppler, endoscopy`]]},{heading:`PKDL versus leprosy versus vitiligo`,columns:[`Feature`,`PKDL`,`Leprosy`,`Vitiligo`],rows:[[`Colour`,`Hypopigmented macules, papules, nodules`,`Hypopigmented or erythematous patches`,`Depigmented (milky white)`],[`Sensation`,`Normal`,`Reduced or absent`,`Normal`],[`Nerves`,`Not thickened`,`Thickened peripheral nerves`,`Normal`],[`Distribution`,`Starts around mouth and chin, spreads symmetrically`,`Asymmetric, any site`,`Periorificial, acral, segmental`],[`History`,`Treated VL months to years earlier`,`Contact with leprosy`,`Family history, autoimmunity`],[`Test`,`rK39 positive; skin PCR`,`Slit-skin smear AFB`,`Wood lamp accentuation`]]},{heading:`Classification of VL outcomes and timing`,columns:[`Term`,`Definition`,`When assessed`],rows:[[`Initial cure`,`Afebrile, spleen regressing, Hb rising at end of treatment`,`End of treatment`],[`Definitive cure`,`Symptom-free with no relapse`,`6 months after treatment`],[`Primary unresponsiveness`,`No initial cure`,`End of treatment`],[`Relapse`,`Return of symptoms with parasites after initial cure`,`Usually within 6-12 months`],[`PKDL`,`Dermal lesions with parasites after VL`,`Months to years (India); within 6 months (Sudan)`]]}],redFlags:[`Hb under 5 g/dL, breathlessness or heart failure from anaemia`,`Active bleeding - epistaxis, gum or GI bleeding - or platelets under 20,000`,`Fever with signs of pneumonia, sepsis or dysentery in a VL patient - secondary infection kills`,`Jaundice, ascites or generalised oedema`,`VL in a person with HIV, in pregnancy, or in a child under 2`,`Relapse after a completed course, or no response by end of treatment`,`Eye pain, redness or blurred vision during miltefosine (PKDL course)`],pearls:[`**Fever over 2 weeks + splenomegaly + endemic area = suspected kala-azar** - test with rK39.`,`**One dose, 10 mg/kg** - single-dose liposomal amphotericin B is first line in India.`,`**rK39 cannot diagnose relapse** - it stays positive for years.`,`Indian VL is **anthroponotic**, so **PKDL is the reservoir** that elimination must chase.`,`**Lymph nodes - Sudan; skin darkening - India.**`,`Elimination target: **under 1 per 10,000 population per year at block level** - achieved by India in 2023.`,`**Miltefosine** = oral, teratogenic, contraception during and for months after.`,`Spray **up to 6 feet** - the sandfly is a weak, low-flying hopper.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old man from Muzaffarpur, Bihar, has fever for 6 weeks, weight loss and a spleen palpable 12 cm below the costal margin. Hb is 7 g/dL, WBC 2,400/microlitre and platelets 80,000. The rK39 rapid test is positive. What is the recommended first-line treatment under the national programme?`,options:[`Sodium stibogluconate 20 mg/kg/day for 30 days`,`Miltefosine 50 mg twice daily for 28 days`,`Liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg IV as a single dose`,`Amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg on alternate days for 15 doses`],answer:2,explanation:`Single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg is the first-line regimen in India, curing over 95% with one hospital day. Sodium stibogluconate is abandoned in Bihar because over 60% are unresponsive, miltefosine monotherapy was withdrawn after relapse rose to 10-20%, and deoxycholate amphotericin is a nephrotoxic backup only where LAmB is unavailable.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q2`,stem:`A woman treated for kala-azar 8 months ago presents with fever and an enlarging spleen. Her rK39 test is positive. What is the most appropriate next step to confirm relapse?`,options:[`Repeat the rK39 test after 2 weeks`,`Splenic or bone marrow aspirate for LD bodies (or PCR)`,`Leishmanin skin test`,`Serum globulin and albumin-globulin ratio`],answer:1,explanation:`rK39 detects antibody that persists for years after cure, so a positive result cannot distinguish relapse from past infection; relapse needs parasitological confirmation by splenic or marrow aspirate, or PCR. A repeat rK39 adds nothing, the leishmanin test is negative in active disease and positive after cure, and globulin levels are non-specific.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q3`,stem:`Which feature best explains why post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis is a priority for the Indian elimination programme?`,options:[`It is a fatal condition if untreated`,`It is transmitted directly from person to person by contact`,`Its skin lesions harbour parasites that infect sandflies in an anthroponotic cycle`,`It always develops within 6 months of VL treatment`],answer:2,explanation:`Indian VL is anthroponotic: humans are the only reservoir, and PKDL nodules contain parasites that sandflies pick up, sustaining transmission after VL cases fall. PKDL itself is not fatal, is not spread by contact, and in India appears months to years (median 2-3 years) after VL - the within-6-months pattern is typical of Sudan.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old man with HIV (CD4 90 cells/microlitre) from Saran district is diagnosed with visceral leishmaniasis. Which regimen does WHO (2022) recommend in South Asia?`,options:[`Liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg single dose alone`,`Paromomycin IM for 21 days`,`Liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg on alternate days to a total of 30 mg/kg plus miltefosine 100 mg/day for 14 days`,`Sodium stibogluconate plus paromomycin for 17 days`],answer:2,explanation:`WHO 2022 recommends LAmB 30 mg/kg total (5 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11) combined with miltefosine for 14 days in South Asian HIV-VL, followed by ART and secondary prophylaxis while CD4 is under 200. Single-dose LAmB alone has high failure and relapse in HIV, paromomycin monotherapy is not used here, and SSG plus paromomycin was the old East African regimen.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q5`,stem:`Indoor residual spraying against Phlebotomus argentipes is done only up to a height of about 6 feet on walls. What is the reason?`,options:[`The sandfly is a weak, hopping flier that rests low on walls`,`Insecticide above 6 feet is toxic to the household`,`The sandfly breeds only on roofs`,`Pyrethroids lose activity at higher levels`],answer:0,explanation:`Phlebotomus argentipes is a small, weak hopping flier that rests on the lower parts of walls and cattle sheds, so spraying up to 6 feet covers its resting sites efficiently. Toxicity is not the reason, the sandfly breeds in moist soil and wall cracks rather than on roofs, and pyrethroid activity does not depend on height.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the clinical features, diagnosis and management of visceral leishmaniasis. Add a note on the kala-azar elimination programme in India.`,openingLines:[`Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) is a chronic, potentially fatal infection of the reticulo-endothelial system by Leishmania donovani, transmitted in India by the sandfly Phlebotomus argentipes.`,`It presents as prolonged fever with massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia in residents of Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and eastern UP, and is treated with single-dose liposomal amphotericin B.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and agent`,points:[`L. donovani; anthroponotic in India; vector P. argentipes (weak, low flier).`,`54 districts, 633 blocks in 4 states; cases under 1,000 a year since 2022.`,`Incubation 2-6 months; most infections asymptomatic.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Amastigotes in macrophages; Th1 failure with high IL-10.`,`Hypersplenism and marrow infiltration: pancytopenia; polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fever over 2 weeks, double-quotidian; patient ambulant.`,`Massive soft splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, weight loss, dark skin, bleeding, pallor.`,`Lymphadenopathy uncommon in India.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Case definition: fever over 2 weeks + splenomegaly in an endemic area.`,`rK39 RDT (95-99% sensitive); splenic aspirate 93-99%; marrow 53-86%; PCR in HIV and PKDL.`,`CBC pancytopenia, reversed A:G ratio; screen HIV and TB.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`LAmB 10 mg/kg single dose IV (first line).`,`Combinations: LAmB + miltefosine 7 days; LAmB + paromomycin 10 days; miltefosine + paromomycin 10 days.`,`HIV-VL: LAmB 30 mg/kg total + miltefosine 14 days, ART, secondary prophylaxis.`,`Supportive: treat infections, nutrition, transfusion.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and PKDL`,points:[`Definitive cure at 6 months; watch for PKDL for 3 years.`,`PKDL treated with miltefosine 12 weeks or LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly x 3 weeks.`]},{heading:`Elimination programme`,points:[`Target under 1 case per 10,000 per year at block level - achieved 2023.`,`Early diagnosis and complete treatment, IRS with alpha-cypermethrin twice a year, active case detection, KAMIS surveillance, ASHA incentives, wage compensation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle of L. donovani (sandfly promastigote and human amastigote)`,`Diagnostic and treatment flow chart from suspected case to definitive cure`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment including HIV-VL and PKDL`,marks:3},{part:`Elimination programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`rK39`,`LAmB 10 mg/kg`,`PKDL`,`IRS`,`under 1 per 10,000`,`KAMIS`]},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL).`,openingLines:[`PKDL is a dermal form of leishmaniasis in which parasites persist in the skin, appearing months to years after apparent cure of visceral leishmaniasis.`,`In anthroponotic Indian VL it is the hidden reservoir that sustains transmission, so finding and treating PKDL is central to elimination.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and pathogenesis`,points:[`Follows 5-15% of treated VL in India (median 2-3 years); up to 50% in Sudan within 6 months.`,`Post-treatment immune response against persistent skin parasites.`]},{heading:`Clinical types`,points:[`Macular (commonest in India), papular, nodular, polymorphic.`,`Starts around mouth and chin; normal sensation; nerves not thickened.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Clinical + endemic area + rK39 positive; slit-skin smear or skin PCR to confirm.`,`Differentiate leprosy and vitiligo.`]},{heading:`Treatment and programme`,points:[`Miltefosine 12 weeks (allometric) or LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly x 3 weeks (WHO 2022).`,`Eye symptoms on miltefosine - stop and refer.`,`House-to-house PKDL search in villages with VL in last 3 years; wage compensation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: PKDL vs leprosy vs vitiligo`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and importance`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical types and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PKDL`,`macular`,`nodular`,`miltefosine 12 weeks`,`reservoir`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Leishmaniasis chapter`,`WHO. Guideline for the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis in HIV co-infected patients in East Africa and South-East Asia, 2022`,`WHO. Guideline for the treatment of PKDL in East Africa and South-East Asia, 2022`,`WHO. Control of the leishmaniases, Technical Report Series 949, 2010`,`WHO. Guideline for the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis in East Africa (paromomycin + miltefosine), 2024`,`National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC), MoHFW. Kala-azar elimination programme operational guidelines, 2024 update`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`,`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`]});var a=[];a.push({id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic`,title:`Sexually transmitted infections: syndromic management and aetiological diagnosis`,oneLiner:`**Syndromic management** treats an STI by the **syndrome** the patient presents with (urethral discharge, vaginal discharge, genital ulcer, lower abdominal pain, inguinal bubo, scrotal swelling) using a flow chart that covers every common cause at the **first visit**, with India's **NACO colour-coded kits 1-7**, while **aetiological diagnosis** (Gram stain, NAAT, syphilis serology) confirms the organism where a laboratory exists - always completed by the **4 Cs: compliance, counselling, condoms and contact (partner) treatment**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`STI`,`RTI`,`syndromic case management`,`NACO kits`,`Suraksha clinic`,`DSRC`,`urethral discharge`,`genital ulcer disease`,`GUD`,`gonorrhoea`,`chlamydia`,`chancroid`,`LGV`,`donovanosis`,`herpes genitalis`,`benzathine penicillin`,`cefixime azithromycin`,`partner notification`,`expedited partner therapy`,`doxy-PEP`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Sexually transmitted infections (STIs)** are infections spread mainly by vaginal, anal or oral sex; **reproductive tract infections (RTIs)** is the wider Indian programme term that also includes endogenous infections (bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis) and iatrogenic infections after abortion, delivery or IUD insertion. [NACO 2014]`,`WHO estimates about **374 million new curable STIs** each year in people aged 15-49 - **trichomoniasis 156 million, chlamydia 129 million, gonorrhoea 82 million and syphilis 7.1 million** (2020 estimates); syphilis rose to about **8 million** new adult cases in 2022. [WHO 2024]`,`More than **1 million curable STIs are acquired every day** worldwide, and most are **asymptomatic**, especially chlamydia and gonorrhoea in women - which is why syndromic management alone misses a large share of infections. [WHO 2021]`,`In India, NACO estimates that about **6% of the adult population** has one or more STI/RTI at any time; the burden is highest in **key populations** - female sex workers, men who have sex with men, transgender people, injecting drug users and migrant workers. [NACO 2014]`,`STI services are delivered through about **1,100+ Designated STI/RTI Clinics (DSRCs) branded 'Suraksha Clinics'** in district hospitals and medical colleges, and through targeted interventions under **NACP Phase V (2021-2026)**. [NACP-V 2021]`,`**Herpes simplex type 2** has overtaken chancroid as the **commonest cause of genital ulcer disease** in India and worldwide; chancroid and donovanosis are now rare, while syphilis is resurging in many cities. [Harrison 22e]`,`STIs matter beyond the genitals: an ulcerative STI raises the risk of acquiring **HIV 2-4 fold**, untreated chlamydia and gonorrhoea cause **PID, ectopic pregnancy and tubal infertility**, and untreated syphilis in pregnancy causes **stillbirth and congenital syphilis**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Urethral discharge** in men: **Neisseria gonorrhoeae** and **Chlamydia trachomatis** (often together), then **Mycoplasma genitalium**, Trichomonas vaginalis, and rarely HSV or adenovirus. [WHO 2021]`,`**Vaginal discharge**: commonest causes are the endogenous **bacterial vaginosis** (Gardnerella and anaerobes) and **vulvovaginal candidiasis**, then the STI **Trichomonas vaginalis**; **cervicitis** from gonorrhoea or chlamydia is less common and often silent. [WHO 2021]`,`**Genital ulcer**: **HSV-2 (and HSV-1)**, **Treponema pallidum (syphilis)**, **Haemophilus ducreyi (chancroid)**, **Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1-L3 (LGV)** and **Klebsiella granulomatis (donovanosis)**; non-infective causes include fixed drug eruption, Behcet disease and trauma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower abdominal pain in women (PID)**: gonorrhoea, chlamydia, M. genitalium and mixed anaerobes ascending from the cervix. [CDC 2021]`,`**Acute scrotal swelling (epididymo-orchitis)**: under 35 years usually gonorrhoea or chlamydia; over 35 years or after instrumentation usually **enteric Gram-negative bacilli** - but **testicular torsion** must be excluded first. [CDC 2021]`,`Risk factors: **age 15-24 years**, a new partner or more than one partner in the last 3 months, a partner with an STI, inconsistent condom use, sex work, MSM, alcohol and drug use, previous STI and HIV infection. [WHO 2021]`,`Mother-to-child transmission occurs for **syphilis, HIV, hepatitis B, HSV, gonorrhoea and chlamydia** (ophthalmia neonatorum, neonatal pneumonia). [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the syndromes look the way they do`,points:[`**Gonococcus** adheres to columnar epithelium by **pili and Opa proteins**, invades, and triggers an intense **neutrophil response** - producing the profuse purulent discharge, the **Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** in neutrophils and the short incubation of **2-5 days**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chlamydia** is an **obligate intracellular** bacterium with an elementary body (infectious) and reticulate body (replicating) cycle; it causes a milder, mucoid discharge after **7-21 days** and chronic subclinical inflammation that scars the tubes - and beta-lactams do not reach it, hence **doxycycline or azithromycin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Treponema pallidum** enters through micro-abrasions and causes an **obliterative endarteritis** with plasma cells; the resulting ischaemic, **indurated, painless chancre** contrasts with the **painful, soft, ragged** ulcer of chancroid, where H. ducreyi toxin causes necrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV** replicates in epithelium to form vesicles that rupture into **multiple shallow painful erosions**, then travels up sensory nerves to establish **latency in sacral dorsal root ganglia** - which explains recurrences and asymptomatic shedding. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LGV serovars L1-L3** invade lymphatics and cause a **suppurative lymphadenitis**; nodes above and below the inguinal ligament produce the **groove sign**, and rectal LGV in MSM causes **proctocolitis** mimicking inflammatory bowel disease. [CDC 2021]`,`**Bacterial vaginosis** is not an infection in the classic sense but a **loss of hydrogen-peroxide-producing lactobacilli**, with overgrowth of anaerobes that release **amines (fishy odour)** and raise pH above **4.5**. [CDC 2021]`,`Inflamed or ulcerated genital mucosa recruits **CD4 target cells** and breaches the epithelial barrier - the mechanism by which STIs amplify **HIV acquisition and shedding**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification - the STI syndromes and the NACO kits`,points:[`NACO recognises **seven syndromes**: urethral discharge, vaginal discharge (vaginitis and cervicitis), genital ulcer (herpetic and non-herpetic), lower abdominal pain, inguinal bubo, scrotal swelling, and neonatal conjunctivitis, plus anorectal discharge and genital warts. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 1 - Grey**: urethral discharge, cervicitis, anorectal discharge, painful scrotal swelling - **azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg, both single oral doses**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 2 - Green**: vaginitis (BV, trichomonas, candida) - **secnidazole 2 g single dose + fluconazole 150 mg single dose**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 3 - White**: non-herpetic genital ulcer - **benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM single dose (with skin test where required by local policy) + azithromycin 1 g single dose**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 4 - Blue**: non-herpetic genital ulcer in penicillin allergy - **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 15 days + azithromycin 1 g single dose**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 5 - Red**: herpetic genital ulcer - **acyclovir 400 mg TDS for 7 days**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 6 - Yellow**: lower abdominal pain (PID) - **cefixime 400 mg single dose + metronidazole 400 mg BD for 14 days + doxycycline 100 mg BD for 14 days**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 7 - Black**: inguinal bubo - **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 21 days + azithromycin 1 g single dose**; fluctuant nodes are aspirated through healthy skin, never incised. [NACO 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Take a **sexual history using the 5 Ps** - partners (number, gender), practices (vaginal, anal, oral), protection (condoms), past STIs, and pregnancy intention - in private, with a non-judgemental tone. [CDC 2021]`,`**Gonococcal urethritis**: sudden **purulent discharge and dysuria** 2-5 days after exposure; **chlamydial urethritis** is milder, mucoid and often noticed only as morning crusting; **up to 50% of chlamydia in men** and 70-80% in women is asymptomatic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vaginal discharge**: ask colour, odour and itch - **thin grey fishy** (BV, worse after sex), **frothy yellow-green with soreness** (trichomoniasis), **curdy white with intense itch** (candidiasis); post-coital bleeding or deep dyspareunia suggests cervicitis or PID. [WHO 2021]`,`**Genital ulcer**: ask pain, number and preceding vesicles - **painful grouped vesicles with prodromal tingling** (herpes), **single painless ulcer** (syphilis), **painful ragged ulcers with tender suppurative nodes** (chancroid), **painless beefy-red ulcer that bleeds** (donovanosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inguinal bubo**: a painful node mass weeks after a small transient ulcer that the patient may not recall suggests **LGV**; rectal pain, tenesmus and bloody discharge in MSM suggest **LGV proctitis**. [CDC 2021]`,`Ask about **extragenital symptoms**: sore throat after oral sex (pharyngeal gonorrhoea is usually silent), joint pain and skin pustules (**disseminated gonococcal infection**), rash on palms and soles (secondary syphilis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical presentations**: asymptomatic screening-detected infection, recurrent 'UTI' with sterile pyuria, infertility, reactive arthritis with conjunctivitis and urethritis after chlamydia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examine in good light with a chaperone: **milk the urethra** from base to meatus if no discharge is visible, retract the foreskin, and palpate the testes, epididymis and inguinal nodes. [NACO 2014]`,`In women, do a **speculum examination**: vaginal discharge on the walls suggests vaginitis, while **mucopurulent discharge from the os, friability and contact bleeding** suggest cervicitis; a **'strawberry cervix'** (punctate haemorrhages) suggests trichomoniasis. [CDC 2021]`,`A **bimanual examination** with **cervical motion tenderness, uterine or adnexal tenderness** in a sexually active woman with lower abdominal pain is enough to start PID treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`**Syphilitic chancre**: single, round, clean-based, **indurated (button-like)**, painless, with rubbery non-tender bilateral nodes; **chancroid**: multiple, soft, ragged undermined edges, purulent base, painful, with unilateral tender bubo that may rupture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LGV groove sign**: enlarged inguinal and femoral nodes separated by the inguinal ligament - seen in about 10-20%; **donovanosis pseudobubo**: subcutaneous granulation tissue, not a true node. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Warts (condylomata acuminata)** from HPV 6/11 are cauliflower-like; **condylomata lata** of secondary syphilis are flat, moist and highly infectious - do not confuse them. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **systemic clues**: oral ulcers, generalised lymphadenopathy, rash, jaundice (hepatitis B), oral candidiasis or weight loss (HIV). [NACO 2014]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Urethral discharge vs UTI**: urethritis has discharge and urethral dysuria with a normal urine culture; cystitis has frequency, urgency and suprapubic pain with bacteriuria. [CDC 2021]`,`**Vaginal discharge**: physiological discharge (clear, odourless, mid-cycle), foreign body (retained tampon), cervical ectropion and **cervical cancer** (blood-stained, foul) must be separated from vaginitis. [WHO 2021]`,`**Genital ulcer non-infective causes**: **fixed drug eruption** (recurs at the same site after NSAIDs, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole), **Behcet disease** (recurrent oral plus genital ulcers, pathergy), trauma, and **squamous cell carcinoma** in an older man with a non-healing ulcer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower abdominal pain**: **ectopic pregnancy** (always do a urine pregnancy test), appendicitis, ovarian torsion, endometriosis and UTI. [CDC 2021]`,`**Scrotal swelling**: **testicular torsion** (sudden, young, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex - surgical emergency), mumps orchitis, tuberculous epididymitis, hernia and tumour. [CDC 2021]`,`**Inguinal bubo**: pyogenic lymphadenitis from a leg wound, tuberculous adenitis, filarial lymphadenitis, plague (very rare) and lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Gram stain of urethral discharge**: **2 or more polymorphs per oil-immersion field** confirms urethritis; **Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** make gonorrhoea - sensitivity over 95% in symptomatic men, but poor in cervical samples. [CDC 2021]`,`**Vaginal discharge bedside tests**: **pH paper** (over 4.5 in BV and trichomoniasis, normal 3.8-4.5 in candidiasis), **whiff test** with 10% KOH, **saline wet mount** (motile trichomonads, clue cells) and **KOH mount** (pseudohyphae). [CDC 2021]`,`**Amsel criteria** for BV - **3 of 4**: thin homogeneous discharge, pH over 4.5, positive whiff test, **clue cells over 20%** of epithelial cells; the **Nugent score of 7-10** on Gram stain is the laboratory standard. [CDC 2021]`,`**NAAT** (nucleic acid amplification test) on **first-void urine in men** and **vaginal swab in women** is the test of choice for gonorrhoea and chlamydia (sensitivity over 90%); use rectal and pharyngeal swabs for extragenital sites in MSM. [WHO 2021]`,`**Gonococcal culture** on Thayer-Martin medium with **antimicrobial susceptibility testing** is needed in treatment failure, because ceftriaxone-resistant strains are rising; India is part of the WHO **Enhanced Gonococcal Antimicrobial Surveillance Programme**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Every STI patient** is offered **syphilis serology (RPR/VDRL with TPHA or a rapid treponemal test), HIV testing** and, where indicated, hepatitis B and C testing. [NACO 2014]`,`**Genital ulcer**: **HSV PCR** of the ulcer base is best for herpes (Tzanck smear is insensitive), **dark-field microscopy or treponemal PCR** for a chancre, and **tissue smear for Donovan bodies** (Giemsa) for donovanosis. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Treat on the **first visit** with the syndromic flow chart - the main advantage of syndromic management is that **no patient leaves untreated** while waiting for results. [WHO 2021]`,`**Gonorrhoea**: WHO 2024 recommends **ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose**; CDC 2021 uses **ceftriaxone 500 mg IM** (1 g if 150 kg or more); the NACO kit uses **cefixime 400 mg orally** - an acceptable option where injections are impractical, but cefixime is less reliable for pharyngeal infection. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chlamydia**: **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days** is now preferred over azithromycin 1 g single dose (better cure of rectal infection); azithromycin is used in pregnancy and when adherence is doubtful. [WHO 2024]`,`**Trichomoniasis**: **metronidazole 500 mg BD for 7 days** in women (better than the 2 g single dose); men may take 2 g single dose; avoid alcohol for 24 hours (48-72 hours after tinidazole or secnidazole). [CDC 2021]`,`**Bacterial vaginosis**: **metronidazole 400-500 mg BD for 7 days** or 0.75% gel; **candidiasis**: **fluconazole 150 mg single oral dose** or clotrimazole 500 mg vaginal tablet; partners of BV or candida patients are **not** treated routinely. [CDC 2021]`,`**First-episode genital herpes**: **acyclovir 400 mg TDS for 7-10 days** (or valacyclovir 1 g BD); recurrences: acyclovir 800 mg TDS for 2 days or episodic short courses; more than 6 recurrences a year: **suppressive acyclovir 400 mg BD**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Syphilis**: **benzathine penicillin G 2.4 MU IM single dose** (1.2 MU into each buttock) for primary, secondary and early latent syphilis - full detail in the Syphilis topic. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chancroid**: **azithromycin 1 g single dose** or ceftriaxone 250 mg IM; **donovanosis**: azithromycin 1 g weekly or 500 mg daily for **at least 3 weeks** and until healed; **LGV**: **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 21 days**. [CDC 2021]`,`Complete every visit with the **4 Cs** - **compliance** (take all doses), **counselling** (risk reduction, abstain for 7 days after treatment), **condoms** (demonstrate and provide), and **contact tracing** (partners). [NACO 2014]`]},{heading:`Partner management`,points:[`Notify and treat **all partners from the last 60 days** (or the last partner if none in 60 days) for urethral discharge, cervicitis and PID; for primary syphilis, the last **3 months plus duration of symptoms**, for secondary syphilis **6 months**, and for early latent **1 year**. [CDC 2021]`,`Partners are treated **epidemiologically** - for the same syndrome as the index patient - without waiting for their own test results. [NACO 2014]`,`Methods: **patient referral** (index patient informs, with a partner card), **provider referral** (the clinic contacts the partner), and **expedited partner therapy** (giving the drugs for the partner), which is legal and used in many countries but less common in India. [CDC 2021]`,`Both partners **abstain until 7 days after both** have completed treatment and symptoms resolve, to prevent the **ping-pong reinfection**. [CDC 2021]`,`Retest for chlamydia and gonorrhoea **3 months after treatment** because reinfection, not treatment failure, explains most positives. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`Admit **PID** if surgical emergency cannot be excluded, there is a **tubo-ovarian abscess**, pregnancy, high fever with vomiting, or failure of oral therapy at 72 hours; give **ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily + doxycycline 100 mg BD + metronidazole 500 mg BD**, switching to oral after 24-48 hours of improvement for 14 days total. [CDC 2021]`,`**Disseminated gonococcal infection** (arthritis-dermatitis syndrome, septic arthritis): **ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily**, for at least 7 days for arthritis; meningitis and endocarditis need 10-14 days and 4 weeks respectively. [CDC 2021]`,`**Severe primary herpes** with urinary retention, aseptic meningitis or disseminated disease: **acyclovir 5-10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** for 2-7 days then oral to complete 10 days; urinary retention may need a **suprapubic catheter**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Acute painful scrotal swelling** in a young man is **torsion until proven otherwise** - urgent surgical review and Doppler, do not delay exploration beyond 6 hours. [CDC 2021]`,`**Ophthalmia neonatorum** (purulent conjunctivitis in the first month): **ceftriaxone 25-50 mg/kg IV/IM single dose** (max 250 mg) for gonococcal; erythromycin 50 mg/kg/day for 14 days for chlamydial; treat both parents. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **doxycycline and tetracyclines are contraindicated** - use **azithromycin 1 g** for chlamydia, ceftriaxone for gonorrhoea, metronidazole is safe for BV and trichomonas, and syphilis is treated **only with penicillin** (desensitise if allergic). [CDC 2021]`,`**Pregnancy screening**: every pregnant woman has **syphilis and HIV testing at the first ANC visit**, ideally with a point-of-care **dual HIV/syphilis rapid test**, under India's push for **elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis (EVTHS)**. [WHO 2021]`,`**HIV co-infection**: the same regimens apply, but herpes is more severe and longer (acyclovir 400 mg TDS for 5-10 days, suppression often needed) and syphilis follow-up serology is more frequent. [CDC 2021]`,`**MSM**: test all exposed sites (urethra, rectum, pharynx) with NAAT; consider **rectal LGV**, and offer **HIV PrEP**, hepatitis A and B and HPV vaccination. [WHO 2021]`,`**Adolescents and children**: an STI in a prepubertal child is **sexual abuse until proven otherwise** - report under the **POCSO Act 2012**; consent and confidentiality rules apply to adolescents. [NACO 2014]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where no microscope exists, syndromic management is the standard; add a **speculum and risk score** for cervicitis because vaginal discharge alone predicts cervical infection poorly. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Women**: PID leads to **tubal infertility (about 10-20% after one episode)**, **ectopic pregnancy** and **chronic pelvic pain**; gonorrhoea and chlamydia cause Fitz-Hugh-Curtis **perihepatitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Men**: epididymo-orchitis, urethral stricture after repeated gonorrhoea, and **reactive arthritis** after chlamydia (HLA-B27). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonates**: ophthalmia neonatorum (blindness), chlamydial pneumonia, congenital syphilis, neonatal herpes (high mortality when disseminated). [CDC 2021]`,`**LGV and donovanosis**: genital elephantiasis (esthiomene), rectal stricture, fistulae; **HPV**: cervical, anal and penile cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Treated bacterial STIs are cured in **over 95%**; the prognosis is limited by **reinfection**, rising **gonococcal resistance** and the lifelong nature of HSV and HIV. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer to the **Suraksha Clinic / DSRC** for laboratory diagnosis, partner services and confidential counselling; link to the **ICTC** for HIV testing. [NACP-V 2021]`,`Review urethral discharge at **7 days**: persistent symptoms mean **non-adherence, reinfection or resistant organisms** - test with culture/NAAT and treat **M. genitalium** (doxycycline 7 days then moxifloxacin 400 mg daily for 7 days) or trichomonas. [CDC 2021]`,`Review genital ulcers at **7 days**: a non-healing ulcer needs **biopsy, dark-field, HSV PCR and HIV testing** and referral. [NACO 2014]`,`Review PID at **72 hours**: no improvement means admission, ultrasound for tubo-ovarian abscess and IV therapy. [CDC 2021]`,`Repeat **syphilis serology at 6 and 12 months** after treatment and **HIV testing at 3 months** if the first test was in the window period. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`**Condoms** used correctly and consistently reduce transmission of gonorrhoea, chlamydia, trichomonas and HIV substantially, with lesser protection against herpes, HPV and syphilis on uncovered skin. [WHO 2021]`,`**HPV vaccination** (see the Adult vaccination topic) and **hepatitis B vaccination** prevent two viral STIs; **voluntary medical male circumcision** reduces female-to-male HIV acquisition by about 60%. [WHO 2021]`,`**Doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis (doxy-PEP)**: **doxycycline 200 mg within 72 hours** of condomless sex reduced syphilis and chlamydia by about two-thirds in MSM and transgender women; CDC recommends it (2024) for those with a bacterial STI in the last year - not part of NACO policy. [CDC 2024]`,`**NACP Phase V (2021-2026)** aims to eliminate **vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis** and to provide free syndromic kits and testing in all DSRCs, with STI services integrated into **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs**. [NACP-V 2021]`,`Report STIs through the **SIMS (Strategic Information Management System)** of NACO; maintain **confidentiality** under the HIV and AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017. [NACO 2017]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2021** - Guidelines for the management of symptomatic STIs: recommends **speculum examination plus point-of-care tests** where available and moves away from purely syndromic treatment of vaginal discharge for cervicitis. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO 2024** - updated treatment recommendations: **ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose** for gonorrhoea, **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days** as preferred chlamydia treatment, and **benzathine penicillin** for syphilis with alternatives during shortages. [WHO 2024]`,`**CDC 2021** STI treatment guidelines: ceftriaxone **500 mg** (no routine co-treatment with azithromycin unless chlamydia is not excluded), metronidazole 7 days for trichomoniasis in women, and **metronidazole added to all PID regimens**. [CDC 2021]`,`**CDC 2024** - doxy-PEP recommended for MSM and transgender women with a recent bacterial STI; concern remains about **tetracycline resistance** in gonococci and commensals. [CDC 2024]`,`**Rising resistance**: ceftriaxone-resistant gonorrhoea (penA mosaic strains) is increasing in Asia - culture and susceptibility in every treatment failure; the NACO kit (2014) still uses oral **cefixime** - note the difference from WHO 2024. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Commonest viva: **'name the NACO kits and their colours'** - Grey, Green, White, Blue, Red, Yellow, Black for kits 1-7 (urethral, vaginal, non-herpetic ulcer, ulcer in penicillin allergy, herpetic, PID, bubo). [NACO 2014]`,`**Advantages of syndromic management**: same-day treatment, no laboratory needed, cheap, can be done by trained paramedics; **disadvantages**: overtreatment, misses asymptomatic infection, poor for cervicitis, promotes resistance. [WHO 2021]`,`Common mistake: treating **vaginal discharge as an STI** and notifying partners for BV or candidiasis - only trichomoniasis and cervicitis need partner treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`Common mistake: **incising a bubo** - aspirate through healthy skin with a wide-bore needle to prevent a sinus. [NACO 2014]`,`Always say **'offer HIV and syphilis testing and do partner management'** at the end of any STI answer. [NACO 2014]`]}],tables:[{heading:`NACO colour-coded STI/RTI kits`,columns:[`Kit (colour)`,`Syndrome`,`Contents`,`Key cautions`],rows:[[`Kit 1 (Grey)`,`Urethral discharge, cervicitis, anorectal discharge, scrotal swelling`,`Azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg, single oral doses`,`Cefixime weaker for pharyngeal gonorrhoea; exclude torsion`],[`Kit 2 (Green)`,`Vaginitis (BV, trichomonas, candida)`,`Secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg, single doses`,`No alcohol for 72 h; metronidazole 7 days preferred in pregnancy`],[`Kit 3 (White)`,`Non-herpetic genital ulcer`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM + azithromycin 1 g`,`Observe 30 min for anaphylaxis; keep adrenaline ready`],[`Kit 4 (Blue)`,`Non-herpetic ulcer, penicillin allergy`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD x 15 days + azithromycin 1 g`,`Doxycycline contraindicated in pregnancy - desensitise instead`],[`Kit 5 (Red)`,`Herpetic genital ulcer`,`Acyclovir 400 mg TDS x 7 days`,`Start within 72 h for best effect; suppression if 6+ recurrences/year`],[`Kit 6 (Yellow)`,`Lower abdominal pain (PID)`,`Cefixime 400 mg single + metronidazole 400 mg BD x 14 days + doxycycline 100 mg BD x 14 days`,`Pregnancy test first; admit if TOA, pregnancy or failure at 72 h`],[`Kit 7 (Black)`,`Inguinal bubo`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD x 21 days + azithromycin 1 g`,`Aspirate, never incise, fluctuant nodes`]]},{heading:`Drug table - aetiological treatment (WHO 2024 / CDC 2021)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone (gonorrhoea)`,`1 g IM (WHO 2024) or 500 mg IM (CDC 2021)`,`Single dose`,`Beta-lactam allergy; rising resistance in Asia`],[`Doxycycline (chlamydia, LGV)`,`100 mg orally BD`,`7 days chlamydia; 21 days LGV`,`Pregnancy, children under 8; photosensitivity, oesophagitis - take upright with water`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g orally`,`Single dose (chlamydia in pregnancy, chancroid); weekly x 3+ for donovanosis`,`GI upset; QT prolongation; M. genitalium macrolide resistance`],[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`2.4 MU deep IM (1.2 MU each buttock)`,`Single dose early syphilis; weekly x 3 late latent`,`Anaphylaxis (rare), Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, never IV`],[`Metronidazole`,`500 mg orally BD`,`7 days (trichomonas in women, BV)`,`Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; metallic taste`],[`Fluconazole`,`150 mg orally`,`Single dose; 3 doses 72 h apart in severe VVC`,`Avoid in first trimester (use topical clotrimazole); drug interactions`],[`Acyclovir`,`400 mg orally TDS`,`7-10 days first episode; 400 mg BD suppression`,`Reduce dose in CKD; IV form can crystallise in renal tubules - hydrate`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of genital ulcer disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Syphilis`,`Herpes`,`Chancroid`,`LGV`,`Donovanosis`],rows:[[`Organism`,`Treponema pallidum`,`HSV-2 / HSV-1`,`Haemophilus ducreyi`,`C. trachomatis L1-L3`,`Klebsiella granulomatis`],[`Incubation`,`9-90 days (mean 21)`,`2-7 days`,`3-10 days`,`3-30 days`,`1-4 weeks (up to 6 months)`],[`Ulcer`,`Single, indurated, clean base`,`Multiple grouped vesicles then shallow erosions`,`Multiple, soft, ragged, purulent`,`Small, transient, often unnoticed`,`Beefy red, bleeds on touch, painless`],[`Pain`,`Painless`,`Painful`,`Very painful`,`Painless`,`Painless`],[`Nodes`,`Bilateral, rubbery, non-tender`,`Bilateral, tender`,`Unilateral, tender, suppurative bubo`,`Large painful bubo, groove sign`,`Pseudobubo (subcutaneous granuloma)`],[`Test`,`Dark-field, RPR + TPHA`,`HSV PCR`,`Culture/PCR (rarely available)`,`Chlamydia NAAT with LGV genotyping`,`Donovan bodies on tissue smear`],[`Treatment`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM`,`Acyclovir 400 mg TDS 7-10 days`,`Azithromycin 1 g single`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD 21 days`,`Azithromycin 1 g weekly 3+ weeks`]]},{heading:`Bedside investigations of vaginal discharge`,columns:[`Test`,`Bacterial vaginosis`,`Trichomoniasis`,`Candidiasis`,`Normal`],rows:[[`Discharge`,`Thin, grey, homogeneous, fishy`,`Frothy, yellow-green, profuse`,`Thick, white, curdy`,`Clear or white, scant`],[`pH`,`Over 4.5`,`Over 4.5 (often 5-6)`,`4.0-4.5`,`3.8-4.5`],[`Whiff test (10% KOH)`,`Positive`,`Often positive`,`Negative`,`Negative`],[`Microscopy`,`Clue cells over 20%, few lactobacilli`,`Motile trichomonads (sensitivity 50-60%), many WBC`,`Budding yeast, pseudohyphae on KOH`,`Lactobacilli, few WBC`],[`Partner treatment`,`No`,`Yes`,`No (unless symptomatic balanitis)`,`Not applicable`]]},{heading:`Syndromic vs aetiological management`,columns:[`Aspect`,`Syndromic`,`Aetiological`],rows:[[`Basis`,`Symptoms and signs grouped into a syndrome`,`Identification of the organism`],[`Timing of treatment`,`First visit`,`After results (unless point-of-care)`],[`Cost and setting`,`Cheap, any PHC, paramedics can use`,`Needs laboratory, trained staff, NAAT expensive`],[`Accuracy`,`Good for urethral discharge and GUD; poor for cervicitis`,`High; detects asymptomatic infection`],[`Drawbacks`,`Overtreatment, resistance, misses asymptomatic cases`,`Delay, loss to follow-up, cost`],[`Best use`,`Primary care, where no lab exists`,`DSRC, medical college, treatment failure, surveillance`]]}],redFlags:[`Acute painful scrotal swelling in a young man - suspect testicular torsion, urgent surgical exploration`,`Lower abdominal pain with a positive pregnancy test or shock - ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise`,`PID with tubo-ovarian abscess, high fever, vomiting or no response to oral therapy in 72 hours`,`Fever with arthritis and pustular skin lesions - disseminated gonococcal infection needing IV ceftriaxone`,`Primary herpes with urinary retention, severe headache or neck stiffness`,`Purulent conjunctivitis in a neonate - ophthalmia neonatorum`,`STI in a prepubertal child - sexual abuse, report under POCSO`,`Non-healing genital ulcer beyond 2-3 weeks of treatment - biopsy for carcinoma and test for HIV`],pearls:[`NACO kits 1-7 colours in order: **Grey, Green, White, Blue, Red, Yellow, Black** - 'Good Girls Wear Blue, Red, Yellow, Black'.`,`Painful ulcer: herpes and chancroid; painless ulcer: syphilis, LGV and donovanosis.`,`The **groove sign** is LGV; the **pseudobubo** is donovanosis; the **button-like induration** is syphilis.`,`Gram stain with **2 or more PMN per oil-immersion field** confirms urethritis in a man.`,`Partners are treated for trichomoniasis and cervicitis, not for BV or candidiasis.`,`The **4 Cs** of STI care: compliance, counselling, condoms, contact tracing.`,`Aspirate a bubo through healthy skin - never incise.`,`Doxycycline is now the preferred chlamydia drug (WHO 2024); azithromycin in pregnancy.`,`Every STI patient gets an **HIV and syphilis test** - an STI is an HIV risk marker.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old truck driver has purulent urethral discharge for 3 days. At the PHC there is no laboratory. Which NACO kit should be given?`,options:[`Kit 2 (Green) - secnidazole and fluconazole`,`Kit 1 (Grey) - azithromycin 1 g and cefixime 400 mg`,`Kit 3 (White) - benzathine penicillin and azithromycin`,`Kit 7 (Black) - doxycycline for 21 days and azithromycin`],answer:1,explanation:`Urethral discharge is covered by the Grey kit, which treats both gonorrhoea (cefixime) and chlamydia (azithromycin) at the first visit. The Green kit is for vaginitis, the White kit is for non-herpetic genital ulcer, and the Black kit is for inguinal bubo.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q2`,stem:`A woman has thin grey vaginal discharge with a fishy odour, pH 5.0, and clue cells on wet mount. Which statement about her management is correct?`,options:[`Her partner must be treated to prevent recurrence`,`Fluconazole 150 mg single dose is the treatment of choice`,`Metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days is appropriate, and the partner need not be treated`,`She needs ceftriaxone for presumed cervicitis`],answer:2,explanation:`These are Amsel criteria for bacterial vaginosis, an endogenous loss of lactobacilli treated with metronidazole for 7 days; partner treatment does not reduce recurrence. Fluconazole treats candidiasis, which has a normal pH, and ceftriaxone is for gonococcal cervicitis, not suggested here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q3`,stem:`A man has multiple painful, soft, ragged genital ulcers with a purulent base and a tender, fluctuant unilateral inguinal node. The most likely organism is:`,options:[`Haemophilus ducreyi`,`Treponema pallidum`,`Klebsiella granulomatis`,`Chlamydia trachomatis L2`],answer:0,explanation:`Painful soft ragged ulcers with a suppurative unilateral bubo are classic chancroid caused by H. ducreyi. Syphilis gives a single painless indurated ulcer, donovanosis a painless beefy-red ulcer with a pseudobubo, and LGV a transient painless ulcer followed by a large bubo with the groove sign.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q4`,stem:`According to WHO 2024 STI treatment recommendations, the preferred treatment for uncomplicated urogenital chlamydia in a non-pregnant adult is:`,options:[`Azithromycin 1 g orally single dose`,`Amoxicillin 500 mg thrice daily for 7 days`,`Ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose`,`Doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days`],answer:3,explanation:`WHO 2024 (and CDC 2021) prefer doxycycline for 7 days because it cures rectal chlamydia better than single-dose azithromycin. Azithromycin remains the choice in pregnancy, amoxicillin is a pregnancy alternative only, and ceftriaxone does not treat intracellular chlamydia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q5`,stem:`A 19-year-old man has sudden severe left testicular pain for 2 hours with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex. He had unprotected sex last week. The next step is:`,options:[`Give the Grey kit and review in 7 days`,`Urgent surgical referral for possible testicular torsion`,`Ultrasound after 48 hours if pain persists`,`Doxycycline for 10 days for epididymo-orchitis`],answer:1,explanation:`Sudden pain, a high-riding testis and loss of the cremasteric reflex mean torsion, a surgical emergency where testicular salvage falls sharply after 6 hours. Treating it as STI-related epididymo-orchitis with the Grey kit or doxycycline, or delaying imaging, risks losing the testis.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the syndromic approach to sexually transmitted infections. Give the NACO colour-coded kits and outline the management of genital ulcer disease.`,openingLines:[`Syndromic management treats an STI on the first visit by the group of symptoms and signs (syndrome) using a flow chart that covers all common causes, without waiting for laboratory confirmation.`,`India delivers it through Suraksha Clinics (DSRCs) under NACP Phase V using seven colour-coded pre-packed kits, completed by the 4 Cs - compliance, counselling, condoms and contact treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale and burden`,points:[`About 374 million curable STIs a year globally; about 6% of Indian adults have an STI/RTI.`,`STIs raise HIV risk 2-4 fold and cause infertility, ectopic pregnancy and congenital syphilis.`,`Syndromic approach: same-day treatment, cheap, no laboratory; drawbacks - overtreatment, misses asymptomatic and cervical infection.`]},{heading:`The syndromes and NACO kits`,points:[`Kit 1 Grey - urethral discharge/cervicitis: azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg.`,`Kit 2 Green - vaginitis: secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg.`,`Kit 3 White - non-herpetic GUD: benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM + azithromycin 1 g; Kit 4 Blue - same with penicillin allergy: doxycycline 100 mg BD 15 days + azithromycin 1 g.`,`Kit 5 Red - herpetic GUD: acyclovir 400 mg TDS 7 days.`,`Kit 6 Yellow - PID: cefixime 400 mg + metronidazole 400 mg BD 14 days + doxycycline 100 mg BD 14 days; Kit 7 Black - bubo: doxycycline 100 mg BD 21 days + azithromycin 1 g.`]},{heading:`Genital ulcer - history and examination`,points:[`Sexual history with 5 Ps; pain, number, vesicles, recurrences, nodes.`,`Painful grouped vesicles - herpes; single painless indurated - syphilis; painful ragged with bubo - chancroid; beefy red - donovanosis; transient with groove sign - LGV.`]},{heading:`Genital ulcer - flow chart`,points:[`Vesicles or recurrent history: treat herpes (Kit 5).`,`No vesicles: treat syphilis and chancroid together (Kit 3, or Kit 4 if allergic and not pregnant).`,`Review at 7 days; non-healing ulcer - biopsy, dark-field, HSV PCR, HIV test, refer.`]},{heading:`Aetiological confirmation`,points:[`RPR/VDRL with TPHA, HSV PCR, Donovan bodies on tissue smear; HIV test for all.`]},{heading:`The 4 Cs and partner management`,points:[`Partners in last 3 months (primary syphilis) treated epidemiologically; abstain 7 days; condoms provided.`,`Follow-up syphilis serology at 6 and 12 months; HIV retest at 3 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of genital ulcer disease: ulcer, vesicles yes or no, Kit 5 vs Kit 3/4, review at 7 days`,`Table of NACO kits 1-7 with colours`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, rationale, advantages and drawbacks`,marks:2},{part:`NACO kits table`,marks:3},{part:`GUD flow chart and differential`,marks:3},{part:`4 Cs, partner management, follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`syndromic management`,`NACO kits`,`genital ulcer`,`benzathine penicillin`,`partner notification`]},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between the causes of vaginal discharge: bacterial vaginosis, trichomoniasis and candidiasis.`,openingLines:[`Vaginal discharge is the commonest RTI syndrome in women; most is due to endogenous vaginitis (bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis) rather than an STI.`,`Only trichomoniasis among the three is sexually transmitted and needs partner treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`BV: thin grey homogeneous fishy discharge, little itch; trichomoniasis: frothy yellow-green, soreness, strawberry cervix; candidiasis: thick curdy white, intense itch, normal pH.`]},{heading:`Bedside tests`,points:[`pH over 4.5 in BV and trichomoniasis; 4.0-4.5 in candida.`,`Whiff positive in BV; clue cells over 20% (Amsel 3 of 4; Nugent 7-10); motile trichomonads on saline mount; pseudohyphae on KOH.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`BV: metronidazole 500 mg BD 7 days; trichomoniasis: metronidazole 500 mg BD 7 days (women) plus partner; candida: fluconazole 150 mg single dose. Syndromic: Kit 2 Green.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table of BV, trichomoniasis and candidiasis`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Bedside tests`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and partner`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Amsel criteria`,`clue cells`,`whiff test`,`trichomonas`,`candida`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Sexually transmitted infections: overview and clinical approach`,`NACO. National Guidelines on Prevention, Management and Control of Reproductive Tract Infections including Sexually Transmitted Infections, 2014`,`NACO. National AIDS and STD Control Programme Phase V (2021-2026) strategy document`,`WHO. Guidelines for the management of symptomatic sexually transmitted infections, 2021`,`WHO. Recommendations for the treatment of Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis and Treponema pallidum (syphilis), 2024`,`CDC. Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021 (MMWR Recomm Rep 70(4))`,`CDC. Clinical guidelines on the use of doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis for bacterial STI prevention, 2024`]});var o=[];o.push({id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses`,title:`Herpes viruses: HSV, VZV, EBV and CMV`,oneLiner:`Human herpesviruses are **double-stranded DNA viruses that establish lifelong latency** after primary infection - **HSV and VZV in sensory ganglia**, **EBV in B cells**, **CMV in myeloid cells** - so disease is either a primary illness or a **reactivation when cell-mediated immunity falls**; the alpha-herpesviruses respond to **acyclovir/valacyclovir** (viral thymidine kinase), while CMV needs **ganciclovir/valganciclovir** or letermovir.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`HSV-1`,`HSV-2`,`herpes simplex`,`herpes labialis`,`genital herpes`,`HSV encephalitis`,`varicella`,`herpes zoster`,`shingles`,`Hutchinson sign`,`postherpetic neuralgia`,`Ramsay Hunt`,`infectious mononucleosis`,`Paul-Bunnell`,`Monospot`,`EBV VCA EBNA`,`cytomegalovirus`,`congenital CMV`,`acyclovir`,`valacyclovir`,`valganciclovir`,`letermovir`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`The **Herpesviridae** that infect humans are eight enveloped **double-stranded DNA viruses** (HHV-1 to HHV-8); the defining biological property is **latency with periodic reactivation** for the life of the host. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV-1** infects about **3.8 billion people under 50 (64%)** worldwide and **HSV-2** about **520 million aged 15-49 (13%)**; genital herpes from either type affects about **846 million** people aged 15-49. [WHO 2024]`,`In India, **HSV-2 is the commonest cause of genital ulcer disease** in STI clinics, having overtaken chancroid and syphilis; HSV-2 roughly **triples the risk of acquiring HIV**. [NACO STI/RTI 2024]`,`**Varicella** seroprevalence in Indian adults is lower than in temperate countries (about **70-85%** by early adulthood), so a substantial pool of **susceptible adults and pregnant women** develops severe adult chickenpox. [IAP 2023]`,`**Herpes zoster** has a lifetime risk of about **1 in 3**; incidence rises steeply after **50 years** (about 10 per 1000 person-years after 80) and is **15-25 times higher in HIV**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBV** seroprevalence exceeds **90% of adults**; in India most children are infected silently before 5 years, so classic **infectious mononucleosis (IM)** is seen mainly in adolescents and young adults of urban, higher-income families. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV** seroprevalence in Indian adults is **over 90%**, so most transplant recipients are R+; **congenital CMV** is the commonest congenital infection worldwide (about **0.5-1% of live births**) and the leading **non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HHV-6 and HHV-7** cause roseola infantum (exanthem subitum); **HHV-8** causes **Kaposi sarcoma**, primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease - mentioned here only to complete the family. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Virology, transmission and risk factors`,points:[`**Alpha-herpesvirinae** (HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV): short replication cycle, lytic in epithelium, **latency in sensory (dorsal root and cranial nerve) ganglia** - hence vesicles in a nerve distribution. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beta-herpesvirinae** (CMV, HHV-6, HHV-7): slow cycle, **cytomegaly with owl-eye inclusions**, latency in **monocytes, myeloid progenitors and endothelium** - hence transmission by blood, organs and marrow. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gamma-herpesvirinae** (EBV, HHV-8): **lymphotropic and oncogenic**; EBV enters B cells through **CD21 (complement receptor 2)** and stays latent in memory B cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transmission**: HSV by direct mucosal contact, often from **asymptomatic shedding** (most genital transmission); VZV by **airborne droplets and vesicle fluid** (secondary attack rate about 90% in household contacts); EBV by **saliva (kissing disease)**; CMV by saliva, urine, sex, breast milk, transplacental, blood and organs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zoster risk factors**: age over 50, **HIV**, haematological cancer, transplant, **high-dose steroids, JAK inhibitors** (tofacitinib, baricitinib), anti-TNF, diabetes and CKD. [ACIP 2022]`,`**CMV disease risk in transplant** is highest in **donor-positive/recipient-negative (D+/R-)** solid organ transplant and in **R+ allogeneic stem-cell transplant**; anti-thymocyte globulin and alemtuzumab raise risk further. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**HSV reactivation triggers**: fever (herpes febrilis), UV light, menstruation, stress, trauma, dental work and immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella incubation** is **10-21 days** (up to 28 days after VZIG); infectivity runs from **48 hours before the rash until all lesions are crusted** (usually 5-7 days). [Red Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and how the drugs work`,points:[`**Latency**: HSV and VZV travel retrograde along sensory axons to ganglia and express only **latency-associated transcripts (LAT)**; falling **T-cell immunity** allows reactivation and anterograde spread back to skin - explaining **dermatomal zoster** and recurrent lip or genital lesions at the same site. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zoster pain** comes from **haemorrhagic inflammation of the ganglion** and peripheral nerve; persistent central sensitisation and loss of large-fibre inhibition produce **postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)** with allodynia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ramsay Hunt syndrome** is VZV reactivation in the **geniculate ganglion**: lower motor neurone facial palsy, ear pain and vesicles in the external auditory canal, sometimes with vertigo and deafness (CN VIII). [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV encephalitis** (adults, nearly always HSV-1) is a **haemorrhagic necrotising encephalitis of the medial temporal and orbitofrontal lobes**, explaining olfactory hallucinations, personality change, aphasia and temporal-lobe seizures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IM is an immune disease**: EBV-infected B cells drive a massive **CD8 T-cell expansion** - the **atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells)**; polyclonal B-cell activation produces **heterophile antibodies** that agglutinate sheep and horse red cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acyclovir** is a guanosine analogue that is **monophosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase** (so it is active only in infected cells), then converted to the triphosphate, which **inhibits viral DNA polymerase and terminates the chain**; oral bioavailability is only **15-30%**, which is why **valacyclovir** (prodrug, about 55%) allows 2-3 doses a day. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ganciclovir** is phosphorylated by the CMV **UL97 kinase**; resistance arises from **UL97 or UL54 (polymerase)** mutations; **foscarnet** acts directly on the polymerase and needs no activation, so it works against TK-deficient HSV and UL97-mutant CMV. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Letermovir** blocks the **CMV terminase complex (UL56)** and is not myelotoxic but has no activity against HSV/VZV (add acyclovir); **maribavir** inhibits UL97 and is used for refractory or resistant CMV - it **antagonises ganciclovir**, so never co-prescribe. [ECIL 2019]`,`**Why CMV causes graft and marrow damage**: CMV is immunomodulatory - it raises risk of **acute rejection, opportunistic infections (Pneumocystis, fungi), EBV-PTLD** and graft loss (the **indirect effects**). [AST IDCOP 2019]`]},{heading:`Classification of clinical syndromes`,points:[`**HSV**: primary gingivostomatitis, herpes labialis, **genital herpes** (first-episode primary, first-episode non-primary, recurrent), herpetic whitlow, **herpes gladiatorum**, keratitis, **eczema herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption)**, encephalitis, meningitis (HSV-2, Mollaret), neonatal herpes and disseminated disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonatal HSV** is classified as **SEM (skin, eye, mouth)**, **CNS disease** and **disseminated disease** - the class decides the duration of IV acyclovir (14 versus 21 days). [Red Book 2024]`,`**VZV**: primary varicella, **herpes zoster** (dermatomal, multi-dermatomal, disseminated - over 20 vesicles outside the primary and adjacent dermatomes), **herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO)**, Ramsay Hunt, **zoster sine herpete**, and VZV vasculopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBV**: IM, chronic active EBV, **EBV-HLH**, oral hairy leukoplakia, and EBV-associated cancers - **Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, NK/T-cell lymphoma, PTLD**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV in transplant**: **CMV infection** (virus detected by PCR) versus **CMV disease** - either **CMV syndrome** (fever, malaise, cytopenia plus DNAemia) or **tissue-invasive disease** (colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, retinitis); this distinction decides pre-emptive versus treatment doses. [CMV Drug Development Forum 2017]`,`**Congenital CMV** is classified as **symptomatic** (moderate-severe: microcephaly, chorioretinitis, CNS calcification, hepatosplenomegaly, petechiae) versus **asymptomatic or isolated SNHL** - only moderate-severe disease gets valganciclovir. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - HSV`,points:[`**Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis** in a child: high fever, drooling, painful **vesicles and ulcers on gums, tongue and palate**, cervical nodes; **gingivitis** distinguishes it from herpangina (posterior pharynx only). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpes labialis**: prodrome of **tingling or burning for 6-24 hours**, then grouped vesicles at the vermilion border that crust in 7-10 days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**First-episode primary genital herpes**: multiple **painful grouped vesicles that ulcerate**, bilateral tender inguinal nodes, fever, dysuria (may cause **urinary retention** from sacral radiculitis) and aseptic meningitis; lasts **2-3 weeks** untreated. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Recurrent genital herpes** is milder, unilateral, shorter (**5-10 days**), with prodrome; HSV-2 recurs far more often genitally (about **4 per year**) than HSV-1 (under 1 per year). [CDC STI 2021]`,`**HSV keratitis**: red painful eye, photophobia, **dendritic ulcer staining with fluorescein**; repeated stromal episodes are a major cause of **corneal blindness**; topical steroids on an epithelial dendrite cause a **geographic ulcer**. [AAO 2023]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: acute fever, headache, **altered behaviour, aphasia, focal seizures** over days; untreated mortality is about **70%**, falling to about **20%** with early acyclovir. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eczema herpeticum**: monomorphic **punched-out erosions** on atopic or burned skin with fever - a dermatological emergency needing systemic acyclovir. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - VZV (varicella and zoster)`,points:[`**Varicella**: 1-2 days of mild prodrome (more in adults), then an intensely itchy rash that **starts on the trunk and spreads centrifugally**, evolving macule-papule-**dewdrop vesicle on a rose petal**-pustule-crust in crops, so **lesions of all stages coexist**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adult varicella** is more severe: **varicella pneumonia** in 1 in 400 adults (higher in **smokers and pregnancy**), presenting on days 3-5 with cough, breathlessness and hypoxia. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Herpes zoster**: **prodromal pain, burning or itch for 2-5 days** (mistaken for MI, renal colic or cholecystitis), then a **unilateral dermatomal vesicular band that does not cross the midline**; thoracic dermatomes are commonest (over 50%), then ophthalmic V1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO)**: V1 rash on forehead and upper eyelid; **Hutchinson sign** (vesicles on the **tip or side of the nose**, nasociliary branch) predicts eye involvement - conjunctivitis, keratitis, **anterior uveitis**, raised intraocular pressure, retinitis. [AAO 2023]`,`**Zoster in a young adult** or multi-dermatomal zoster is a **marker of HIV** - offer an HIV test. [NACO 2021]`,`**Disseminated zoster** or **visceral VZV** (pneumonitis, hepatitis, encephalitis) occurs in transplant, haematological cancer and advanced HIV; it may present as severe abdominal pain before any rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VZV vasculopathy**: stroke or TIA weeks to months after HZO (contralateral hemiparesis from carotid/MCA involvement); risk of stroke is raised for about **3 months after zoster**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - EBV and CMV`,points:[`**Infectious mononucleosis** (age 15-24): **fever, exudative pharyngitis, posterior and anterior cervical lymphadenopathy** (the triad), marked fatigue, **splenomegaly in about 50%**, hepatomegaly, **palatal petechiae** and **eyelid oedema (Hoagland sign)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`An **amoxicillin/ampicillin rash** - itchy maculopapular, from immune complexes, not true penicillin allergy - follows in about **30%** (older series up to 90%); it should **not be labelled as penicillin allergy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IM complications**: **splenic rupture (0.1-0.5%, usually in the first 3 weeks)**, airway obstruction from tonsillar hypertrophy, autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (cold, anti-i), thrombocytopenia, hepatitis, Guillain-Barre, encephalitis, and **EBV-HLH**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fatigue** after IM can last for months; IM is a recognised trigger of **post-infectious fatigue and ME/CFS** (see the Post-acute infection syndromes topic). [NICE NG206 2021]`,`**CMV mononucleosis** in the immunocompetent: prolonged fever (often 2-3 weeks), malaise, **mild hepatitis**, atypical lymphocytes, but **little pharyngitis or lymphadenopathy** and a **negative heterophile test**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV in transplant** peaks **1-6 months after transplant** (or after stopping prophylaxis - **late-onset CMV**): fever, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, raised transaminases, colitis with diarrhoea and bleeding, pneumonitis (worst in lung and stem-cell transplant). [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**CMV in advanced HIV (CD4 under 50)**: **retinitis** (floaters, field loss; fundus shows **haemorrhagic necrosis - 'pizza pie' or 'cottage cheese and ketchup'**), oesophagitis with large shallow ulcers, colitis, polyradiculopathy and adrenalitis. [NACO 2021]`,`**Congenital CMV** at birth: **SGA, microcephaly, 'blueberry muffin' purpura, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, chorioretinitis, periventricular calcification**; about **10-15% are symptomatic** and up to **10-15% of asymptomatic** babies develop later hearing loss. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base** in a dermatome = zoster; in the same site repeatedly = recurrent HSV (**zosteriform HSV** can mimic zoster but recurs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hutchinson sign** (nasal tip vesicles) means about a **2-4-fold higher risk of ocular involvement** - examine with fluorescein and refer to ophthalmology the same day. [AAO 2023]`,`**Polymorphic rash with lesions in all stages, centripetal (trunk-heavy) distribution, and involvement of scalp and mucosa** = varicella; contrast smallpox/mpox with **lesions in one stage, centrifugal, on palms and soles**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Posterior cervical nodes plus palatal petechiae plus splenomegaly** in a febrile young adult = IM until proved otherwise; **gently palpate the spleen** and avoid vigorous examination. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Facial palsy with vesicles in the ear canal or on the palate** = Ramsay Hunt; it has a **worse facial recovery than Bell palsy** (about 50-70% full recovery). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpetic whitlow** - painful vesicles on a fingertip - must **not be incised** (risk of spread and secondary infection). [Harrison 22e]`,`In an immunosuppressed patient with fever, **look at the fundus** (CMV retinitis), mouth (oral hairy leukoplakia - EBV, a white non-removable lateral tongue lesion) and whole skin (disseminated zoster). [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Genital ulcer**: herpes (multiple painful vesicles/shallow ulcers), **primary syphilis** (single painless indurated chancre), **chancroid** (painful ragged undermined ulcer, suppurative bubo), LGV and donovanosis - treat syndromically as per NACO (see the STI topic). [NACO STI/RTI 2024]`,`**Zoster before the rash** mimics angina, biliary or renal colic, appendicitis and radiculopathy - a burning unilateral dermatomal pain with hyperaesthesia in an older adult should prompt daily skin checks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella versus mpox**: mpox has **prominent lymphadenopathy**, deep-seated **monomorphic** lesions, palms and soles, and anogenital lesions; varicella is pleomorphic, itchy, without marked nodes. [WHO 2024]`,`**Varicella versus hand-foot-mouth disease**: HFMD has oval grey vesicles on palms, soles and buttocks with oral ulcers in a young child, without truncal crops. [IAP 2023]`,`**IM-like illness (heterophile-negative)**: **CMV, acute HIV seroconversion, toxoplasmosis**, HHV-6, adenovirus, streptococcal pharyngitis, and drug reactions (DRESS); **always consider acute HIV** - order HIV antigen/antibody test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exudative pharyngitis**: group A streptococcus (no splenomegaly, no posterior nodes, Centor score), **diphtheria** (grey adherent membrane that bleeds on removal, bull neck) - critical in unvaccinated Indian children. [IAP 2023]`,`**HSV encephalitis** versus Japanese encephalitis/AES, scrub typhus meningoencephalitis, autoimmune (anti-NMDAR) encephalitis - **start acyclovir empirically** in any encephalitis while CSF PCR is pending. [BIA/ABN 2012]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Most HSV and zoster is a **clinical diagnosis**; when needed, **swab the base of an unroofed vesicle for HSV/VZV PCR** - more sensitive than viral culture or a **Tzanck smear** (multinucleated giant cells, cannot tell HSV from VZV). [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Type-specific HSV serology** (gG-based IgG) helps only in recurrent undiagnosed genital symptoms or a partner of an HSV-2-positive person; **HSV IgM is unreliable** and should not be ordered. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: **CSF PCR for HSV** (sensitivity about 96%, specificity 99%; may be negative in the first 72 hours - repeat if suspicion persists); CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, raised protein, red cells; **MRI FLAIR temporal-lobe hyperintensity**; EEG periodic lateralised discharges. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**IM**: CBC with **lymphocytes 50% or more and atypical lymphocytes 10% or more** (Hoagland), raised ALT in 80-90%; **heterophile antibody (Monospot/Paul-Bunnell)** sensitivity about **85%** but **false negative in 25% in week 1** and in most children under 4. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBV-specific serology**: **VCA IgM positive with EBNA negative = acute infection**; VCA IgG plus EBNA IgG = past infection (EBNA appears **6-12 weeks** after onset); EBV PCR in blood is used for PTLD monitoring and EBV-HLH, not for routine IM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV in the immunocompromised**: quantitative **CMV DNA PCR in plasma** (reported in **IU/mL** to the WHO standard) for diagnosis, pre-emptive thresholds and response; tissue-invasive disease needs **biopsy with immunohistochemistry** (owl-eye inclusions) - colitis can occur with low or undetectable blood PCR. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**CMV in pregnancy**: **CMV IgM is not diagnostic** (persists months, false positives); use **IgG seroconversion** or **IgM positive with low IgG avidity** (under 30% suggests primary infection within 3 months); confirm fetal infection by **amniotic fluid PCR** after **20-21 weeks and 6-8 weeks after maternal infection**. [ECCI 2023]`,`**Congenital CMV**: **saliva or urine CMV PCR within the first 21 days** of life (after 21 days it cannot distinguish congenital from postnatal infection; dried blood spot PCR can be used retrospectively); a positive saliva test is confirmed on urine. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**First-episode genital herpes**: **acyclovir 400 mg oral three times daily** or **valacyclovir 1 g twice daily** for **7-10 days** (extend if not healed); saline bathing, analgesia, lidocaine gel, pass urine in a warm bath; screen for HIV and syphilis. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Recurrent genital herpes (episodic)**: start within 24 hours of prodrome - **valacyclovir 500 mg twice daily for 3 days** or **1 g once daily for 5 days**, or **acyclovir 800 mg twice daily for 5 days** or 800 mg three times daily for 2 days. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Suppressive therapy** for 6 or more recurrences a year (or to cut transmission): **valacyclovir 500 mg once daily** (1 g once daily if 10 or more recurrences) or **acyclovir 400 mg twice daily**; reduces recurrences by 70-80% and transmission by about 50%; review annually. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Herpes labialis**: **valacyclovir 2 g twice daily for 1 day** at prodrome; topical acyclovir cream adds little. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpes zoster**: within **72 hours of rash onset** (or later if new vesicles are still forming, eye involvement or immunosuppression) - **valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days**, **acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days** or famciclovir 500 mg three times daily for 7 days; antivirals speed healing and reduce acute pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zoster pain**: paracetamol plus NSAID, then tramadol or a short opioid for severe acute pain; adding **gabapentin or amitriptyline** early is reasonable; **oral steroids do not prevent PHN** and are not routine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella in a healthy child**: supportive - paracetamol (**never aspirin - Reye syndrome**; avoid NSAIDs - linked to invasive group A streptococcal infection), calamine, antihistamine, nails cut; **acyclovir 20 mg/kg (max 800 mg) four times daily for 5 days** for those over 12 years, adolescents, secondary household cases and chronic skin or lung disease. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Varicella in an adult**: **acyclovir 800 mg five times daily or valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days** if started **within 24 hours of rash**; exclude from work or school **until all lesions crust**. [Red Book 2024]`,`**IM**: rest, fluids, paracetamol, saline gargles; **no amoxicillin**; **avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for at least 3-4 weeks** (longer if spleen still palpable); **acyclovir and routine steroids are not indicated**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: suspected encephalitis, HZO with eye signs, disseminated or visceral zoster, varicella pneumonia, varicella or primary HSV in pregnancy with complications, any herpes infection in a neonate or immunocompromised host, IM with airway obstruction or suspected splenic rupture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly for 14-21 days** (use ideal body weight in obesity), started **within 6 hours of arrival** without waiting for MRI or PCR; repeat CSF PCR at end of therapy in complicated cases. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**Acyclovir safety**: infuse over **1 hour** with good **hydration** (crystal nephropathy); **reduce dose for CrCl under 50 mL/min**; watch for neurotoxicity (confusion, tremor, myoclonus) in elderly and CKD patients - easily mistaken for worsening encephalitis. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Severe varicella or disseminated zoster** (pneumonia, encephalitis, hepatitis, immunocompromised): **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** for 7-10 days, switch to oral when afebrile and no new lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HZO**: oral valacyclovir/acyclovir for **7-10 days** plus **same-day ophthalmology**; topical corticosteroids for stromal keratitis or uveitis **only under ophthalmologist** with antiviral cover; cycloplegics for pain. [AAO 2023]`,`**IM with impending airway obstruction**: **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day (or dexamethasone)** tapered over 1-2 weeks, ENT review; steroids also for severe haemolytic anaemia or thrombocytopenia. **Splenic rupture** (left upper quadrant pain, shoulder tip pain, shock) - resuscitate and call surgery; non-operative management in stable cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV disease treatment**: **ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly** or **valganciclovir 900 mg oral twice daily** (non-severe disease) for **at least 2 weeks and until 1-2 negative PCRs a week apart**, then consider secondary prophylaxis; renal dose adjustment is mandatory; monitor CBC twice weekly (**neutropenia**). [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Resistant or refractory CMV** (rising viral load after 2 weeks of adequate therapy): send **UL97/UL54 genotyping**, reduce immunosuppression, switch to **foscarnet 90 mg/kg IV 12-hourly** (nephrotoxic, Ca/Mg/K loss, genital ulcers) or **maribavir 400 mg twice daily**. [AST IDCOP 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy - genital herpes**: primary infection in the **third trimester** carries a neonatal transmission risk of **30-50%** versus **under 1-3%** for recurrent disease; give acyclovir for the episode, **suppressive acyclovir 400 mg three times daily from 36 weeks**, and offer **caesarean section** if primary lesions or prodrome are present at labour (or first episode within 6 weeks of delivery). [BASHH/RCOG 2014]`,`**Pregnancy - varicella**: give **oral acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days** if presenting **within 24 hours of rash** (from 20 weeks; consider before 20 weeks); admit if chest symptoms, haemorrhagic rash or neurological signs; **congenital varicella syndrome** risk is about **0.4% before 13 weeks and 2% at 13-20 weeks**. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Neonatal varicella**: maternal rash **from 5 days before to 2 days after delivery** exposes the baby without protective antibody (mortality up to 30% untreated) - give **VZIG** to the baby and IV acyclovir if lesions appear. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Varicella exposure in a susceptible pregnant woman or immunocompromised person**: check VZV IgG; if negative, give **VZIG within 10 days** of exposure, or **oral acyclovir/valacyclovir from day 7 to day 14 after exposure** (preferred by UKHSA since 2019; guidance reissued 2023). [UKHSA Green Book 2023]`,`**Congenital CMV**: **oral valganciclovir 16 mg/kg twice daily for 6 months**, started within the first month, for **moderate to severe symptomatic disease**; improves hearing and neurodevelopmental outcome; monitor neutrophils weekly for 6 weeks then monthly. [ECCI 2023]`,`**Primary maternal CMV in the periconception period or first trimester**: **valacyclovir 8 g/day (2 g four times daily)** until amniocentesis reduces vertical transmission (Shahar-Nissan RCT 2020); **CMV hyperimmune globulin is not recommended** routinely (negative RCTs). [ECCI 2023]`,`**Transplant**: **antiviral prophylaxis** (valganciclovir 900 mg once daily for 3-6 months; **6 months in kidney D+/R-**, 6-12 months in lung) or **pre-emptive therapy** (weekly CMV PCR for 12 weeks, treat above a centre threshold); **letermovir 480 mg daily** to day 100 (extendable to day 200) in **CMV R+ allogeneic HSCT** and an alternative to valganciclovir in **kidney D+/R-** (no myelotoxicity). [AST IDCOP 2019; FDA 2023]`,`**CKD**: acyclovir, valacyclovir, ganciclovir and valganciclovir are renally cleared - **dose by creatinine clearance**; valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for zoster becomes 1 g twice daily at CrCl 30-49 and 1 g daily at 10-29 mL/min. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**HIV**: zoster is treated as usual but for **7-10 days or until crusted**; CMV retinitis is treated with valganciclovir induction plus **early ART** (within 2 weeks), watching for immune recovery uveitis; acyclovir-resistant HSV needs foscarnet. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)** - pain persisting **90 days or more after rash onset** - affects about **10-18% of zoster patients**, rising to over 30% after 80; risk factors are **age over 50, severe acute pain, severe rash, prodromal pain and HZO**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PHN treatment ladder**: **5% lidocaine plaster** (up to 3 plasters, 12 hours on/12 off) for focal allodynia; **pregabalin 75 mg twice daily** (titrate to 150-300 mg twice daily) or **gabapentin 300 mg at night** titrated to 1800-3600 mg/day; **amitriptyline 10 mg at night** titrated to 25-75 mg (avoid in elderly with cardiac disease; nortriptyline preferred); **capsaicin 8% patch**; tramadol as add-on. [NICE CG173 2020]`,`**Varicella complications**: **secondary bacterial skin infection** (staphylococcal, invasive group A streptococcus, necrotising fasciitis), pneumonia, **acute cerebellar ataxia** (1 in 4000 children, benign), encephalitis, Reye syndrome (aspirin), purpura fulminans and hepatitis. [Red Book 2024]`,`**HZO** leads to chronic ocular disease in up to **50%** without antivirals - stromal keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy, glaucoma, cataract; acute retinal necrosis threatens sight in days. [AAO 2023]`,`**HSV encephalitis** survivors often have **memory loss, behaviour change and epilepsy**; about **25% develop anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis** within weeks (relapse with movement disorder) - test and treat with immunotherapy. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**EBV long-term**: **chronic active EBV**, **EBV-HLH** (high ferritin, cytopenias - see the HLH topic), PTLD in transplant, and a 4-fold higher risk of **Hodgkin lymphoma**; EBV infection is now considered a **near-necessary cause of multiple sclerosis** (32-fold risk after EBV; Bjornevik 2022). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital CMV outcome**: symptomatic infants have about **40-60%** long-term sequelae (SNHL, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability, epilepsy, visual loss); **hearing loss can be late-onset and progressive**, so audiology continues to 5-6 years. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer the same day**: suspected encephalitis, HZO with eye pain or red eye or Hutchinson sign, Ramsay Hunt (ENT), zoster or varicella in pregnancy or immunosuppression, eczema herpeticum, neonatal vesicles, IM with stridor or left upper quadrant pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Follow-up**: zoster - review at 1 week for healing and pain, and at **4-6 weeks** to detect early PHN; IM - repeat CBC and LFT in 2-4 weeks, clear for sport only when spleen is impalpable and at least 3-4 weeks have passed; HSV encephalitis - neuropsychology and seizure follow-up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV, Shingrix)**: two **0.5 mL IM doses 2-6 months apart** for **all adults 50 years and over** and adults **18 and over who are immunocompromised**; efficacy about **97% (50-69 years)** and **90% (70 and over)** against zoster and about 90% against PHN; available in India since **2023**. [ACIP 2022]`,`RZV can be given **after an episode of zoster** (once the rash has resolved) and to those who previously had live zoster vaccine; it is non-live and safe in immunosuppression; expect injection-site pain and 1-3 days of systemic reactogenicity. [ACIP 2022]`,`**Varicella vaccine** (live attenuated, Oka strain) is not in India's UIP; IAP recommends **2 doses at 15 months and 3-6 months later (or 4-6 years)**; susceptible adults, healthcare workers and women planning pregnancy get **2 doses 4-8 weeks apart**, avoiding pregnancy for 1 month after; **post-exposure vaccination within 3-5 days** prevents or modifies disease. [IAP 2023]`,`**HSV prevention**: condoms reduce HSV-2 transmission by about 30%, **abstain during lesions or prodrome**, daily suppressive valacyclovir in discordant couples; disclose to partners; **no licensed HSV vaccine** exists. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**CMV in pregnancy**: counsel all pregnant women on **hygiene** - hand-washing after nappy changes and contact with a toddler's saliva, not sharing cups, cutlery or kissing on the mouth - reduces seroconversion; routine universal CMV serology screening is not recommended in India. [ECCI 2023]`,`**Healthcare infection control**: varicella and disseminated zoster need **airborne plus contact precautions** (negative-pressure room if available); localised zoster in an immunocompetent patient needs **lesions covered and contact precautions**; only immune staff should care for them. [CDC 2007]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACIP 2022 (in force 2023-26)**: RZV extended to **immunocompromised adults aged 18 and over**; India licensed RZV in 2023 and API/ICMR adult-immunisation advice now lists it for adults 50 and over and those on immunosuppression. [ACIP 2022]`,`**ECCI 2023** (European Congenital CMV Initiative consensus): **valacyclovir 8 g/day** for primary maternal infection in the periconception period/first trimester; IgG avidity-based diagnosis; valganciclovir for 6 months for moderate-severe congenital CMV; hygiene counselling for all pregnant women. [ECCI 2023]`,`**FDA 2023**: **letermovir** approved for CMV prophylaxis in **adult kidney transplant D+/R-** (up to 200 days), after an RCT showed non-inferiority to valganciclovir with much less leucopenia; HSCT use extended to **day 200** in high-risk patients. [FDA 2023]`,`**WHO 2024 HSV estimates**: about **846 million** aged 15-49 live with genital herpes; WHO prioritises HSV vaccine development and lists HSV in the **Global Health Sector Strategy on STIs 2022-2030**. [WHO 2024]`,`**ZEDS (Zoster Eye Disease Study, 2024)**: suppressive **valacyclovir 1 g daily for 1 year** in HZO eye disease reduced flares of new or worsening disease over 18 months, though the 12-month primary end-point was not significant - use is now an ophthalmologist decision. [ZEDS 2024]`,`**UKHSA varicella PEP guidance (2023)**: confirms the switch from VZIG to **oral antivirals given days 7-14 after exposure** for most susceptible pregnant and immunosuppressed contacts; VZIG reserved for neonates and those who cannot take antivirals. [UKHSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic long question**: 'Herpes zoster - clinical features, complications and management including PHN and prevention' - write pathogenesis of latency, dermatome diagram, HZO with Hutchinson sign, antiviral doses, PHN ladder, RZV. [DNB pattern]`,`**Classic short notes**: Hutchinson sign; Ramsay Hunt syndrome; Monospot and EBV serology interpretation; congenital CMV; acyclovir mechanism and resistance; HSV encephalitis; varicella in pregnancy. [DNB pattern]`,`**Common mistake 1**: prescribing **acyclovir 400 mg** for zoster - VZV is about **10 times less sensitive** than HSV, so the zoster dose is **800 mg five times a day**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Common mistake 2**: treating a sore throat with amoxicillin in a young adult with posterior nodes - causes the **IM rash** and a false penicillin-allergy label for life. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake 3**: waiting for MRI or CSF PCR before starting **IV acyclovir** in encephalitis, and ordering **HSV IgM or CMV IgM alone** as diagnostic tests. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**Viva**: Why is acyclovir non-toxic to host cells? (needs viral TK for first phosphorylation). Why is letermovir not enough alone? (no HSV/VZV activity). Which CMV patient is highest risk? (**D+/R- SOT**). When is the heterophile test negative? (**week 1, children under 4, CMV/HIV/toxoplasma**). [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antiviral drugs for herpesviruses`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Acyclovir (HSV)`,`Genital first episode 400 mg oral TDS; recurrence 800 mg BD; suppression 400 mg BD`,`7-10 days; 5 days; continuous with annual review`,`Headache, nausea; crystal nephropathy with IV; reduce in CrCl under 50`],[`Acyclovir (VZV)`,`800 mg oral five times daily; child varicella 20 mg/kg (max 800 mg) QID`,`7 days (zoster, adult varicella); 5 days (child)`,`Poor oral bioavailability (15-30%) - adherence problem`],[`Acyclovir IV`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly over 1 h (ideal body weight); neonate 20 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`HSV encephalitis 14-21 days; neonatal 14 days SEM, 21 days CNS/disseminated`,`Hydrate; neurotoxicity in elderly/CKD; phlebitis`],[`Valacyclovir`,`Zoster 1 g TDS; first genital 1 g BD; recurrence 500 mg BD; suppression 500 mg-1 g OD; labialis 2 g BD`,`7 days; 7-10 days; 3 days; continuous; 1 day`,`Renal dose; TTP/HUS reported at 8 g/day in advanced HIV`],[`Famciclovir`,`Zoster 500 mg TDS; recurrent genital 1 g BD`,`7 days; 1 day`,`Headache; renal dose adjustment`],[`Ganciclovir`,`5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly (induction); 5 mg/kg OD (maintenance)`,`At least 2 weeks and until PCR negative`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia; teratogenic; renal dosing`],[`Valganciclovir`,`Treatment 900 mg oral BD; prophylaxis 900 mg OD; congenital CMV 16 mg/kg BD`,`2-3 weeks; 3-6 months (D+/R- kidney 6 months); 6 months`,`Leucopenia; take with food; renal dosing`],[`Letermovir`,`480 mg oral/IV OD (240 mg with ciclosporin)`,`To day 100 post-HSCT (up to 200); up to 200 days kidney D+/R-`,`No HSV/VZV cover - add acyclovir; raises tacrolimus, ciclosporin levels`],[`Foscarnet`,`90 mg/kg IV 12-hourly (induction)`,`Until resolution; for resistant HSV/CMV`,`Nephrotoxicity, low Ca/Mg/K/PO4, seizures, genital ulcers`],[`Maribavir`,`400 mg oral BD`,`8 weeks for refractory/resistant CMV`,`Taste disturbance; antagonises ganciclovir; not for CMV retinitis or CNS`]]},{heading:`Classification of human herpesviruses`,columns:[`Virus`,`Subfamily`,`Site of latency`,`Primary infection`,`Reactivation / key disease`],rows:[[`HSV-1 (HHV-1)`,`Alpha`,`Trigeminal ganglion`,`Gingivostomatitis`,`Herpes labialis, keratitis, encephalitis`],[`HSV-2 (HHV-2)`,`Alpha`,`Sacral ganglia`,`Primary genital herpes`,`Recurrent genital herpes, meningitis, neonatal herpes`],[`VZV (HHV-3)`,`Alpha`,`Dorsal root and cranial ganglia`,`Varicella`,`Herpes zoster, HZO, Ramsay Hunt, PHN`],[`EBV (HHV-4)`,`Gamma`,`Memory B cells`,`Infectious mononucleosis`,`Burkitt, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin, PTLD, hairy leukoplakia`],[`CMV (HHV-5)`,`Beta`,`Monocytes, myeloid progenitors`,`CMV mononucleosis, congenital CMV`,`Transplant CMV disease, HIV retinitis and colitis`],[`HHV-6 and HHV-7`,`Beta`,`T cells, monocytes`,`Roseola infantum`,`Encephalitis after HSCT, drug reaction (DRESS)`],[`HHV-8`,`Gamma`,`B cells, endothelium`,`Often silent`,`Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, Castleman`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of vesicular and mononucleosis-like illness`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Confirmatory test`],rows:[[`Varicella`,`Itchy pleomorphic crops, trunk-heavy, scalp involved`,`Clinical; vesicle VZV PCR`],[`Herpes zoster`,`Unilateral dermatomal band, prodromal pain, older adult`,`Clinical; VZV PCR if atypical`],[`Mpox`,`Prominent nodes, monomorphic deep lesions, palms/soles, anogenital`,`Lesion swab MPXV PCR`],[`Hand-foot-mouth disease`,`Young child, oral ulcers, oval vesicles on palms, soles, buttocks`,`Clinical; enterovirus PCR`],[`Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)`,`Exudative pharyngitis, posterior nodes, splenomegaly, atypical lymphocytes`,`Monospot; VCA IgM with EBNA negative`],[`CMV mononucleosis`,`Prolonged fever, mild hepatitis, little pharyngitis`,`CMV IgM with low IgG avidity; heterophile negative`],[`Acute HIV`,`Fever, rash, mucosal ulcers, risk exposure`,`HIV p24 antigen/antibody (4th generation) or viral load`],[`Streptococcal pharyngitis`,`Anterior nodes only, no splenomegaly, Centor 3-4`,`Rapid antigen test or throat culture`]]},{heading:`Key investigations and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Use`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`Vesicle swab HSV/VZV PCR`,`Atypical vesicles, genital ulcer, immunocompromised`,`Most sensitive; types HSV-1/2 and VZV; Tzanck cannot type`],[`CSF HSV PCR`,`Encephalitis`,`Sensitivity about 96%; may be negative in first 72 h - repeat`],[`Heterophile (Monospot)`,`Suspected IM`,`Sensitivity about 85%; negative in 25% in week 1 and in children under 4`],[`EBV VCA IgM / VCA IgG / EBNA IgG`,`Heterophile-negative IM`,`VCA IgM+ EBNA- acute; VCA IgG+ EBNA+ past`],[`CBC (Hoagland)`,`IM`,`Lymphocytes 50% or more with atypical lymphocytes 10% or more`],[`CMV IgG avidity`,`Pregnancy with positive IgM`,`Low avidity (under 30%) suggests primary infection in last 3 months`],[`Plasma CMV DNA PCR (IU/mL)`,`Transplant, HIV, treatment response`,`Trend matters; centre-specific pre-emptive threshold`],[`Neonatal saliva/urine CMV PCR`,`Suspected congenital CMV, failed hearing screen`,`Must be within first 21 days of life`]]},{heading:`Postherpetic neuralgia - drug ladder`,columns:[`Step`,`Drug and dose`,`Note`],rows:[[`Prevent`,`Antiviral within 72 h; RZV vaccine 50 years and over`,`Steroids do not prevent PHN`],[`Focal allodynia`,`Lidocaine 5% plaster, up to 3 for 12 h on/12 h off`,`Best tolerated in elderly`],[`First-line systemic`,`Pregabalin 75 mg BD to 300 mg BD, or gabapentin to 1800-3600 mg/day`,`Dizziness, sedation; renal dose`],[`First-line alternative`,`Amitriptyline or nortriptyline 10 mg at night to 25-75 mg`,`Anticholinergic; ECG in cardiac disease`],[`Second-line`,`Capsaicin 8% patch (specialist); tramadol add-on`,`Tramadol - serotonin syndrome with TCA/SSRI`],[`Refractory`,`Pain clinic: nerve blocks, combination therapy`,`Screen for depression and sleep loss`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with confusion, behaviour change, aphasia or seizures - start IV acyclovir for HSV encephalitis and admit`,`Vesicles on the tip of the nose, red painful eye or reduced vision in facial zoster - same-day ophthalmology`,`Varicella with breathlessness, cough or SpO2 under 94%, especially in an adult, smoker or pregnant woman`,`Any vesicular rash in a neonate, or maternal varicella from 5 days before to 2 days after delivery`,`Zoster or varicella that is disseminated, haemorrhagic or in an immunocompromised patient`,`Sore throat with stridor, drooling or inability to swallow in infectious mononucleosis`,`Left upper quadrant or shoulder-tip pain with hypotension in a patient with IM - splenic rupture`,`Fever, cytopenia and rising transaminases 1-6 months after a transplant - suspect CMV disease`],pearls:[`**Zoster dose is double the HSV dose**: acyclovir 800 mg five times daily, valacyclovir 1 g three times daily, for 7 days.`,`**Hutchinson sign** - nasal tip vesicles mean the nasociliary nerve and therefore the eye are involved.`,`**Varicella is centripetal and pleomorphic; smallpox and mpox are centrifugal and monomorphic.**`,`**Amoxicillin rash in IM** is not penicillin allergy - do not label the patient.`,`**Monospot is negative in week 1 and in children under 4** - repeat or send EBV VCA IgM/EBNA.`,`**CMV D+/R-** is the highest-risk transplant pairing; letermovir has no HSV/VZV cover - add acyclovir.`,`**Congenital CMV** must be diagnosed on saliva or urine PCR in the first 21 days - after that it cannot be called congenital.`,`**HSV encephalitis - start acyclovir first, image later**; 25% go on to anti-NMDAR encephalitis.`,`**PHN = pain 90 days after rash**; lidocaine plaster, pregabalin/gabapentin or a TCA; steroids do not prevent it.`,`**Zoster in a young adult - test for HIV.**`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old man has had burning pain over the right forehead for 3 days and now has vesicles on the forehead, upper eyelid and the tip of the nose. Visual acuity is normal. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Topical acyclovir cream and review in a week`,`Oral prednisolone alone to prevent postherpetic neuralgia`,`Oral valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days and same-day ophthalmology review`,`Oral acyclovir 400 mg three times daily for 5 days`],answer:2,explanation:`Vesicles on the nasal tip (Hutchinson sign) mark nasociliary involvement and a high risk of eye disease, so herpes zoster ophthalmicus needs systemic antiviral at zoster doses (valacyclovir 1 g TDS for 7-10 days) and a same-day slit-lamp examination. Topical acyclovir does not treat ganglionic infection, steroids alone are harmful and do not prevent PHN, and 400 mg acyclovir is the HSV dose - VZV needs 800 mg five times a day.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q2`,stem:`A 19-year-old student has fever, exudative tonsillitis, posterior cervical nodes and a palpable spleen. On day 4 of illness the heterophile (Monospot) test is negative. CBC shows 58% lymphocytes with 14% atypical lymphocytes. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Send EBV VCA IgM and EBNA IgG, and an HIV antigen/antibody test`,`Start amoxicillin for streptococcal pharyngitis`,`Start oral acyclovir for EBV infection`,`Arrange a bone marrow examination for lymphoma`],answer:0,explanation:`The heterophile test is falsely negative in about a quarter of IM cases in the first week, and Hoagland criteria are met, so EBV-specific serology (VCA IgM positive, EBNA negative = acute) confirms it; acute HIV is a key heterophile-negative mimic and should be tested. Amoxicillin causes the IM rash, acyclovir has no clinical benefit in IM, and marrow examination is unwarranted with a typical self-limiting picture.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q3`,stem:`A CMV-seronegative man receives a kidney from a CMV-seropositive deceased donor. Which prophylaxis strategy is recommended?`,options:[`No prophylaxis; test CMV IgM monthly`,`Acyclovir 400 mg twice daily for 1 month`,`Valganciclovir 900 mg once daily for 6 months, or letermovir as an alternative`,`Ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV twice daily for 2 weeks only`],answer:2,explanation:`D+/R- kidney recipients are at highest risk of primary CMV disease, so guidelines recommend 6 months of valganciclovir prophylaxis; since 2023 letermovir is an approved alternative with less leucopenia. IgM is useless for monitoring in transplant (quantitative PCR is used), acyclovir is too weak against CMV, and a 2-week IV course is a treatment regimen, not prophylaxis, leaving late-onset disease uncovered.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q4`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman at 28 weeks of gestation develops a varicella rash 12 hours ago. She has no chest symptoms. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Varicella vaccine immediately`,`VZIG intramuscularly only`,`Reassurance; antivirals are contraindicated in pregnancy`,`Oral acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days with advice to report chest symptoms`],answer:3,explanation:`RCOG advises oral acyclovir for pregnant women from 20 weeks who present within 24 hours of the rash, because it shortens fever and symptoms and may reduce varicella pneumonia; she must report any breathlessness for admission. Live varicella vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy and useless once disease has started, VZIG is post-exposure prophylaxis with no role after the rash appears, and acyclovir is not contraindicated in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q5`,stem:`A 72-year-old man with CKD (creatinine clearance 25 mL/min) is admitted with HSV encephalitis and started on IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly. On day 3 he becomes more confused with myoclonus, although his fever has settled and MRI is unchanged. What is the most likely explanation?`,options:[`Acyclovir neurotoxicity from failure to adjust the dose for renal function`,`Development of acyclovir-resistant HSV`,`Progression of encephalitis needing a switch to ganciclovir`,`Post-HSV anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis`],answer:0,explanation:`Acyclovir is renally cleared, so at CrCl 25 mL/min the dosing interval must be extended (10 mg/kg 12-hourly); accumulation causes confusion, tremor and myoclonus that mimic worsening encephalitis while fever and imaging improve. Acyclovir resistance is rare in immunocompetent patients and takes longer, ganciclovir offers nothing extra for HSV, and anti-NMDAR encephalitis typically appears 2-6 weeks after HSV encephalitis, not on day 3.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, clinical features, complications and management of herpes zoster. Add a note on postherpetic neuralgia and its prevention.`,openingLines:[`Herpes zoster is the reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus (an alpha-herpesvirus) from a dorsal root or cranial nerve ganglion, producing a painful unilateral dermatomal vesicular eruption.`,`Lifetime risk is about 1 in 3, rising steeply after 50 years and in immunosuppression; its main burden is postherpetic neuralgia and herpes zoster ophthalmicus.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Primary varicella seeds sensory ganglia; virus stays latent, controlled by VZV-specific T cells.`,`Age, HIV, cancer, transplant, steroids and JAK inhibitors reduce T-cell immunity; virus reactivates and travels down the nerve.`,`Ganglionitis and neuritis cause prodromal pain; central sensitisation explains PHN.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Prodrome of burning pain 2-5 days; then grouped vesicles on erythematous base in one dermatome, not crossing the midline.`,`Thoracic dermatomes commonest; V1 (HZO) second; Hutchinson sign predicts ocular disease.`,`Variants: Ramsay Hunt (geniculate), zoster sine herpete, disseminated zoster (over 20 vesicles outside dermatome).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Clinical; vesicle-base VZV PCR if atypical or immunocompromised; Tzanck shows giant cells but cannot type.`,`Test for HIV in a young patient or multi-dermatomal disease.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Valacyclovir 1 g TDS or acyclovir 800 mg five times daily or famciclovir 500 mg TDS for 7 days, within 72 h.`,`IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly for disseminated disease or immunocompromise.`,`HZO: same-day ophthalmology; Ramsay Hunt: add steroid, ENT review.`,`Analgesia: paracetamol, NSAID, early gabapentinoid or TCA; renal dose adjustment.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`PHN, HZO keratitis and uveitis, acute retinal necrosis, facial palsy, motor zoster, VZV vasculopathy and stroke, secondary bacterial infection.`]},{heading:`Postherpetic neuralgia and prevention`,points:[`PHN is pain 90 days or more after rash onset in 10-18%; risk rises with age, severe acute pain and HZO.`,`Treat with lidocaine 5% plaster, pregabalin or gabapentin, amitriptyline or nortriptyline; capsaicin 8%; pain clinic.`,`Prevent with recombinant zoster vaccine - 2 doses 2-6 months apart from 50 years, or 18 years if immunocompromised (about 90% efficacy against PHN).`]}],mustDraw:[`Dermatome map showing a thoracic band and V1 with Hutchinson sign`,`PHN treatment ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and variants`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and management`,marks:3},{part:`PHN and prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`VZV latency`,`Hutchinson sign`,`valacyclovir`,`PHN`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`,`Ramsay Hunt`]},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on congenital cytomegalovirus infection.`,openingLines:[`Congenital CMV is intrauterine infection with cytomegalovirus (HHV-5), the commonest congenital infection (about 0.5-1% of live births) and the leading non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss.`,`Transmission is 30-40% after primary maternal infection and 1-2% after reactivation or reinfection, with the worst outcome after first-trimester infection.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`10-15% symptomatic: SGA, microcephaly, periventricular calcification, chorioretinitis, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, blueberry muffin purpura.`,`Asymptomatic infants still risk late, progressive SNHL.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Maternal: IgG seroconversion or IgM with low avidity; amniotic fluid PCR after 20-21 weeks.`,`Neonate: saliva or urine CMV PCR within 21 days; cranial ultrasound/MRI, fundus, audiology.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Valganciclovir 16 mg/kg BD for 6 months, started in the first month, for moderate-severe symptomatic disease; monitor neutrophils.`,`Primary maternal infection in first trimester: valacyclovir 8 g/day to reduce transmission (ECCI 2023).`]},{heading:`Prevention and follow-up`,points:[`Hygiene counselling: hand-washing after nappies, no sharing utensils with toddlers.`,`Audiology and development follow-up to 5-6 years.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: maternal infection to fetal diagnosis to neonatal testing within 21 days`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`congenital CMV`,`SNHL`,`valganciclovir`,`IgG avidity`,`21 days`,`valacyclovir 8 g`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on HSV, VZV, EBV and CMV`,`CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021 (MMWR) - genital herpes`,`Razonable RR, Humar A. Cytomegalovirus in solid organ transplant recipients - AST Infectious Diseases Community of Practice guidelines, Clinical Transplantation, 2019`,`Leruez-Ville M, et al. Consensus recommendation for prenatal, neonatal and postnatal management of congenital CMV infection from the European Congenital Infection Initiative (ECCI), Lancet Regional Health Europe, 2024`,`ACIP. Use of recombinant zoster vaccine in immunocompromised adults aged 19 years and older, MMWR 2022`,`RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 13: Chickenpox in Pregnancy (2015, current) and BASHH/RCOG Management of Genital Herpes in Pregnancy, 2014`,`NACO National Technical Guidelines on STI/RTI, 2024 and NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021`,`IAP Guidebook on Immunization 2023 (Advisory Committee on Vaccines and Immunization Practices)`]});var s=[],c=[];c.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised`,title:`Fever in the immunocompromised: neutropenic fever, transplant, steroids and biologics`,oneLiner:`**Febrile neutropenia** is a single oral temperature of **38.3 C or more** (or 38.0 C sustained over 1 hour) with an **ANC under 500/mm3** (or expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours) - a **medical emergency** in which a broad-spectrum **antipseudomonal beta-lactam (piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime) is given within 60 minutes**, risk is stratified by the **MASCC score (21 or more = low risk)**, and every patient starting steroids, transplant immunosuppression or a biologic is first screened for **latent TB and hepatitis B**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`febrile neutropenia`,`neutropenic sepsis`,`MASCC score`,`CISNE`,`IDSA/ASCO 2018`,`piperacillin-tazobactam`,`cefepime`,`meropenem`,`empirical antifungal`,`galactomannan`,`transplant infection timeline`,`CMV`,`Pneumocystis jirovecii`,`anti-TNF`,`rituximab HBV reactivation`,`latent TB screening`,`IGRA`,`G-CSF`,`febrile neutropenia outpatient`,`immunosuppressed host`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Febrile neutropenia (FN)**: a single oral temperature of **38.3 C or more**, or **38.0 C or more sustained over 1 hour**, with an **absolute neutrophil count (ANC) under 500/mm3** or under 1000/mm3 and expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Profound neutropenia** is an ANC **under 100/mm3**; **prolonged neutropenia** lasts **more than 7 days** - both define the high-risk host for bacteraemia and invasive mould infection. [IDSA 2010]`,`FN complicates chemotherapy in **10-50% of solid tumour** patients and **over 80% of haematological malignancy** patients; a clinical or microbiological source is found in only **20-30%**, and bacteraemia in **10-25%**. [IDSA 2010]`,`Overall in-hospital mortality of FN in adults is about **5-10%**, rising to **over 20%** with Gram-negative bacteraemia, septic shock or each additional comorbidity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indian burden**: series from tertiary cancer centres (Tata Memorial, AIIMS, CMC Vellore) show **Gram-negative bacilli cause about 60-70%** of bacteraemias in FN (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas), unlike the Gram-positive predominance in Western centres. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Carbapenem resistance** in Klebsiella pneumoniae now exceeds half of isolates in ICMR AMRSN hospital data, which is why Indian guidance asks every unit to choose its first-line FN regimen from its own antibiogram. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`The wider **immunocompromised host** includes solid organ and stem cell transplant recipients, people on **prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more**, biologics (anti-TNF, rituximab, JAK inhibitors), advanced HIV, asplenia, and uncontrolled diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`Fever in these patients is often **blunted** (steroids, elderly, uraemia), so **any new deterioration, hypothermia or unexplained tachycardia** is treated as FN or sepsis until proved otherwise. [NICE NG151 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors - matching the defect to the organism`,points:[`**Neutropenia** (chemotherapy, aplastic anaemia, leukaemia): **Gram-negative bacilli** (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas), viridans streptococci, S. aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci, then **Candida and Aspergillus** after 7-10 days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cell-mediated (T-cell) defect** (steroids, calcineurin inhibitors, HIV, transplant, lymphoma): **intracellular pathogens** - Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Listeria, Nocardia, Salmonella, PJP, Cryptococcus, CMV, VZV, Toxoplasma, Strongyloides hyperinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Humoral defect and asplenia** (rituximab, myeloma, CLL, splenectomy): **encapsulated bacteria** - Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis - with fulminant sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-TNF agents** (infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept): **TB reactivation** (risk raised 4-20 times, often extrapulmonary or disseminated), histoplasmosis, Listeria, Legionella and HBV reactivation. [ACR 2022]`,`**Rituximab (anti-CD20)**: **hepatitis B reactivation** (risk over 10% in HBsAg-positive, can be fatal), hypogammaglobulinaemia, PML and prolonged poor vaccine response for 6 months. [AGA 2025]`,`**JAK inhibitors** (tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib): **herpes zoster** at 2-3 times the background rate, TB reactivation and opportunistic infections. [ACR 2022]`,`**Barrier breaks** add their own organisms: **mucositis** (viridans streptococci, anaerobes, Candida), **central venous catheters** (coagulase-negative staphylococci, S. aureus, Candida), and neutropenic enterocolitis (Clostridium septicum, Gram-negatives). [IDSA 2010]`,`Host risk factors for poor outcome: **age over 60**, haematological malignancy, **ANC under 100 for over 7 days**, hypotension, pneumonia, renal or hepatic dysfunction, uncontrolled cancer and prior fungal infection. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs vanish and why the drugs are chosen`,points:[`Neutrophils make **pus, infiltrates and inflammation**; without them, pneumonia may show **no sputum or consolidation**, UTI **no pyuria**, and cellulitis only faint erythema and pain - so **fever may be the only sign**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chemotherapy injures the **gut mucosa**, letting endogenous gut flora translocate; this is why **Gram-negative bacilli and Pseudomonas** must be covered empirically and why bacteraemia arises without a visible focus. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gram-negative bacteraemia in neutropenia can kill within **hours**; each hour of delay in the first antibiotic dose raises mortality, which is why IDSA/ASCO and NICE set a **door-to-needle time of 60 minutes**. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Moulds (Aspergillus, Mucorales)** are cleared by neutrophils and macrophages; risk rises steeply after **10-14 days of neutropenia**, explaining the rule to escalate to antifungal cover when fever persists beyond **4-7 days** of antibiotics. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Corticosteroids** redistribute lymphocytes, inhibit cytokine transcription (NF-kB), block neutrophil migration and lower the hypothalamic set-point - hence **masked fever, low CRP rise and T-cell opportunists** such as PJP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha** is essential for **granuloma maintenance**; blocking it dissolves granulomas that contain latent TB, which is why TB under anti-TNF is often disseminated and why screening precedes the first dose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**B-cell depletion** removes the antibody that controls HBV cccDNA in hepatocytes; HBV replication rebounds and the hepatitis flare comes on **immune reconstitution**, often weeks to months after the last rituximab dose. [AGA 2025]`,`Transplant immunosuppression follows a **predictable timeline** (net state of immunosuppression), so the month after transplant points to the likely pathogen. [Fishman, NEJM 2007]`]},{heading:`Classification - risk stratification that decides inpatient or outpatient care`,points:[`The **MASCC risk index** (Multinational Association for Supportive Care in Cancer) scores 7 items to a **maximum of 26**; a score of **21 or more = low risk** (serious complications under 5%), under 21 = high risk. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`MASCC items: burden of illness **none or mild 5**, moderate 3; **no hypotension 5**; **no COPD 4**; **solid tumour or haematological cancer with no previous fungal infection 4**; **no dehydration needing IV fluids 3**; **outpatient at onset 3**; **age under 60 years 2**. [MASCC 2000]`,`The **CISNE score** (Clinical Index of Stable Febrile Neutropenia) is used for **apparently stable solid tumour** patients: ECOG 2 or more, stress hyperglycaemia, COPD, cardiovascular disease, mucositis grade 2 or more, monocytes under 200 - **score 0 = low risk**. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`IDSA clinical high risk regardless of score: **expected neutropenia over 7 days, ANC 100 or less**, haemodynamic instability, new pneumonia, abdominal pain, neurological change, catheter infection, hepatic or renal dysfunction, or **AML/allogeneic HSCT**. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Transplant timeline**: **0-1 month** - nosocomial, donor-derived, surgical-site, C. difficile, HSV; **1-6 months** - opportunists (CMV, PJP, BK virus, Listeria, Nocardia, Toxoplasma, TB, Aspergillus); **over 6 months** - community infections, late CMV, EBV-driven PTLD. [Fishman, NEJM 2007]`,`**Steroid thresholds**: prednisolone **20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more** counts as immunosuppressive for live-vaccine purposes; **20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more** is the threshold for PJP prophylaxis in cancer patients. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Ask the **date and regimen of the last chemotherapy**: the ANC nadir is usually **7-14 days** after most cytotoxic cycles, so fever in that window is FN until the count is back. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Ask about **rigors after flushing a central line** (catheter-related bloodstream infection), mouth ulcers (mucositis), perianal pain, diarrhoea and abdominal pain (**neutropenic enterocolitis**, C. difficile). [IDSA 2010]`,`Ask about **dry cough and breathlessness with falling SpO2** - PJP under steroids or transplant drugs classically has **hypoxia out of proportion to the chest X-ray**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **headache or confusion** (Cryptococcus, Listeria, toxoplasma, PML), **visual symptoms** (CMV retinitis, candida endophthalmitis) and **sinus pain, black nasal discharge or facial swelling** (mucormycosis, aspergillosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the **transplant date, organ, induction agent, rejection treatment** (pulse steroids or ATG reset the clock to the high-risk window), CMV donor-recipient status and current prophylaxis. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`For biologics, ask the **drug, start date and screening done**: IGRA or TST, chest X-ray, HBsAg and anti-HBc, and whether **TB preventive treatment** was given. [ACR 2022]`,`Ask about **TB contact, previous TB, travel, soil and farm work** (Nocardia, histoplasma, melioidosis, Strongyloides) and pets or undercooked food (toxoplasma, Listeria, Salmonella). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and what they mean`,points:[`Record vitals with **NEWS2** and lactate: **SBP under 90, MAP under 65, RR 22 or more, SpO2 under 94%** or altered mentation mean neutropenic sepsis - start resuscitation and antibiotics at once. [SSC 2026]`,`**Do not perform a digital rectal examination** or insert rectal thermometers or suppositories in neutropenia - risk of bacteraemia and perianal abscess; inspect the perianal area instead. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Inspect **all catheter sites and tunnels**: erythema or pus over 2 cm along the tunnel is a **tunnel infection** that needs catheter removal. [IDSA 2009]`,`Examine the **mouth** (mucositis, thrush, HSV ulcers), **sinuses and palate** (black eschar = mucormycosis until proved otherwise) and **fundi** (candida endophthalmitis, CMV retinitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for skin lesions: **ecthyma gangrenosum** (painless necrotic ulcer with red halo) = **Pseudomonas** bacteraemia; tender nodules may be disseminated Candida, Fusarium or Nocardia; **dermatomal vesicles** = zoster. [Harrison 22e]`,`Right iliac fossa tenderness with fever and diarrhoea in neutropenia suggests **neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis)**; bowel wall over 4 mm on CT confirms. [IDSA 2010]`,`Absent or minimal signs are the rule; **repeat a full head-to-toe examination daily**, because foci declare themselves as the ANC recovers. [IDSA 2010]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever in the immunocompromised`,points:[`**Non-infectious fever** is common: **tumour fever**, transfusion reactions, drug fever (cytarabine, bleomycin, ATG, amphotericin), engraftment syndrome, GVHD and VTE - but these are diagnoses of exclusion after cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute rejection** in a transplant recipient can cause low-grade fever with graft dysfunction (raised creatinine, liver enzymes) - distinguished by biopsy, not by antibiotics. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS)** - worsening of a known infection as neutrophils or CD4 recover - mimics treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis** should be suspected when fever persists with cytopenias, ferritin over 10,000 and splenomegaly despite negative cultures (see the HLH topic). [HLH-2004]`,`**Endemic Indian infections** still occur in the immunosuppressed - malaria, dengue, scrub typhus, enteric fever - and must be tested for in parallel (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**PJP versus bacterial pneumonia**: PJP gives subacute dry cough, bilateral ground-glass change, high LDH and desaturation on exertion; bacterial pneumonia is lobar with sputum (if neutrophils exist). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`Before antibiotics: **two sets of blood cultures** - one from each lumen of any central line and one peripheral - but **never delay the first dose beyond 60 minutes** to obtain them. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Differential time to positivity**: a central-line culture turning positive **2 hours or more before** the peripheral one indicates catheter-related bloodstream infection. [IDSA 2009]`,`First-line panel: **CBC with differential** (ANC = WBC x (neutrophils + bands) %), creatinine, electrolytes, LFT, **lactate**, urine culture (even without pyuria), sputum, stool C. difficile if diarrhoea, and **chest X-ray** if respiratory signs. [IDSA 2010]`,`In India add a **malaria RDT and smear and dengue NS1** in season - endemic fevers are not suspended by chemotherapy. [ICMR 2022]`,`**High-resolution CT chest** is the investigation of choice for persistent fever beyond 4-7 days in high-risk neutropenia; nodules with a **halo sign** suggest invasive aspergillosis, the **reverse halo** suggests mucormycosis. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Serum galactomannan** (positive index 0.5 or more in serum, 1.0 in BAL) and **beta-D-glucan** support invasive fungal disease; galactomannan sensitivity falls on mould-active prophylaxis. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Transplant panel**: CMV quantitative PCR, BK virus PCR (kidney), EBV PCR, calcineurin inhibitor levels, and **BAL with PJP PCR or immunofluorescence** for infiltrates. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`Procalcitonin and CRP help less in neutropenia and are **not used to withhold antibiotics**; serial values may support de-escalation. [ECIL-4 2013]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - low-risk febrile neutropenia`,points:[`**First dose of antibiotic within 1 hour of triage in every patient**, before the risk assessment is complete; then decide outpatient or inpatient care with MASCC or CISNE. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Low-risk oral regimen**: **ciprofloxacin 750 mg orally twice daily plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg orally three times daily** (or 500/125 mg); levofloxacin or moxifloxacin 400 mg once daily are alternatives. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Do **not** use an oral fluoroquinolone regimen in patients who were already on **fluoroquinolone prophylaxis** - admit and give IV beta-lactam instead. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Outpatient care needs: **observation for at least 4 hours** after the first dose, tolerance of oral drugs, a caregiver, a telephone, transport and **reach of the hospital within 1 hour**, with review at 24-48 hours. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Penicillin allergy (non-severe): **ciprofloxacin plus clindamycin 300 mg orally 6-hourly** is the IDSA/ASCO alternative. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Re-admit if fever persists beyond **48-72 hours**, a new focus appears, cultures grow, the patient cannot take oral drugs or any red flag develops. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`In a patient on **steroids or biologics** with fever but a normal ANC, manage the focus as in a normal host but with a **lower threshold for admission, cultures and chest imaging**; hold the biologic during active infection. [ACR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency - high-risk febrile neutropenia`,points:[`**Empirical IV monotherapy with an antipseudomonal beta-lactam**: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly** or **cefepime 2 g IV 8-hourly**; **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly** where ESBL rates are high, in septic shock or with prior ESBL colonisation. [IDSA 2010; ICMR 2022]`,`Give piperacillin-tazobactam and meropenem as **extended or continuous infusions** in sepsis for better time above MIC. [SSC 2026]`,`Add **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly** (AUC 400-600) only for **haemodynamic instability, pneumonia, suspected catheter infection, skin or soft tissue infection, MRSA colonisation, severe mucositis on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis, or Gram-positive cocci in blood** - not routinely. [IDSA 2010]`,`Septic shock or known **CRE colonisation**: follow the local antibiogram - **ceftazidime-avibactam 2.5 g IV 8-hourly**, plus **aztreonam** for metallo-beta-lactamase (NDM) producers, which are common in India; add an aminoglycoside in shock. [IDSA AMR 2024; ICMR 2022]`,`**Persistent fever at 4-7 days** in high-risk patients with expected neutropenia over 7 days: start **empirical antifungal** - **caspofungin 70 mg IV day 1 then 50 mg daily** or **liposomal amphotericin B 3 mg/kg/day**; or a **pre-emptive** strategy guided by CT and galactomannan. [IDSA 2010; ECIL 2017]`,`Proven or probable **invasive aspergillosis**: **voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly for 2 doses then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly** (trough 1-5.5 mg/L) or isavuconazole; **mucormycosis**: liposomal amphotericin B 5-10 mg/kg/day plus surgery. [IDSA Aspergillus 2016; ECMM 2019]`,`**Duration**: in a stable patient with no source, **stop empirical antibiotics after 72 hours of apyrexia** irrespective of ANC (ECIL-4); IDSA 2010 continued until ANC over 500 - modern practice follows ECIL, supported by the How Long trial. [ECIL-4 2013]`,`**G-CSF** (filgrastim 5 mcg/kg/day SC) is **not routine** for established FN; consider it only in high-risk patients (sepsis, pneumonia, invasive fungal infection, expected long neutropenia). [ASCO 2015]`,`**Remove the central line** for S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Candida or mycobacterial bloodstream infection, tunnel or port-pocket infection, or persistent bacteraemia over 72 hours. [IDSA 2009]`]},{heading:`Special situations - transplant, steroids, biologics, pregnancy and CKD`,points:[`**Solid organ transplant fever**: cultures, CMV PCR, chest imaging and graft function; **never stop all immunosuppression** without the transplant team - reduce antimetabolite (mycophenolate) first in serious infection. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Drug interactions**: macrolides, azoles and rifampicin alter **tacrolimus and cyclosporine levels** (azoles raise, rifampicin lowers); check levels within 3-5 days of starting or stopping them. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**CMV prophylaxis** after SOT: **valganciclovir 900 mg orally once daily** (renally adjusted) for **3-6 months** in D+/R- or R+; in allogeneic HSCT **letermovir 480 mg daily** to day 100. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**PJP prophylaxis**: **cotrimoxazole one double-strength (160/800 mg) tablet daily or thrice weekly** for 6-12 months after SOT, during HSCT and with prednisolone **20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more**. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Before a biologic**: screen **IGRA or TST plus chest X-ray**, **HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs**, anti-HCV, HIV, and VZV serology; give TB preventive treatment for at least **1 month before** starting anti-TNF (complete the course). [ACR 2022; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**HBV before rituximab or chemotherapy**: HBsAg-positive - **entecavir 0.5 mg or tenofovir 300 mg daily** started before and continued **12-18 months** after the last dose; HBsAg-negative anti-HBc-positive on anti-CD20 - prophylaxis too (or HBV DNA monitoring every 1-3 months for lower-risk drugs). [AGA 2025]`,`**Pregnancy** with FN or on immunosuppression: piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime and meropenem are acceptable; avoid fluoroquinolones as first choice and voriconazole (teratogenic) - use liposomal amphotericin for fungal cover. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: reduce cefepime (neurotoxicity, non-convulsive status), piperacillin-tazobactam and meropenem by eGFR; monitor vancomycin by AUC or trough; amphotericin B worsens renal function - prefer the liposomal form. [Sanford 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Septic shock** develops in 5-10% of FN episodes and carries **mortality of 30-50%**, highest with Pseudomonas and carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis** mortality is 30-60% and **mucormycosis** 40-80%; early CT and galactomannan-guided therapy improve survival. [ECMM 2019]`,`**Neutropenic enterocolitis** can perforate; manage with bowel rest, broad cover including anaerobes (piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem) and surgery for perforation or uncontrolled bleeding. [IDSA 2010]`,`**CMV disease** after transplant causes pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis and graft rejection; **disseminated TB** under anti-TNF often presents as PUO with miliary or extrapulmonary disease. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**HBV reactivation** can cause fulminant hepatitis with mortality up to 20-30% in reported series - and it is almost entirely preventable by screening. [AGA 2025]`,`Delayed chemotherapy, dose reduction and longer hospital stay follow each FN episode - this is why **primary G-CSF prophylaxis** is given when the regimen's FN risk is **20% or more**. [ASCO 2015]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer the same day** to the oncology or transplant centre any patient with fever within 6 weeks of chemotherapy, any transplant recipient with fever, and anyone on a biologic with fever and hypotension or hypoxia. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`In-patient monitoring: **vitals 4-hourly**, daily CBC, creatinine and LFT, daily examination, blood cultures again if fever persists at 48-72 hours, and **drug levels** (vancomycin, voriconazole, tacrolimus). [IDSA 2010]`,`**De-escalate** once cultures are known and the patient is stable; switch to oral therapy in low-risk patients afebrile for 24-48 hours. [ECIL-4 2013]`,`On biologics: **annual IGRA or symptom screening** if TB exposure continues, ALT and HBV DNA every 1-3 months in anti-HBc-positive patients not on prophylaxis. [AGA 2025]`,`Teach the patient to **check the temperature at any chill** and to come at once at **38.0 C or more** - give a written neutropenic alert card. [NICE NG151 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Antibacterial prophylaxis** with **levofloxacin 500-750 mg once daily** is advised only for high-risk patients expected to have **ANC 100 or less for over 7 days** (AML, HSCT); not routinely in solid tumours. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Antifungal prophylaxis**: **posaconazole 300 mg delayed-release tablet twice daily on day 1 then 300 mg once daily** in AML/MDS induction and GVHD; fluconazole 400 mg daily in allogeneic HSCT pre-engraftment. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Antiviral prophylaxis**: **aciclovir 400-800 mg twice daily** for HSV-seropositive patients during HSCT or leukaemia induction. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Vaccinate before immunosuppression**: inactivated vaccines at least **2 weeks before**, live vaccines at least **4 weeks before**; avoid live vaccines (MMR, varicella, oral typhoid, yellow fever) during therapy. [IDSA 2013; ACR 2022]`,`Give **annual inactivated influenza**, **pneumococcal** (PCV20 alone, or PCV15/PCV13 then PPSV23), and **recombinant zoster vaccine** (2 doses) to adults 19 years or older who are immunocompromised. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Hand hygiene, a safe-food diet** (no unwashed raw produce, raw eggs, unpasteurised dairy), avoidance of construction dust and gardening during neutropenia; HEPA rooms for allogeneic HSCT. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Household contacts should receive **all routine inactivated vaccines and influenza vaccine**; they may receive MMR and varicella (avoid contact with a vaccine rash). [IDSA 2013]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** (adult guideline update): screen with **NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS rather than qSOFA alone**, give antibiotics **within 1 hour** in probable shock, use **balanced crystalloids** and **prolonged beta-lactam infusions** - applied to neutropenic sepsis. [SSC 2026]`,`**IDSA AMR guidance 2024**: for MBL-producing (NDM) Enterobacterales use **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam** or cefiderocol; for carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas, ceftolozane-tazobactam or ceftazidime-avibactam - relevant to Indian FN units. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**AGA 2025 HBV reactivation guideline**: universal **HBsAg and anti-HBc** testing before immunosuppressive therapy, and antiviral prophylaxis (entecavir or tenofovir) for moderate and high-risk drug classes including **anti-CD20 agents**. [AGA 2025]`,`**CDC 2023** recommends **hepatitis B screening at least once in all adults** with the triple panel (HBsAg, anti-HBs, total anti-HBc). [CDC 2023]`,`**ACIP 2024**: PCV20 or PCV21 for adults with immunocompromise; recombinant zoster vaccine for immunocompromised adults from age 19. [ACIP 2024]`,`**WHO TPT 2024 (Consolidated guidelines Module 1)** keeps **3HP, 1HP, 6H and 4R** as options for latent TB before biologics; NTEP 2021 TPT guidance lists people starting anti-TNF, dialysis and transplant as eligible for testing and treatment. [WHO 2024; NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **define febrile neutropenia, outline MASCC and give empirical therapy** - write the definition, the score items with 21 cut-off, and the drug with dose and the 1-hour rule. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Common mistake: **adding vancomycin routinely** - it has no survival benefit unless one of the specific indications is present. [IDSA 2010]`,`Common mistake: **waiting for the ANC result** before giving the antibiotic; give the first dose on clinical suspicion. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Common mistake: **rectal examination or enema** in a neutropenic patient. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Viva: why ceftriaxone is not empirical FN therapy - **no reliable antipseudomonal activity**. [IDSA 2010]`,`Viva: what to screen before infliximab - **IGRA/TST and chest X-ray, HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HCV, HIV**, vaccination status. [ACR 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`MASCC risk index (low risk = 21 or more of 26)`,columns:[`Characteristic`,`Points`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Burden of illness: none or mild symptoms`,`5`,`Clinician's global judgement`],[`Burden of illness: moderate symptoms`,`3`,`Severe symptoms or moribund score 0`],[`No hypotension (SBP 90 mmHg or more)`,`5`,`Hypotension excludes low risk in practice`],[`No chronic obstructive pulmonary disease`,`4`,`Active COPD needing treatment counts`],[`Solid tumour, or haematological cancer with no previous fungal infection`,`4`,`Prior invasive fungal infection removes these points`],[`No dehydration needing IV fluids`,`3`,`Inability to drink counts as dehydration`],[`Outpatient at onset of fever`,`3`,`Hospital-onset FN is higher risk`],[`Age under 60 years`,`2`,`Older age loses 2 points`]]},{heading:`Drugs in febrile neutropenia and the immunocompromised host`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly (extended 4-h infusion in sepsis)`,`Until 72 h afebrile and stable (ECIL-4) or per source`,`Hypersensitivity, hypokalaemia, AKI with vancomycin; reduce if eGFR under 40`],[`Cefepime`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`As above`,`Neurotoxicity and non-convulsive status in CKD - dose by eGFR`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly (2 g 8-hourly for CNS)`,`As above; de-escalate on cultures`,`Seizures (less than imipenem), C. difficile; valproate levels fall`],[`Ciprofloxacin + amoxicillin-clavulanate (low risk, oral)`,`Cipro 750 mg BD + amox-clav 625 mg TDS orally`,`Until afebrile 48 h and ANC recovering; usually 7 days`,`Not if on FQ prophylaxis; QT prolongation, tendinopathy`],[`Vancomycin (selected indications)`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly; AUC 400-600`,`Stop at 48-72 h if no Gram-positive grown`,`Nephrotoxicity, infusion reaction; monitor levels`],[`Caspofungin`,`70 mg IV day 1, then 50 mg IV daily`,`Until neutrophil recovery and fever resolves`,`Hepatotoxicity; few interactions`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`3 mg/kg/day IV (5-10 mg/kg for mucor)`,`Weeks; per response`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions`],[`Voriconazole`,`6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x 2, then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly; oral 200-300 mg BD`,`6-12 weeks for aspergillosis; trough 1-5.5 mg/L`,`Visual disturbance, hepatitis, QT; raises tacrolimus; teratogenic`],[`Cotrimoxazole (PJP prophylaxis)`,`1 DS tablet (160/800 mg) daily or thrice weekly`,`6-12 months post-SOT; while on 20 mg prednisolone 4 weeks or more`,`Rash, hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression, SJS`],[`Valganciclovir (CMV prophylaxis)`,`900 mg orally once daily (renal adjustment)`,`3-6 months post-SOT`,`Leucopenia, neutropenia; renal dosing`],[`Entecavir / tenofovir (HBV prophylaxis)`,`Entecavir 0.5 mg or TDF 300 mg orally daily`,`Start before therapy; continue 12-18 months after last dose`,`TDF - renal and bone toxicity; entecavir - lactic acidosis rare`]]},{heading:`Immune defect, typical drugs and the organisms to expect`,columns:[`Immune defect`,`Examples`,`Typical organisms`,`Empirical thinking`],rows:[[`Neutropenia`,`Chemotherapy, AML, aplastic anaemia`,`Gram-negative bacilli, Pseudomonas, viridans strep, Candida, Aspergillus`,`Antipseudomonal beta-lactam within 1 h; antifungal at 4-7 days`],[`T-cell (cell-mediated)`,`Steroids, tacrolimus, HIV, lymphoma`,`TB, Listeria, Nocardia, PJP, Cryptococcus, CMV, VZV, Toxoplasma`,`Sputum/BAL, CSF; cotrimoxazole for PJP`],[`B-cell / humoral, asplenia`,`Rituximab, myeloma, CLL, splenectomy`,`Pneumococcus, H. influenzae, meningococcus; HBV reactivation`,`Ceftriaxone early; vaccinate; HBV prophylaxis`],[`Anti-TNF blockade`,`Infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept`,`TB (often extrapulmonary), histoplasma, Listeria, Legionella`,`Screen and treat latent TB before; think disseminated TB`],[`JAK inhibition`,`Tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib`,`Herpes zoster, TB, opportunists`,`Recombinant zoster vaccine beforehand`],[`Barrier break`,`Central line, mucositis, surgery`,`CoNS, S. aureus, Candida, anaerobes`,`Vancomycin if line infection; remove line for S. aureus/Candida`]]},{heading:`Infection timeline after solid organ transplant`,columns:[`Period`,`Main infections`,`Why`,`Prophylaxis that changes it`],rows:[[`0-1 month`,`Surgical-site, line and urinary infection, C. difficile, donor-derived, HSV, Candida`,`Surgery, devices, hospital flora`,`Perioperative antibiotics, aciclovir`],[`1-6 months`,`CMV, PJP, BK virus, Listeria, Nocardia, Toxoplasma, TB reactivation, Aspergillus`,`Peak T-cell suppression`,`Valganciclovir, cotrimoxazole, TPT`],[`Over 6 months`,`Community pneumonia, UTI, late CMV, EBV-PTLD, VZV, endemic fungi`,`Maintenance immunosuppression lower`,`Vaccination, sun protection, surveillance`],[`After rejection treatment`,`Resets to the 1-6 month pattern`,`Pulse steroids or ATG`,`Restart CMV and PJP prophylaxis`]]},{heading:`Screening before steroids, biologics and transplant`,columns:[`Test`,`Positive result`,`Action before starting`],rows:[[`IGRA or TST (5 mm or more) + chest X-ray`,`Latent TB infection`,`TPT (3HP, 4R or 6H) at least 1 month before anti-TNF; rule out active TB first`],[`HBsAg`,`Chronic HBV`,`Entecavir or tenofovir before therapy, continue 12-18 months after`],[`Anti-HBc (HBsAg negative)`,`Resolved HBV`,`Prophylaxis with anti-CD20 or HSCT; otherwise HBV DNA every 1-3 months`],[`Anti-HCV, HIV`,`Chronic infection`,`Treat HCV with DAA; ART and OI prophylaxis`],[`VZV IgG`,`Seronegative`,`Varicella vaccine at least 4 weeks before; recombinant zoster vaccine if seropositive`],[`Strongyloides serology or empirical ivermectin`,`Exposure in endemic India`,`Ivermectin 200 mcg/kg for 2 days before high-dose steroids`]]}],redFlags:[`Temperature of 38.0 C or more within 6 weeks of chemotherapy - treat as neutropenic sepsis and give antibiotics within 1 hour`,`SBP under 90 mmHg, lactate 2 mmol/L or more, RR 22 or more or SpO2 under 94% in any immunocompromised patient`,`Black eschar on the palate or nasal turbinate, or periorbital swelling - suspected mucormycosis`,`New hypoxia with near-normal chest X-ray on steroids or after transplant - suspected PJP`,`Headache, confusion or seizure in a transplant recipient or on a biologic - CNS infection (Listeria, Cryptococcus, Toxoplasma)`,`Jaundice or ALT rise after rituximab or chemotherapy - HBV reactivation`,`Right iliac fossa pain with fever in neutropenia - neutropenic enterocolitis`,`Fever with tunnel infection or rigors on flushing a central line`],pearls:[`FN = 38.3 once or 38.0 for 1 hour, with ANC under 500 - and the antibiotic goes in within 60 minutes.`,`MASCC 21 or more of 26 = low risk; CISNE 0 = low risk for stable solid tumours.`,`Low-risk oral: cipro + amox-clav - but never if already on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis.`,`High risk: pip-taz 4.5 g 6-hourly or cefepime 2 g 8-hourly; meropenem if ESBL likely; ceftriaxone has no place.`,`Vancomycin only for the 'SPIRIT' indications: Shock, Pneumonia, Infection of line, Rash/skin, Identified Gram-positive, Tissue (mucositis) with FQ prophylaxis.`,`Fever at day 4-7 despite antibiotics in high-risk FN = think mould; CT chest and galactomannan.`,`Ecthyma gangrenosum = Pseudomonas bacteraemia.`,`Anti-TNF = TB; rituximab = HBV; JAK inhibitor = zoster; steroid = PJP.`,`No rectal examination in neutropenia.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q1`,stem:`A 48-year-old woman on adjuvant chemotherapy for breast cancer presents 10 days after her last cycle with a temperature of 38.6 C. She is alert, BP 118/76 mmHg, and her ANC is 300/mm3. Her MASCC score is 23. She was not on antibiotic prophylaxis. What is the most appropriate management after the first dose is given in the emergency room?`,options:[`Admit and start IV meropenem plus vancomycin`,`Observe for 4 hours, then discharge on oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate with review in 24 hours`,`Discharge on oral cefixime alone and repeat CBC in a week`,`Start G-CSF and withhold antibiotics until cultures return`],answer:1,explanation:`A MASCC score of 21 or more defines low-risk FN; IDSA/ASCO 2018 allows outpatient oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate after the first dose and a period of observation, if she has support and access. Meropenem with vancomycin is excessive for a stable low-risk patient, cefixime lacks antipseudomonal cover, and withholding antibiotics in FN is dangerous.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q2`,stem:`In high-risk febrile neutropenia, which of the following is an accepted indication to add vancomycin to the empirical beta-lactam?`,options:[`Persistent fever at 48 hours without any other change`,`ANC under 100/mm3`,`Suspected catheter-related infection with redness at the exit site`,`Age over 60 years`],answer:2,explanation:`IDSA lists suspected catheter-related infection, skin or soft-tissue infection, pneumonia, haemodynamic instability, MRSA colonisation and Gram-positive cocci in blood as reasons to add vancomycin. Persistent fever alone, profound neutropenia and older age are not indications - routine vancomycin adds toxicity without lowering mortality.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with Crohn disease is to start infliximab. His IGRA is positive and chest X-ray is normal; he has no symptoms. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start infliximab and repeat IGRA in 6 months`,`Start TB preventive treatment and begin infliximab after at least 1 month of it`,`Give full four-drug antitubercular treatment for 6 months before infliximab`,`Abandon infliximab permanently`],answer:1,explanation:`A positive IGRA with no active disease is latent TB infection; TB preventive treatment (for example 3HP or 4R) is started and anti-TNF therapy can begin after about 1 month while TPT is completed. Starting infliximab untreated risks disseminated TB, four-drug ATT is for active disease, and biologics need not be abandoned.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q4`,stem:`A kidney transplant recipient presents 3 months after transplant with fever, leucopenia and diarrhoea. He was CMV donor-positive, recipient-negative and stopped valganciclovir early due to cost. Which pathogen is most likely?`,options:[`Cytomegalovirus`,`Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus wound infection`,`EBV-driven post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease`,`Streptococcus pneumoniae`],answer:0,explanation:`The 1-6 month window is the period of opportunistic infection, and a D+/R- recipient off prophylaxis is at the highest risk of CMV disease, which classically gives fever, leucopenia and colitis. Wound MRSA belongs to the first month, PTLD usually appears later, and pneumococcus causes lobar pneumonia rather than leucopenia with colitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old man with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is HBsAg negative and anti-HBc positive. He will receive R-CHOP. According to current guidance, what is advised?`,options:[`No action, since HBsAg is negative`,`Hepatitis B vaccination only`,`Check ALT once after the course ends`,`Antiviral prophylaxis with entecavir or tenofovir during and for at least 12 months after rituximab`],answer:3,explanation:`Anti-CD20 therapy carries a high risk of HBV reactivation even in HBsAg-negative, anti-HBc-positive patients, so AGA 2025 recommends entecavir or tenofovir prophylaxis continued 12-18 months after the last dose. Doing nothing ignores occult HBV, vaccination does not suppress replication, and a single late ALT misses an early flare.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define febrile neutropenia. Describe its risk stratification and the management of a patient presenting to a district hospital with febrile neutropenia after chemotherapy.`,openingLines:[`Febrile neutropenia is a single oral temperature of 38.3 C or more, or 38.0 C sustained for 1 hour, with an ANC under 500/mm3 or expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours (IDSA).`,`It is an oncological emergency: Gram-negative bacteraemia can kill within hours, so the first antipseudomonal antibiotic dose is given within 60 minutes of arrival.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`Definition as above; profound neutropenia ANC under 100, prolonged over 7 days.`,`Occurs in 10-50% of solid tumour and over 80% of haematological malignancy patients; mortality 5-10%.`,`In India Gram-negative bacilli cause 60-70% of bacteraemias, with high ESBL and carbapenem resistance.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Absent neutrophils - no pus, minimal signs, fever often the only sign.`,`Mucositis and gut translocation of Gram-negatives; line infections with Gram-positives; fungi after 7-10 days.`]},{heading:`Initial assessment (first hour)`,points:[`Triage on suspicion; vitals, NEWS2, lactate; give the first dose of antibiotic within 60 minutes.`,`Two sets of blood cultures (line and peripheral), CBC, renal and liver tests, urine culture, chest X-ray; malaria and dengue tests in season.`,`Search mouth, lungs, skin, line sites and perianal area; no rectal examination.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`MASCC score: burden of illness 5/3, no hypotension 5, no COPD 4, solid tumour or no prior fungal infection 4, no dehydration 3, outpatient 3, age under 60 2; 21 or more = low risk.`,`CISNE for stable solid tumour; IDSA clinical high-risk features override the score.`]},{heading:`Treatment - low risk`,points:[`Oral ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg TDS after 4 h observation, with 24-48 h review; not if on FQ prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Treatment - high risk`,points:[`Piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly or cefepime 2 g IV 8-hourly; meropenem 1 g 8-hourly if ESBL likely or septic shock.`,`Add vancomycin only for specific indications; ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam for NDM CRE per antibiogram.`,`Fever at 4-7 days: CT chest, galactomannan, empirical caspofungin or liposomal amphotericin B.`,`Stop after 72 h apyrexia if stable and no source (ECIL-4); remove infected lines.`]},{heading:`Prevention and referral`,points:[`Levofloxacin prophylaxis in high-risk only; posaconazole in AML induction; primary G-CSF if regimen FN risk 20% or more.`,`Refer to the oncology centre after stabilisation and the first dose; neutropenic alert card and patient education.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: fever after chemotherapy - first dose in 60 min - MASCC - low-risk oral vs high-risk IV - reassess at 48-72 h - antifungal at 4-7 days`,`MASCC score table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Initial assessment and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Risk stratification (MASCC)`,marks:2},{part:`Empirical antibiotic management`,marks:3},{part:`Persistent fever, duration and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`febrile neutropenia`,`MASCC`,`piperacillin-tazobactam`,`cefepime`,`empirical antifungal`,`ECIL-4`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on screening and prophylaxis before starting biologic therapy.`,openingLines:[`Biologics such as anti-TNF agents, rituximab and JAK inhibitors reactivate latent infections - chiefly tuberculosis, hepatitis B and herpes zoster.`,`Screening and prophylaxis before the first dose prevent most of these infections and is mandatory standard of care.`],answer:[{heading:`Tuberculosis`,points:[`Symptom screen, IGRA or TST and chest X-ray; exclude active TB.`,`Latent TB: TPT (3HP, 4R or 6H) at least 1 month before anti-TNF; complete the course.`]},{heading:`Hepatitis B and other viruses`,points:[`HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs; HBsAg-positive or anti-HBc-positive on anti-CD20 - entecavir or tenofovir until 12-18 months after therapy.`,`Anti-HCV and HIV testing; VZV serology.`]},{heading:`Vaccination`,points:[`Inactivated vaccines 2 weeks before, live vaccines 4 weeks before; influenza yearly, pneumococcal, recombinant zoster vaccine, hepatitis B if non-immune.`]},{heading:`Other prophylaxis and counselling`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole for PJP with prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more; ivermectin for Strongyloides risk before high-dose steroids.`,`Hold biologic during serious infection; report fever early.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: test - positive result - action before biologic`],markSplit:[{part:`TB screening and TPT`,marks:2},{part:`HBV and other viral screening`,marks:1.5},{part:`Vaccination and other prophylaxis`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`anti-TNF`,`IGRA`,`TPT`,`HBV reactivation`,`rituximab`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections in patients with cancer; Infections in transplant recipients`,`Freifeld AG et al. IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Use of Antimicrobial Agents in Neutropenic Patients with Cancer, 2010 update`,`Taplitz RA et al. Outpatient Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Adults Treated for Malignancy: ASCO/IDSA Guideline Update, 2018; and Antimicrobial Prophylaxis ASCO/IDSA, 2018`,`Averbuch D et al. ECIL-4 European guidelines for empirical antibacterial therapy for febrile neutropenic patients, 2013`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022; ICMR AMRSN Annual Report 2023`,`IDSA Guidance on the Treatment of Antimicrobial-Resistant Gram-Negative Infections, 2024`,`AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on Prevention and Treatment of Hepatitis B Virus Reactivation, 2025; ACR Guideline for Vaccinations in Rheumatic and Musculoskeletal Diseases, 2022`,`NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB Preventive Treatment in India, 2021; WHO Consolidated Guidelines on TB Module 1: Prevention, 2024`]});var l=[];l.push({id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology`,title:`Microbiology for the clinician: cultures, serology, PCR and rapid diagnostic tests`,oneLiner:`Clinical microbiology is **diagnostic stewardship** - the **right specimen, taken the right way, on the right day of illness, before the first antibiotic** - read with its limits: **culture with MIC-based susceptibility** is the only test that gives the organism and the drug, **NAAT/PCR** gives speed but not full susceptibility, **IgM serology** turns positive only from about day 5, and **CRP and procalcitonin** support, never replace, clinical judgement.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`blood culture`,`culture and sensitivity`,`antimicrobial susceptibility testing`,`AST`,`MIC`,`minimum inhibitory concentration`,`breakpoint`,`CLSI M100`,`EUCAST`,`CBNAAT`,`Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra`,`Truenat`,`multiplex PCR`,`BioFire FilmArray`,`MALDI-TOF`,`procalcitonin`,`CRP`,`IgM ELISA`,`paired sera`,`rapid diagnostic test`,`diagnostic stewardship`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and the Indian laboratory landscape`,points:[`**Diagnostic stewardship** is the coordinated choice of **which test, on which patient, from which specimen, at what time** so that results change management - the laboratory twin of antimicrobial stewardship. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Microbiological tests are either **direct** (they find the organism or its parts - microscopy, culture, antigen, nucleic acid) or **indirect** (they find the host's antibody response - serology). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antimicrobial resistance** caused an estimated **1.27 million deaths directly and 4.95 million associated deaths worldwide in 2019**, with India among the countries carrying the largest absolute burden. [GRAM Lancet 2022]`,`The **ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance Network (AMRSN)** reports that **E. coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae** are the commonest isolates from Indian hospitals and that carbapenem susceptibility of K. pneumoniae has fallen to **under half** in recent annual reports. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`The **National Essential Diagnostics List (NEDL)** of ICMR lists the tests each tier should offer - smear microscopy, malaria RDT, Gram stain and simple serology at primary level, and culture with susceptibility at district hospitals. [ICMR NEDL 2019]`,`Most Indian microbiology laboratories interpret susceptibility using **CLSI M100** breakpoints, which are revised **every year** - a report must state the edition used. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`Blood culture is still underused in India: many patients with fever receive empirical antibiotics **before any culture**, which permanently lowers the yield of every later specimen. [ICMR 2022]`,`The family physician's job is to **order a test only when its result will change what is done**, collect it correctly, and read it in the light of **pre-test probability**. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Why tests mislead: pre-analytical errors and sources of false results`,points:[`Up to **70% of laboratory errors are pre-analytical** - wrong specimen, wrong container, wrong time, poor labelling or delay in transport - and no analyser can correct them. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Prior antibiotics** sharply cut culture yield: in septic patients, blood cultures drawn after antibiotics were positive in **19.4% versus 31.4%** when drawn before. [FABLED 2019]`,`**Low blood volume** is the commonest cause of a false-negative blood culture: yield rises by about **3% for every extra mL** of blood cultured in adults. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Skin contamination** from poor antisepsis creates false-positive cultures with coagulase-negative staphylococci, leading to unnecessary vancomycin, longer stay and extra cost. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**Colonisation is not infection**: Candida in sputum, organisms on a chronic ulcer swab, or bacteria in the urine of a catheterised patient without symptoms usually need no treatment. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Serology before seroconversion** gives a false negative (IgM sent on day 2), and **background seropositivity** in endemic areas gives false positives on a single titre. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Cross-reactivity** confounds serology - dengue IgM with Zika and Japanese encephalitis, Widal with other Gram-negative infections and prior vaccination, and IgM tests with malaria and polyclonal activation. [WHO 2025]`,`**Predictive value depends on prevalence**: a test with 95% specificity used in a low-prevalence population will give more false than true positives - test the patient, not the population. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`How the tests work: the science behind each method`,points:[`**Culture** grows viable organisms; it is slow (24-72 h for bacteria, weeks for mycobacteria) but is the only route to **phenotypic susceptibility** and typing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Automated blood culture systems** (BacT/ALERT, BACTEC) detect **CO2 produced by growing organisms** with a sensor at the base of the bottle and flag positives continuously; bottles are incubated for **5 days** as standard. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry** identifies an isolate within minutes from its **ribosomal protein fingerprint**, cutting a day or more off identification from a positive bottle or plate. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Antigen tests** (NS1, malaria HRP2/pLDH, cryptococcal antigen, urinary pneumococcal and Legionella antigen) detect microbial proteins or polysaccharides and are positive **early**, while the organism is present. [WHO 2025]`,`**Antibody tests** detect the host response: **IgM appears first (about days 5-7)** and **IgG later (about days 10-14)**, so serology lags behind the illness and diagnoses it best on **paired sera**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAAT/PCR)** amplify a target gene sequence millions of times; real-time PCR reads it as it amplifies, giving results in **1-3 hours** and, with a cycle threshold, a rough load. [Harrison 22e]`,`NAAT detects **DNA, not viability** - it can stay positive after cure (TB, bacterial DNA in a treated valve) and cannot be used as a **test of cure**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Genotypic resistance tests** look for known resistance genes (mecA, vanA, KPC, NDM, OXA-48, rpoB) - a detected gene predicts resistance well, but an undetected gene does not prove susceptibility. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification of diagnostic tests (the one examiners ask)`,points:[`**Microscopy**: Gram stain, Ziehl-Neelsen and fluorescent (auramine) staining for AFB, India ink, KOH mount, thick and thin malaria smears - cheap, rapid, operator-dependent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Culture**: aerobic and anaerobic bacterial, blood culture, mycobacterial (**solid LJ 4-8 weeks; liquid MGIT 960 usually 1-2 weeks, up to 6 weeks**), fungal and viral culture. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Antigen detection**: ELISA or immunochromatographic lateral flow - NS1, HBsAg, p24, malaria RDT, cryptococcal antigen, urinary LAM. [WHO 2025]`,`**Antibody detection**: ELISA, agglutination (Widal, Weil-Felix, MAT), immunofluorescence (IFA, gold standard for scrub typhus), immunoblot. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Molecular**: single-target PCR, real-time quantitative PCR (viral load), cartridge NAAT (**CBNAAT/Xpert, Truenat**), multiplex syndromic panels, line probe assay and sequencing (16S rRNA, targeted and metagenomic NGS). [WHO 2024]`,`**Host-response biomarkers**: CRP, procalcitonin, ESR, ferritin, lactate - they measure inflammation or illness severity, not the organism. [SSC 2021]`,`Each test is judged by **sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV and turnaround time** against a reference standard - a rule-out test needs high sensitivity, a rule-in test high specificity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Blood culture: indications and technique`,points:[`**Indications**: suspected sepsis or bacteraemia, fever with rigors, suspected **infective endocarditis**, enteric fever, febrile neutropenia, PUO, new fever in a hospitalised or catheterised patient, and severe pneumonia or pyelonephritis needing admission. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**One set = one aerobic plus one anaerobic bottle from one venepuncture**; take **2 sets (up to 3-4) from separate sites** before the first antibiotic - never a single set. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Volume**: **20 mL per set in adults (8-10 mL per bottle)**, so 40-60 mL in total; children by weight (about **1-3 mL in neonates**, never less than 1 mL), in paediatric bottles. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Detection rises with the number of sets: about **73% of bacteraemias with one set, 90% with two and 98% with three** - one set also cannot tell a contaminant from a pathogen. [Lee 2007]`,`**Skin antisepsis**: **2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol** (or tincture of iodine), applied with friction and allowed to **dry for 30 seconds**; wipe bottle tops with 70% alcohol; do not palpate the site after cleaning. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`Draw by **fresh peripheral venepuncture**; avoid drawing from an existing cannula except when **paired catheter and peripheral cultures** are taken for suspected catheter infection (see the Bloodstream infection topic). [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`In suspected **endocarditis** take **3 sets at least 30 minutes apart** before antibiotics in a stable patient; in sepsis, cultures must not delay antibiotics beyond **1 hour**. [ESC 2023]`,`Label each bottle with **site and time**, send at **room temperature** within 2 hours - never refrigerate - and tell the laboratory if brucella, fungi or a slow grower such as HACEK is suspected. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`In **suspected enteric fever** blood culture is positive in about **60-80% in week 1**, falling later; **bone marrow culture** stays positive in **80-95%** even after antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Reading a culture result: pathogen or contaminant`,points:[`**Always pathogens** in blood: **S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, beta-haemolytic streptococci, Candida** and Salmonella - never dismiss one positive bottle. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Usual contaminants**: **coagulase-negative staphylococci, Corynebacterium (diphtheroids), Bacillus (not anthracis), Cutibacterium (Propionibacterium) and Micrococcus** - especially if in only **one of two or more sets**. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`A skin organism becomes significant when it grows in **two or more sets drawn separately**, with a prosthetic valve, a long line or a cardiac device in place. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Time to positivity** helps: true pathogens usually flag within **12-24 hours**, while contaminants often take **more than 48 hours**; a short time to positivity suggests a high bacterial load. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`The **Gram stain of a positive bottle** is the first actionable result - Gram-positive cocci in clusters means staphylococcus until proved otherwise and justifies MRSA cover until speciation. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`The **blood culture contamination rate** is a quality marker: the long-standing benchmark is **under 3%**, and **1% or less** is now the target. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**Urine**: a clean-catch midstream sample processed within 2 hours (or refrigerated at 4 C for up to 24 hours); **10^5 CFU/mL** is the classic cut-off, lower counts count in symptomatic patients - details are in the UTI topic. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Sputum** is acceptable for culture only if it has **under 10 squamous epithelial cells and over 25 neutrophils per low-power field** - otherwise it is saliva. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Mixed growth of three or more organisms** in urine or a superficial swab usually means contamination - repeat the specimen rather than treat it. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Antimicrobial susceptibility testing, MIC and the sensitivity report`,points:[`The **minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)** is the **lowest concentration of an antibiotic that inhibits visible growth** of an organism in vitro, read in two-fold dilutions (0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4 mg/L). [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Broth microdilution** is the reference method; **gradient strips (E-test)** and **automated systems (VITEK 2, BD Phoenix)** give MICs, while **Kirby-Bauer disc diffusion** on Mueller-Hinton agar gives zone diameters. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`A **clinical breakpoint** is the MIC (or zone) that separates likely success from likely failure at a standard dose; it is set from **PK/PD targets, MIC distributions and clinical outcomes**. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`CLSI categories: **S (susceptible), SDD (susceptible dose-dependent - needs a higher dose, e.g. cefepime), I (intermediate), R (resistant)** and NS (non-susceptible); EUCAST redefined **I as susceptible, increased exposure** in 2019. [EUCAST 2025]`,`**Never choose a drug by comparing MIC numbers across drugs** - each drug has its own breakpoint, so an MIC of 4 may be very susceptible for one agent and resistant for another. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Vancomycin for S. aureus must be tested by MIC, not disc**: S at 2 mg/L or less, VISA 4-8, VRSA 16 or more; **colistin is tested only by broth microdilution** and CLSI has no susceptible category (I at 2 mg/L or less). [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Salmonella** fluoroquinolone susceptibility is read by **ciprofloxacin MIC (S at 0.06 mg/L or less)** or pefloxacin disc - most Indian S. Typhi are non-susceptible, so ciprofloxacin is no longer empirical treatment. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Phenotypic resistance tests**: cefoxitin disc for **MRSA**, **D-test** for inducible clindamycin resistance, ESBL confirmation, and **mCIM with eCIM** to detect a carbapenemase and separate metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM) from serine enzymes (KPC, OXA-48). [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Selective (cascade) reporting** releases broad-spectrum results only if narrow agents are resistant - it steers prescribers to the narrowest effective drug, as recommended by ICMR. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`A **cumulative antibiogram** (first isolate per patient, at least 30 isolates per organism, updated yearly) guides local empirical therapy. [CLSI M39 2022]`]},{heading:`Molecular diagnostics: CBNAAT, Truenat, multiplex PCR and sequencing`,points:[`**CBNAAT (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra)** is a cartridge real-time PCR that detects **M. tuberculosis and rpoB mutations (rifampicin resistance)** in about **2 hours**; Ultra is more sensitive and adds a **'trace' category**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Truenat (MTB, MTB Plus, MTB-RIF Dx)** is an Indian, battery-operated, chip-based micro-PCR deployable at **primary health centre level**; NTEP uses Xpert or Truenat as the **upfront first test for all presumptive TB**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Line probe assays** (first-line for isoniazid via katG and inhA and rifampicin; second-line for fluoroquinolones) and **Xpert MTB/XDR** detect resistance to isoniazid and fluoroquinolones within 1-2 days. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Multiplex syndromic panels** (e.g. BioFire FilmArray) test one specimen for many targets in about **1 hour**: blood culture identification with resistance genes (mecA, vanA/B, CTX-M, KPC, NDM, OXA-48, VIM, IMP), respiratory, gastrointestinal and **meningitis/encephalitis (14 targets)** panels. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Panels can **over-call** (colonisers and dead organisms in stool and respiratory panels) and **miss** organisms not on the list; the meningitis panel does not replace **CSF culture and cryptococcal antigen**. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Disease-specific PCR**: dengue RT-PCR in days 1-5; scrub typhus PCR (47-kDa or 56-kDa gene) on **eschar** or buffy coat; leptospira PCR on blood in week 1 and urine after it; HSV PCR on CSF. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Quantitative PCR (viral load)** monitors HIV, HBV, HCV and CMV treatment rather than diagnosing acute fever. [NACO 2021]`,`**16S rRNA PCR and sequencing** on culture-negative tissue (valve, bone, joint, abscess) after antibiotics, and **targeted next-generation sequencing** for drug-resistant TB, are tertiary tools. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Serology: IgM and IgG timing, paired sera and avidity`,points:[`**IgM** becomes detectable at about **days 5-7** of illness, peaks at 2-3 weeks and usually fades over **2-3 months** - but in scrub typhus and leptospirosis it can persist for many months. [ICMR 2022]`,`**IgG** rises from about **days 10-14** and persists for years; on its own it means **past infection** unless a **fourfold rise** is shown in paired sera. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paired sera** are taken in the acute phase and **10-14 days later**; a **fourfold or greater rise in titre** (or seroconversion) is the serological proof of acute infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **secondary dengue** IgG rises **early (by days 1-3)** and IgM may be low or absent, so a high IgG with low IgM early in illness suggests secondary infection rather than past exposure. [WHO 2025]`,`**IgG avidity** distinguishes recent from old infection: **low avidity means recent infection**, high avidity in toxoplasma excludes infection in roughly the previous **4 months** - used chiefly in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **Widal test** has poor sensitivity and specificity in endemic India; a single titre should not be used to diagnose enteric fever, and ICMR does not recommend it. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Weil-Felix** is obsolete for scrub typhus; use **IgM ELISA** (from day 5) with **IFA** as the reference, and the **MAT** remains the reference for leptospirosis (single titre of 1:400 or more, or a fourfold rise). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Window period**: fourth-generation HIV antigen-antibody tests detect infection from about **2-6 weeks** after exposure; a negative test inside the window needs repeating. [NACO 2021]`,`**Immunosuppressed patients** (advanced HIV, transplant, rituximab, hypogammaglobulinaemia) may never mount antibody - use antigen or NAAT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Rapid diagnostic tests at the point of care`,points:[`**Bivalent malaria RDT** detects **HRP2 (P. falciparum)** and **pan-pLDH (all species)**; HRP2 persists **2-4 weeks after cure**, so it cannot be used for test of cure, and **pfhrp2/3 gene deletions** cause false negatives. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`A negative malaria RDT in a strongly suspected case needs a **thick and thin smear**, repeated **12-hourly for up to 3 samples**; the smear also gives species and parasite density. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Dengue NS1 antigen** is positive in **days 1-5**; a combined NS1-IgM-IgG card helps across the illness, but ELISA (MAC-ELISA) is the confirmatory test under NCVBDC. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**rK39 RDT** diagnoses visceral leishmaniasis in a compatible clinical case in endemic districts; it stays positive for years, so it cannot diagnose relapse. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Cryptococcal antigen lateral flow** on serum in advanced HIV and **urine LF-LAM** in seriously ill people living with HIV are WHO-recommended bedside tests (see the HIV topics). [WHO 2022]`,`**Typhoid RDTs** (Typhidot, TUBEX) have inadequate accuracy and are **not recommended** for diagnosis; blood culture remains the standard. [WHO 2023]`,`**Urine dipstick**: **nitrite** (Enterobacterales convert nitrate) and **leucocyte esterase** together have a high negative predictive value for cystitis in women, but dipsticks are unreliable in older and catheterised patients. [IDSA 2019]`,`Antigen RDTs for **influenza, SARS-CoV-2 and group A streptococcus** have good specificity but modest sensitivity - a negative result in a high-probability patient needs PCR or culture. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Biomarkers: CRP and procalcitonin`,points:[`**C-reactive protein** is made by **hepatocytes under IL-6**; it rises from **4-6 hours**, doubles about every 8 hours, peaks at **36-50 hours** and has a half-life of about **19 hours**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Procalcitonin (PCT)** is the precursor of calcitonin; in bacterial infection many tissues release it under **IL-1, IL-6 and TNF**, while **interferon-gamma in viral infection suppresses it** - which is why PCT separates bacterial from viral better than CRP. [Harrison 22e]`,`PCT rises within **2-4 hours**, peaks at **6-24 hours** and has a half-life of about **24 hours**; normal is **under 0.05 ng/mL**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower respiratory tract infection algorithm**: PCT **under 0.1** strongly discourages antibiotics, **0.1-0.25** discourages, **0.25-0.5** encourages and **over 0.5 ng/mL** strongly encourages them. [ProHOSP 2009]`,`**Stopping rule in sepsis**: consider stopping antibiotics when PCT falls by **80% or more from its peak** or to **under 0.5 ng/mL** in a clinically improving patient. [SSC 2021]`,`PCT is **falsely raised** by major surgery, trauma, burns, cardiogenic shock, heat stroke, **medullary thyroid and small-cell lung cancer**, anti-thymocyte globulin, the first 48 hours of life and **falciparum malaria**; it is mildly higher in CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`PCT may be **falsely low** in early infection (first hours), localised infection (abscess, empyema), and atypical or intracellular organisms such as Mycoplasma and some rickettsial infections. [Harrison 22e]`,`In primary care, NICE uses **point-of-care CRP** in suspected lower respiratory infection: **under 20 mg/L no antibiotic, 20-100 mg/L consider a delayed prescription, over 100 mg/L offer an antibiotic**. [NICE CG191 2019]`,`Neither CRP nor PCT should be used to **withhold antibiotics from a patient who looks septic**; Surviving Sepsis advises against PCT to decide when to start. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Already on antibiotics**: still take blood cultures - draw just **before the next dose**; use PCR, antigen tests or bone marrow culture (enteric fever), which are less affected. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: screen for **asymptomatic bacteriuria by urine culture** at the first antenatal visit; use **IgG avidity** for toxoplasma and CMV IgG positives; serology is otherwise interpreted as in non-pregnant adults. [WHO ANC 2016]`,`**Neonates**: PCT rises physiologically in the **first 48 hours**, and CRP is slow - serial values and blood culture (at least 1 mL) are needed for early-onset sepsis. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Immunocompromised and neutropenic**: fever may be the only sign, antibody tests fail, and fungal antigens (galactomannan, beta-D-glucan), CMV PCR and repeated cultures are needed (see the Fever in the immunocompromised topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: baseline PCT is higher, so use trends rather than one value; culture any dialysis catheter and peritoneal fluid early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Older adults**: blunted fever and atypical presentations; **asymptomatic bacteriuria is common (up to 15-50% in long-term care)** and a positive urine culture alone should not be treated. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: rely on **Gram stain, smear microscopy, RDTs and NEDL tests**, and use the **NTEP sample transport** and district public health laboratory (IDSP) hub-and-spoke system for culture and NAAT. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Biosafety**: suspected TB, brucella, melioidosis or anthrax specimens must be flagged so the laboratory handles them in **biosafety level 2-3** conditions. [WHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications of poor testing and how to avoid them`,points:[`**Treating contaminants** (single CoNS bottle) exposes patients to vancomycin toxicity, longer stays and C. difficile; repeat cultures before treating a skin organism. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria** or a catheter urine culture without symptoms drives resistance and C. difficile, with no benefit except in pregnancy and before urological surgery. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Diagnosing typhoid by Widal** leads to overtreatment of viral fevers and missed dengue, malaria and scrub typhus. [ICMR 2022]`,`**False reassurance from early IgM** - a negative dengue or scrub IgM on day 2 means nothing; the test must be repeated after day 5. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Using PCR as test of cure** (persistent TB NAAT positivity) leads to wrongful labels of treatment failure; monitor TB response with smear and culture. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Ignoring a positive bottle** of S. aureus or Candida is a serious error - each carries high mortality and always needs treatment and source search. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Late cultures** after empirical broad-spectrum therapy prevent de-escalation and lock the patient onto carbapenems. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Communicating with the laboratory, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Write a **clinical summary on every request**: working diagnosis, day of illness, antibiotics already given, travel and exposure - it changes media, incubation and which tests are added. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`The laboratory should **phone critical results** - positive blood culture Gram stain, positive CSF, malaria parasites, carbapenemase producer - and the clinician must act the same day. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Review every culture at 48-72 hours** and de-escalate or stop - this 'antibiotic time-out' is the key stewardship step. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Repeat blood cultures** are needed for **S. aureus and Candida bloodstream infection** (until negative) and for endocarditis, but not routinely for Gram-negative bacteraemia that is responding. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Serial PCT** (day 0, then every 1-2 days) is useful only to **shorten** therapy; a single value is less useful than the trend. [ADAPT-Sepsis 2025]`,`Send isolates of public-health importance (cholera, diphtheria, carbapenemase producers, unusual resistance) to **reference or ICMR laboratories** and notify clusters on **IHIP** under IDSP. [IDSP 2024]`,`Viral fevers with outbreak potential (Nipah, Zika, Chandipura, avian influenza) are tested through the **ICMR-DHR Viral Research and Diagnostic Laboratories (VRDL)** network and NIV Pune. [ICMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and the national programme link`,points:[`Good specimen practice is itself **infection prevention**: correct antisepsis, closed blood culture systems and safe needle disposal protect both patient and staff. [WHO 2020]`,`**Laboratory waste**: microbiology cultures and stocks go into the **yellow category** and must be **pre-treated (autoclaved) on site** before disposal under the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`The **National Action Plan on AMR** and **NCDC's National AMR Surveillance Network (NARS-Net)** depend on quality culture and AST data from district and medical-college laboratories. [NCDC 2023]`,`The **ICMR AMR surveillance and stewardship programme** links microbiology reporting to hospital antimicrobial stewardship (AMSP) committees. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**NTEP** provides free NAAT, culture and drug-susceptibility testing through a network of intermediate and national reference laboratories. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Free Diagnostics Service Initiative** of the National Health Mission makes essential tests free at public facilities, including at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** level. [NHM 2019]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**IDSA/ASM Guide to Utilization of the Microbiology Laboratory (2024 update)** sets the specimen, volume and test-choice standards for each syndrome, including 20 mL per adult blood culture set. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**CLSI M100** is revised yearly (**34th edition 2024, 35th edition 2025**); recent cycles **lowered piperacillin-tazobactam (2022) and aminoglycoside (2023) breakpoints** for Enterobacterales and added breakpoints for new agents such as sulbactam-durlobactam. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**EUCAST breakpoint tables** (version 14.0 2024, 15.0 2025) keep the 'I = susceptible, increased exposure' definition; reports from labs using EUCAST and CLSI are not interchangeable. [EUCAST 2025]`,`**WHO TB diagnostics module (2024 update)** groups NAATs by complexity, endorses low-complexity automated NAATs (Xpert, Truenat) as initial tests and adds **targeted next-generation sequencing** for drug resistance. [WHO 2024]`,`**ADAPT-Sepsis (2025)**: daily **PCT-guided** stopping safely shortened antibiotic duration in sepsis, whereas a **CRP-guided** strategy did not. [ADAPT-Sepsis 2025]`,`**CLSI M47 (2022)** and CDC guidance (2024) set the **blood culture contamination target at 1% or less**, promoting initial-specimen diversion devices and dedicated phlebotomy teams. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**NTEP** continues to scale **upfront NAAT for every presumptive TB patient**, replacing sputum smear as the first test wherever NAAT is available. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'How do you collect a blood culture?'** - two sets, separate sites, 20 mL per set, chlorhexidine-alcohol dried, before antibiotics, room temperature within 2 hours. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Classic viva: **'What is MIC and breakpoint?'** - lowest concentration preventing visible growth; breakpoint separates S from R; never compare MICs between drugs. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`Classic viva: **'When is IgM ELISA useful?'** - from day 5 onwards; NS1 or PCR before that; paired sera for proof. [ICMR 2022]`,`Common mistake: **sending Widal** for fever of 3 days - it has no place; blood culture is the test. [ICMR 2022]`,`Common mistake: treating **CoNS in one bottle** as a pathogen, or **asymptomatic bacteriuria** in an older adult. [IDSA 2019]`,`Common mistake: using **PCT to withhold antibiotics in a septic patient** - PCT helps to stop, not to start. [SSC 2021]`,`Know why **colistin and vancomycin (for S. aureus)** cannot be reported by disc diffusion - large molecules diffuse poorly in agar. [CLSI M100 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Report-guided de-escalation: narrow drugs to switch to once the culture is back`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cefazolin (MSSA bacteraemia)`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`14 days uncomplicated, 4-6 weeks complicated (from first negative culture)`,`Rash, rare anaphylaxis; preferred over vancomycin for MSSA`],[`Cloxacillin (MSSA, alternative)`,`2 g IV 4-6-hourly`,`As for cefazolin`,`Phlebitis, hepatitis, interstitial nephritis; frequent dosing`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA)`,`Load 20-35 mg/kg IV, then 15-20 mg/kg 8-12-hourly`,`AUC/MIC target 400-600; trough monitoring if AUC not available`,`Nephrotoxicity (worse with pip-tazo), red man syndrome; not for MSSA`],[`Ceftriaxone (susceptible Enterobacterales)`,`1-2 g IV once daily`,`7 days for uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia with source control`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; ineffective if ESBL`],[`Ertapenem (ESBL producer, stable)`,`1 g IV once daily`,`7-14 days by source`,`Seizures (rare), C. difficile; no Pseudomonas or Acinetobacter cover`],[`Nitrofurantoin (ESBL E. coli cystitis)`,`100 mg oral 12-hourly (modified release)`,`5 days`,`Avoid if eGFR under 30, in pyelonephritis and at term; pulmonary fibrosis with long use`],[`Ampicillin (Enterococcus faecalis, ampicillin-S)`,`2 g IV 4-hourly`,`7-14 days; 6 weeks in endocarditis`,`Rash, C. difficile; E. faecium is usually resistant`],[`Azithromycin (S. Typhi, azithromycin-S)`,`1 g oral once daily (child 20 mg/kg)`,`7 days`,`Diarrhoea, QT prolongation; preferred over ciprofloxacin in India`],[`Fluconazole (fluconazole-S Candida, step-down)`,`800 mg (12 mg/kg) load, then 400 mg (6 mg/kg) daily`,`14 days after first negative culture and symptom resolution`,`Hepatotoxicity, QT prolongation, interactions; C. krusei is intrinsically resistant`]]},{heading:`Classification of microbiological tests`,columns:[`Method`,`What it detects`,`Turnaround`,`Strength`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Microscopy (Gram, ZN, smear)`,`Organism morphology`,`Minutes-1 hour`,`Cheap, fast, guides empirical choice`,`Operator-dependent; low sensitivity (AFB smear)`],[`Culture and AST`,`Viable organism and its phenotypic susceptibility`,`24-72 h (TB 1-8 weeks)`,`Gold standard; gives MIC`,`Slow; killed by prior antibiotics`],[`Antigen tests (NS1, HRP2, CrAg)`,`Microbial protein or polysaccharide`,`15-60 min`,`Early, bedside`,`Antigen may persist after cure (HRP2)`],[`Antibody serology (IgM, IgG)`,`Host immune response`,`Hours`,`Useful after day 5 and for past exposure`,`Lags illness; cross-reacts; fails in immunosuppression`],[`NAAT/PCR (CBNAAT, Truenat)`,`Specific nucleic acid`,`1-3 h`,`Sensitive, rapid, some resistance genes`,`Detects dead organisms; limited targets`],[`Multiplex syndromic panels`,`Many organisms and resistance genes`,`About 1 h`,`Broad, fast`,`Costly; over-calls colonisers`],[`Biomarkers (CRP, PCT)`,`Host inflammation`,`1 h`,`Trend guides duration`,`Not organism-specific; false positives`]]},{heading:`Contaminant versus true bacteraemia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Favours contaminant`,`Favours true bacteraemia`],rows:[[`Organism`,`CoNS, diphtheroids, Bacillus, Cutibacterium, Micrococcus`,`S. aureus, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, streptococci, Candida`],[`Number of positive sets`,`One of two or more sets`,`Two or more separate sets`],[`Time to positivity`,`Over 48 hours`,`Under 24 hours`],[`Clinical picture`,`Well, no device, other cause for fever`,`Sepsis, prosthetic valve, long line, device`],[`Same organism at a site`,`No matching focus`,`Same organism from urine, pus or line tip`],[`Action`,`Repeat 2 sets before treating`,`Treat, search for source, repeat cultures if S. aureus or Candida`]]},{heading:`Investigations: timing and cut-offs that change decisions`,columns:[`Test`,`Best window`,`Cut-off or interpretation`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Blood culture`,`Before first antibiotic`,`Any growth of a true pathogen is significant`,`Low volume, single set, contamination`],[`Dengue NS1 / RT-PCR`,`Days 1-5`,`Positive confirms current dengue`,`Falls after day 5; negative does not exclude`],[`IgM ELISA (dengue, scrub, lepto)`,`From day 5`,`Positive suggests recent infection`,`False negative before day 5; persists months`],[`Paired IgG`,`Acute and 10-14 days later`,`Fourfold rise confirms acute infection`,`Retrospective only`],[`Leptospira MAT`,`Second week onwards`,`Single titre 1:400 or more, or fourfold rise`,`Reference labs only`],[`Procalcitonin`,`Serial, 0 and 24-48 h`,`Under 0.25 (LRTI) bacterial unlikely; stop at 80% fall or under 0.5 ng/mL`,`Raised by surgery, trauma, malaria`],[`CRP (primary care LRTI)`,`At presentation`,`Under 20 no antibiotic; 20-100 delayed; over 100 treat`,`Non-specific; slow to rise`],[`Urine culture`,`Clean catch, plated within 2 h`,`10^5 CFU/mL classic cut-off`,`Asymptomatic bacteriuria needs no treatment`]]},{heading:`Specimen collection at a glance`,columns:[`Specimen`,`How to collect`,`Transport`,`Common error`],rows:[[`Blood culture`,`2 sets, 20 mL per set, chlorhexidine-alcohol`,`Room temperature, within 2 h`,`Single set, under-filled bottles`],[`Urine`,`Midstream clean catch; catheter port, not bag`,`Within 2 h or 4 C up to 24 h`,`Culture of catheter bag urine`],[`Sputum`,`Deep cough, early morning, before antibiotic`,`Within 2 h`,`Saliva sent (over 10 squamous cells/LPF)`],[`CSF`,`Lumbar puncture, 3-4 tubes, 1-2 mL each`,`Immediately, never refrigerate for culture`,`Delay after antibiotics`],[`Pus / tissue`,`Aspirate or tissue in sterile container`,`Within 2 h; anaerobic transport if needed`,`Surface swab of an ulcer`],[`Stool`,`Fresh stool in sterile container`,`Within 2 h or Cary-Blair medium`,`Testing formed stool or after 3 days in hospital`]]}],redFlags:[`Gram-positive cocci in clusters, Gram-negative bacilli or yeast in a positive blood culture - start or adjust therapy the same day`,`Positive blood culture for S. aureus or Candida in any patient - always pathogenic, needs admission and source search`,`Carbapenemase producer (NDM, OXA-48, KPC) reported in a sick patient - urgent ID input and contact precautions`,`Positive CSF Gram stain, cryptococcal antigen or malaria parasites on smear - treat as an emergency`,`Rising PCT or lactate with clinical deterioration despite 'appropriate' antibiotics - look for an undrained source or resistant organism`,`Rifampicin resistance detected on CBNAAT or Truenat - refer to the NTEP DR-TB centre the same week`,`Suspected outbreak organism (cholera, diphtheria, Nipah) - notify on IHIP immediately`],pearls:[`**Two sets, 20 mL each, before the first antibiotic** - the volume, not the brand of bottle, decides the yield.`,`**One bottle of CoNS is a contaminant until a second set says otherwise.**`,`**S. aureus and Candida in blood are never contaminants.**`,`**IgM from day 5, NS1 and PCR before it** - the day of illness picks the test.`,`**MIC is drug-specific**: compare each MIC with its own breakpoint, never with another drug's MIC.`,`**PCT stops antibiotics; it does not start them** - stop at an 80% fall or under 0.5 ng/mL.`,`**NAAT detects DNA, not life** - never a test of cure for TB.`,`**Widal is not a test for enteric fever in India** - blood culture in week 1, bone marrow if already treated.`,`**Colonisation is not infection** - treat the patient, not the swab.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q1`,stem:`A 56-year-old man with fever and rigors is to start empirical antibiotics. What is the recommended blood culture practice before the first dose in an adult?`,options:[`One set of 5 mL drawn from the existing cannula`,`Two sets of about 20 mL each from separate venepunctures`,`One aerobic bottle of 10 mL from the antecubital fossa`,`Three sets drawn only after the fever spikes above 39 C`],answer:1,explanation:`Two sets of about 20 mL each (8-10 mL per aerobic and anaerobic bottle) from separate venepunctures detect about 90% of bacteraemias and let a contaminant be told from a pathogen. A single set or small volume misses cases, drawing from an old cannula raises contamination, and waiting for a fever spike only delays cultures and antibiotics.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman admitted with cellulitis has two blood culture sets taken. One aerobic bottle grows coagulase-negative staphylococci after 52 hours; the other set is sterile. She is improving on cefazolin and has no prosthetic device. What is the best interpretation?`,options:[`Probable contaminant; continue current therapy without adding vancomycin`,`Methicillin-resistant staphylococcal bacteraemia requiring vancomycin`,`Infective endocarditis until proved otherwise; start gentamicin`,`Catheter-related bloodstream infection requiring line removal`],answer:0,explanation:`A skin organism (coagulase-negative staphylococci) in only one of two sets, flagging after 48 hours, in an improving patient without a device is a classic contaminant. Adding vancomycin for presumed MRSA bacteraemia, labelling it endocarditis, or blaming a line she does not have would expose her to toxicity without benefit; repeat cultures only if she deteriorates.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q3`,stem:`A patient with fever of 2 days, myalgia and platelets of 1.1 lakh/microlitre has a negative dengue IgM ELISA. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Exclude dengue and start ceftriaxone for enteric fever`,`Send a Widal test to look for typhoid`,`Send dengue NS1 antigen now and repeat IgM after day 5 if needed`,`Send paired IgG on day 3 to look for a fourfold rise`],answer:2,explanation:`IgM usually appears only from about day 5, so a negative IgM on day 2 means nothing; NS1 antigen (or RT-PCR) is the test for days 1-5. Excluding dengue on this result and starting ceftriaxone is the classic error, the Widal test is not recommended in India, and paired IgG needs samples 10-14 days apart, not on day 3.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q4`,stem:`A Klebsiella pneumoniae isolate from blood has an MIC of 1 mg/L to drug X (susceptible breakpoint 1 mg/L or less) and an MIC of 4 mg/L to drug Y (susceptible breakpoint 16 mg/L or less). Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Drug X is more active because its MIC number is lower`,`Both drugs are resistant because the MICs are above 0.5 mg/L`,`MICs of different drugs can be compared directly once in the same units`,`Drug Y is further below its own breakpoint and is the more reliable choice on MIC grounds`],answer:3,explanation:`An MIC is meaningful only against that drug's own breakpoint: drug Y at 4 mg/L sits two dilutions below its breakpoint of 16, while drug X at 1 mg/L is right at its breakpoint, so Y has more margin. Choosing drug X because its number is lower, or comparing MICs across drugs in the same units, is the classic error; neither drug is resistant by its breakpoint.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q5`,stem:`In which of the following is serum procalcitonin most likely to be raised in the absence of bacterial infection?`,options:[`Uncomplicated influenza A`,`Medullary carcinoma of the thyroid`,`Systemic lupus erythematosus flare`,`Infectious mononucleosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Procalcitonin is the calcitonin precursor, so medullary thyroid carcinoma (a C-cell tumour) raises it without infection, as do major surgery, trauma, burns and falciparum malaria. Viral infections such as influenza and infectious mononucleosis keep it low because interferon-gamma suppresses PCT, and lupus flares typically raise CRP-like markers little and PCT not at all.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the collection and interpretation of blood cultures. Explain the antimicrobial susceptibility report including MIC and breakpoints, and add a note on rapid molecular tests used in febrile illness.`,openingLines:[`Blood culture is the gold-standard test for bacteraemia and the only route to the organism and its phenotypic susceptibility (MIC), on which de-escalation depends.`,`Its yield is decided before the sample reaches the laboratory - by volume, number of sets, antisepsis and timing before the first antibiotic.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Sepsis, fever with rigors, suspected endocarditis, enteric fever, febrile neutropenia, PUO, hospital-onset fever.`,`Severe pneumonia, pyelonephritis or meningitis needing admission.`]},{heading:`Technique`,points:[`Two sets (aerobic plus anaerobic) from separate venepunctures; 20 mL per set in adults; 1-3 mL in neonates.`,`2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol, dry 30 seconds; bottle tops wiped; no palpation after cleaning.`,`Before antibiotics; within 1 hour in sepsis; 3 sets 30 minutes apart in suspected endocarditis.`,`Room temperature, to the laboratory within 2 hours; clinical details on the form.`]},{heading:`Processing`,points:[`Automated CO2-detecting systems, 5-day incubation; Gram stain of positive bottle phoned to clinician.`,`Identification by MALDI-TOF or biochemicals; AST by disc diffusion, gradient strip, VITEK or broth microdilution.`]},{heading:`Interpretation`,points:[`Always pathogens: S. aureus, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, streptococci, Candida.`,`Likely contaminants: CoNS, diphtheroids, Bacillus, Cutibacterium in one set, time to positivity over 48 h.`,`Contamination benchmark under 3%, target 1% or less.`]},{heading:`Susceptibility report, MIC and breakpoints`,points:[`MIC = lowest concentration inhibiting visible growth; breakpoint separates S, SDD, I and R (CLSI M100 2024).`,`Never compare MICs across drugs; vancomycin (S. aureus) and colistin by MIC only.`,`Phenotypic tests: cefoxitin for MRSA, D-test, ESBL, mCIM/eCIM for carbapenemase.`,`Selective reporting and cumulative antibiogram guide therapy and stewardship.`]},{heading:`Rapid molecular tests`,points:[`CBNAAT/Truenat: TB and rifampicin resistance in 2 hours; NTEP upfront NAAT.`,`Multiplex panels: blood culture ID with resistance genes, meningitis/encephalitis, respiratory panels in 1 hour.`,`Disease PCR: dengue RT-PCR days 1-5, scrub eschar PCR, HSV CSF PCR.`,`Limitations: DNA not viability, limited targets, cost, over-calling colonisers.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: specimen collection to Gram stain to identification to AST to de-escalation`,`Table: contaminant versus true bacteraemia`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications and technique`,marks:3},{part:`Processing and interpretation`,marks:2},{part:`MIC, breakpoints and the report`,marks:3},{part:`Rapid molecular tests`,marks:2}],keywords:[`blood culture`,`MIC`,`breakpoint`,`CLSI`,`CBNAAT`,`multiplex PCR`,`contaminant`]},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on procalcitonin and C-reactive protein in the management of infection.`,openingLines:[`CRP (an IL-6-driven hepatic acute-phase protein) and procalcitonin (the calcitonin precursor released by tissues in bacterial infection) are host-response biomarkers, not tests for an organism.`,`Their main value is in trends: PCT helps to shorten antibiotic courses, CRP helps primary-care decisions in respiratory infection.`],answer:[{heading:`Biology`,points:[`CRP: rises 4-6 h, peaks 36-50 h, half-life 19 h.`,`PCT: rises 2-4 h, peaks 6-24 h, half-life 24 h; suppressed by interferon-gamma in viral infection.`]},{heading:`Uses and cut-offs`,points:[`LRTI: PCT under 0.1 strongly against, 0.25-0.5 for, over 0.5 ng/mL strongly for antibiotics.`,`Sepsis: stop when PCT falls 80% from peak or under 0.5 ng/mL (SSC 2021; ADAPT-Sepsis 2025).`,`Primary care CRP: under 20 no antibiotic, 20-100 delayed, over 100 mg/L treat (NICE).`]},{heading:`Pitfalls`,points:[`PCT raised by surgery, trauma, burns, cardiogenic shock, medullary thyroid cancer, malaria, neonates.`,`Low in early or localised infection; do not withhold antibiotics in sepsis on a low value.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of PCT and CRP kinetics over 72 hours`],markSplit:[{part:`Biology and kinetics`,marks:2},{part:`Uses and cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Pitfalls`,marks:1}],keywords:[`procalcitonin`,`CRP`,`antibiotic stewardship`,`ADAPT-Sepsis`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Laboratory diagnosis of infectious diseases`,`Miller JM et al. A Guide to Utilization of the Microbiology Laboratory for Diagnosis of Infectious Diseases: 2024 Update by IDSA and ASM. Clin Infect Dis 2024`,`CLSI M100 Performance Standards for Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing, 34th edition, 2024`,`CLSI M47 Principles and Procedures for Blood Cultures, 2nd edition, 2022`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022, and ICMR AMRSN Annual Report 2023`,`WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis, Module 3: Diagnosis, 2024 update`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) Training Modules and Diagnostic Algorithms, MoHFW, 2024`,`Surviving Sepsis Campaign International Guidelines 2021; Dark P et al. ADAPT-Sepsis trial, JAMA 2025`]});var u=[];u.push({id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb`,title:`Drug-resistant TB: MDR, pre-XDR and the BPaLM regimen`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Drug-resistant TB** is TB caused by M. tuberculosis resistant to one or more anti-TB drugs - **RR/MDR-TB** (rifampicin with or without isoniazid), **pre-XDR** (plus a fluoroquinolone) and **XDR** (plus bedaquiline or linezolid); under **NTEP** every diagnosed patient gets **universal DST by NAAT**, and most MDR/RR-TB and pre-XDR patients aged 14 or more are now treated with the all-oral **6-month BPaLM/BPaL regimen**.`,keywords:[`MDR-TB`,`RR-TB`,`pre-XDR`,`XDR-TB`,`BPaLM`,`BPaL`,`bedaquiline`,`pretomanid`,`linezolid`,`moxifloxacin`,`shorter oral regimen`,`line probe assay`,`CBNAAT`,`Truenat`,`universal DST`,`PMDT`,`Ni-kshay`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana`,`QTcF`,`Hr-TB`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Rifampicin-resistant TB (RR-TB)**: resistance to rifampicin detected by genotypic or phenotypic test, with or without resistance to other drugs; it is managed exactly like MDR-TB. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**MDR-TB**: resistance to at least **rifampicin and isoniazid**; **pre-XDR-TB**: MDR/RR-TB plus resistance to **any fluoroquinolone** (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin). [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**XDR-TB (2021 definition)**: MDR/RR-TB plus resistance to a fluoroquinolone **and at least one more Group A drug (bedaquiline or linezolid)**; the old definition based on second-line injectables was retired in 2021 because injectables are no longer core drugs. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**Hr-TB**: isoniazid-resistant, rifampicin-susceptible TB - a separate, commoner entity with its own 6-month regimen. [WHO 2025]`,`Globally about **4 lakh people developed MDR/RR-TB in 2023**, and only about two in five were diagnosed and started on treatment; India carries the largest single share, about **one quarter of the global MDR/RR-TB burden**. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`The **First National Anti-TB Drug Resistance Survey (2014-16)** found MDR-TB in **2.8% of new** and **11.6% of previously treated** patients, any isoniazid resistance in 11% of new patients, and **fluoroquinolone resistance in about 22% of MDR-TB** - the reason second-line LPA is mandatory. [NDRS India 2018]`,`India notified **over 60,000 MDR/RR-TB patients in 2023**, most through rapid molecular tests at peripheral NAAT sites. [India TB Report 2024]`,`Treatment success for MDR/RR-TB globally is about **68%** with older long regimens; the 6-month BPaLM regimen achieved favourable outcomes in about **89%** in TB-PRACTECAL. [WHO Global TB Report 2024; Nyang'wa NEJM 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Resistance arises by **spontaneous chromosomal mutation** (no plasmids) selected by inadequate therapy - monotherapy, erratic supply, poor adherence or adding a single drug to a failing regimen (**acquired resistance**). [Harrison 22e]`,`Most MDR-TB in India is now **primary (transmitted) resistance** - a new patient infected with a resistant strain - so absence of previous treatment does not exclude MDR-TB. [NDRS India 2018]`,`High-risk groups for testing priority: **previously treated** patients (relapse, treatment after loss to follow-up, failure), **contacts of DR-TB**, non-converters at end of intensive phase, PLHIV, and healthcare workers. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Private-sector irrational prescribing** - unsupervised fluoroquinolone courses for cough, non-standard regimens - drives fluoroquinolone resistance and pre-XDR. [India TB Report 2024]`,`**Diabetes, undernutrition, alcohol, tobacco and HIV** raise TB risk and worsen DR-TB outcomes; malabsorption (HIV, diabetes gastroparesis) lowers drug levels. [WHO 2025]`,`Congregate settings - **prisons, hostels, slums, hospitals without airborne infection control** - sustain transmission of resistant strains. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and drug mechanisms`,points:[`**Rifampicin resistance**: over **95% due to mutations in the 81-bp rifampicin-resistance-determining region (RRDR) of rpoB** - the exact target that Xpert/CBNAAT and Truenat probe, so a rapid test gives near-definitive rifampicin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isoniazid resistance**: **katG** mutations cause high-level resistance; **inhA promoter** mutations cause low-level resistance with **cross-resistance to ethionamide** - LPA reports which, and it decides whether high-dose isoniazid or ethionamide is still useful. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Fluoroquinolone resistance**: **gyrA/gyrB** mutations in DNA gyrase; second-line LPA detects them and defines pre-XDR. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Bedaquiline** (diarylquinoline) blocks the **c subunit of mycobacterial ATP synthase (atpE)**, killing both replicating and dormant bacilli; its very long terminal half-life (about 5 months) means the drug lingers after stopping. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Rv0678 (mmpR5) mutations** up-regulate the MmpS5-MmpL5 efflux pump and give **cross-resistance between bedaquiline and clofazimine** - a key emerging threat. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Pretomanid** (nitroimidazole prodrug, activated by the bacterial **Ddn nitroreductase**) inhibits mycolic-acid synthesis in replicating bacilli and releases **nitric oxide** that kills hypoxic, non-replicating bacilli - the sterilising partner in BPaL. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Linezolid** binds the **23S rRNA of the 50S subunit**; it also inhibits **human mitochondrial protein synthesis**, which explains time- and dose-dependent **myelosuppression, peripheral and optic neuropathy and lactic acidosis**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**QT prolongation** with bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, clofazimine and delamanid is due to **hERG potassium-channel blockade**; effects are additive, so ECG monitoring is built into every regimen. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification and drug grouping`,points:[`Classify by resistance pattern: **mono-resistant, poly-resistant, Hr-TB, RR-TB, MDR-TB, pre-XDR, XDR-TB** - examiners expect the 2021 WHO definitions. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`Classify by history: **new** (under 1 month of anti-TB treatment) versus **previously treated** (relapse, treatment after loss to follow-up, treatment failure, other). [NTEP 2024]`,`**WHO Group A** (use all three if possible): **levofloxacin or moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, linezolid**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Group B** (add one or both): **clofazimine, cycloserine or terizidone**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Group C** (to complete a regimen): **ethambutol, delamanid, pyrazinamide, imipenem-cilastatin or meropenem (with clavulanate), amikacin, ethionamide or prothionamide, p-aminosalicylic acid**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Kanamycin and capreomycin** are no longer recommended (worse outcomes); **amikacin** only if susceptible and audiometry is possible. [WHO 2025]`,`NTEP regimen families: **BPaLM/BPaL (6 months)**, **shorter oral bedaquiline-containing regimen (9-11 months)**, **longer oral M/XDR-TB regimen (18-20 months)**, and the **Hr-TB regimen (6 months)**. [NTEP PMDT 2021; NTEP BPaLM 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Symptoms are **identical to drug-sensitive TB** - cough over 2 weeks, fever, night sweats, weight loss, haemoptysis - resistance is a laboratory, not a clinical, diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspect DR-TB when a patient on first-line treatment **remains smear or culture positive at month 2-3**, or **deteriorates clinically or radiologically** despite adherence. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Ask a structured **previous-treatment history**: number of courses, drugs used, private versus programme, duration, adherence, and any **fluoroquinolone courses for cough** (pre-XDR risk). [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Ask about **contact with a known DR-TB patient** in the household or workplace - treat as DR-TB until DST returns if the patient is sick. [WHO 2025]`,`Screen for **comorbidity and drug-safety issues**: diabetes, HIV, alcohol, liver or kidney disease, psychiatric illness (cycloserine), pregnancy, palpitations or syncope (QT), and existing neuropathy or poor vision (linezolid). [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`Ask about **social barriers to adherence** - migration, daily-wage loss, alcohol, stigma, distance to the DR-TB centre - these predict loss to follow-up more than biology. [India TB Report 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Record **weight and BMI** at baseline - doses are weight-banded and a BMI under 18.5 qualifies for nutrition support; failure to gain weight is an early sign of failure. [NTEP 2024]`,`Look for **chronic lung damage**: clubbing, bronchial breathing, crepitations over upper zones, tracheal deviation from fibrosis, and signs of **cor pulmonale**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Examine for **extrapulmonary disease** - lymph nodes, effusion, spine tenderness, meningism - because CNS, osteoarticular or disseminated TB excludes BPaLM and needs a longer, individualised regimen. [WHO 2025]`,`Baseline **visual acuity (Snellen) and colour vision (Ishihara)** and a **peripheral neuropathy screen** (vibration, ankle jerks) are mandatory before linezolid. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`Check **pulse rhythm and BP**; palpitations or bradycardia point to QT risk; look for **jaundice and hepatomegaly** before pretomanid and pyrazinamide. [WHO 2025]`,`Look for signs of **HIV** (oral thrush, lymphadenopathy, wasting) and **diabetes** complications - both change drug choice and monitoring. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Non-adherent drug-sensitive TB**: persistent positivity due to missed doses - DST is sensitive and the fix is supervised treatment, not a new regimen. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM)**: smear positive but **NAAT negative for MTB**, or culture grows a non-MTB species; common in bronchiectasis and post-TB lungs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-TB lung disease**: fibrosis, bronchiectasis and cavities with chronic cough but **negative culture** - not active resistance. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis**: haemoptysis and cavity with fungal ball in a treated patient; **Aspergillus IgG** positive, TB tests negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung cancer** in an older smoker with a non-resolving mass or nodes - CT and biopsy before labelling treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paradoxical reaction or TB-IRIS** in PLHIV after ART: worsening nodes or infiltrates with falling bacillary load - do not change the TB regimen. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Universal DST**: every presumptive TB patient should get a **NAAT (CBNAAT/Xpert or Truenat)** as the first test, which reports MTB and **rifampicin resistance in about 2 hours**. [NTEP 2024]`,`On rifampicin resistance, send a **second sample** for **first-line LPA** (isoniazid: katG, inhA) and **second-line LPA** (fluoroquinolones: gyrA/gyrB; amikacin: rrs, eis) - the FQ result decides **BPaLM versus BPaL**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Do not wait** for LPA to start treatment in an RR-TB patient: start BPaLM (or the shorter regimen) and modify when the FQ result arrives. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`Send **liquid culture (MGIT) and phenotypic DST** for bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, delamanid and pretomanid in pre-XDR, failure or contacts of DR-TB - genotypic tests for these drugs are still limited. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Targeted next-generation sequencing (tNGS)** detects resistance to many drugs at once, including bedaquiline and linezolid; WHO recommended it in 2024 and it is used at Indian reference laboratories. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`Baseline safety tests: **CBC, LFT, creatinine, K, Mg, Ca, TSH, blood glucose/HbA1c, HIV, pregnancy test, ECG with QTcF**, and audiometry if amikacin is planned. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**QTcF (Fridericia) over 500 ms** on any ECG means stop QT-prolonging drugs and correct electrolytes; **450-500 ms** means repeat ECG and closer monitoring. [WHO 2025]`,`Monitoring of response: **monthly sputum smear and culture**; **culture conversion** = two consecutive negative cultures taken at least 30 days apart. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Management: regimens and doses`,points:[`**BPaLM** for MDR/RR-TB aged **14 years or more**, FQ-susceptible or FQ status awaited: **bedaquiline + pretomanid + linezolid + moxifloxacin for 26 weeks (6 months)**. [WHO 2025; NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**BPaL** (moxifloxacin dropped) is used in **pre-XDR-TB** (FQ-resistant) for the same 26 weeks. [WHO 2025]`,`Doses: **bedaquiline 400 mg OD for 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times a week for 24 weeks**; **pretomanid 200 mg OD**; **linezolid 600 mg OD**; **moxifloxacin 400 mg OD** - all with food, for 26 weeks. [WHO 2025; NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Linezolid toxicity**: reduce to **300 mg OD** or interrupt for myelosuppression or neuropathy; ZeNix showed 600 mg for 26 weeks kept efficacy with less toxicity than 1200 mg. [Conradie NEJM 2022]`,`**BPaLM is not used** in pregnancy or breastfeeding, children under 14, **CNS, osteoarticular or disseminated TB**, or after more than 1 month of previous bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid or delamanid. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Shorter oral bedaquiline-containing regimen (9-11 months)**: 4-6 months of **bedaquiline (6 months), levofloxacin, clofazimine, pyrazinamide, ethambutol, high-dose isoniazid, ethionamide**, then 5 months of **levofloxacin, clofazimine, pyrazinamide, ethambutol** - for FQ-susceptible patients not eligible for BPaLM. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Longer oral M/XDR-TB regimen (18-20 months)**: all Group A plus Group B drugs (**bedaquiline, levofloxacin, linezolid, clofazimine, cycloserine**), individualised by DST - for XDR-TB, extensive EPTB, failure or intolerance. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Hr-TB**: **6 months of levofloxacin + rifampicin + ethambutol + pyrazinamide** once rifampicin susceptibility is confirmed. [WHO 2025; NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Adjuncts: **pyridoxine** with high-dose isoniazid, cycloserine or linezolid; **nutrition support**, smoking and alcohol cessation, and glycaemic control. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and DR-TB centres`,points:[`Treatment is initiated at a **Nodal/District DR-TB Centre** after pre-treatment evaluation; admission is for **sick patients, severe comorbidity, adverse events or where ambulatory care is not safe** - ambulatory care is the default. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Admit for **massive haemoptysis, respiratory failure, severe malnutrition, QTcF over 500 ms, hepatitis with jaundice, severe anaemia or neutropenia, psychosis, seizures** or pregnancy needing an individualised regimen. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Drug-induced hepatitis** (ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or over 5 times without, or bilirubin over 2 times): stop pretomanid, pyrazinamide and other hepatotoxic drugs, recheck LFT weekly, reintroduce under expert advice. [WHO 2025]`,`**QTcF over 500 ms**: stop bedaquiline, moxifloxacin and clofazimine, correct **K (keep over 4 mmol/L) and Mg**, repeat ECG, reintroduce one at a time once QTcF falls. [WHO 2025]`,`**Severe myelosuppression or optic neuritis** on linezolid: stop linezolid; optic neuritis means permanent discontinuation. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Airborne infection control** on the ward: separate DR-TB rooms with cross-ventilation, N95 respirators for staff, surgical masks for patients, cough hygiene. [NTEP Airborne Infection Control 2010]`,`**Massive haemoptysis** in cavitary DR-TB: lateral decubitus with bleeding side down, airway protection, tranexamic acid, and **bronchial artery embolisation**; consider **surgical resection** for localised disease with persistent positivity. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: BPaLM is avoided (no pretomanid safety data); use an individualised oral regimen with bedaquiline, linezolid, levofloxacin, clofazimine and cycloserine, avoiding **ethionamide (teratogenic)** and **aminoglycosides (fetal ototoxicity)**. [WHO 2025]`,`WHO 2025 added the 6-month **BDLLfxC** regimen (bedaquiline, delamanid, linezolid, levofloxacin, clofazimine) usable in **children and pregnant women** - adoption in India is under programme review. [WHO 2025]`,`**Children under 14**: shorter or longer oral regimens; **bedaquiline and delamanid are allowed at all ages**, with child-friendly formulations. [WHO 2025]`,`**PLHIV**: start **ART within 2-8 weeks** of DR-TB treatment; use **dolutegravir** - **efavirenz** induces CYP3A4 and lowers bedaquiline and pretomanid levels, and ritonavir raises bedaquiline levels. [WHO 2025]`,`Without rifampicin in the regimen, **dolutegravir is given at the standard 50 mg once daily** - no twice-daily dosing is needed. [WHO 2025]`,`**CKD**: adjust **levofloxacin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide and cycloserine** to creatinine clearance; bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin need no adjustment but monitor linezolid toxicity closely. [WHO 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: screen all; fluoroquinolones cause **dysglycaemia**, and linezolid neuropathy is commoner - aim for good glycaemic control from day 1. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Treatment outcomes: **cured, treatment completed, failed, died, lost to follow-up, not evaluated** - treatment success is cured plus completed. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**Linezolid**: anaemia, thrombocytopenia, **peripheral neuropathy (often irreversible)**, **optic neuropathy**, lactic acidosis and serotonin syndrome with SSRIs. [WHO 2025]`,`**Bedaquiline**: QT prolongation, nausea, arthralgia, raised transaminases; **clofazimine**: brown-black skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, QT prolongation. [WHO 2025]`,`**Cycloserine**: depression, psychosis, suicidal ideation and seizures; **ethionamide**: GI upset, hypothyroidism; **amikacin**: irreversible hearing loss and nephrotoxicity. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pretomanid**: hepatotoxicity, and peripheral neuropathy when combined with linezolid; animal testicular toxicity has not been shown in humans. [WHO 2025]`,`Poor-prognosis features: **XDR-TB, HIV with low CD4, BMI under 16, extensive cavitation, diabetes, alcohol use and previous failure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relapse** after BPaLM is uncommon (under 5% in trials); most unfavourable outcomes are due to death or loss to follow-up rather than failure. [Nyang'wa NEJM 2022]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Sputum smear and culture monthly** throughout treatment; in BPaLM, culture positive at **month 4 or later** means evaluate for failure and repeat DST. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**ECG**: baseline, **week 2, then monthly** (more often if on several QT drugs or electrolyte loss); keep K and Mg normal. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**CBC** baseline, weeks 2 and 4, then monthly (linezolid); **LFT** monthly (pretomanid, pyrazinamide); **TSH** at 3 and 6 months with ethionamide or PAS. [WHO 2025]`,`**Visual acuity, colour vision and neuropathy screening monthly** on linezolid; refer to ophthalmology for any drop. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Weight monthly** and dose re-banding; **post-treatment follow-up** with symptom check and sputum culture **every 6 months for 2 years**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Every visit, adverse event and result is entered in **Ni-kshay**; serious adverse events go through **aDSM (active drug-safety monitoring)** and PvPI. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, adherence and programme link`,points:[`**Prevent DR-TB** by treating drug-sensitive TB correctly - fixed-dose combinations, daily regimens, supervised adherence and **no fluoroquinolones for undiagnosed cough**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Contact tracing**: screen all household contacts of DR-TB with symptoms and chest X-ray; give **levofloxacin TPT for 6 months** to eligible contacts of MDR/RR-TB (FQ-susceptible) - see the latent TB topic. [WHO 2024 Module 1; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Adherence tools**: treatment supporter, **video-observed treatment**, MERM pill boxes, family DOT, Ni-kshay reminders; differentiated care for high-risk patients. [WHO 2025]`,`**Ni-kshay** is the notification and case-management portal; notification of every TB case, public or private, is **mandatory** (Gazette 2012, 2018). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana**: direct benefit transfer of **Rs 1000 per month** (raised from Rs 500 in November 2024) for the full duration of treatment; energy-dense nutrition supplements for underweight patients. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan**: Ni-kshay Mitras provide monthly food baskets; the **RATIONS trial** showed nutrition support reduced TB incidence in contacts by about 39-48%. [Bhargava Lancet 2023]`,`Free drugs, transport support, **DR-TB centres** with counselling, and **airborne infection control** in every facility. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2022**: 6-month **BPaLM/BPaL** recommended for MDR/RR-TB and pre-XDR aged 14 or more, replacing most 18-20 month regimens. [WHO 2022 Module 4]`,`**India 2024**: MoHFW approved **BPaLM** for NTEP in September 2024, with phased national roll-out from 2025 using domestically produced pretomanid. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**WHO 2024-2025**: new 6-month options from endTB (for example **BLMZ, BLLfxCZ, BDLLfxZ**) and BEAT-Tuberculosis (**BDLLfxC**), with BDLLfxC usable in children and pregnancy. [WHO 2025 Module 4]`,`**WHO 2024 Module 3**: **targeted NGS** endorsed for detecting resistance to multiple drugs, including bedaquiline and linezolid. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**WHO 2024 Module 1**: **6 months levofloxacin** TPT for contacts of MDR/RR-TB. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** increased to **Rs 1000 per month** in November 2024; Ni-kshay Mitra support extended to household contacts. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define MDR, pre-XDR and XDR **using the 2021 definitions** - quoting injectables in XDR is now a mark-losing error. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`Write BPaLM doses exactly: **B 400 mg OD x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly; Pa 200 mg OD; L 600 mg OD; M 400 mg OD; 26 weeks**. [WHO 2025]`,`Classic viva: **why drop moxifloxacin in pre-XDR?** - the strain is FQ-resistant, so it adds only QT toxicity. [WHO 2025]`,`Classic viva: **how does Xpert detect rifampicin resistance?** - molecular beacons/probes over the 81-bp RRDR of rpoB. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **adding one drug to a failing regimen** - this is how resistance is amplified. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **baseline ECG, vision and pregnancy test** before BPaLM. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in DR-TB`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Bedaquiline (B)`,`400 mg oral OD x 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times weekly; with food`,`24 weeks (BPaLM 26-week course)`,`QT prolongation, hepatitis, arthralgia; avoid with efavirenz`],[`Pretomanid (Pa)`,`200 mg oral OD with food`,`26 weeks`,`Hepatotoxicity, neuropathy with linezolid; not in pregnancy or under 14 years`],[`Linezolid (L)`,`600 mg oral OD (reduce to 300 mg for toxicity)`,`26 weeks in BPaLM`,`Myelosuppression, peripheral and optic neuropathy, lactic acidosis, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Moxifloxacin (M)`,`400 mg oral OD`,`26 weeks; dropped in pre-XDR (BPaL)`,`QT prolongation, dysglycaemia, tendinopathy`],[`Levofloxacin`,`750-1000 mg oral OD by weight`,`Shorter, longer and Hr-TB regimens; 6 months as TPT`,`Tendinopathy, QT, dysglycaemia; adjust in CKD`],[`Clofazimine`,`100 mg oral OD`,`Throughout shorter or longer regimen`,`Skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, QT prolongation`],[`Cycloserine`,`10-15 mg/kg/day oral (usually 500-750 mg)`,`Longer regimen`,`Depression, psychosis, seizures; give pyridoxine; avoid in epilepsy and severe CKD`],[`Delamanid`,`100 mg oral BD`,`24 weeks or longer`,`QT prolongation; allowed in children`]]},{heading:`Classification of drug-resistant TB (WHO 2021)`,columns:[`Type`,`Resistant to`,`NTEP regimen`],rows:[[`Hr-TB`,`Isoniazid, rifampicin susceptible`,`6 months levofloxacin + rifampicin + ethambutol + pyrazinamide`],[`RR-TB / MDR-TB`,`Rifampicin (with or without isoniazid)`,`BPaLM 6 months; shorter oral regimen if not eligible`],[`Pre-XDR-TB`,`MDR/RR plus any fluoroquinolone`,`BPaL 6 months; else longer oral regimen`],[`XDR-TB`,`MDR/RR plus fluoroquinolone plus bedaquiline or linezolid`,`Individualised longer regimen at expert centre`]]},{heading:`NTEP regimen comparison`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Drugs`,`Duration`,`Who`],rows:[[`BPaLM`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin`,`26 weeks`,`MDR/RR-TB, 14 years or more, not pregnant, no severe EPTB`],[`BPaL`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid`,`26 weeks`,`Pre-XDR-TB, same eligibility`],[`Shorter oral Bdq regimen`,`Bdq(6) Lfx Cfz Z E Hh Eto, then Lfx Cfz Z E`,`9-11 months`,`FQ-susceptible, not BPaLM eligible`],[`Longer oral M/XDR regimen`,`Bdq, Lfx, Lzd, Cfz, Cs, individualised`,`18-20 months`,`XDR, failure, extensive EPTB, intolerance`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests for drug resistance`,columns:[`Test`,`Detects`,`Turnaround`,`Use`],rows:[[`CBNAAT/Xpert, Truenat`,`MTB plus rifampicin resistance (rpoB RRDR)`,`About 2 hours`,`First test for every presumptive TB (universal DST)`],[`First-line LPA (MTBDRplus)`,`Rifampicin (rpoB), isoniazid (katG high-level, inhA low-level)`,`2-3 days`,`Hr-TB and isoniazid pattern`],[`Second-line LPA (MTBDRsl)`,`Fluoroquinolones (gyrA/B), amikacin (rrs, eis)`,`2-3 days`,`Every RR-TB; decides BPaLM versus BPaL`],[`Liquid culture DST (MGIT)`,`Bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, delamanid, pretomanid`,`4-6 weeks`,`Pre-XDR, failure, DR-TB contacts`],[`Targeted NGS`,`Many drugs including bedaquiline and linezolid`,`Days`,`Reference laboratories; complex resistance`]]},{heading:`Monitoring schedule on BPaLM`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Action threshold`],rows:[[`ECG with QTcF`,`Baseline, week 2, then monthly`,`Over 500 ms - stop QT drugs, correct K and Mg`],[`CBC`,`Baseline, weeks 2 and 4, then monthly`,`Hb under 8 g/dL or platelets under 50,000 - hold or reduce linezolid`],[`LFT`,`Baseline then monthly`,`ALT over 5 x ULN, or over 3 x with symptoms or jaundice - stop hepatotoxic drugs`],[`Vision and neuropathy screen`,`Monthly`,`Any drop in acuity or colour vision - stop linezolid`],[`Sputum smear and culture`,`Monthly`,`Positive at month 4 or later - evaluate for failure, repeat DST`]]}],redFlags:[`Massive haemoptysis (over 100-200 mL in 24 hours) or respiratory failure`,`QTcF over 500 ms, syncope or palpitations on bedaquiline, moxifloxacin or clofazimine`,`Jaundice or ALT over 5 times ULN on treatment`,`Sudden drop in vision or colour vision on linezolid`,`Haemoglobin under 8 g/dL, platelets under 50,000 or neutropenia on linezolid`,`Psychosis, suicidal thoughts or seizures on cycloserine`,`Culture still positive at month 4 or clinical worsening - suspect failure or XDR`,`Pregnancy, CNS TB or HIV with low CD4 - needs DR-TB centre and individualised regimen`],pearls:[`BPaLM = **B**edaquiline, **P**retom**a**nid, **L**inezolid, **M**oxifloxacin - 6 months, 14 years and above.`,`Pre-XDR: drop the **M** (BPaL) - the FQ no longer works.`,`XDR 2021 = MDR/RR + FQ + **bedaquiline or linezolid**; injectables are out of the definition.`,`Xpert reads the **81-bp RRDR of rpoB**; over 95% of rifampicin resistance lives there.`,`katG = high-level isoniazid resistance; **inhA = low-level plus ethionamide cross-resistance**.`,`Bedaquiline and clofazimine share resistance through **Rv0678** efflux.`,`QT drugs in DR-TB: **B, M, C, D** - bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, clofazimine, delamanid.`,`Never add a single drug to a failing regimen.`,`Ni-kshay Poshan is **Rs 1000 per month** since November 2024.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old man with sputum CBNAAT showing MTB detected, rifampicin resistance detected, has a second-line line probe assay that shows a gyrA mutation. Which regimen is preferred under current WHO and NTEP guidance?`,options:[`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin for 6 months`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid and linezolid for 6 months`,`Shorter oral bedaquiline regimen with levofloxacin for 9-11 months`,`Isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol with added levofloxacin`],answer:1,explanation:`A gyrA mutation means fluoroquinolone resistance, so this is pre-XDR-TB and BPaL (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid) for 26 weeks is used. BPaLM keeps moxifloxacin, which adds only QT toxicity against an FQ-resistant strain. The shorter regimen relies on levofloxacin and needs FQ susceptibility, and adding a drug to a first-line regimen amplifies resistance.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q2`,stem:`According to the 2021 WHO definitions, XDR-TB is MDR/RR-TB with resistance to a fluoroquinolone and to which additional drug?`,options:[`Amikacin, kanamycin or capreomycin`,`Ethionamide or cycloserine`,`Bedaquiline or linezolid`,`Pyrazinamide or ethambutol`],answer:2,explanation:`Since 2021 XDR-TB means MDR/RR-TB plus fluoroquinolone resistance plus resistance to at least one other Group A drug, which is bedaquiline or linezolid. The second-line injectables (amikacin, kanamycin, capreomycin) defined XDR in the old 2006 definition but are no longer core drugs. Ethionamide, cycloserine, pyrazinamide and ethambutol are Group B or C drugs and do not define XDR.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q3`,stem:`A woman on BPaLM for 8 weeks has an ECG showing QTcF 512 ms. Serum potassium is 3.1 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate immediate step?`,options:[`Stop bedaquiline and moxifloxacin, correct potassium and magnesium, and repeat the ECG`,`Continue all drugs and repeat the ECG in 1 month`,`Stop linezolid only and continue the rest`,`Switch moxifloxacin to levofloxacin and continue bedaquiline`],answer:0,explanation:`A QTcF over 500 ms is the threshold to stop QT-prolonging drugs (bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, clofazimine) and correct hypokalaemia and magnesium before cautious reintroduction. Continuing and waiting a month risks torsades. Linezolid does not prolong the QT interval. Levofloxacin also prolongs QT, so swapping fluoroquinolones while keeping bedaquiline does not remove the danger.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q4`,stem:`An isolate shows an inhA promoter mutation with no katG mutation. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`It causes high-level isoniazid resistance and isoniazid must never be used`,`It predicts bedaquiline cross-resistance`,`It indicates rifampicin resistance`,`It causes low-level isoniazid resistance with cross-resistance to ethionamide`],answer:3,explanation:`inhA promoter mutations cause low-level isoniazid resistance, so high-dose isoniazid may still work, but they also cause cross-resistance to ethionamide because both drugs act on InhA. High-level isoniazid resistance comes from katG mutations. Bedaquiline cross-resistance (with clofazimine) comes from Rv0678 efflux mutations, and rifampicin resistance from rpoB mutations.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q5`,stem:`Which of the following patients with MDR-TB is NOT eligible for the BPaLM regimen under NTEP?`,options:[`A 16-year-old boy with pulmonary MDR-TB`,`A 40-year-old diabetic man with cavitary disease`,`A woman who is 14 weeks pregnant`,`A man living with HIV on dolutegravir-based ART`],answer:2,explanation:`BPaLM is not used in pregnancy or breastfeeding because pretomanid has no human safety data, so the pregnant woman needs an individualised oral regimen. Age 14 years or more is eligible, so the 16-year-old qualifies. Diabetes and cavitation are not exclusions. PLHIV are eligible when ART is dolutegravir-based, avoiding efavirenz, which lowers bedaquiline and pretomanid levels.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify drug-resistant tuberculosis. Describe the diagnosis and management of MDR/RR-TB under NTEP, including the BPaLM regimen and its monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Drug-resistant TB is disease caused by M. tuberculosis resistant to one or more anti-TB drugs; RR/MDR-TB involves rifampicin with or without isoniazid, pre-XDR adds fluoroquinolone resistance and XDR adds bedaquiline or linezolid resistance (WHO 2021).`,`India carries about a quarter of the global MDR/RR-TB burden, and NTEP now offers universal DST by NAAT and a 6-month all-oral BPaLM regimen (approved 2024).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By resistance: mono, poly, Hr-TB, RR-TB, MDR-TB, pre-XDR, XDR (2021 definitions).`,`By history: new versus previously treated (relapse, after loss to follow-up, failure).`,`WHO drug groups A (Lfx/Mfx, Bdq, Lzd), B (Cfz, Cs), C (E, Dlm, Z, carbapenem, Am, Eto, PAS).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Universal DST: CBNAAT/Truenat for every presumptive TB - MTB and rifampicin resistance in 2 hours.`,`RR detected: first-line and second-line LPA on a second sample; FQ result decides BPaLM versus BPaL.`,`Liquid culture DST for Bdq, Lzd, Cfz, Dlm in pre-XDR or failure; tNGS at reference laboratories.`,`Pre-treatment evaluation: CBC, LFT, RFT, K, Mg, TSH, glucose, HIV, pregnancy test, ECG QTcF, vision.`]},{heading:`BPaLM regimen`,points:[`Bedaquiline 400 mg OD x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly; pretomanid 200 mg OD; linezolid 600 mg OD; moxifloxacin 400 mg OD - 26 weeks.`,`Eligible: 14 years or more, not pregnant or breastfeeding, no CNS/osteoarticular/disseminated TB, under 1 month prior exposure to these drugs.`,`Pre-XDR: BPaL (drop moxifloxacin).`]},{heading:`Other regimens`,points:[`Shorter oral Bdq regimen 9-11 months for FQ-susceptible patients not eligible for BPaLM.`,`Longer oral 18-20 months individualised regimen for XDR, failure, extensive EPTB.`,`Hr-TB: 6 months Lfx + R + E + Z.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and adverse events`,points:[`Monthly smear and culture; culture conversion = 2 negatives 30 days apart.`,`ECG at baseline, week 2, monthly - stop QT drugs if QTcF over 500 ms.`,`CBC, vision, colour vision, neuropathy monthly for linezolid; LFT monthly for pretomanid.`,`Post-treatment follow-up 6-monthly for 2 years; aDSM reporting.`]},{heading:`Programme support`,points:[`Ni-kshay notification and tracking; Ni-kshay Poshan Rs 1000/month; Ni-kshay Mitra food baskets.`,`Adherence: treatment supporter, VOT, counselling; contact screening and 6 months levofloxacin TPT for eligible contacts.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagnostic algorithm: NAAT, then LPA, then BPaLM or BPaL`,`Table of BPaLM drugs, doses and monitoring`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm`,marks:2},{part:`BPaLM and other regimens with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Monitoring and adverse events`,marks:2},{part:`Programme support and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`MDR-TB`,`pre-XDR`,`BPaLM`,`LPA`,`QTcF`,`Ni-kshay`]},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on bedaquiline and linezolid in drug-resistant TB: mechanism, dose and monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Bedaquiline (a diarylquinoline ATP-synthase inhibitor) and linezolid (an oxazolidinone) are WHO Group A drugs and the backbone of the 6-month BPaLM regimen.`,`Their main toxicities - QT prolongation and mitochondrial myelosuppression and neuropathy - set the monitoring schedule.`],answer:[{heading:`Bedaquiline`,points:[`Inhibits the c subunit of mycobacterial ATP synthase (atpE); kills dormant bacilli.`,`400 mg OD x 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times a week for 24 weeks, with food.`,`QT prolongation (hERG); ECG baseline, week 2, monthly; stop if QTcF over 500 ms.`,`Efavirenz lowers levels - use dolutegravir; Rv0678 mutations cause cross-resistance with clofazimine.`]},{heading:`Linezolid`,points:[`Binds 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit; also inhibits human mitochondrial protein synthesis.`,`600 mg OD for 26 weeks in BPaLM; reduce to 300 mg for toxicity.`,`Myelosuppression, peripheral and optic neuropathy, lactic acidosis, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs.`,`Monthly CBC, visual acuity, colour vision and neuropathy screen; stop for optic neuritis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: bedaquiline versus linezolid - mechanism, dose, toxicity, monitoring`],markSplit:[{part:`Bedaquiline`,marks:2.5},{part:`Linezolid`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`bedaquiline`,`linezolid`,`ATP synthase`,`QT`,`optic neuropathy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis chapter`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. Module 4: treatment and care, 2025`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. Module 3: diagnosis - rapid diagnostics for TB detection, 2024 update`,`Guidelines for Programmatic Management of Drug-Resistant Tuberculosis in India (PMDT), NTEP, MoHFW, 2021`,`NTEP Guidance on BPaLM regimen for MDR/RR-TB, Central TB Division, MoHFW, 2024`,`India TB Report 2024, Central TB Division, MoHFW`,`WHO Global Tuberculosis Report 2024`,`Nyang'wa BT et al. A 24-week, all-oral regimen for rifampin-resistant tuberculosis (TB-PRACTECAL). N Engl J Med 2022`]});var d=[];d.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia`,title:`Approach to fever with thrombocytopenia and fever with jaundice`,oneLiner:`**Fever with thrombocytopenia** (platelets under **150,000/uL**) and **fever with jaundice** are syndromic labels for a short, treatable list in India - **dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis, enteric fever and sepsis/DIC** - with **HLH and TTP** as the mimics that kill if missed; the **fever-jaundice-AKI triad** means **leptospirosis or severe falciparum malaria** until proved otherwise, and the first hour needs a **smear plus RDT, a blood culture, and empirical doxycycline (plus ceftriaxone if toxic)**, never a platelet count alone.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`fever with thrombocytopenia`,`fever with jaundice`,`acute febrile illness`,`Weil disease`,`fever jaundice AKI`,`tropical fever`,`DIC`,`ISTH DIC score`,`TTP`,`PLASMIC score`,`thrombotic microangiopathy`,`pseudothrombocytopenia`,`artesunate`,`doxycycline`,`platelet transfusion`,`hepatitis E`,`ascending cholangitis`,`HLH`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Thrombocytopenia** is a platelet count **below 150,000/uL**; it is mild at 100,000-150,000, moderate at 50,000-100,000 and severe **below 50,000**, and spontaneous bleeding is unusual until counts fall **below 10,000-20,000** unless platelet function is also impaired. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with thrombocytopenia** is one of the syndromes in which the ICMR asks the clinician to classify **acute febrile illness - fever plus what** - because each syndrome carries a short, treatable differential. [ICMR 2022]`,`In Indian hospital series, **dengue is the commonest cause** of fever with thrombocytopenia, followed by **malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis and enteric fever**; together these explain most of the cases in which a cause is found. [API 2019]`,`**Fever with jaundice** (bilirubin over **2-3 mg/dL** with visible icterus) in India is led by **acute viral hepatitis (A and E), leptospirosis, falciparum malaria and scrub typhus**, with **ascending cholangitis, liver abscess and sepsis** as the surgical and bacterial causes. [API 2019]`,`The **fever-jaundice-AKI triad** (with or without bleeding) is the classic presentation of **Weil disease (severe leptospirosis)**, but in India it is equally often **severe falciparum malaria, scrub typhus or Gram-negative sepsis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The burden is **seasonal - July to November** after the monsoon - with regional hot-spots: leptospirosis in Kerala, coastal Karnataka, Gujarat and Maharashtra; scrub typhus in the sub-Himalayan belt, Tamil Nadu and the North-East; malaria in Odisha, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and the North-East. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Dengue cases reported to NCVBDC have exceeded **2 lakh in peak years (2022-2024)**, and India reports the largest number of malaria cases in the WHO South-East Asia Region despite a steep fall since 2015. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`The case-fatality of these syndromes is driven by **delay** - missed falciparum malaria, missed leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage and untreated scrub typhus each carry mortality above **10-30%** once multi-organ failure sets in. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infective causes of fever with thrombocytopenia**: dengue, malaria (P. falciparum and P. vivax), scrub typhus and other rickettsioses, leptospirosis, enteric fever, sepsis with DIC, acute HIV, EBV and CMV, hantavirus, Kyasanur Forest disease and severe COVID-19. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-infective and mimics**: **haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)**, **thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)**, haemolytic uraemic syndrome, acute leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, SLE, drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia and **heparin-induced thrombocytopenia** in hospitalised patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Causes of fever with jaundice**: viral hepatitis A, E, B (and D), leptospirosis, falciparum malaria (and severe vivax), scrub typhus, enteric hepatitis, dengue hepatitis, **ascending cholangitis**, pyogenic or amoebic liver abscess, sepsis-associated cholestasis, haemolysis (G6PD deficiency with infection), and drug-induced liver injury (anti-TB drugs, co-amoxiclav). [API 2019]`,`**Exposure clues**: wading in flood water, paddy work, sewer work and rat exposure (leptospirosis); grass, farm and forest exposure (scrub typhus); travel or residence in a malarious district; day-biting mosquitoes and stored water (dengue); unsafe water or street food (hepatitis A and E, enteric fever). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Host factors that change the list**: pregnancy (hepatitis E, HELLP, acute fatty liver, severe malaria), **alcohol and cirrhosis**, diabetes (melioidosis, liver abscess), splenectomy (overwhelming sepsis, severe malaria), and HIV or immunosuppression (disseminated infection, HLH). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug history** matters in both syndromes: **heparin, linezolid, vancomycin, beta-lactams, valproate and quinine** cause thrombocytopenia, and **NSAIDs or aspirin** taken for fever convert dengue thrombocytopenia into bleeding. [API 2019]`,`**Kyasanur Forest disease (KFD)** - a tick-borne flavivirus of the Western Ghats - presents in the dry season (November-June) with fever, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia and bleeding in forest workers; see the One Health and zoonoses topic. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the platelets fall and the bilirubin rises`,points:[`**Dengue**: the virus suppresses megakaryopoiesis early, then **anti-NS1 and anti-platelet antibodies** and complement drive peripheral destruction; the nadir comes at **defervescence (day 3-7)**, the same time as plasma leak. [WHO 2025]`,`**Malaria**: thrombocytopenia comes from **splenic sequestration, immune clearance of platelets coated with parasite antigen, and platelet activation** in cytoadherent microvessels; it is almost universal in falciparum and common in vivax but is **not itself a WHO severity criterion**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Rickettsial infection and leptospirosis** cause **endothelial vasculitis**, so platelets are consumed on injured endothelium; in leptospirosis the same vasculitis produces **pulmonary haemorrhage, conjunctival suffusion and AKI**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis-induced coagulopathy and DIC**: tissue factor on monocytes and endothelium triggers thrombin generation, natural anticoagulants (antithrombin, protein C) fall and fibrinolysis is suppressed (**PAI-1**), giving microvascular thrombosis, consumption of platelets and factors, and organ failure. [ISTH 2019]`,`**TTP**: severe deficiency of **ADAMTS13 (under 10%)**, usually from an autoantibody, leaves ultra-large von Willebrand multimers that build platelet-rich microthrombi - so platelets fall, red cells fragment (**schistocytes**) and the brain, heart and kidney become ischaemic while **coagulation tests stay normal**. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Jaundice in malaria** is mixed: **haemolysis** raises unconjugated bilirubin, while hepatocyte dysfunction and cholestasis raise conjugated bilirubin; transaminases are usually **under 200-300 U/L**, which separates it from viral hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice in leptospirosis** is **cholestatic with bilirubin out of proportion** to modestly raised transaminases (usually under 5 times normal) and a raised **CK** from myositis; the kidney lesion is tubulo-interstitial with **non-oliguric, hypokalaemic AKI** from proximal tubular sodium and potassium wasting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viral hepatitis** is hepatocellular: **ALT in the thousands**, ALT usually above AST, and the prodromal fever typically **settles as jaundice appears** - persistence of fever after jaundice should prompt a search for another cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cholangitis** is bacterial infection above an obstructed bile duct; raised biliary pressure drives bacteria and endotoxin into the blood, producing **Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain)** and septic shock. [TG18]`]},{heading:`Classification - the syndromic frame examiners ask for`,points:[`**Fever with thrombocytopenia alone** (no organ dysfunction): dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, enteric fever and viral fevers - usually OPD or ward care with daily counts. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fever with thrombocytopenia and bleeding**: severe dengue, DIC in sepsis, leptospirosis, KFD, meningococcaemia, acute leukaemia and, rarely, viral haemorrhagic fever - always an admission. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with thrombocytopenia and anaemia**: malaria, **TTP/HUS (with schistocytes)**, HLH, leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, kala-azar and infective endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with jaundice - three patterns**: **prehepatic** (haemolysis: malaria, G6PD, HUS), **hepatocellular** (viral hepatitis, dengue, scrub typhus, drug injury, enteric) and **cholestatic** (leptospirosis, cholangitis, sepsis, liver abscess). [API 2019]`,`**Fever-jaundice-AKI (plus or minus bleeding or ARDS)** is the most dangerous combination - **leptospirosis, severe falciparum malaria, scrub typhus, Gram-negative sepsis, hantavirus and HUS** - and should be managed in HDU or ICU from the first hour. [API 2019]`,`**Bicytopenia or pancytopenia with fever, splenomegaly and very high ferritin** is HLH until shown otherwise - apply the **HScore** (see the HLH topic). [HLH-2004]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Fix the **day of illness**: dengue platelets fall from **day 3-7**, scrub typhus and leptospirosis complications appear at **day 5-10**, and viral hepatitis jaundice comes after a **3-7 day prodrome** in which fever settles. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fever pattern**: abrupt fever with retro-orbital pain and myalgia (dengue); rigors and sweats (malaria, cholangitis, liver abscess); severe **calf myalgia, headache and red eyes** (leptospirosis); headache, cough and a hidden eschar (scrub typhus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding history**: gum bleeding, epistaxis, petechiae and menorrhagia (thrombocytopenia), **haemoptysis** (leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage), melaena (dengue, DIC) and oozing from venepuncture sites (DIC). [WHO 2025]`,`**Jaundice history**: dark urine and pale stools (cholestasis), **cola-coloured urine** (haemoglobinuria from malaria or G6PD haemolysis - historically 'blackwater fever'), RUQ pain with rigors (cholangitis, liver abscess) and itching. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine output and breathlessness**: reduced urine or oliguria suggests AKI; breathlessness on day 4-7 suggests leptospiral or malarial ARDS, dengue plasma leak with effusion, or scrub typhus pneumonitis. [API 2019]`,`**Neurological symptoms**: confusion, seizures or coma point to **cerebral malaria**, scrub meningoencephalitis, leptospiral aseptic meningitis, hepatic encephalopathy or **TTP (fluctuating focal signs)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **drugs and toxins**: self-medicated NSAIDs, antibiotics before cultures, anti-TB drugs, alcohol, herbal remedies and paracetamol overdose. [API 2019]`,`Ask the **LMP in every woman aged 15-49**: pregnancy brings in hepatitis E, HELLP, acute fatty liver of pregnancy and severe malaria. [FOGSI 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Vitals first**: pulse, BP, **pulse pressure (20 mmHg or less = compensated dengue shock)**, respiratory rate, SpO2, temperature, capillary refill and urine output; score with **NEWS2** rather than qSOFA alone. [SSC 2026]`,`**Eschar** - a painless black scab with a red rim in the axilla, groin, waistband or under the breasts - is diagnostic of scrub typhus in the right setting; undress fully to find it. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Conjunctival suffusion** (redness without discharge), calf tenderness and subconjunctival haemorrhage point to **leptospirosis**; a flushed face, petechiae and a positive **tourniquet test** point to dengue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** suggests malaria, enteric fever, kala-azar, HLH, leukaemia, infective endocarditis or brucellosis; **tender hepatomegaly** suggests dengue, viral hepatitis or liver abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Purpura fulminans** or a rapidly spreading purpuric rash with shock suggests **meningococcaemia or DIC** - give the first antibiotic within the hour. [SSC 2026]`,`**Murphy sign, RUQ tenderness and a palpable gall bladder** suggest cholangitis or cholecystitis; **intercostal tenderness** over the liver suggests liver abscess. [TG18]`,`**Pallor with jaundice and no tender liver** suggests haemolysis (malaria, G6PD, TTP); **lymphadenopathy** suggests scrub typhus, EBV, HIV, TB or lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding signs**: petechiae over legs, gum oozing, venepuncture bleeding and fundal haemorrhages; check the fundus in TTP and severe thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - the discriminators`,points:[`**Dengue vs malaria**: dengue has **leucopenia with rising haematocrit** and a falling platelet count at defervescence; malaria has anaemia, splenomegaly, rigors and a positive smear or RDT. [WHO 2025]`,`**Leptospirosis vs viral hepatitis**: leptospirosis has **neutrophilia, raised CK, AKI and bilirubin out of proportion to modest transaminases**; viral hepatitis has ALT in the thousands, normal creatinine and fever that settles once jaundice appears. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus vs leptospirosis**: both cause fever, myalgia, AKI and jaundice; **eschar, lymphadenopathy and pneumonitis** favour scrub, while **conjunctival suffusion, calf pain and pulmonary haemorrhage** favour leptospirosis - doxycycline covers both. [ICMR 2022]`,`**DIC vs TTP**: DIC has a **prolonged PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen and very high D-dimer**; TTP has **normal coagulation, schistocytes over 1%, LDH very high and ADAMTS13 under 10%**. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Severe malaria vs sepsis**: both cause shock, AKI and jaundice; malaria shows parasites or a positive RDT, while sepsis has a focus or positive culture - in a malarious area, **treat both until one is excluded**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Cholangitis vs hepatitis**: cholangitis has **rigors, RUQ pain, raised ALP and GGT** and a dilated duct on ultrasound; hepatitis has hepatocellular enzymes and a normal duct. [TG18]`,`**HLH vs severe sepsis**: HLH has **ferritin often over 3000-10,000 ng/mL, falling ESR, low fibrinogen, raised triglycerides and splenomegaly** with persistent fever despite antibiotics. [HLH-2004]`,`**Pseudothrombocytopenia** (EDTA-induced platelet clumping) gives a falsely low count in a well patient with no bleeding; confirm on a **peripheral smear or a citrate tube** before acting. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line in OPD or district hospital`,points:[`**CBC with haematocrit and a peripheral smear** read by a person: platelet clumps, parasites, schistocytes, toxic granulation, atypical lymphocytes and blasts each change the diagnosis. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Malaria RDT plus thick and thin smear** in every febrile patient in an endemic area; repeat the smear **every 12-24 hours for 3 sets** if negative but suspicion is high. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Dengue NS1 antigen (day 1-5)** and **IgM MAC-ELISA (from day 5)**; scrub typhus and leptospira **IgM ELISA from day 5**; an early negative IgM is falsely reassuring. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Liver tests** (bilirubin fractions, AST, ALT, ALP, GGT, albumin) and **renal function with electrolytes** - hypokalaemia with AKI suggests leptospirosis; **AST or ALT over 1000 U/L** in dengue is severe organ involvement. [WHO 2025]`,`**Two sets of blood cultures before the first antibiotic** in any toxic or prolonged fever; culture is the only way to find enteric fever and melioidosis. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Urine**: haemoglobinuria (dipstick blood with no red cells) suggests intravascular haemolysis; proteinuria and casts suggest leptospiral or malarial nephropathy. [API 2019]`,`**Ultrasound abdomen**: gall-bladder wall oedema, ascites and pleural effusion (dengue plasma leak), dilated bile ducts (cholangitis), liver abscess, splenomegaly and renal size. [TG18]`,`**Bedside glucose and lactate**: hypoglycaemia in malaria and sepsis; **lactate 2 mmol/L or more** is abnormal and 4 or more is high risk. [SSC 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and interpretation cut-offs`,points:[`**Coagulation screen** - PT/INR, aPTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer - and the **ISTH overt DIC score**: platelets, D-dimer rise, PT prolongation and fibrinogen; a score of **5 or more** is overt DIC. [ISTH 2019]`,`**Sepsis-induced coagulopathy (SIC) score** uses platelets, INR and SOFA; a score of **4 or more** identifies early coagulopathy before overt DIC. [ISTH 2019]`,`**Haemolysis screen**: LDH, reticulocytes, haptoglobin, indirect bilirubin and **direct antiglobulin test**; schistocytes **over 1%** on a smear support a thrombotic microangiopathy. [ICSH 2021]`,`**PLASMIC score** (7 items: platelets under 30,000, haemolysis, no active cancer, no transplant, MCV under 90 fL, INR under 1.5, creatinine under 2.0 mg/dL); a score of **6-7 means high probability of severe ADAMTS13 deficiency** - start plasma exchange before the level returns. [ISTH 2020]`,`**HLH screen**: ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, soluble CD25 and bone-marrow examination; ferritin **over 10,000 ng/mL** is highly suggestive in adults. [HLH-2004]`,`**Leptospira microscopic agglutination test (MAT)** is the reference test: a single titre of **1:400 or more** in an endemic area, or a **fourfold rise** in paired sera, is diagnostic; **PCR** on blood in the first week is positive before antibodies. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Scrub typhus IgM ELISA** uses a locally validated optical density cut-off; **PCR on eschar or blood** confirms early disease; Weil-Felix (OX-K) is insensitive and should not be used to exclude it. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Viral hepatitis markers**: IgM anti-HAV, **IgM anti-HEV**, HBsAg with IgM anti-HBc, and anti-HCV; **CT or MRCP** for suspected biliary obstruction, and **ERCP** for drainage. [TG18]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Give **paracetamol only** - 500-1000 mg orally every 6 hours, maximum 3-4 g a day (lower in liver disease and alcohol use) - until dengue is excluded; **no NSAIDs, aspirin or intramuscular injections**. [WHO 2025]`,`Advise **oral fluids 2.5-3 L a day** (ORS, soups, coconut water) and passing urine at least every 6 hours; record intake and output on a home chart. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Uncomplicated P. vivax**: chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days (10, 10, 5 mg/kg) plus **primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days** after G6PD testing; **uncomplicated P. falciparum**: artesunate plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine for 3 days (artemether-lumefantrine in the North-East) plus single-dose primaquine 0.75 mg/kg. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Suspected scrub typhus, leptospirosis or rickettsia** after day 3-5 with negative malaria and dengue tests: **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days**; **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days** in pregnancy or doxycycline intolerance. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Suspected acute viral hepatitis A or E** with normal INR, no encephalopathy and tolerating oral intake: rest, oral fluids, avoid alcohol and hepatotoxic drugs, and review LFT and INR in **1 week**. [API 2019]`,`**Daily review** in the critical phase: CBC and haematocrit, warning signs, urine output and bleeding; **refer the same day** if any red flag appears. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Do not treat a platelet count** - in a stable dengue patient, platelets of 20,000 without bleeding need monitoring, not a transfusion. [WHO 2025]`,`**Notify** dengue, malaria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, hepatitis A/E and clusters of fever on the **IHIP (IDSP) portal**. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** any fever with thrombocytopenia or jaundice plus bleeding, warning signs, shock, SpO2 under 94%, oliguria, encephalopathy, INR over 1.5, pregnancy or social isolation. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Severe AUF with thrombocytopenia or jaundice and no diagnosis yet**: take cultures, then give **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily plus doxycycline 100 mg (oral or IV) twice daily** within the first hour; add **IV artesunate** if malaria cannot be excluded. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Severe malaria**: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then once daily** (3 mg/kg in children under 20 kg) until oral intake, then a full 3-day oral ACT; check glucose 4-hourly. [WHO 2025]`,`**Severe leptospirosis**: **IV benzylpenicillin 1.5 million units 6-hourly** or **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily for 7 days**; doxycycline is the oral alternative; pulmonary haemorrhage needs early ventilation and dialysis for oligo-anuric AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe scrub typhus**: **IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin combination** is now preferred over either alone for severe disease (INTREST trial, 2025); see the scrub typhus topic for doses. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Dengue with warning signs or shock**: crystalloid by the haematocrit-guided ladder (see the Dengue topic); **platelet transfusion only for significant bleeding**, not for a number. [WHO 2025]`,`**DIC**: treat the cause; transfuse **platelets to keep over 50,000** in active bleeding, **FFP 15-30 mL/kg** for prolonged PT/aPTT with bleeding, and **cryoprecipitate** for fibrinogen under 1.5 g/L. [ISTH 2019]`,`**Ascending cholangitis**: blood cultures, IV antibiotics (piperacillin-tazobactam or ceftriaxone plus metronidazole), and **biliary drainage (ERCP) within 24 hours** for moderate and immediately for severe (Tokyo grade III) disease. [TG18]`,`**Suspected TTP**: urgent **plasma exchange**, methylprednisolone and rituximab, with caplacizumab where available; **avoid platelet transfusion** unless life-threatening bleeding - refer to a haematology centre. [ISTH 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy with fever and jaundice**: think **hepatitis E (maternal mortality up to 20-25% in the third trimester)**, HELLP syndrome, acute fatty liver of pregnancy and severe malaria; IV artesunate is used in all trimesters for severe malaria. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: doxycycline is avoided - use **azithromycin** for scrub typhus and leptospirosis; primaquine is withheld until after delivery and breastfeeding of a G6PD-unknown infant. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Elderly**: fever may be absent and delirium the only sign; hidden sepsis, cholangitis from stones and drug-induced thrombocytopenia are commoner. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: leptospirosis and malaria AKI need early dialysis; doxycycline and ceftriaxone need **no renal dose change**, but penicillin G and many cephalosporins do. [Sanford 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: think **melioidosis** (fever, hepatosplenic abscesses, thrombocytopenia) and **pyogenic liver abscess (Klebsiella)** - blood culture and ultrasound before closing the case. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Children**: dengue shock appears at defervescence with a narrow pulse pressure, and enteric hepatitis is commoner; doxycycline is safe for short courses in children of all ages (**2.2 mg/kg twice daily**). [AAP 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if tests are unavailable and the patient is toxic, give **artesunate (or an ACT) plus doxycycline plus ceftriaxone** and refer; do not wait for results to start treatment. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Bleeding**: GI bleeding in dengue and DIC, **pulmonary haemorrhage in leptospirosis (mortality often over 50%)**, intracranial haemorrhage in TTP and severe thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AKI**: leptospirosis (non-oliguric, hypokalaemic), falciparum malaria (acute tubular necrosis, haemoglobinuria), scrub typhus and sepsis; most recover if dialysed in time. [API 2019]`,`**ARDS** in falciparum malaria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus and dengue; it can appear after parasite clearance in malaria. [WHO 2025]`,`**Acute liver failure** (INR 1.5 or more with encephalopathy) from hepatitis A, E or B, dengue and drugs - list early for transplant evaluation using King's College criteria. [EASL 2017]`,`**Secondary HLH** after dengue, EBV, TB, scrub typhus or leishmaniasis presents as fever that persists despite treatment with worsening cytopenias. [HLH-2004]`,`**Prognosis**: with early treatment, mortality is under 1% for dengue and uncomplicated malaria, but severe malaria, Weil disease and severe scrub typhus carry **10-30% mortality**, highest with multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer same day** to a hospital with ICU, dialysis and blood bank for shock, bleeding, oliguria, altered sensorium, respiratory distress, INR over 1.5 or rapidly falling platelets with rising haematocrit. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Monitor in the ward**: vitals and urine output 2-4 hourly, haematocrit and platelets **12-24 hourly** in the critical phase, electrolytes, LFT, creatinine and glucose daily. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Malaria follow-up**: a smear on **day 3** (parasitaemia should fall by over 75%) and on day 28 for treatment failure; vivax patients complete 14 days of primaquine. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Discharge** a dengue patient when afebrile for 48 hours without antipyretics, appetite and urine output are normal, haematocrit is stable and **platelets are over 50,000** and rising. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Viral hepatitis follow-up**: LFT and INR at 1-2 weeks until normal; **HBsAg at 6 months** to define chronic infection. [API 2019]`,`**Persistent fever with cytopenias** after 5-7 days of appropriate treatment needs **ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen and bone-marrow examination** for HLH, leukaemia, kala-azar and TB. [HLH-2004]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**Vector control** under **NCVBDC**: source reduction of Aedes breeding (weekly 'dry day'), insecticide-treated nets and indoor residual spray for malaria. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Leptospirosis prophylaxis**: **doxycycline 200 mg orally once a week** during exposure in flood-hit or high-risk groups (farmers, sanitation workers), as used by Kerala and Maharashtra health departments. [NPPCL 2023]`,`**Hepatitis A and E** are prevented by safe water and sanitation under **Jal Jeevan Mission and Swachh Bharat**; hepatitis B by vaccination under **UIP and NVHCP**. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Counsel** every dengue-risk patient to avoid NSAIDs, keep fluids up, and return on warning signs; counsel forest and farm workers on protective clothing and repellents against mites and ticks. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Notify** through **IDSP/IHIP**: clusters of fever with thrombocytopenia or jaundice trigger a rapid response team; see the National communicable disease programmes topic. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Guidelines for clinical management of arboviral diseases (2025)** cover dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever together and recommend **against prophylactic platelet transfusion** in dengue without bleeding. [WHO 2025]`,`The older **Indian national dengue guideline (NVBDCP 2014)** allowed prophylactic platelets below 10,000; current WHO and ICMR practice reserves transfusion for **significant bleeding** - follow the newer advice and document the reason if you transfuse. [WHO 2025]`,`**INTREST trial (NEJM 2025)**: in severe scrub typhus, **combined IV doxycycline and azithromycin** reduced the composite of death, persistent complications and fever compared with either drug alone. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** recommends against **qSOFA as a single screening tool**, favouring NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS, and keeps antibiotics within 1 hour when shock is likely. [SSC 2026]`,`**NCVBDC National Drug Policy on Malaria** and the **National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027** keep artesunate-based ACT and target zero indigenous cases by 2027 and elimination by 2030. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**WHO Guidelines for malaria (2025 update)** keep IV artesunate for severe malaria in all age groups and trimesters, and single low-dose primaquine for falciparum transmission blocking. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic long question**: 'Approach to a patient with fever and thrombocytopenia' - open with the syndromic frame, list five tropical causes and three mimics (HLH, TTP, DIC), then day-of-illness tests and the empirical first hour. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Classic short note**: 'Fever with jaundice and AKI' - Weil disease, severe falciparum malaria, scrub typhus, sepsis, hantavirus; give the lab discriminators. [API 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: transfusing platelets in stable dengue to a number - it does not prevent bleeding and can cause volume overload. [WHO 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: giving ceftriaxone alone to a patient with fever and thrombocytopenia - it misses scrub typhus and rickettsia; **add doxycycline**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Viva**: 'Why is the platelet count low but PT normal?' - points to dengue, malaria, ITP or TTP rather than DIC; then look for schistocytes. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Viva**: 'In viral hepatitis, when does fever settle?' - usually when jaundice appears; fever persisting after jaundice suggests leptospirosis, malaria, cholangitis or abscess. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Fever with thrombocytopenia - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clinical clue`,`Laboratory clue`,`Confirmatory test`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Dengue`,`Retro-orbital pain, myalgia, rash, leak at defervescence`,`Leucopenia, rising haematocrit, AST over ALT`,`NS1 (day 1-5), IgM MAC-ELISA (day 5+)`,`Paracetamol, fluids by haematocrit`],[`Malaria`,`Rigors, sweats, splenomegaly, travel`,`Anaemia, raised indirect bilirubin, low platelets`,`Smear plus RDT`,`ACT; IV artesunate if severe`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, lymphadenopathy, cough`,`Raised transaminases, variable WBC`,`IgM ELISA (day 5+), PCR on eschar`,`Doxycycline or azithromycin`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Conjunctival suffusion, calf pain, flood exposure`,`Neutrophilia, raised CK, hypokalaemic AKI`,`IgM ELISA, MAT 1:400 or more, PCR`,`Doxycycline; IV penicillin or ceftriaxone`],[`Enteric fever`,`Step-ladder fever, relative bradycardia, constipation`,`Leucopenia, eosinopenia, mild LFT rise`,`Blood culture`,`Azithromycin or ceftriaxone`],[`Sepsis with DIC`,`Focus, shock, ooze from lines`,`Long PT/aPTT, low fibrinogen, high D-dimer`,`Cultures, ISTH DIC score 5+`,`Antibiotics within 1 h, source control`],[`TTP`,`Fluctuating neurology, little fever`,`Schistocytes over 1%, LDH high, normal PT`,`ADAMTS13 under 10%, PLASMIC 6-7`,`Plasma exchange, steroid, rituximab`],[`HLH`,`Persistent fever, splenomegaly, cytopenias`,`Ferritin very high, low fibrinogen, high TG`,`HLH-2004 criteria, HScore, marrow`,`Treat trigger; dexamethasone, refer`]]},{heading:`Fever with jaundice - pattern and discriminators`,columns:[`Cause`,`Bilirubin and enzymes`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Viral hepatitis A or E`,`ALT in thousands, ALT over AST`,`Fever settles as jaundice appears; normal creatinine`,`IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HEV`],[`Leptospirosis (Weil)`,`High bilirubin, ALT under 5x normal, high CK`,`AKI with hypokalaemia, suffusion, haemoptysis`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, PCR`],[`Falciparum malaria`,`Mixed bilirubin, ALT usually under 300`,`Anaemia, splenomegaly, haemoglobinuria`,`Smear, RDT`],[`Scrub typhus`,`ALT and AST 100-500, mild bilirubin`,`Eschar, lymph nodes, pneumonitis`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`],[`Ascending cholangitis`,`Raised ALP and GGT, conjugated bilirubin`,`Charcot triad, dilated CBD`,`Ultrasound, MRCP`],[`Liver abscess`,`ALP raised, mild bilirubin`,`Tender liver, intercostal tenderness`,`Ultrasound, amoebic serology`],[`Haemolysis (G6PD, TTP)`,`Unconjugated bilirubin, normal ALT`,`Pallor, dark urine, LDH high`,`Smear, retics, G6PD, DAT`],[`Sepsis cholestasis`,`Conjugated bilirubin, modest enzymes`,`Shock with a non-hepatic focus`,`Cultures, lactate`]]},{heading:`Drug table - empirical and specific therapy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg orally 6-hourly; child 15 mg/kg`,`While febrile; max 3-4 g/day (2 g in liver disease)`,`Hepatotoxic in overdose; no NSAID or aspirin in dengue`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg orally or IV twice daily; child 2.2 mg/kg`,`7 days`,`Oesophagitis (take upright with water); avoid in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg orally or IV once daily`,`5 days (scrub); 7 days at 1 g (enteric)`,`QT prolongation; safe in pregnancy`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV once daily (1 g for leptospirosis)`,`7 days or by culture`,`Biliary sludge; no Orientia/Rickettsia cover`],[`Benzylpenicillin`,`1.5 million units IV 6-hourly`,`7 days (severe leptospirosis)`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction; renal dose change`],[`Artesunate IV`,`2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily (3 mg/kg under 20 kg)`,`Until oral intake (minimum 24 h), then 3-day ACT`,`Delayed haemolysis at 1-3 weeks; check Hb`],[`Artesunate + SP`,`AS 4 mg/kg daily + SP (25/1.25 mg/kg) day 1`,`3 days + primaquine 0.75 mg/kg once`,`SP: sulfa allergy, Stevens-Johnson syndrome`],[`Chloroquine + primaquine (vivax)`,`CQ 10, 10, 5 mg/kg; PQ 0.25 mg/kg daily`,`3 days CQ; 14 days PQ`,`PQ haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; not in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Platelet count`,`Under 150,000 (severe under 50,000; bleeding risk under 10,000-20,000)`,`Grade of thrombocytopenia; confirm on smear`],[`Haematocrit`,`Rise of 20% over baseline`,`Dengue plasma leak`],[`AST/ALT in dengue`,`Over 1000 U/L`,`Severe dengue organ involvement`],[`ISTH overt DIC score`,`5 or more`,`Overt DIC; repeat daily`],[`SIC score`,`4 or more`,`Sepsis-induced coagulopathy`],[`Schistocytes on smear`,`Over 1%`,`Thrombotic microangiopathy`],[`PLASMIC score`,`6-7`,`High risk of severe ADAMTS13 deficiency (TTP)`],[`Leptospira MAT`,`1:400 single titre or fourfold rise`,`Confirmed leptospirosis`],[`Ferritin`,`Over 10,000 ng/mL`,`Strongly suggests HLH in adults`],[`Lactate`,`2 or more abnormal; 4 or more high risk`,`Hypoperfusion in sepsis or severe malaria`]]},{heading:`DIC vs TTP vs HLH - the three mimics`,columns:[`Feature`,`DIC`,`TTP`,`HLH`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Tissue factor-driven thrombin, consumption`,`ADAMTS13 deficiency, VWF microthrombi`,`Uncontrolled T-cell and macrophage activation`],[`PT and aPTT`,`Prolonged`,`Normal`,`Often prolonged`],[`Fibrinogen`,`Low`,`Normal`,`Low (under 1.5 g/L)`],[`Schistocytes`,`Few`,`Many (over 1%)`,`Few`],[`Ferritin`,`Raised`,`Mildly raised`,`Very high (often over 3000-10,000)`],[`Splenomegaly`,`No`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Key treatment`,`Treat cause, replace products`,`Plasma exchange, steroids, rituximab`,`Treat trigger, dexamethasone, etoposide`]]}],redFlags:[`Bleeding (haemoptysis, melaena, haematemesis, venepuncture ooze) with fever and thrombocytopenia`,`Shock or pulse pressure 20 mmHg or less, cold peripheries, capillary refill over 2 seconds`,`Fever with jaundice and oliguria or rising creatinine (fever-jaundice-AKI triad)`,`Breathlessness, SpO2 under 94% or haemoptysis (leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage, ARDS)`,`Altered sensorium, seizures or focal neurology (cerebral malaria, TTP, hepatic encephalopathy)`,`INR over 1.5 with jaundice, or hypoglycaemia`,`Schistocytes on the smear or a rapidly falling haemoglobin with low platelets`,`Fever persisting beyond 5-7 days of treatment with worsening cytopenias and very high ferritin`],pearls:[`**Fever plus what** - classify acute fever by its companion: thrombocytopenia, jaundice, AKI, rash, ARDS or altered sensorium.`,`**Low platelets with a normal PT** is not DIC - look for dengue, malaria, ITP or TTP.`,`**Bilirubin high, ALT modest, CK raised, potassium low, creatinine rising** - leptospirosis.`,`In viral hepatitis **fever goes when jaundice comes**; persistent fever with jaundice means look again.`,`**Treat the patient, not the platelet count** - no prophylactic platelets in stable dengue.`,`**Ceftriaxone alone misses scrub** - add doxycycline in severe undifferentiated fever.`,`Mnemonic for fever-jaundice-AKI: **'LMS-SH'** - Leptospirosis, Malaria, Scrub, Sepsis, Hantavirus/HUS.`,`**Check the smear before trusting a low count** - EDTA pseudothrombocytopenia is common.`,`**Pregnant, febrile and jaundiced** - hepatitis E, HELLP, AFLP and malaria; deliver the right test the same day.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old paddy farmer from coastal Karnataka presents on day 6 of fever with calf pain, red eyes, jaundice and reduced urine. Bilirubin is 14 mg/dL, ALT 110 U/L, CK 2400 U/L, creatinine 3.8 mg/dL and potassium 3.0 mmol/L. Platelets are 60,000/uL. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Acute hepatitis E`,`Severe leptospirosis (Weil disease)`,`Dengue with hepatitis`,`Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura`],answer:1,explanation:`Weil disease fits every clue: flood or paddy exposure, conjunctival suffusion, calf myalgia with a raised CK, bilirubin out of proportion to modestly raised ALT, and non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI. Hepatitis E would show ALT in the thousands and normal creatinine; dengue hepatitis has AST over ALT without marked cholestasis; TTP has little fever, schistocytes and normal transaminases.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q2`,stem:`A patient with fever and confusion has platelets of 18,000/uL, haemoglobin 7.8 g/dL, LDH 1900 U/L, schistocytes 4% on the smear, PT and aPTT normal, and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL. Malaria RDT and smear are negative. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Transfuse four units of random-donor platelets`,`Start IV ceftriaxone and await blood culture`,`Start urgent plasma exchange with corticosteroids and send ADAMTS13`,`Give FFP and cryoprecipitate for DIC`],answer:2,explanation:`Thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic haemolysis with schistocytes over 1%, neurological signs and normal coagulation give a high PLASMIC score, so TTP is likely and plasma exchange must start before the ADAMTS13 result. Platelet transfusion can worsen microthrombosis; ceftriaxone alone treats nothing here; DIC is unlikely when PT and aPTT are normal.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q3`,stem:`A 22-year-old with confirmed dengue on day 5 is haemodynamically stable, eating, passing urine and has no bleeding. Platelets are 14,000/uL. According to current WHO guidance, what is the correct approach?`,options:[`Close monitoring with oral fluids and paracetamol; no prophylactic platelet transfusion`,`Prophylactic platelet transfusion because the count is under 20,000`,`Start oral prednisolone to raise the platelet count`,`Give IV immunoglobulin`],answer:0,explanation:`WHO arboviral guidance (2025) recommends against prophylactic platelet transfusion in stable dengue without bleeding; it does not prevent bleeding and risks fluid overload. Steroids and IV immunoglobulin have no proven benefit in dengue thrombocytopenia. The older Indian 2014 threshold of 10,000 is superseded and would not apply at 14,000 anyway.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q4`,stem:`Which finding best separates disseminated intravascular coagulation from thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura in a febrile patient with low platelets?`,options:[`Raised LDH`,`Presence of fever`,`Raised serum creatinine`,`Prolonged PT with low fibrinogen`],answer:3,explanation:`DIC consumes clotting factors, so PT and aPTT are prolonged and fibrinogen falls; in TTP the coagulation screen is characteristically normal because the lesion is VWF-platelet microthrombi from ADAMTS13 deficiency. Raised LDH, fever and a raised creatinine occur in both and do not discriminate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q5`,stem:`A 40-year-old from a malaria-endemic district arrives on day 7 of fever with jaundice, hypotension and platelets of 40,000/uL. The malaria RDT is unavailable at night. Blood cultures have been drawn. Which empirical regimen is most appropriate in the first hour?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone alone`,`IV artesunate plus ceftriaxone plus doxycycline`,`Chloroquine plus primaquine`,`Meropenem plus vancomycin`],answer:1,explanation:`Severe undifferentiated fever with jaundice in an endemic area must cover severe malaria (IV artesunate), Gram-negative sepsis and leptospirosis (ceftriaxone) and scrub typhus or rickettsia (doxycycline) until tests narrow it. Ceftriaxone alone misses malaria and scrub; chloroquine has no role in severe malaria; meropenem with vancomycin is broad yet misses malaria and intracellular organisms.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, approach to diagnosis and management of a patient presenting with fever and thrombocytopenia.`,openingLines:[`Fever with thrombocytopenia - fever with a platelet count below 150,000/uL - is a syndromic presentation of acute febrile illness in India, dominated by dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis and enteric fever.`,`The approach rests on the day of illness, the companion syndrome (bleeding, jaundice, AKI, anaemia), a smear-led first panel and an empirical first hour that covers what kills, while excluding the mimics DIC, TTP and HLH.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`Thrombocytopenia under 150,000/uL; severe under 50,000; bleeding risk under 10,000-20,000.`,`Seasonal post-monsoon burden; dengue the commonest cause in Indian series.`]},{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Infective: dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis, enteric fever, sepsis/DIC, viral (HIV, EBV, hantavirus, KFD).`,`Non-infective mimics: TTP/HUS, HLH, leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, drug-induced, pseudothrombocytopenia.`]},{heading:`Mechanisms`,points:[`Marrow suppression (dengue, viral), immune destruction (dengue, malaria), sequestration (malaria), consumption (DIC, vasculitis), microthrombi (TTP).`]},{heading:`Clinical approach`,points:[`Day of illness, exposure, bleeding, jaundice, urine output, drugs, pregnancy.`,`Examination: vitals and pulse pressure, eschar, conjunctival suffusion, spleen, liver, purpura, fundus.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`First line: CBC with haematocrit and smear, malaria RDT and smear, NS1/IgM, LFT, RFT with potassium, blood cultures, urine, ultrasound.`,`Second line: PT/aPTT/fibrinogen/D-dimer (ISTH DIC score), LDH, retics, schistocytes, PLASMIC, ferritin, MAT, scrub PCR, marrow.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Paracetamol only; oral fluids; no NSAID or IM injection; daily counts in critical phase.`,`Specific: ACT or IV artesunate; doxycycline or azithromycin for scrub and lepto; ceftriaxone or penicillin for severe lepto; azithromycin or ceftriaxone for enteric.`,`Severe undifferentiated: cultures then ceftriaxone plus doxycycline within 1 h, plus artesunate if malaria not excluded.`,`Platelets only for significant bleeding (dengue) or to keep over 50,000 in bleeding DIC; avoid in TTP.`]},{heading:`Red flags, prevention and notification`,points:[`Admit for bleeding, shock, oliguria, ARDS, encephalopathy, jaundice with INR over 1.5.`,`Vector control (NCVBDC), leptospirosis doxycycline prophylaxis, IDSP/IHIP notification.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: fever with thrombocytopenia - stable vs unstable, first panel, empirical first hour, then DIC/TTP/HLH branch`,`Table: dengue vs malaria vs scrub vs leptospirosis vs enteric - clue, lab, test, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical approach`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Management including empirical therapy`,marks:3},{part:`Red flags and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`fever with thrombocytopenia`,`dengue`,`malaria`,`scrub typhus`,`leptospirosis`,`DIC`,`TTP`,`HLH`,`doxycycline`,`artesunate`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate leptospirosis, falciparum malaria and acute viral hepatitis in a patient with fever and jaundice.`,openingLines:[`Fever with jaundice in India is led by acute viral hepatitis, leptospirosis and falciparum malaria; each has a distinct biochemical signature.`,`The discriminators are the timing of fever relative to jaundice, the transaminase-to-bilirubin ratio, the kidney and the blood film.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Leptospirosis: flood/paddy exposure, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, haemoptysis.`,`Malaria: rigors, anaemia, splenomegaly, dark urine.`,`Viral hepatitis: prodrome then jaundice; fever settles as jaundice appears; tender liver.`]},{heading:`Laboratory`,points:[`Leptospirosis: neutrophilia, high bilirubin with ALT under 5x normal, CK raised, hypokalaemic non-oliguric AKI.`,`Malaria: anaemia, mixed bilirubin, ALT under 300, parasites on smear or RDT positive.`,`Viral hepatitis: ALT in thousands, normal creatinine, IgM anti-HAV or anti-HEV.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Leptospirosis: doxycycline; IV penicillin or ceftriaxone if severe; dialysis.`,`Malaria: IV artesunate for severe; ACT for uncomplicated.`,`Viral hepatitis: supportive; watch INR and encephalopathy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table: leptospirosis vs falciparum malaria vs viral hepatitis`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Laboratory differences`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`fever with jaundice`,`Weil disease`,`falciparum malaria`,`hepatitis E`,`CK`,`MAT`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022 (Acute undifferentiated febrile illness)`,`WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever, 2025`,`WHO Guidelines for malaria, 2025 update; NCVBDC National Drug Policy on Malaria and National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027`,`ISTH guidance on sepsis-induced coagulopathy and DIC, 2019; ISTH guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of TTP, 2020`,`Tokyo Guidelines 2018 (TG18) for acute cholangitis`,`Varghese GM et al. Intravenous treatment for scrub typhus (INTREST). N Engl J Med 2025`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022; API Expert Consensus on tropical fevers`]});var f=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d];export{f as default};